window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"IN-1":{"unit":"IN-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01","title":"Skin Layers, Junctions, Dermis, and Adnexa","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter builds the skin from the surface down: the epidermal layers and the keratinocytes that mature through them, the resident melanocytes, Langerhans cells, and Merkel cells, the junctions that hold keratinocytes to each other and to the basement membrane, the dermis and hypodermis, and the glands, hair, and nails. Board questions on skin usually describe a blister, a biopsy report, or a pattern of sweating or hair loss and ask which structure or protein has failed. If you know which protein holds each level together and what happens when it fails, you can predict the split level, the type of blister, how it heals, and the histologic term a pathologist would use.","prerequisites":["Epithelial tissue classification and the general structure of cell junctions","Intermediate filaments, actin, and the cadherin and integrin adhesion families","Collagen types I, III, IV, and VII and where each is found","Sympathetic transmitters and receptors, including the cholinergic sympathetic exception","Type II and type IV hypersensitivity and complement activation"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology","how":"Langerhans cells start the sensitization phase of allergic contact dermatitis, and pemphigus and pemphigoid are antibody-mediated (type II) injuries to adhesion proteins."},{"to":"Neuroscience","how":"Meissner, Pacinian, and Merkel endings feed large myelinated afferents into the dorsal column pathway, while free nerve endings feed the spinothalamic pathway."},{"to":"Biochemistry and genetics","how":"Melanin is made from tyrosine by tyrosinase, and mutations in keratins, laminin 332, and collagens XVII and VII cause the inherited epidermolysis bullosa groups."},{"to":"Endocrinology","how":"Androgens drive sebaceous gland growth and the miniaturization of scalp follicles, and puberty activates apocrine glands."},{"to":"Pulmonary and gastrointestinal pathophysiology","how":"Defective CFTR in the eccrine sweat duct produces the high sweat chloride used to diagnose cystic fibrosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Keratinocyte","def":"The main cell of the epidermis, which divides in the basal layer and matures into a dead, keratin-filled corneocyte as it moves to the surface."},{"term":"Corneocyte","def":"A flat, anucleate cell of the stratum corneum made of keratin filaments inside a cross-linked cornified envelope."},{"term":"Desmosome","def":"A spot junction that joins two cells through desmoglein and desmocollin cadherins and anchors keratin filaments on each side."},{"term":"Hemidesmosome","def":"A junction on the basal surface of basal keratinocytes that attaches their keratin filaments to the basement membrane."},{"term":"Basement membrane zone","def":"The layered junction between epidermis and dermis: lamina lucida, lamina densa, and sublamina densa anchoring fibrils."},{"term":"Epidermal melanin unit","def":"One melanocyte together with the group of keratinocytes, about 30 to 40, to which it supplies melanosomes."},{"term":"Acantholysis","def":"Loss of cohesion between keratinocytes, so that they separate and round up within the epidermis."},{"term":"Nikolsky sign","def":"Separation of the epidermis when apparently normal skin is rubbed sideways, seen when the split is within the epidermis."},{"term":"Holocrine secretion","def":"Secretion in which the whole cell fills with product and breaks down to release it, as in sebaceous glands."},{"term":"Pilosebaceous unit","def":"A hair follicle with its sebaceous gland and arrector pili muscle, the unit involved in acne."},{"term":"Bulge","def":"The region of the outer root sheath at the arrector pili insertion that holds the follicle's stem cells."},{"term":"Telogen effluvium","def":"Diffuse, nonscarring hair shedding that begins about 2 to 3 months after a stressor pushes many follicles into telogen at once."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C01","obj":"IN-1.1","title":"Epidermal layers and keratinocyte maturation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The epidermis is a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with no blood vessels; it is fed by diffusion from capillary loops in the dermal papillae. Most of its cells are keratinocytes, which are born in the deepest layer and pushed toward the surface. Each layer is therefore one stage in the life of the same cell, and its name describes how that stage looks under the microscope."},{"type":"p","text":"The stratum basale is a single row of dividing stem and transit-amplifying cells on the basement membrane. They make keratins 5 and 14, a type II and type I pair that forms a flexible filament network anchored to hemidesmosomes below and desmosomes at the sides. As daughter cells enter the stratum spinosum they switch to keratins 1 and 10 and build thicker filament bundles that insert into many desmosomes. When cells shrink during tissue processing these junctions stretch into the short projections that give the layer its name."},{"type":"p","text":"In the stratum granulosum the cells assemble the barrier. Basophilic keratohyalin granules hold profilaggrin and loricrin; profilaggrin is cleaved to filaggrin, which packs keratin filaments into dense bundles, and its breakdown products help the corneum hold water. Lamellar bodies discharge lipid between the cells, where enzymes convert it to ceramides, cholesterol, and free fatty acids that form sheets blocking water loss. Transglutaminase cross-links involucrin, loricrin, and other proteins beneath the plasma membrane into the cornified envelope, and the cell then destroys its nucleus and organelles in a specialized form of programmed death."},{"type":"p","text":"The stratum corneum is made of flat, anucleate corneocytes set in lipid, often described as bricks and mortar. Proteases slowly digest the remaining desmosomes so single cells shed from the surface, and the trip from basal layer to shedding takes about 4 weeks. Thick skin of the palms and soles adds a thin, pale-staining stratum lucidum between the granulosum and corneum, has a far thicker corneum, and lacks hair follicles."},{"type":"table","title":"Epidermal layers, deep to surface","cols":["Layer","Keratinocyte state","Key molecules and structures"],"rows":[["Stratum basale","Single row of dividing cells on the basement membrane","Keratins 5 and 14; hemidesmosomes"],["Stratum spinosum","Several rows of polygonal cells joined by many desmosomes","Keratins 1 and 10; desmosomes"],["Stratum granulosum","Flattening cells assembling the barrier","Keratohyalin granules (profilaggrin, loricrin); lamellar bodies"],["Stratum lucidum","Thin, clear band; thick skin only","Densely packed keratin"],["Stratum corneum","Flat dead corneocytes, no nuclei","Cornified envelope; intercellular lipid sheets"]],"caption":"Surface to deep, the order is corneum, lucidum (thick skin only), granulosum, spinosum, basale."},{"type":"flow","title":"Keratinocyte maturation, basale to corneum","steps":["Basal stem cell divides, expressing keratins 5 and 14","Daughter cell enters spinosum and switches to keratins 1 and 10","Granulosum: keratohyalin granules form, lamellar bodies release lipid","Cornified envelope cross-linked; nucleus and organelles degraded","Anucleate corneocyte reaches the surface and is shed"],"caption":"The whole journey takes about 4 weeks in normal skin and only a few days in psoriasis."},{"type":"p","text":"A defect at each stage produces a distinct disease. Keratin 5 or 14 mutations make basal cells fragile (epidermolysis bullosa simplex, C04), and keratin 1 or 10 mutations make suprabasal cells fragile (epidermolytic ichthyosis). Loss-of-function filaggrin mutations weaken the barrier, causing ichthyosis vulgaris and raising the risk of atopic dermatitis. In psoriasis, transit shortens so much that cells reach the surface with nuclei still present (C08)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If lamellar bodies could not deliver their lipids but keratin and the cornified envelope formed normally, how would the skin behave?","explain":"Corneocytes would form, but without lipid sheets between them water would leak out freely. Transepidermal water loss rises and the skin becomes dry, scaly, and easily penetrated. Harlequin ichthyosis, caused by ABCA12 mutations that block lipid loading of lamellar bodies, is the severe inherited form.","check":"The stratum granulosum paragraph and the bricks and mortar description of the corneum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C02","obj":"IN-1.2","title":"Melanocytes, Langerhans cells, and Merkel cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three non-keratinocyte cells live in the epidermis, and each sits where its job requires. They are hard to tell apart on routine stains, so boards identify them by layer, origin, marker, and function."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanocytes arise from neural crest cells that migrate to the skin during embryonic life and settle in the stratum basale, roughly 1 for every 4 to 10 basal keratinocytes depending on region. They attach to the basement membrane but not to keratinocytes by desmosomes, so they look like pale clear cells on routine stains. Inside membrane-bound melanosomes, tyrosinase converts tyrosine to melanin. Melanocytes then extend dendrites between keratinocytes, and the keratinocytes take up the melanosome-filled tips. In each keratinocyte the melanin gathers over the sun-facing side of the nucleus as a cap that absorbs ultraviolet light before it reaches DNA."},{"type":"flow","title":"Melanin synthesis and transfer","steps":["Tyrosinase oxidizes tyrosine to DOPA, then DOPA to dopaquinone","Dopaquinone forms eumelanin, or pheomelanin when cysteine is added","Melanosomes mature and move out along melanocyte dendrites","Keratinocytes engulf dendrite tips containing melanosomes","Melanin forms a cap over each keratinocyte nucleus"],"caption":"Alpha-MSH acting on the melanocortin 1 receptor favors brown-black eumelanin; ultraviolet light increases tyrosinase activity and transfer, producing a tan."},{"type":"p","text":"Skin color differs between people because of the number, size, melanin type, and breakdown rate of melanosomes, not because of melanocyte number, which is about the same in all skin colors. This explains two contrasting white-skin disorders. In oculocutaneous albinism type 1, melanocytes are present in normal numbers but tyrosinase is defective, so they cannot make pigment. In vitiligo, autoimmune destruction removes the melanocytes themselves."},{"type":"p","text":"Langerhans cells are dendritic antigen-presenting cells, mainly in the stratum spinosum, that are classically described as bone marrow derived. They express CD1a, langerin (CD207), and S-100, and on electron microscopy they contain rod-shaped Birbeck granules with a terminal bulb, the tennis-racket shape. They capture antigens and haptens, travel through dermal lymphatics to regional lymph nodes, and present them to naive T cells. This is the sensitization step of allergic contact dermatitis, a type IV reaction. Langerhans cell histiocytosis is a clonal proliferation of cells with the same markers."},{"type":"p","text":"Merkel cells sit in the stratum basale, joined to keratinocytes by desmosomes, and contain dense-core neurosecretory granules. Each contacts the expanded disc ending of a large myelinated sensory axon, forming a slowly adapting mechanoreceptor that signals sustained pressure, edges, and texture, so they are most numerous in fingertips and lips. Merkel cell carcinoma is an aggressive neuroendocrine skin cancer, often linked to Merkel cell polyomavirus."},{"type":"table","title":"Resident non-keratinocyte cells","cols":["Cell","Layer","Origin","Identifying features","Function"],"rows":[["Melanocyte","Stratum basale","Neural crest","Pale cell with dendrites; melanosomes; tyrosinase","Makes melanin and transfers it to keratinocytes"],["Langerhans cell","Mainly stratum spinosum","Bone marrow (classic teaching)","Birbeck granules; CD1a, langerin (CD207), S-100","Captures antigen and presents it in lymph nodes"],["Merkel cell","Stratum basale","Debated: epidermal or neural crest","Neurosecretory granules; contact with sensory disc endings","Slowly adapting touch receptor"]],"caption":"Two cells sit in the basal layer (melanocytes and Merkel cells); the antigen-presenting cell sits higher, in the spinosum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C03","obj":"IN-1.3","title":"Desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, adherens junctions, and autoimmune blistering","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The epidermis resists shearing because junctions tie the keratin network of every keratinocyte to its neighbors and to the basement membrane, turning the sheet into one mechanical unit. Remove any link and the tissue tears at that point, filling with fluid as a blister. The level of the tear identifies the protein that failed."},{"type":"p","text":"Desmosomes join keratinocyte to keratinocyte. Their calcium-dependent cadherins, desmogleins and desmocollins, bridge the space between cells, and a cytoplasmic plaque of plakoglobin, plakophilin, and desmoplakin anchors them to keratin. Desmoglein 3 is concentrated in the lower epidermis and in oral mucosa, while desmoglein 1 is concentrated in the upper epidermis and is scarce in mucosa. Adherens junctions also join neighboring cells, but through E-cadherin linked by catenins to actin; they organize the sheet and its signaling, and loss of E-cadherin lets carcinoma cells detach and invade. Hemidesmosomes join the basal cell to the basement membrane: integrin alpha6 beta4 binds laminin 332, the transmembrane protein BP180 (collagen XVII) reaches into the lamina lucida, and BP230 and plectin link these to keratins 5 and 14 inside the cell."},{"type":"table","title":"Keratinocyte junctions","cols":["Junction","Connects","Adhesion proteins","Cytoskeleton","Disease when targeted"],"rows":[["Desmosome","Cell to cell","Desmogleins 1 and 3, desmocollins","Keratin, through desmoplakin","Pemphigus vulgaris and foliaceus; staphylococcal scalded skin"],["Adherens junction","Cell to cell","E-cadherin","Actin, through catenins","Loss of E-cadherin aids carcinoma invasion"],["Hemidesmosome","Basal cell to basement membrane","BP180 (collagen XVII), BP230, integrin alpha6 beta4","Keratins 5 and 14, through plectin and BP230","Bullous pemphigoid; junctional epidermolysis bullosa"]],"caption":"Desmosome problems split the epidermis from within; hemidesmosome problems lift the whole epidermis off the basement membrane."},{"type":"p","text":"In pemphigus vulgaris, IgG autoantibodies against desmoglein 3, often with desmoglein 1, cause acantholysis just above the basal layer. The basal cells stay attached by their hemidesmosomes and line the floor in a tombstone row. The roof is thin, so blisters are flaccid and rupture into erosions, oral lesions are nearly universal, the Nikolsky sign is positive, and direct immunofluorescence shows IgG between keratinocytes in a net-like pattern. In pemphigus foliaceus, antibodies only against desmoglein 1 split the epidermis just under the stratum corneum, producing crusted superficial erosions without mouth involvement. Staphylococcal exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1 at the same level, causing bullous impetigo and scalded skin syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"In bullous pemphigoid, IgG against BP180 and BP230 separates the entire epidermis from the basement membrane. The roof is full thickness, so blisters are tense, the Nikolsky sign is negative, mucosa is less often involved, and patients are usually older adults. Immunofluorescence shows linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane, and biopsies contain many eosinophils."},{"type":"flow","title":"Bullous pemphigoid pathogenesis","steps":["IgG autoantibodies bind BP180 and BP230 at hemidesmosomes","Bound antibody activates complement along the basement membrane","Mast cells degranulate; eosinophils and neutrophils are recruited","Released proteases cleave the hemidesmosome attachments","Subepidermal split forms a tense, fluid-filled blister"],"caption":"Complement and inflammatory cells, not antibody binding alone, cause most of the damage, which is why linear C3 accompanies IgG."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient whose antibodies target only desmoglein 1 have skin blisters but a normal mouth?","explain":"Oral mucosa contains abundant desmoglein 3 and little desmoglein 1, so intact desmoglein 3 keeps mucosal cells attached. In skin, the upper epidermis relies mainly on desmoglein 1, so it splits there. When antibodies to desmoglein 3 are added, the mucosa fails as well, as in pemphigus vulgaris.","check":"The distribution of desmogleins 1 and 3 and the pemphigus paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C04","obj":"IN-1.4","title":"The dermal-epidermal junction and the level of the split","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Below the basal keratinocyte lies the basement membrane zone, which electron microscopy resolves into three layers. The lamina lucida is a pale band crossed by anchoring filaments of laminin 332 and the extracellular part of collagen XVII, which tie hemidesmosomes downward. The lamina densa is a sheet of type IV collagen with nidogen and perlecan, the same network found in all basement membranes. Below it, anchoring fibrils of type VII collagen loop from the lamina densa into the papillary dermis and trap its type I and III collagen fibers."},{"type":"p","text":"Each component is a link in a chain, and the chain breaks at the weakest link. A blister therefore forms just above the structure that still holds, and its floor and roof tell you the level. Inherited defects (epidermolysis bullosa) and autoantibodies follow the same map."},{"type":"table","title":"Split level by failed component","cols":["Level of split","Failed protein","Inherited disease","Acquired disease","Healing"],"rows":[["Within basal keratinocytes","Keratin 5 or 14; plectin","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","None classic","Usually without scarring"],["Lamina lucida","Laminin 332, collagen XVII, integrin alpha6 beta4","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa","Bullous pemphigoid (BP180, BP230)","Usually without scarring; atrophy possible in junctional forms"],["Below the lamina densa","Type VII collagen","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Scarring and milia"]],"caption":"Deeper splits tear the papillary dermis, so they heal with scarring; splits above the lamina densa leave the basement membrane on the floor."},{"type":"p","text":"Depth explains the clinical picture. Splits at or above the lamina densa leave an intact basement membrane on which keratinocytes can migrate, so they usually heal without scars. Splits below it disrupt the papillary dermis, so they heal with scarring and milia (small keratin cysts), and the recessive form of dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa produces fused fingers and a high risk of squamous cell carcinoma in chronic wounds. Integrin alpha6 beta4 defects cause junctional disease with pyloric atresia because the same integrin anchors gut epithelium. Antibodies to type IV collagen do not blister the skin: anti-glomerular basement membrane disease targets the alpha3 chain, which is present in kidney and lung basement membranes but not in the epidermal lamina densa."},{"type":"example","title":"Using the split level to separate two lookalike diseases","text":"Two adults have tense subepidermal blisters with linear IgG along the basement membrane. One heals cleanly; the other heals with scars and milia on the hands and elbows. Scarring means a split below the lamina densa, so the second patient's antibodies target type VII collagen (epidermolysis bullosa acquisita), not BP180 or BP230. The lab can confirm this with salt-split skin: incubating normal skin in 1 M sodium chloride separates it through the lamina lucida. Bullous pemphigoid antibodies bind the roof (the hemidesmosome side), while antibodies to type VII collagen bind the floor (the dermal side)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C05","obj":"IN-1.5","title":"Papillary dermis, reticular dermis, and hypodermis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The dermis is the mesoderm-derived connective tissue that gives skin its strength, recoil, blood supply, and most of its sensation. It has two layers built for different loads, and beneath it the hypodermis (subcutis) joins skin to deeper fascia."},{"type":"p","text":"The papillary dermis is a thin layer of loose connective tissue with fine type III and type I collagen, thin elastic fibers, and many fibroblasts, mast cells, and macrophages. It rises into dermal papillae that interlock with the downward epidermal ridges (rete ridges), which enlarges the contact surface and resists shear. Capillary loops in each papilla nourish the avascular epidermis by diffusion, and in hairless (glabrous) skin many papillae hold a Meissner corpuscle."},{"type":"p","text":"The reticular dermis is much thicker dense irregular connective tissue. Coarse bundles of type I collagen give tensile strength, coarse elastic fibers give recoil, and the bases of hair follicles and glands sit here. Collagen bundles run in preferred directions that form the skin tension (cleavage) lines, so an incision along them gapes less and leaves a finer scar. The hypodermis is not part of the skin proper; it is loose connective tissue and fat lobules divided by fibrous septa that insulate, store energy, cushion, and carry the larger vessels and nerves."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers beneath the epidermis","cols":["Layer","Connective tissue","Vessels","Receptors and contents"],"rows":[["Papillary dermis","Loose; fine type III and I collagen, thin elastic fibers","Capillary loops in papillae; superficial (subpapillary) plexus at its base","Meissner corpuscles in glabrous skin; free nerve endings"],["Reticular dermis","Dense irregular; coarse type I collagen, coarse elastic fibers","Deep plexus at the dermis-hypodermis border","Follicle bases, glands, Ruffini endings, some Pacinian corpuscles"],["Hypodermis","Loose tissue with fat lobules and fibrous septa","Larger arteries and veins","Pacinian corpuscles; deep coils of some sweat glands"]],"caption":"Two horizontal vascular plexuses, connected by vertical vessels, supply the dermis; the epidermis itself has no vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"Arteriovenous anastomoses, most numerous in the fingers, toes, and ears, let sympathetic nerves divert blood toward or away from the surface plexus, which is a major route of heat loss. Receptor depth follows function. Meissner corpuscles sit just under the epidermis and adapt quickly, signaling light touch and low-frequency flutter. Pacinian corpuscles lie deep, and their onion-like capsule filters out steady pressure so they respond only to rapid change and high-frequency vibration. Free nerve endings of thin A delta and C fibers extend into the epidermis and signal pain, temperature, and itch."},{"type":"table","title":"Cutaneous receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Adaptation","Main modality","Afferent fiber"],"rows":[["Meissner corpuscle","Dermal papillae of glabrous skin","Rapid","Light touch, low-frequency vibration","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Merkel cell-neurite complex","Stratum basale","Slow","Sustained pressure, texture, edges","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Ruffini ending","Reticular dermis","Slow","Skin stretch","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Pacinian corpuscle","Deep dermis and hypodermis","Very rapid","High-frequency vibration, rapid pressure change","Large myelinated (A beta)"],["Free nerve ending","Dermis, extending into epidermis","Mostly slow","Pain, temperature, itch","Thin myelinated (A delta) and unmyelinated (C)"]],"caption":"Superficial receptors give fine spatial detail; deep receptors sense vibration transmitted through tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Each layer has its own disorders. Chronic sun exposure degrades papillary dermal collagen and deposits abnormal elastic material (solar elastosis), producing wrinkles. Inherited collagen defects such as Ehlers-Danlos syndrome make the dermis hyperextensible and fragile. Inflammation centered in the hypodermal septa, as in erythema nodosum, produces tender nodules rather than surface change."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C06","obj":"IN-1.6","title":"Eccrine, apocrine, and sebaceous glands","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Skin glands are separated by three questions: where they open, how they release product, and what controls them. Eccrine glands open directly onto the surface; apocrine and sebaceous glands usually open into hair follicles, so neither is found on the palms and soles."},{"type":"table","title":"Skin glands compared","cols":["Gland","Location and opening","Secretion","Control","Linked disorder"],"rows":[["Eccrine","Nearly all skin, densest on palms, soles, forehead; opens on surface","Watery sweat; merocrine","Sympathetic cholinergic fibers on muscarinic (M3) receptors","Hyperhidrosis; salty sweat in cystic fibrosis"],["Apocrine","Axilla, areola, anogenital skin; opens into follicle","Viscous, odorless until skin bacteria act on it","Mainly adrenergic; active after puberty","Body odor"],["Sebaceous","All hair-bearing skin; opens into follicle; none on palms and soles","Sebum; holocrine","Androgens","Acne"]],"caption":"Modified apocrine glands include ceruminous glands of the ear canal and glands of Moll in the eyelid; meibomian glands are modified sebaceous glands that open directly on the lid margin."},{"type":"p","text":"Eccrine glands are the main tool of evaporative cooling. A coiled secretory portion in the deep dermis makes a fluid close to plasma in sodium and chloride, and the duct then reabsorbs sodium through ENaC and chloride through CFTR, so normal sweat reaching the surface is hypotonic. In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR blocks ductal chloride reabsorption, which is why sweat chloride is high (about 60 mmol/L or more supports the diagnosis). These glands break the usual sympathetic rule: postganglionic sympathetic fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. Hyperhidrosis is sweating beyond thermal need; the primary focal form affects palms, soles, and axillae through excess sympathetic drive to normal glands, while secondary generalized forms follow fever, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia, or pheochromocytoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Apocrine glands have a wide lumen and empty into the follicle above the sebaceous duct. They enlarge at puberty and respond mainly to adrenergic stimulation such as emotional stress. Sebaceous glands release sebum (triglycerides, wax esters, squalene) by holocrine secretion: basal cells divide, fill with lipid, die, and break apart. Androgens, largely converted to dihydrotestosterone within the gland, enlarge sebaceous glands and increase output, which explains their activity at birth (maternal hormones) and puberty."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of acne","steps":["Androgens enlarge sebaceous glands and increase sebum output","Abnormal keratinization plugs the follicle, forming a microcomedo","Cutibacterium acnes multiplies in the lipid-rich, low-oxygen plug","Bacterial lipases and TLR2 signaling trigger inflammation","Follicle wall ruptures into dermis, forming papules, pustules, nodules"],"caption":"Acne is a disease of the pilosebaceous unit, so it spares the palms and soles, which have no sebaceous glands."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A person taking a drug with strong antimuscarinic action exercises in hot weather. What happens to sweating and body temperature?","explain":"Eccrine glands are driven by acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors even though the fibers are sympathetic, so sweating stops. Without evaporative cooling, heat is retained and core temperature rises, and the skin is hot, dry, and flushed. Apocrine secretion, which is mainly adrenergic, is little affected and contributes almost nothing to cooling.","check":"The control column of the gland table and the eccrine paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C07","obj":"IN-1.7","title":"Hair follicle, hair cycle, and nail unit","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A hair follicle is a downgrowth of epidermis into the dermis. Its deepest part, the bulb, holds matrix cells that divide rapidly around the dermal papilla, a cluster of specialized fibroblasts and capillaries whose signals start growth and set hair size. Matrix cells form the hair shaft and the inner root sheath, and bulb melanocytes color the shaft. The outer root sheath is continuous with the epidermis. Where the arrector pili muscle inserts, the outer root sheath forms the bulge, which holds multipotent stem cells that rebuild the lower follicle at each new cycle and help repair the epidermis after wounds."},{"type":"p","text":"The arrector pili is smooth muscle running from the bulge to the papillary dermis. Sympathetic alpha1-adrenergic stimulation contracts it, raising the hair and pressing on the sebaceous gland. Diseases that destroy the bulge cause scarring, permanent alopecia, while telogen effluvium, alopecia areata, and androgenetic alopecia spare the bulge and allow regrowth."},{"type":"table","title":"The scalp hair cycle","cols":["Phase","Duration","Share of scalp hairs","What happens"],"rows":[["Anagen","About 2 to 7 years","About 85 to 90%","Matrix cells divide and the shaft lengthens; duration sets maximum length"],["Catagen","About 2 to 3 weeks","About 1%","Matrix cells undergo apoptosis; lower follicle regresses toward the bulge"],["Telogen","About 3 months","About 10 to 15%","Club hair rests, then is shed as a new anagen hair grows beneath it"]],"caption":"Shedding about 50 to 100 scalp hairs a day is normal because follicles cycle out of step with each other."},{"type":"flow","title":"Telogen effluvium","steps":["Stressor: high fever, childbirth, surgery, severe illness, crash diet","Many anagen follicles enter catagen early and together","Follicles rest in telogen for about 3 months","New anagen hairs push out the club hairs","Diffuse shedding begins 2 to 3 months after the stressor"],"caption":"The bulge is spared, so the loss is nonscarring and hair regrows over months once the stressor is gone."},{"type":"example","title":"Using timing to name the hair loss","text":"A woman notices diffuse shedding of whole hairs with small white bulbs 3 months after delivering a baby. Another patient loses most of her scalp hair 2 weeks after starting chemotherapy. Work from the cycle. Club hairs that fall about 3 months after a stressor are telogen hairs, so the first patient has telogen effluvium. Chemotherapy halts mitosis in matrix cells of growing anagen follicles, so the shaft narrows and breaks within days to weeks, which is anagen effluvium. The delay is the length of telogen; the speed is the dependence of anagen on dividing cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The nail plate is hard keratin made by the nail matrix, which lies under the proximal nail fold; the pale lunula is the visible distal edge of the matrix. The plate slides forward over the nail bed, whose vascular dermis makes the nail look pink, and its free edge overhangs the hyponychium. The cuticle, made by the proximal fold (eponychium), seals the plate, and lateral folds frame its sides. Fingernails grow about 3 mm per month and toenails about 1 mm per month. Matrix injury causes permanent deformity, and a brief arrest of matrix growth during severe illness leaves a transverse groove (Beau line), the nail counterpart of telogen effluvium."},{"type":"p","text":"An ingrown nail usually involves the great toe. When the lateral edge of the plate is cut short and curved, or compressed by tight footwear, it grows into the soft tissue of the lateral nail fold. The plate then acts as a foreign body, provoking inflammation, granulation tissue, and often bacterial infection of the fold (paronychia)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-01-01-C08","obj":"IN-1.8","title":"Histologic terms in skin pathology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dermatopathology terms name the abnormal layer or process, so each points to a mechanism: a layer too thick, a maturation step skipped, or cell connections failing."},{"type":"table","title":"Six core terms","cols":["Term","Meaning","Mechanism","Example diseases"],"rows":[["Hyperkeratosis","Thickened stratum corneum","More corneocytes made, or normal ones shed too slowly","Callus, ichthyosis vulgaris, psoriasis"],["Parakeratosis","Nuclei retained in the stratum corneum","Maturation so fast that nuclei are not destroyed","Psoriasis, actinic keratosis"],["Acanthosis","Thickened stratum spinosum (epidermal hyperplasia)","Increased keratinocyte proliferation","Psoriasis, seborrheic keratosis, chronic eczema"],["Spongiosis","Fluid between keratinocytes that remain attached","Intercellular edema from epidermal inflammation","Atopic and allergic contact dermatitis"],["Acantholysis","Keratinocytes lose attachment and separate","Desmosome failure, usually from anti-desmoglein antibodies","Pemphigus vulgaris and foliaceus"],["Dyskeratosis","Single keratinocytes keratinize early and abnormally","Disordered maturation of individual cells","Actinic keratosis, squamous cell carcinoma, Darier disease"]],"caption":"Two pairs are often confused: hyperkeratosis (corneum) versus acanthosis (spinosum), and spongiosis (edema, cells attached) versus acantholysis (cells detached)."},{"type":"p","text":"Psoriasis shows how several terms arise from one mechanism. Dendritic cells release IL-23, which sustains Th17 cells, and their IL-17 and IL-22 drive keratinocyte proliferation. Transit through the epidermis falls from about 4 weeks to a few days, so the spinous layer thickens with long, regular rete ridges (acanthosis), cells reach the surface without passing through a normal granular layer, and nuclei are retained (parakeratosis) in a thick scale. Neutrophils collect in the corneum as Munro microabscesses. Capillaries in the papillae dilate while the epidermis over them thins, so lifting a scale causes pinpoint bleeding (the Auspitz sign)."},{"type":"p","text":"Eczematous dermatitis is the prototype of spongiosis. Inflammation, from Th2 responses in atopic dermatitis or hapten-specific T cells in allergic contact dermatitis, lets fluid enter the epidermis and push keratinocytes apart while their desmosomes hold, so the junctions appear prominent. Severe spongiosis forms small intraepidermal vesicles, and chronic scratching adds acanthosis and hyperkeratosis (lichenification). Acantholysis differs because adhesion itself is lost: in pemphigus, detached keratinocytes round up and float freely in the blister. Dyskeratosis signals disordered maturation of individual cells and, with atypia, is a feature of dysplasia and squamous cell carcinoma, where keratin pearls form."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Skin structure ties the Part I disciplines together through one idea: each level is held by specific proteins, and failure of a protein produces a predictable lesion. Embryology supplies the cells, with ectoderm forming keratinocytes, neural crest forming melanocytes, mesoderm forming the dermis, and hematopoietic precursors supplying Langerhans cells. Biochemistry and genetics explain inherited fragility (keratins, laminin 332, collagens XVII and VII), barrier failure (filaggrin), and pigment disorders (tyrosinase). Immunology explains the acquired forms, with type II autoantibodies against desmogleins and hemidesmosome proteins and type IV sensitization through Langerhans cells. Microbiology appears where bacterial toxins cleave desmoglein 1 and where Cutibacterium acnes inflames a plugged follicle."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology uses the same map in reverse. A biopsy reports a split level and a histologic term, and immunofluorescence reports the pattern of antibody, and from these the target protein can be inferred. The limits are real: clinical blister type overlaps, early lesions may lack the classic picture, and some diseases involve more than one antigen, so split level, immunofluorescence, and the clinical setting are read together rather than relying on any single finding."}],"summary":["Keratinocytes mature from basale to corneum over about 4 weeks, switching from keratins 5 and 14 to 1 and 10; stratum lucidum appears only in thick skin of the palms and soles.","The barrier forms in the granulosum: filaggrin bundles keratin, lamellar bodies secrete lipid, and transglutaminase builds the cornified envelope.","Melanocytes (neural crest, basal layer) transfer melanosomes to keratinocytes; skin color reflects melanosome activity, not melanocyte number.","Langerhans cells (CD1a, langerin, Birbeck granules) carry antigen to lymph nodes; Merkel cells are slowly adapting touch receptors in the basal layer.","Anti-desmoglein antibodies split the epidermis from within (pemphigus, flaccid blisters, Nikolsky positive); anti-BP180 and BP230 antibodies lift it off the basement membrane (bullous pemphigoid, tense blisters).","Split level predicts the protein: keratin 5 or 14 within basal cells, laminin 332 or collagen XVII in the lamina lucida, type VII collagen below the lamina densa with scarring and milia.","The papillary dermis is loose with capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles; the reticular dermis is dense type I collagen; Pacinian corpuscles lie deep.","Eccrine glands are sympathetic but muscarinic and open on the surface; apocrine and sebaceous glands open into follicles, and androgen-driven sebum underlies acne.","Telogen effluvium appears about 2 to 3 months after a stressor because telogen lasts about 3 months; bulge destruction causes permanent, scarring alopecia.","Hyperkeratosis and acanthosis describe thick corneum and spinosum; parakeratosis means retained nuclei; spongiosis is edema with cells attached, acantholysis is cells detached."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Dendritic cells and antigen presentation; hypersensitivity diseases"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic sensations, tactile receptors; body temperature regulation and sweating"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Amino acid conversion to specialized products, melanin synthesis"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Integumentary system"}]},"IN-10":{"unit":"IN-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01","title":"Skin Trauma and Wound Healing","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Skin repairs itself in a fixed order: a clot, an inflammatory cleanup, a vascular and fibroblastic rebuild, and months of quiet remodeling that converts granulation tissue into scar. This chapter follows that sequence cell by cell and molecule by molecule, then uses it to explain why a sutured wound and an open one heal differently, why infection, ischemia, diabetes, glucocorticoids, and vitamin C or zinc deficiency stall repair, and how scars become hypertrophic, keloidal, or contracted. It closes with the injuries that test the same machinery under extreme load: burns, electrical and lightning current, ionizing radiation, sustained pressure, auricular hematoma, and brown recluse envenomation.","prerequisites":["Layers of the epidermis and dermis, adnexal structures, and the dermal-epidermal junction (IN-1)","Acute inflammation: vascular changes, leukocyte margination, and chemotaxis","Platelet activation, the coagulation cascade, and fibrinolysis","Collagen synthesis, hydroxylation, and cross-linking, and the roles of vitamin C, copper, and zinc (IN-5)","Cell cycle control and the radiosensitivity of rapidly dividing tissue"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology and inflammation (IM units)","how":"Neutrophil then macrophage recruitment, and the switch of macrophages toward a growth-factor-secreting phenotype, is the same program that governs resolution versus fibrosis anywhere in the body."},{"to":"Renal physiology and pathology (RENL units)","how":"Rhabdomyolysis after electrical injury or prolonged pressure delivers filtered myoglobin to the tubules, which explains pigmented casts and acute kidney injury."},{"to":"Cardiovascular physiology (CARD units)","how":"Burn shock is a distributive and hypovolemic problem driven by capillary leak, and electrical current reaches the heart during its vulnerable period to cause fibrillation."},{"to":"Endocrine and metabolic physiology (ENDO units)","how":"The catecholamine, cortisol, and glucagon surge after a large burn produces the hypermetabolic, protein-catabolic state, and hyperglycemia in diabetes impairs leukocyte function and microvascular flow."},{"to":"Neoplasia and skin cancer (IN-12, IN-13)","how":"Chronic unstable burn scars and irradiated skin accumulate mutations and give rise to squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma, and angiosarcoma years after the injury."}],"terms":[{"term":"Granulation tissue","def":"The pink, soft, moist repair tissue of proliferating fibroblasts, new capillaries, and loose type III collagen matrix that fills a tissue defect."},{"term":"Myofibroblast","def":"A fibroblast that expresses alpha smooth muscle actin and generates the contractile force that shrinks an open wound."},{"term":"Angiogenesis","def":"Formation of new capillaries from existing vessels, driven in wounds mainly by vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) released under hypoxic and macrophage-derived signals."},{"term":"Re-epithelialization","def":"Migration and proliferation of keratinocytes from the wound margins and residual adnexa to restore a continuous epidermal barrier."},{"term":"Matrix metalloproteinase (MMP)","def":"A zinc-dependent protease that degrades collagen and other matrix proteins, allowing cell migration during repair and collagen turnover during remodeling."},{"term":"Primary intention","def":"Healing of a clean, approximated wound with minimal granulation tissue and a thin linear scar."},{"term":"Secondary intention","def":"Healing of an open defect by abundant granulation tissue, wound contraction, and a larger scar."},{"term":"Eschar","def":"Adherent, avascular, denatured tissue covering a deep burn or necrotic wound, impenetrable to leukocytes and systemically delivered agents."},{"term":"Keloid","def":"An abnormal scar of disorganized type I and type III collagen that grows beyond the original wound borders and recurs after excision."},{"term":"Contracture","def":"A fixed deformity and loss of joint motion produced by excessive and persistent wound contraction and scar shortening."},{"term":"Dehiscence","def":"Separation of a surgical wound because mechanical load exceeds the tensile strength of the immature scar."},{"term":"Obliterative endarteritis","def":"Progressive intimal thickening and luminal narrowing of small arteries after radiation injury, producing chronic ischemia, fibrosis, and poor healing."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C01","obj":"IN-10.1","title":"The four phases of cutaneous wound healing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Repair of a full-thickness skin wound runs through four phases: hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling. They overlap in time but not in sequence, and each produces the signals that recruit the cells of the next, so a block at any step stalls everything downstream."},{"type":"p","text":"Hemostasis begins within seconds. Transected vessels constrict, platelets adhere to exposed subendothelial collagen and von Willebrand factor, aggregate, and degranulate, and the coagulation cascade converts fibrinogen to a fibrin mesh. The clot does two jobs: it stops bleeding and becomes the provisional scaffold that migrating cells crawl through. Platelet alpha granules release platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta), the first chemotactic and fibrogenic signals of repair."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammation dominates the first three days. Neutrophils arrive within 6 to 24 hours and debride the wound by phagocytosis and by releasing proteases and reactive oxygen species. Monocyte-derived macrophages predominate by about day 3. The macrophage is the pivotal cell of repair: it completes debridement, including clearance of apoptotic neutrophils, then shifts to a reparative phenotype secreting PDGF, TGF-beta, fibroblast growth factor, and VEGF. Experimentally macrophage-depleted wounds form little granulation tissue, and wounds with persistent neutrophilic inflammation, as in chronic infection, stay stuck in this phase."},{"type":"p","text":"Proliferation runs from about day 3 to week 3. Fibroblasts migrate along the fibrin scaffold, proliferate under PDGF and FGF, and deposit a loose provisional matrix of fibronectin, hyaluronan, proteoglycans, and type III collagen. VEGF, released in response to hypoxia and macrophage signals, drives capillary sprouting, and the immature leakiness of those vessels is why granulation tissue is edematous. Keratinocytes at the margins lose their anchorage, migrate across the moist wound bed, then proliferate to restore the barrier. Myofibroblasts appear and begin to contract the defect."},{"type":"p","text":"Remodeling takes weeks to more than a year. Zinc-dependent matrix metalloproteinases, held in check by tissue inhibitors of metalloproteinases, degrade the provisional matrix while fibroblasts replace type III collagen with thicker, cross-linked type I collagen aligned along lines of stress. Capillaries regress and the scar pales. Tensile strength tracks this chemistry rather than the appearance of the wound: about 10 percent of normal skin at one week, rising rapidly over the next four weeks, then plateauing at about 70 to 80 percent by three months. Full strength never returns because the original basket-weave collagen architecture and the adnexa are not rebuilt."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sequence of cutaneous wound healing","steps":["Hemostasis: platelet plug and fibrin clot; PDGF and TGF-beta released","Neutrophils enter within 6 to 24 hours and debride","Macrophages predominate by day 3; secrete PDGF, TGF-beta, FGF, VEGF","Fibroblasts lay down type III collagen; VEGF drives new capillaries","Keratinocytes re-epithelialize; myofibroblasts contract the defect","MMPs remodel matrix; cross-linked type I collagen replaces type III"],"caption":"Each phase generates the signals that recruit the cells of the next, so repair is a chain."},{"type":"table","title":"Phases of healing: timing, cells, and products","cols":["Phase","Timing","Dominant cells","Key mediators","Matrix and outcome"],"rows":[["Hemostasis","Seconds to minutes","Platelets","PDGF, TGF-beta, thromboxane A2","Fibrin clot as scaffold"],["Inflammation","Day 1 to day 3","Neutrophils, then macrophages","IL-1, TNF, chemokines, complement","Debridement; wound still weak"],["Proliferation","Day 3 to week 3","Fibroblasts, endothelium, keratinocytes, myofibroblasts","VEGF, FGF, PDGF, TGF-beta","Granulation tissue, type III collagen"],["Remodeling","Week 3 to over a year","Fibroblasts","MMPs and their inhibitors","Cross-linked type I collagen, 70 to 80 percent strength"]],"caption":"Questions often give a day or week since injury and ask for the dominant cell, collagen type, or expected strength."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A wound is 8 days old and the surface looks closed, yet its tensile strength is far below normal. Why does appearance mislead?","explain":"Why does strength lag epithelial closure by weeks to months?","check":"Compare the proliferation and remodeling rows of the phase table with the collagen types in the remodeling paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C02","obj":"IN-10.2","title":"Primary versus secondary intention, and what delays healing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The same four phases run in every wound, but the size of the defect decides how much of each is required. In healing by primary intention the edges of a clean, narrow wound are approximated, so a small clot bridges the gap, epithelium closes a short distance within 24 to 48 hours, granulation tissue is scant, and a thin linear scar results. In healing by secondary intention a large defect must be filled, so inflammation is more intense and prolonged, granulation tissue is abundant, and myofibroblast contraction shrinks an open wound to a fraction of its original area. Contraction is useful because it reduces the volume to fill and the surface to re-epithelialize, but the same force produces disabling contractures across joints, palms, and the anterior neck."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary versus secondary intention","cols":["Feature","Primary intention","Secondary intention"],"rows":[["Wound","Clean, narrow, edges approximated","Large defect, open, often contaminated"],["Inflammatory phase","Brief and limited","Intense and prolonged"],["Granulation tissue","Minimal","Abundant"],["Wound contraction","Negligible","Marked, myofibroblast driven"],["Re-epithelialization","Across a narrow gap, 24 to 48 hours","Across a wide bed, slow"],["Scar","Thin and linear","Large, with risk of contracture"]],"caption":"Defect size, not a difference in biology, accounts for every entry here."},{"type":"p","text":"Healing fails when any one of its requirements is withdrawn: blood flow, oxygen, nutrient substrate, intact leukocyte function, a clean bed, and mechanical quiet. Infection is the most common local cause of delay, because persistent bacterial products keep neutrophils arriving and their proteases and oxidants degrade matrix as fast as fibroblasts lay it down. Ischemia, from arterial disease, venous hypertension, or pressure, limits the oxygen that hydroxylation, leukocyte oxidative killing, and fibroblast proliferation all require."},{"type":"p","text":"Diabetes mellitus impairs healing on several fronts at once: microangiopathy and endothelial dysfunction reduce perfusion, hyperglycemia impairs neutrophil chemotaxis and phagocytosis, advanced glycation end products stiffen matrix, and sensory neuropathy removes the pain that would force the patient off an injured foot. Glucocorticoids suppress macrophage function and inhibit fibroblast proliferation and collagen transcription, so scars are weak and prone to dehiscence. Malnutrition withholds the amino acids for collagen and the substrate for cell division."},{"type":"p","text":"Two micronutrients are tested repeatedly. Vitamin C (ascorbate) keeps the iron of prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase in the ferrous state, so without it proline and lysine residues are not hydroxylated, the triple helix is unstable, and cross-linking is poor; scurvy therefore causes friable gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, fragile vessels, and wounds that reopen. Zinc is a cofactor for matrix metalloproteinases and for DNA and RNA polymerases, so deficiency slows epithelialization and remodeling. Foreign bodies and necrotic debris sustain inflammation and shelter organisms, and repeated motion disrupts immature matrix."},{"type":"table","title":"Impediments to healing and their mechanisms","cols":["Factor","Mechanism of delay"],"rows":[["Infection","Persistent neutrophilic inflammation; proteases degrade new matrix"],["Ischemia or venous hypertension","Low oxygen limits hydroxylation and leukocyte killing"],["Diabetes mellitus","Microangiopathy, impaired neutrophils, glycated matrix, neuropathy"],["Glucocorticoids","Suppressed macrophages, less collagen synthesis; weak scars"],["Malnutrition","Too little amino acid substrate for collagen"],["Vitamin C deficiency","Hydroxylases inactive; unstable, poorly cross-linked collagen"],["Zinc deficiency","MMPs and polymerases impaired; slow epithelialization"],["Foreign body or necrotic tissue","Nidus for persistent inflammation and infection"],["Motion, tension, or pressure","Mechanical disruption of immature matrix"]],"caption":"Each factor removes one requirement of repair, the level at which questions are written."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two wounds, one deficiency","text":"A man with a three month diet of refined grains and no fresh produce has a 10 day old sutured forearm laceration that has reopened, bleeding gums, and perifollicular hemorrhages. Work backward: bleeding gums and perifollicular hemorrhage point to fragile vessel walls, and vessel walls and scar share one dependence, hydroxylated and cross-linked collagen. Without ascorbate, prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase lose their ferrous iron, so procollagen cannot form a stable triple helix. Granulation tissue still forms, because inflammation and angiogenesis are intact, but it has no tensile strength, so a wound that looked closed splits under load."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C03","obj":"IN-10.3","title":"Abnormal scars and failures of wound repair","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Repair can fail by producing too little matrix or too much. Deficient repair gives dehiscence and chronic ulceration, while excessive repair gives hypertrophic scars, keloids, and contractures, and the distinctions among the excessive forms rest on how much collagen is made, how it is organized, and whether the process respects the original wound boundary."},{"type":"p","text":"A hypertrophic scar is a raised scar in which collagen synthesis, mainly type III, continues beyond the point at which it should have slowed. The excess collagen lies in bundles oriented roughly parallel to the skin surface, the lesion stays within the borders of the original wound, it usually appears within a month or two of injury, and it often flattens and softens over months to years. Hypertrophic scars favor sites of high mechanical tension, such as the sternum, shoulder, and across joints."},{"type":"p","text":"A keloid is a true overgrowth of scar that extends beyond the wound borders into clinically uninvolved skin, which is the single most reliable distinguishing feature. Histologically the collagen is disorganized and nodular, with thick haphazard bundles of both type I and type III collagen, and there is little or no normal fibrillar orientation. Keloids arise most often on the earlobes, chest, shoulders, and upper back, are far more common in people with darker skin and show familial clustering, may continue to enlarge for years, and recur when excised because the surgery itself is another wound. The underlying abnormality is sustained fibroblast activity with excessive TGF-beta signaling and reduced matrix degradation, not simple inflammation."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypertrophic scar versus keloid","cols":["Feature","Hypertrophic scar","Keloid"],"rows":[["Extent","Confined within the original wound borders","Extends beyond the wound into normal skin"],["Collagen","Excess type III in parallel bundles","Disorganized, nodular type I and type III"],["Onset","Within weeks to a couple of months","May begin months later and enlarge for years"],["Natural course","Often regresses and flattens","Persists and recurs after excision"],["Typical sites","High tension sites: sternum, shoulder, over joints","Earlobes, chest, shoulders, upper back"],["Predisposition","Tension, delayed closure, burns","Darker skin, family history, younger age"]],"caption":"Crossing the wound border and recurrence after excision are the two discriminators to rely on."},{"type":"p","text":"Exuberant granulation tissue, sometimes called proud flesh, is granulation tissue that proliferates above the level of the surrounding skin. Because keratinocytes migrate across a surface rather than climbing a mound, the raised tissue mechanically blocks re-epithelialization and the wound stays open and friable. Its histology is ordinary granulation tissue, which distinguishes it from a true neoplasm."},{"type":"p","text":"Dehiscence is mechanical failure of the wound, most often an abdominal incision in the first one to two weeks, when tensile strength is still a small fraction of normal. Any rise in intra-abdominal pressure from coughing, vomiting, or ileus, combined with obesity, malnutrition, glucocorticoids, or infection, can exceed what the immature matrix can hold. Contracture is the opposite problem: myofibroblast contraction and scar shortening continue past the point of usefulness, fixing a joint in flexion or distorting an orifice. Contractures are characteristic after deep burns and on palms, soles, and the anterior neck, where skin is anchored over moving structures."},{"type":"table","title":"Wound complications at a glance","cols":["Problem","Underlying defect","Characteristic finding"],"rows":[["Hypertrophic scar","Continued type III collagen deposition","Raised scar within the wound borders, may regress"],["Keloid","Sustained fibroblast and TGF-beta activity, reduced degradation","Scar extending past the border, recurs after excision"],["Exuberant granulation tissue","Overgrown granulation tissue","Friable tissue above skin level preventing epithelial closure"],["Dehiscence","Load exceeding an immature scar","Wound separation at 1 to 2 weeks, often abdominal"],["Contracture","Excessive, persistent myofibroblast contraction","Fixed deformity or restricted joint motion after deep burns"],["Chronic ulceration","Repair stalled in the inflammatory phase","Nonhealing wound with ischemia, infection, or pressure"]],"caption":"Deficient repair produces ulceration and dehiscence; excessive repair produces hypertrophic scars, keloids, and contractures."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C04","obj":"IN-10.4","title":"Burn depth, burned surface area, and systemic consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A burn denatures protein in proportion to temperature and contact time. Injury begins at skin temperatures of roughly 44 degrees Celsius (111 degrees Fahrenheit) sustained for hours, while water at about 60 degrees Celsius (140 degrees Fahrenheit) causes full-thickness injury in seconds. Depth is judged clinically by three findings that each report on a specific structure: blisters indicate separation at or below the dermal-epidermal junction, blanching on pressure indicates a perfused dermal capillary bed, and preserved sensation indicates surviving dermal nerve endings. A burn that neither blanches nor hurts has lost the dermal plexus and the nerve endings with it."},{"type":"table","title":"Burn depth by structure, appearance, and healing","cols":["Depth","Layers injured","Appearance and blanching","Sensation","Healing"],"rows":[["Superficial","Epidermis only","Red, dry, no blisters, blanches","Painful","3 to 7 days, no scar"],["Superficial partial-thickness","Epidermis and papillary dermis","Blisters, moist, pink, blanches briskly","Very painful","1 to 3 weeks, minimal scar"],["Deep partial-thickness","Into reticular dermis","Blisters, white or mottled, blanches poorly","Reduced, pressure felt","Over 3 weeks, scars and contracts"],["Full-thickness","Entire dermis or deeper","White, leathery, or charred eschar; no blanching","Painless","No residual adnexa; closes only from the margins"]],"caption":"Blisters, blanching, and sensation each map onto a dermal structure, which is why they grade depth."},{"type":"p","text":"Burned surface area is estimated by the rule of nines, and only partial-thickness and full-thickness burns are counted, because superficial epidermal burns do not produce the capillary leak that drives fluid loss. Children have proportionally larger heads and smaller legs, so pediatric charts shift roughly 9 percentage points from the legs to the head in infancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Rule of nines, adult","cols":["Region","Percent of total body surface"],"rows":[["Head and neck","9"],["Each upper limb","9 (4.5 front, 4.5 back)"],["Anterior trunk","18"],["Posterior trunk","18"],["Each lower limb","18 (9 front, 9 back)"],["Perineum","1"]],"caption":"Count only partial-thickness and full-thickness area; the patient's palm with fingers is about 1 percent."},{"type":"p","text":"Large burns cause systemic disease. Thermal injury releases histamine, prostaglandins, bradykinin, and cytokines, and once roughly 20 percent of the body surface is involved, capillary permeability rises in uninjured tissue as well. Plasma and protein shift into the interstitium over the first 24 to 48 hours, so intravascular volume falls while hematocrit rises, producing hypovolemic burn shock with low cardiac output and oliguria. Loss of the barrier plus avascular eschar then creates a culture medium that leukocytes and bloodborne agents reach poorly, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa classically causes invasive burn wound sepsis, with a green-blue, sweet-smelling exudate and dark wound discoloration."},{"type":"p","text":"From about the third day a hypermetabolic state follows: catecholamines, cortisol, and glucagon raise resting energy expenditure sharply, drive muscle proteolysis and lipolysis, and cause hyperglycemia and nitrogen loss. Inhalation injury is suspected with facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in the oropharynx, hoarseness, or a fire in an enclosed space; it injures the airway thermally above the glottis and chemically below it, and carbon monoxide and hydrogen cyanide block oxygen carriage and mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase respectively, so arterial oxygen tension and ordinary pulse oximetry can look normal while tissue is hypoxic. Late consequences are contractures across joints and, after years of a chronically unstable burn scar, squamous cell carcinoma arising in it, known as a Marjolin ulcer."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of burn shock","steps":["Thermal injury releases histamine, prostaglandins, bradykinin, cytokines","Capillary permeability rises locally and, in large burns, systemically","Plasma and protein shift into the interstitium over 24 to 48 hours","Intravascular volume falls while hematocrit rises","Cardiac output and renal perfusion fall: hypovolemic shock, oliguria"],"caption":"Fluid is lost from the vascular space, not from the body, which is why hematocrit rises."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One forearm has blisters that blanch briskly; the other is white, leathery, and painless. Which is deeper, and which needs grafting?","explain":"Which dermal structures must be gone to abolish both blanching and pain?","check":"The blanching and sensation columns of the burn depth table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C05","obj":"IN-10.5","title":"Electrical injury and lightning strike","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Electrical injury is thermal injury distributed by electrical resistance rather than by surface contact. Heat produced in a tissue rises with the square of the current and with the resistance of that tissue, so the tissues that resist most and the sites where current is most concentrated are damaged most. Dry intact skin, especially thick palmar and plantar keratin, resists strongly and takes the brunt of local heating at the contact points, which is why entry and exit wounds are often small, dry, charred craters out of all proportion to the damage inside."},{"type":"p","text":"Once past the stratum corneum, current travels preferentially through nerve, blood vessel, and muscle, which contain water and electrolytes and conduct well, while bone resists strongly and heats intensely, cooking the deep muscle that surrounds it. The limb therefore behaves like a cable with a hot core: superficially viable skin can overlie extensive necrosis of deep muscle and nerve. Because current density is highest where the cross-sectional area is smallest, injury is greatest at the wrist, elbow, and ankle. Swelling within fascial compartments then raises interstitial pressure above capillary pressure and produces compartment syndrome, which adds ischemic necrosis to the thermal necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Necrotic muscle releases myoglobin, creatine kinase, potassium, and phosphate into the circulation. Myoglobin is a small protein that is freely filtered; in the acidic tubular fluid of a volume-depleted patient it precipitates with Tamm-Horsfall protein to form obstructing pigmented casts, and its heme iron catalyzes free radical injury of tubular epithelium, giving acute tubular necrosis. The urine is dark and tests positive for blood on dipstick (which detects heme) while microscopy shows no red cells, a discrepancy that is the diagnostic clue. Hyperkalemia from lysed muscle and hypocalcemia from calcium deposition in damaged muscle follow."},{"type":"flow","title":"From current to acute kidney injury","steps":["Current follows low-resistance nerve, vessel, and muscle; bone heats intensely","Deep muscle necrosis with compartment swelling and added ischemia","Myoglobin, creatine kinase, potassium, phosphate released into blood","Filtered myoglobin precipitates as pigmented casts in acidic tubular fluid","Heme iron generates free radicals and injures tubular cells","Acute tubular necrosis: dark urine, heme-positive dipstick, no red cells"],"caption":"Rhabdomyolysis explains the kidney injury, the hyperkalemia, and the dipstick and microscopy discrepancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Current crossing the thorax reaches the heart. Alternating household current at 50 to 60 hertz is especially dangerous for two reasons: it repeatedly re-stimulates the myocardium until a stimulus lands in the vulnerable period of repolarization and initiates ventricular fibrillation, and it causes tetanic contraction of forearm flexors that locks the hand onto the conductor and prolongs exposure. Lightning and high voltage direct current instead deliver a single massive countershock that depolarizes the whole myocardium at once, producing asystole; the sinus node often resumes a rhythm spontaneously, so death after lightning is more often prolonged respiratory arrest from medullary stunning and tetanic paralysis of the respiratory muscles than failure of the heart. Survivors may show Lichtenberg figures, transient branching fern-like markings that reflect current tracking in skin and superficial vessels rather than true burns, and that fade within about 24 to 48 hours. Keraunoparalysis, a temporary flaccid paralysis with cold, pulseless, mottled limbs from vasospasm, also resolves."},{"type":"table","title":"Household alternating current versus lightning","cols":["Feature","Household alternating current","Lightning"],"rows":[["Exposure","Prolonged; tetany locks the hand to the source","Instantaneous, milliseconds"],["Typical arrhythmia","Ventricular fibrillation","Asystole, often with spontaneous return of rhythm"],["Main cause of death","Fibrillation","Prolonged respiratory arrest after the heart restarts"],["Skin injury","Small entry and exit burns, deep muscle necrosis","Superficial, often with Lichtenberg figures"],["Characteristic sequelae","Rhabdomyolysis, compartment syndrome, cataracts, neuropathy","Keraunoparalysis, tympanic rupture, cataracts"]],"caption":"Duration and waveform, not voltage alone, determine whether the heart fibrillates or arrests."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C06","obj":"IN-10.6","title":"Radiation injury to skin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ionizing radiation injures cells by damaging DNA, and it does so by two routes. Direct action is ionization of the DNA molecule itself, which matters most for densely ionizing particles such as alpha particles and neutrons. Indirect action, which accounts for roughly two thirds of the damage from x-rays and gamma rays, is radiolysis of intracellular water into hydroxyl radicals and other reactive oxygen species that then attack the sugar-phosphate backbone and bases. The decisive lesion is the double-strand break, because a single-strand break can be repaired from the intact complementary strand while double-strand breaks require error-prone repair and, when misrepaired, leave chromosomal deletions and translocations. Oxygen fixes radical damage before it can be chemically reversed, so well-oxygenated tissue is more radiosensitive than hypoxic tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Cells that divide rapidly show injury first, because an unrepaired double-strand break is lethal mainly at mitosis. In skin this means the basal keratinocytes of the epidermis, the matrix cells of hair follicles, and vascular endothelium. The acute reaction therefore reflects loss of the proliferating basal layer rather than immediate cell death at the surface."},{"type":"flow","title":"How ionizing radiation injures skin","steps":["Photon or particle energy deposited in tissue","Direct ionization of DNA plus radiolysis of water to hydroxyl radicals","Single and double-strand DNA breaks; oxygen fixes the damage","Unrepaired double-strand breaks kill dividing basal keratinocytes at mitosis","Loss of the basal layer: erythema, then desquamation","Misrepaired breaks persist in survivors as mutations and later malignancy"],"caption":"The same lesion that causes acute desquamation causes the late cancers, differing only in whether the cell dies or survives with mutation."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute radiation dermatitis evolves over days to weeks in a dose-dependent sequence. A transient erythema may appear within hours from vascular permeability, followed after about two weeks by a sustained erythema as basal cells fail to replace the epidermis. Higher doses produce dry desquamation (scaling with intact barrier) and then moist desquamation, in which the epidermis is lost entirely and serous exudate weeps from a denuded dermis. Epilation accompanies this because follicular matrix cells are equally sensitive. The highest doses cause dermal necrosis and ulceration that will not heal."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic radiation changes appear months to years later and are dominated by vascular and fibroblastic consequences rather than by keratinocyte loss. Endothelial injury and fibrosis thicken and narrow the lumens of small arteries, a process called obliterative endarteritis, which makes the field chronically ischemic. The clinical picture is atrophic, thin, shiny skin with loss of adnexa and of normal pigment pattern, telangiectasias, dermal and subcutaneous fibrosis that may bind skin to deeper tissue, and poikiloderma with mottled hyper- and hypopigmentation. The ischemia and the depleted stem cell pool together explain why irradiated skin heals badly after any later trauma or surgery."},{"type":"p","text":"Late neoplasia reflects the mutations retained in surviving cells, after latency measured in years to decades. Basal cell and squamous cell carcinoma arise in previously irradiated skin, often within chronic radiation dermatitis, and angiosarcoma can arise in the radiation field, classically in the chest wall or breast after therapeutic irradiation. Radiation-associated sarcomas are uncommon but are an important reason that a new nodule in an old radiation field is taken seriously."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute versus chronic radiation skin injury","cols":["Feature","Acute radiation dermatitis","Chronic radiation injury"],"rows":[["Timing","Hours to weeks after exposure","Months to years, progressive"],["Target","Proliferating basal keratinocytes and follicular matrix","Small vessels, fibroblasts, residual stem cells"],["Findings","Erythema, epilation, dry then moist desquamation, ulceration","Atrophy, telangiectasia, fibrosis, poikiloderma, adnexal loss"],["Vascular change","Increased permeability, dilated vessels","Obliterative endarteritis with chronic ischemia"],["Healing","Recovers if basal stem cells survive","Poor; later wounds and surgery heal badly"],["Late risk","Not applicable","Basal cell and squamous cell carcinoma, angiosarcoma in the field"]],"caption":"Acute injury is a proliferation problem; chronic injury is a vascular and fibrotic one."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C07","obj":"IN-10.7","title":"Pressure injury and auricular hematoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A pressure injury (decubitus ulcer) is localized ischemic necrosis from sustained loading of soft tissue over a bony prominence. Capillary flow stops when external pressure exceeds the pressure inside the capillary, roughly 32 millimeters of mercury at the arteriolar end and lower at the venous end, and a seated or recumbent patient easily generates several times that over the sacrum, ischial tuberosities, greater trochanters, heels, and occiput. Healthy people shift position constantly in response to the discomfort of ischemia; a patient who is immobile, sedated, paralyzed, or insensate does not, so the ischemia becomes continuous. Magnitude and duration trade off against each other: high pressure injures quickly, lower pressure over hours."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure alone is not the whole mechanism. Shear, generated when skin is held by friction while the skeleton slides beneath it, as when a patient is dragged up in bed or slides down a raised head of bed, angulates and tears the perforating vessels that supply skin from below. Moisture from incontinence or perspiration macerates the stratum corneum and lowers its resistance to friction, and reperfusion after each ischemic episode adds free radical injury. Because muscle has a higher metabolic rate and lower ischemic tolerance than skin, and because pressure is greatest at the bone interface and dissipates toward the surface, the necrosis is cone-shaped with its base deep: what is visible at the surface is the apex of a much larger lesion."},{"type":"table","title":"Pressure injury stages","cols":["Stage","Findings","Interpretation"],"rows":[["Stage 1","Intact skin with localized nonblanchable erythema","Capillary bed damaged, epidermis intact"],["Stage 2","Partial-thickness loss exposing viable dermis, or a serum-filled blister","Epidermis and upper dermis only"],["Stage 3","Full-thickness loss with visible fat; no fascia, muscle, tendon, or bone","Loss through the dermis"],["Stage 4","Full-thickness loss exposing fascia, muscle, tendon, cartilage, or bone","Deep extension; osteomyelitis risk"],["Unstageable","Base obscured by slough or eschar","Depth cannot be judged yet"],["Deep tissue injury","Persistent deep red, maroon, or purple discoloration, or blood-filled blister","Deep injury under intact skin"]],"caption":"Staging names the deepest tissue visibly involved, so a lesion cannot be staged while eschar hides its base."},{"type":"p","text":"Auricular hematoma illustrates the same principle of a tissue cut off from its blood supply, but by a different route. The cartilage of the auricle is avascular and depends entirely on diffusion of nutrients from the vessels of the overlying perichondrium, to which it is normally tightly apposed. Blunt shearing trauma, typical of wrestling, boxing, and rugby, tears the perichondrial vessels and the resulting hematoma lifts the perichondrium off the cartilage. Diffusion distance now exceeds what cartilage can tolerate, so the separated cartilage becomes ischemic and undergoes necrosis, while the collected blood is an excellent culture medium and perichondritis or chondritis may follow, most often with Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The repair response produces fibrosis along with disorganized new cartilage from surviving perichondrial chondroblasts, and the healed auricle is thickened, lumpy, and permanently distorted: cauliflower ear."},{"type":"flow","title":"Auricular hematoma to cauliflower ear","steps":["Blunt shear tears perichondrial vessels of the auricle","Hematoma collects and separates perichondrium from cartilage","Avascular cartilage loses its only nutrient source by diffusion","Cartilage necrosis, sometimes with secondary infection","Fibrosis plus disorganized new cartilage from perichondrium","Thickened, irregular, permanently deformed auricle"],"caption":"The lesion is a diffusion barrier, so the cartilage dies from separation rather than from the force of the blow itself."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A bedbound patient has a 3 centimeter area of intact but nonblanchable maroon discoloration over the sacrum. Why might the tissue underneath be far more extensively damaged than this appearance suggests?","explain":"Why is the necrosis cone-shaped with its base at the bone?","check":"Re-read the paragraph on shear and the relative ischemic tolerance of muscle and skin, then the deep tissue injury row of the staging table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-10-01-C08","obj":"IN-10.8","title":"Brown recluse envenomation and dermonecrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The brown recluse spider (Loxosceles reclusa) produces a bite that is painless at first yet can progress over days to a necrotic skin ulcer, which makes the mechanism worth understanding: the damage comes from an enzyme in the venom and from the host inflammatory response it provokes, not from mechanical injury or from infection."},{"type":"p","text":"The principal toxin is sphingomyelinase D, a phospholipase that cleaves the choline head group from sphingomyelin in cell membranes to yield ceramide 1-phosphate and choline. Sphingomyelin is abundant in the outer leaflet of endothelial cell and erythrocyte membranes, so the enzyme both destabilizes those membranes directly and generates bioactive lipid mediators. Two consequences follow. First, altered endothelial membranes activate complement and expose a prothrombotic surface, so platelets aggregate and small dermal vessels thrombose. Second, the venom is a powerful chemoattractant: massive neutrophil influx releases proteases and reactive oxygen species into tissue whose blood supply is already failing. Experimental depletion of neutrophils markedly reduces the necrosis, which establishes that much of the lesion is host-mediated rather than purely toxin-mediated."},{"type":"p","text":"The evolution of the lesion follows that mechanism. The bite itself is often unnoticed. Within hours there is local pain, pruritus, and erythema; by 24 to 72 hours a characteristic red, white, and blue appearance may develop, in which an outer ring of erythema (inflammation) surrounds a pale blanched ring (vasoconstriction and ischemia) around a dusky violaceous center (thrombosis and early necrosis). The center sinks below the surrounding skin, becomes a firm eschar over the following week, and sloughs to leave an ulcer that heals slowly by secondary intention with a depressed scar. Gravity-dependent spread of the venom gives some lesions an irregular, downward-extending shape. Because many conditions produce a necrotic ulcer, a confident diagnosis requires the spider, and community-acquired methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection is the usual alternative to consider."},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic loxoscelism is uncommon and occurs mainly in children. The same sphingomyelinase D acting on erythrocyte membranes, together with complement activation, causes intravascular hemolysis with hemoglobinuria, and this can be accompanied by fever, rash, disseminated intravascular coagulation, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury from pigment nephropathy. The important contrast is with the other medically significant North American spider: the black widow (Latrodectus), whose alpha-latrotoxin triggers massive presynaptic release of acetylcholine and norepinephrine and therefore causes painful muscle cramping, rigidity, and autonomic signs with no local necrosis at all."},{"type":"table","title":"Brown recluse versus black widow envenomation","cols":["Feature","Brown recluse (Loxosceles)","Black widow (Latrodectus)"],"rows":[["Toxin","Sphingomyelinase D","Alpha-latrotoxin"],["Mechanism","Membrane lysis, complement, thrombosis, neutrophil influx","Pore formation at the presynaptic terminal with massive neurotransmitter release"],["Local findings","Initially painless, then red, white, and blue lesion with central necrotic ulcer","Immediate pain and a target lesion without necrosis"],["Systemic findings","Rare hemolysis, hemoglobinuria, disseminated intravascular coagulation","Muscle cramps and rigidity, hypertension, diaphoresis, tachycardia"],["Course","Ulcer evolves over days and heals slowly with a scar","Symptoms peak in hours and resolve over 1 to 3 days"]],"caption":"One venom destroys membranes, the other exhausts synapses, which is why only one leaves an ulcer."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every injury in this chapter is read through the same four-phase repair program. Histology and cell biology supply the actors: platelets and the fibrin scaffold, neutrophils, the macrophage as the pivotal signaling cell, fibroblasts and myofibroblasts, endothelium, and keratinocytes migrating from margins and residual adnexa. Biochemistry supplies the rate-limiting chemistry: hydroxylation of proline and lysine needs ascorbate and ferrous iron, cross-linking needs copper-dependent lysyl oxidase, and remodeling needs zinc-dependent metalloproteinases, which is why deficiency states show up as weak scars and dehiscence rather than as absent granulation tissue. Immunology explains the rest of the pattern, since a wound held in persistent neutrophilic inflammation never reaches the proliferative phase."},{"type":"p","text":"The traumatic injuries then stress one requirement each, and the systemic consequences follow from physiology. Burns remove the barrier and, above roughly a fifth of the body surface, open capillaries everywhere, so plasma leaves the vascular space and hematocrit rises as blood pressure falls, with eschar providing a culture medium that leukocytes and bloodborne agents cannot reach. Electrical injury and prolonged pressure destroy muscle, and renal physiology explains the rest: filtered myoglobin, acidic tubular fluid, pigmented casts, and heme-catalyzed oxidant injury. Ionizing radiation acts mainly through double-strand breaks, while ultraviolet light acts mainly through pyrimidine dimers, and both converge on retained mutations that link the acute skin reaction to carcinomas and angiosarcoma years to decades later. The limits are worth stating plainly: burn depth and pressure injury stage are clinical judgments that frequently change as a wound declares itself, blanching and sensation can be unreliable in a swollen or anesthetized limb, a necrotic ulcer is not diagnostic of a recluse bite without the spider, and quoted timelines for each healing phase are averages that shift with wound size, perfusion, infection, and age."}],"summary":["Healing runs hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, remodeling; platelet PDGF and TGF-beta start it and the macrophage, dominant by about day 3, is its pivotal signaling cell.","Granulation tissue is fibroblasts, VEGF-driven capillaries, and type III collagen; remodeling replaces type III with cross-linked type I, so strength is about 10 percent at one week and plateaus at about 70 to 80 percent by three months.","Primary and secondary intention differ only by defect size: secondary intention requires far more granulation tissue and myofibroblast contraction, which also creates contractures.","Healing stalls when any requirement is withdrawn: infection (most common local cause), ischemia, diabetes, glucocorticoids, malnutrition, vitamin C (hydroxylase cofactor), zinc (MMP cofactor), foreign body, or motion.","A hypertrophic scar stays within the wound borders and may regress; a keloid of disorganized type I and III collagen grows past the borders, favors earlobes and darker skin, and recurs after excision.","Burn depth is graded by blisters, blanching, and sensation, so a painless, nonblanching, leathery burn is full-thickness and closes only from the margins; only partial-thickness and full-thickness area counts toward the adult rule of nines (9 head and neck, 9 each arm, 18 front trunk, 18 back trunk, 18 each leg, 1 perineum).","Large burns cause capillary leak with hypovolemic shock and rising hematocrit over 24 to 48 hours, Pseudomonas wound sepsis, a catecholamine and cortisol driven hypermetabolic state, and later contractures or Marjolin ulcer.","Current follows low-resistance nerve, vessel, and muscle, so small entry and exit wounds hide deep necrosis, rhabdomyolysis, and myoglobinuric kidney injury; alternating current causes fibrillation while lightning causes asystole with respiratory arrest and transient Lichtenberg figures.","Ionizing radiation kills dividing basal keratinocytes through double-strand breaks, mostly by way of hydroxyl radicals, giving acute erythema and desquamation, then chronic atrophy, telangiectasia, fibrosis, and obliterative endarteritis, with later carcinoma or angiosarcoma in the field.","Sustained pressure above capillary pressure, about 32 millimeters of mercury, plus shear gives deep cone-shaped ischemic ulcers; an auricular hematoma starves avascular cartilage of perichondrial diffusion; and brown recluse sphingomyelinase D produces thrombosis, neutrophil influx, and a central necrotic ulcer."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Tissue Renewal, Regeneration, and Repair: cutaneous wound healing and pathologic aspects of repair"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases: injury by physical agents, thermal, electrical, and radiation injury"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: capillary fluid exchange and edema"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin; Connective Tissue; Cartilage"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Collagen, fibrous proteins of the extracellular matrix, and vitamin C"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Skin; Renal Disease, acute kidney injury"}]},"IN-11":{"unit":"IN-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01","title":"Immune Responses in Skin Disease","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The skin is an immune organ as much as a barrier. Keratinocytes, mast cells, and Langerhans cells sense microbial and physical danger, start acute inflammation, and carry antigen to regional lymph nodes to prime naive T cells. This chapter follows that response forward: how inflammation resolves, suppurates, or becomes chronic and granulomatous, how the four hypersensitivity types produce distinct skin lesions on distinct timetables, how autoimmune disease writes itself on the skin, and how cytotoxic lymphocytes and vaccines handle cells that display foreign or mutant peptide. Board questions give a lesion, a time course, or an immunofluorescence pattern and ask which mechanism produced it.","prerequisites":["Epidermal layers, keratinocyte differentiation, and the dermal-epidermal junction (IN-1, IN-2)","Innate immunity: pattern recognition receptors, NF-kB signaling, complement activation and C5a","Antigen presentation on MHC class I and class II, costimulation, and naive T cell priming (IM-5, IM-6)","Helper T cell subsets and their signature cytokines (IM-7)","Acute and chronic inflammation, necrosis patterns, and repair by granulation tissue"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology hypersensitivity chapters (IM-11, IM-12)","how":"The skin is where all four hypersensitivity types can be seen and timed, so cutaneous lesions become the readable form of the general mechanisms."},{"to":"Cutaneous infections (IN-9, IN-10)","how":"Whether an organism produces pus, a granuloma, or a toxin-mediated rash follows directly from which immune pathway it triggers."},{"to":"Respiratory and musculoskeletal pathology","how":"Sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, dermatomyositis, and systemic sclerosis present in skin and lung or muscle together, so the skin finding points to the systemic disease."},{"to":"Hematology and leukocyte disorders (HE-4)","how":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency and other neutrophil defects explain skin infections without pus and delayed wound healing."},{"to":"Neoplasia and skin cancer (IN-13)","how":"Tumor immunosurveillance and its escape mechanisms explain why immunosuppressed patients accumulate cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pattern recognition receptor","def":"A germline-encoded receptor, such as a Toll-like receptor, that binds conserved molecular patterns shared by classes of microbes."},{"term":"Antimicrobial peptide","def":"A small cationic peptide, such as a beta-defensin or cathelicidin, that disrupts microbial membranes at the epithelial surface."},{"term":"Langerhans cell","def":"The dendritic cell of the epidermis, identified by CD1a, langerin, and Birbeck granules, that captures antigen and carries it to lymph nodes."},{"term":"Chemotaxis","def":"Directed leukocyte migration along a concentration gradient of a chemoattractant such as C5a, LTB4, or IL-8."},{"term":"Abscess","def":"A localized collection of pus formed when neutrophil enzymes liquefy tissue and granulation tissue walls the cavity off."},{"term":"Granuloma","def":"A compact aggregate of activated epithelioid macrophages, often with giant cells and a lymphocyte rim, that walls off an agent macrophages cannot digest."},{"term":"Caseous necrosis","def":"Granuloma center necrosis with a crumbly amorphous appearance, combining coagulative and liquefactive features, classic for tuberculosis."},{"term":"Interface dermatitis","def":"A histologic pattern of lymphocytes and keratinocyte apoptosis concentrated at the dermal-epidermal junction."},{"term":"Immune complex","def":"An antigen-antibody aggregate that can deposit in vessel walls or basement membranes and activate complement."},{"term":"Acantholysis","def":"Loss of adhesion between keratinocytes, producing an intraepidermal blister, as when autoantibodies bind desmoglein."},{"term":"Immune checkpoint","def":"An inhibitory receptor and ligand pair, such as PD-1 and PD-L1, that dampens activated T cell responses."},{"term":"Virus-like particle","def":"A self-assembled capsid protein shell containing no viral genome, used as a vaccine antigen that mimics virion surface structure."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01-C01","obj":"IN-11.1","title":"Innate defense in the skin and Langerhans cell priming of naive T cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Intact skin defends mechanically and chemically before any immune cell is involved. The cornified layer is a dry, tightly cross-linked barrier, surface lipids and sweat keep the pH at roughly 5, and resident commensals occupy the niches a pathogen would use. Immune defense begins when that barrier is breached, and the first responder is the keratinocyte itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Keratinocytes express Toll-like receptors, the best known being TLR2 for gram-positive cell wall components such as peptidoglycan and lipoteichoic acid and TLR4 for lipopolysaccharide. Ligand binding activates NF-kB, which drives transcription of IL-1 and TNF and of antimicrobial peptides. IL-1 and TNF act locally on dermal endothelium to raise adhesion molecule expression and systemically on the hypothalamus to produce fever. The antimicrobial peptides, chiefly beta-defensins and the cathelicidin LL-37, are cationic and insert into anionic microbial membranes, so they kill bacteria and fungi directly and also recruit leukocytes. Their output rises sharply in wounded skin, which is one reason minor wounds usually do not become infected."},{"type":"p","text":"Mast cells sit along dermal vessels and in the perifollicular connective tissue, loaded with preformed histamine, heparin, tryptase, and TNF. They degranulate within seconds to minutes in response to IgE cross-linking, complement products C3a and C5a, substance P, physical trauma, or heat, and they then synthesize prostaglandin D2 and leukotrienes. Because they are prepositioned at vessels, they convert a chemical alarm into increased blood flow and permeability faster than any recruited cell could."},{"type":"p","text":"Langerhans cells form a dendritic network in the suprabasal epidermis, about 2 to 4 percent of epidermal cells, and are recognized by CD1a, langerin (CD207), and tennis-racket Birbeck granules on electron microscopy. They sample antigen through dendrites that reach between keratinocytes. Naive T cells do not patrol skin; they circulate through lymph nodes, so antigen must be carried to them. Keratinocyte IL-1 and TNF plus the Toll-like receptor signal mature the Langerhans cell: it loses its adhesion to keratinocytes, upregulates CCR7, and follows the chemokines CCL19 and CCL21 into afferent lymphatics and the regional node paracortex. There it presents peptide on MHC class II to CD4 cells, while dermal dendritic cells carrying the same antigen are the main cells that cross-present it on MHC class I to prime CD8 cells, and the required second signal comes from B7 (CD80 and CD86) binding CD28. Without that costimulation the T cell becomes anergic rather than activated, which is how harmless skin antigens are normally tolerated. Primed effector T cells then acquire skin-homing receptors, chiefly cutaneous lymphocyte antigen and CCR4, and return to the dermis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Langerhans cell antigen capture and T cell priming","steps":["Barrier breach; keratinocyte Toll-like receptors activate NF-kB","IL-1 and TNF released; Langerhans cell captures antigen","Langerhans cell matures, upregulates CCR7 and B7","Migration in afferent lymphatics to regional node paracortex","Peptide on MHC plus B7 to CD28 primes the naive T cell","Effector T cells express skin-homing receptors and return to dermis"],"caption":"The afferent limb of cutaneous adaptive immunity: antigen moves to the node because naive T cells cannot come to the skin."},{"type":"table","title":"Innate sentinels of the skin","cols":["Cell","Sensing","Main output","Speed"],"rows":[["Keratinocyte","TLR2, TLR4, injury","IL-1, TNF, beta-defensins, LL-37","Minutes to hours (transcription needed)"],["Mast cell","IgE cross-linking, C3a and C5a, substance P, trauma, heat","Histamine, tryptase, TNF, PGD2, leukotrienes","Seconds to minutes (preformed granules)"],["Langerhans cell","Antigen capture plus keratinocyte cytokines","Antigen presentation with B7 costimulation in the node","Hours to days"],["Dermal macrophage","Opsonins, TLRs","Phagocytosis, IL-12, debris clearance","Hours"]],"caption":"Response speed tracks whether the mediator is preformed, transcribed, or requires cell migration."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient on a drug that blocks CCR7 signaling is sensitized to a new contact allergen. Will allergic contact dermatitis develop normally?","explain":"Decide which limb of the response the drug interrupts and whether antigen can still reach naive T cells.","check":"Compare with the Langerhans cell priming flow in this section and with the sensitization phase in C04."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01-C02","obj":"IN-11.2","title":"Acute inflammation, the leukocyte adhesion cascade, and leukocyte adhesion deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute inflammation has a vascular phase and a cellular phase. In the vascular phase, histamine, bradykinin, prostaglandins, and nitric oxide dilate arterioles, raising local blood flow and producing redness and heat. The same mediators act on postcapillary venules, where endothelial cells contract and pull apart at their junctions, so interendothelial gaps open. Plasma proteins escape, tissue oncotic pressure rises, and protein-rich exudate accumulates as the swelling of the response. Fluid loss concentrates red cells and slows flow (stasis), which lets leukocytes leave the axial stream and run along the endothelium, that is, marginate. Venules rather than capillaries or arterioles leak, because their endothelium responds to these mediators and their wall is thin enough to cross."},{"type":"p","text":"The cellular phase is a fixed sequence of adhesion steps, each using a different receptor pair, which is why a single molecular defect can halt it. Histamine and thrombin cause immediate surface display of preformed P-selectin from Weibel-Palade bodies, while IL-1 and TNF induce E-selectin over several hours. Selectins bind sialylated carbohydrate ligands such as sialyl Lewis X on neutrophils with low affinity and fast off-rates, so the cell tethers, detaches, and tethers again: it rolls. Rolling brings the leukocyte into contact with chemokines, including IL-8, immobilized on endothelial glycosaminoglycans. Chemokine receptor signaling triggers a conformational change in the beta-2 integrins LFA-1 (CD11a and CD18) and Mac-1 (CD11b and CD18) to a high-affinity state; these bind ICAM-1, which IL-1 and TNF have upregulated, and the cell stops. The arrested leukocyte squeezes between endothelial cells using homophilic PECAM-1 (CD31), crosses the basement membrane using secreted collagenases, and then migrates up a gradient of chemoattractants: C5a, leukotriene B4, IL-8, and bacterial formylated peptides such as f-Met-Leu-Phe. Neutrophils dominate the first 6 to 24 hours and monocytes the next 24 to 48, because neutrophils are short lived and the later chemokine profile favors monocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"The leukocyte adhesion cascade","steps":["Mediators dilate arterioles and open venular endothelial gaps","Stasis and margination bring leukocytes to the wall","P-selectin and E-selectin bind sialyl Lewis X; rolling","Endothelial chemokines switch beta-2 integrins to high affinity","LFA-1 and Mac-1 bind ICAM-1; firm adhesion","PECAM-1 guides transmigration across the venule wall","Chemotaxis toward C5a, LTB4, IL-8, bacterial peptides"],"caption":"Each step uses its own receptor pair, so the cascade can be interrupted at a defined molecular point."},{"type":"table","title":"Adhesion molecules by step","cols":["Step","Endothelial molecule","Leukocyte molecule","Induced by"],"rows":[["Rolling, immediate","P-selectin (preformed)","Sialyl Lewis X","Histamine, thrombin"],["Rolling, delayed","E-selectin","Sialyl Lewis X","IL-1, TNF (hours)"],["Naive lymphocyte rolling in nodes","Peripheral node addressin on high endothelial venules","L-selectin","Constitutive"],["Firm adhesion","ICAM-1 (and VCAM-1 for VLA-4)","LFA-1 and Mac-1 (CD18 beta-2 integrins)","IL-1, TNF"],["Transmigration","PECAM-1","PECAM-1","Constitutive"]],"caption":"Note that leukocyte L-selectin is used for lymphocyte entry into nodes, while endothelial selectins drive rolling at inflamed tissue."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1","text":"A newborn has an umbilical cord stump still attached at four weeks, recurrent skin and soft tissue infections with no pus and little erythema, poorly healing wounds, and a blood neutrophil count of 55,000 per microliter. The defect is in ITGB2, which encodes CD18, the beta chain shared by LFA-1 and Mac-1. Rolling is intact, so neutrophils still slow at the vessel wall, but they cannot convert that to firm adhesion, cannot transmigrate, and so accumulate in blood: the high count is the mirror image of the missing tissue infiltrate. Pus is dead neutrophils, so without egress there is none. Delayed cord separation and poor healing follow because leukocyte proteases and growth factors normally remodel that tissue. Contrast Chediak-Higashi syndrome, where neutrophils arrive but cannot fuse granules with phagosomes, and chronic granulomatous disease, where they arrive and ingest but cannot generate superoxide."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01-C03","obj":"IN-11.3","title":"Outcomes of acute inflammation and granuloma formation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Which of three outcomes follows acute inflammation depends on whether the stimulus is cleared and how much tissue architecture survives. Resolution occurs when the injurious agent is removed quickly and the extracellular matrix is intact: mediators are degraded, venular leak stops, neutrophils undergo apoptosis and are phagocytosed by macrophages, edema drains into lymphatics, and macrophages switch to a reparative program that produces IL-10 and TGF-beta along with lipid mediators such as lipoxins and resolvins that actively stop recruitment. Resolution is therefore a programmed event, not simply the fading of a stimulus."},{"type":"p","text":"Suppuration gives an abscess. Pyogenic organisms, Staphylococcus aureus above all, recruit an intense neutrophil infiltrate whose lysosomal proteases and reactive oxygen species digest both microbes and host tissue, producing liquefactive necrosis. The resulting cavity of pus is surrounded by a pyogenic membrane of granulation tissue, which contains the infection but also keeps antibodies, complement, and phagocytes from penetrating well, so abscesses persist until drained and heal by scar."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic inflammation develops when the agent persists, as with a foreign body, a poorly degradable organism, or an ongoing autoimmune stimulus. The infiltrate shifts to macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells, and tissue injury, repair, angiogenesis, and fibrosis proceed at the same time rather than in sequence, which is why chronic lesions show scarring alongside active inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Granulomatous inflammation is a specific form of chronic inflammation driven by a self-reinforcing loop between macrophages and Th1 cells. A macrophage ingests an agent it cannot destroy, presents its peptides on MHC class II, and secretes IL-12. IL-12 and STAT4 signaling commit naive CD4 cells to the Th1 lineage through T-bet, and Th1 cells secrete IFN-gamma. IFN-gamma is the classic macrophage-activating cytokine: it increases phagolysosomal killing, MHC expression, and nitric oxide synthesis, and converts macrophages into large pale epithelioid cells that fuse into multinucleated giant cells. TNF from the activated macrophages maintains the aggregate, which is why TNF inhibitors can reactivate latent tuberculosis. A granuloma walls off what cannot be cleared, at the cost of local tissue destruction and fibrosis."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The macrophage and Th1 loop that builds a granuloma","steps":["Macrophage ingests an indigestible agent","Antigen on MHC class II plus IL-12 release","Naive CD4 cell commits to Th1 (T-bet, STAT4)","Th1 cell secretes IFN-gamma","Macrophage becomes epithelioid, fuses into giant cells","TNF sustains the aggregate and more IL-12 is made, feeding the loop"],"caption":"A positive feedback loop: macrophage IL-12 makes Th1 cells, and Th1 IFN-gamma makes activated macrophages."},{"type":"table","title":"Caseating and noncaseating granulomas","cols":["Feature","Caseating","Noncaseating"],"rows":[["Center","Caseous necrosis, crumbly and amorphous","No central necrosis"],["Typical causes","Tuberculosis, Histoplasma, Coccidioides, Blastomyces","Sarcoidosis, Crohn disease, berylliosis, foreign body, cat-scratch (suppurative center)"],["Mechanism of necrosis","Hypoxia plus mycobacterial lipid and TNF-driven host cell death","Agent contained without extensive cell death"],["Useful stains and findings","Acid-fast bacilli, methenamine silver for fungi","Asteroid or Schaumann bodies in sarcoidosis; polarizable material in foreign body"],["Skin presentation","Scrofuloderma, tuberculous chancre, deep fungal infection","Sarcoid plaques, lupus pernio, lupus vulgaris, granuloma annulare (palisaded necrobiotic)"]],"caption":"Caseation is the discriminator; a granuloma is not a diagnosis until organisms are sought and excluded."},{"type":"p","text":"Two cautions matter for board reasoning. First, the name of the immunodeficiency is misleading: in chronic granulomatous disease granulomas form because NADPH oxidase failure leaves catalase-positive organisms undigested, not because granuloma formation is exaggerated. Second, foreign body granulomas form around suture, talc, or keratin without requiring T cell help, so giant cells may appear with little lymphocytic rim."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01-C04","obj":"IN-11.4","title":"The four hypersensitivity types as skin reactions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypersensitivity is immune injury to the host from a response that is normal in kind. The skin displays all four types, and because each uses a different effector, each has a characteristic latency. Timing is the most useful discriminator."},{"type":"p","text":"Type I is IgE mediated. First exposure drives Th2 help and IL-4 dependent class switching to IgE, which binds FcepsilonRI on mast cells; re-exposure cross-links that IgE and degranulation follows within minutes. Histamine at H1 receptors opens venular gaps, so fluid in the superficial dermis makes a wheal while a deeper leak makes angioedema. A late phase at about 2 to 24 hours, from newly synthesized leukotrienes and recruited eosinophils, explains lesions that return after the first wheal fades."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II is injury by IgG or IgM bound to a fixed tissue antigen, and in skin those antigens are adhesion molecules, so the lesion is a blister. Pemphigus vulgaris antibodies disable desmosomes by binding desmoglein 3 and 1, keratinocytes separate (acantholysis), and the blister is intraepidermal and flaccid. Bullous pemphigoid antibodies bind BP180 and BP230 in hemidesmosomes at the lamina lucida, so the whole epidermis lifts off as a tense subepidermal bulla. Neither needs cell lysis: the antibody blocks adhesion and recruits complement and proteases."},{"type":"p","text":"Type III is immune complex disease. With antigen and antibody in roughly comparable amounts, complexes precipitate in vessel walls, fix complement, generate C5a, and recruit neutrophils whose enzymes destroy the wall. Locally that is the Arthus reaction at 4 to 12 hours; systemically, deposits in small dermal vessels give leukocytoclastic vasculitis with palpable purpura, fibrinoid necrosis, and neutrophil nuclear dust, and complement consumption lowers serum C3 and C4."},{"type":"p","text":"Type IV needs no antibody. A small reactive molecule such as nickel ion or urushiol acts as a hapten, binds skin protein, and is carried by Langerhans cells to the node to prime T cells. On re-exposure those effector T cells return to skin and release IFN-gamma, while CD8 cells kill keratinocytes directly. Recruitment takes time, so the reaction peaks at 48 to 72 hours with spongiotic dermatitis, and the tuberculin reaction is firm induration rather than a soft wheal because the swelling is cellular infiltrate."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypersensitivity types in the skin","cols":["Type","Effector","Key mediators","Skin example","Onset after exposure"],"rows":[["I","IgE on mast cells","Histamine, leukotrienes C4 D4 E4, tryptase, PGD2","Urticaria, angioedema, anaphylaxis","Minutes; late phase about 2 to 24 hours"],["II","IgG against fixed antigen","Complement, proteases, loss of adhesion","Pemphigus vulgaris (desmoglein), bullous pemphigoid (BP180, BP230)","Follows titer; days to weeks"],["III","Antigen-antibody complexes","C3a and C5a, neutrophil enzymes, fibrinoid necrosis","Arthus reaction, leukocytoclastic vasculitis","Arthus 4 to 12 hours; serum sickness 7 to 14 days"],["IV","Sensitized T cells and macrophages","IFN-gamma, TNF, CD8 cytotoxicity","Allergic contact dermatitis, tuberculin reaction","48 to 72 hours"]],"caption":"Mediator class sets the latency: preformed granules in minutes, complement and neutrophils in hours, cell recruitment in days."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing confusable skin lesions","cols":["Pair","Discriminator"],"rows":[["Urticarial wheal and tuberculin induration","Soft plasma edema in minutes versus firm cellular infiltrate at 48 to 72 hours"],["Pemphigus vulgaris and bullous pemphigoid","Intraepidermal flaccid blister with mucosal disease and net-like IgG versus subepidermal tense blister with linear junctional IgG"],["Allergic and irritant contact dermatitis","Allergic needs prior sensitization and spreads past the contact area; irritant is dose-dependent toxicity with sharp borders on first exposure"],["Palpable purpura and thrombocytopenic petechiae","Raised from vessel wall inflammation versus flat and noninflammatory"],["Histaminergic and hereditary angioedema","Pruritic with wheals versus nonpruritic without urticaria and with low C4"]],"caption":"Each discriminator traces back to the mediator and the anatomic plane of the lesion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient develops firm, indurated, nonpruritic plaques 60 hours after using a new topical product, with no wheals at any point. Which hypersensitivity type is this?","explain":"Use the latency and texture of the lesion to name the effector, then predict the infiltrate.","check":"Compare with the hypersensitivity table above."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01-C05","obj":"IN-11.5","title":"Autoimmune skin findings and the diseases they signal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Connective tissue diseases are visible in skin because their targets, nuclear antigens, basement membrane zones, small vessels, and fibroblasts, are all accessible there. Lesion plus biopsy pattern plus antibody narrows the diagnosis far better than any one of the three."},{"type":"p","text":"In systemic lupus erythematosus, ultraviolet light induces keratinocyte apoptosis and translocates nuclear antigens including Ro/SSA to the cell surface and into apoptotic blebs. Impaired clearance of that debris allows autoantibody binding, and the resulting complexes fix complement along the dermal-epidermal junction. The acute lesion is a photosensitive malar erythema across the cheeks and nasal bridge that characteristically spares the nasolabial folds, since those creases are shaded. Biopsy shows interface dermatitis: a lymphocytic infiltrate at the junction with vacuolar change and apoptotic basal keratinocytes, plus epidermal atrophy and dermal mucin. Direct immunofluorescence shows a granular band of IgG, IgM, and C3 along the junction, often called the lupus band, granular because it marks deposited immune complexes rather than antibody bound to one linear structure. Discoid lesions are deeper and follicular, with dense perifollicular inflammation and follicular plugging; because the inflammation destroys the follicle and its stem cell reservoir, discoid lesions scar and cause permanent alopecia and dyspigmentation, while acute malar lesions do not."},{"type":"p","text":"Dermatomyositis is a complement-mediated microangiopathy. Antibody and complement deposit on endothelium of skin and muscle capillaries, membrane attack complex forms, capillaries drop out, and the resulting ischemia explains both the rash and the perifascicular muscle atrophy seen in muscle biopsy. The skin findings are Gottron papules, violaceous flat-topped papules over the knuckles and other extensor bony prominences, and a heliotrope rash, a violaceous periorbital discoloration with lid edema, alongside a shawl sign and photodistributed poikiloderma. Weakness is proximal and symmetric, with elevated creatine kinase, and adult-onset disease carries an increased risk of occult internal malignancy. Polymyositis shares the muscle picture without the characteristic skin lesions, and its muscle biopsy shows CD8 T cell invasion of nonnecrotic fibers rather than perifascicular ischemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic sclerosis combines small-vessel endothelial injury with fibroblast activation. Endothelial damage plus vasospasm produces Raynaud phenomenon, usually the earliest symptom, and TGF-beta and connective tissue growth factor drive fibroblasts to deposit excess type I collagen in the dermis. Clinically the skin becomes thick and bound down, fingers become tapered and immobile (sclerodactyly), the mouth aperture narrows, and the face loses expressive folds. Antibody subsets map to extent and organ risk. Anticentromere marks limited cutaneous disease, the CREST pattern of calcinosis, Raynaud, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasia, with pulmonary arterial hypertension as the feared complication. Anti-Scl-70 (antitopoisomerase I) and anti-RNA polymerase III mark diffuse disease, the first carrying the highest risk of interstitial lung fibrosis and the second the highest risk of scleroderma renal crisis."},{"type":"table","title":"Autoimmune skin findings, histology, and antibodies","cols":["Disease","Skin finding","Biopsy or immunofluorescence","Antibodies"],"rows":[["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Malar rash sparing nasolabial folds; scarring discoid plaques with follicular plugging","Interface dermatitis; granular junctional IgG, IgM, C3","ANA, anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith, anti-Ro/SSA (subacute cutaneous and neonatal lupus)"],["Dermatomyositis","Gottron papules, heliotrope rash, shawl sign; proximal weakness","Interface change with dermal mucin; perifascicular atrophy and capillary loss in muscle","ANA, anti-Mi-2, anti-Jo-1, anti-TIF1-gamma (malignancy association)"],["Limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","Sclerodactyly, telangiectasia, calcinosis, Raynaud","Dermal collagen thickening with loss of adnexa","Anticentromere"],["Diffuse systemic sclerosis","Widespread truncal and proximal skin thickening, Raynaud","Dense dermal and subcutaneous fibrosis","Anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I), anti-RNA polymerase III"]],"caption":"Pattern plus antibody, not the rash alone, identifies the disease and predicts the organ at risk."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01-C06","obj":"IN-11.6","title":"Tumor immunity, immune escape, and skin cancer in transplant recipients","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Tumor cells become immune targets when they display peptides the immune system has not learned to tolerate. These come from mutated proteins (neoantigens), from oncogenic viral proteins such as HPV E6 and E7 or Merkel cell polyomavirus large T antigen, and from reexpressed embryonic or testis antigens. Ultraviolet radiation gives cutaneous tumors a high mutational burden, so sun-exposed skin cancers are unusually antigenic, which is both why immunosurveillance matters there and why checkpoint blockade works well in melanoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Two killers do the work, and they read opposite signals. CD8 cytotoxic T cells require peptide presented on MHC class I, recognized through a T cell receptor with CD8 as coreceptor; after priming by dendritic cells that cross-present tumor antigen, they kill through perforin and granzyme delivery and through Fas ligand, both of which activate caspases and apoptosis. Natural killer cells have no antigen-specific receptor. They integrate activating signals from stress-induced ligands such as MICA and MICB binding NKG2D against inhibitory signals from killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptors that recognize self MHC class I. Normal MHC class I expression therefore restrains NK cells, and its loss releases them, which is the missing self principle. NK cells also kill antibody-coated cells through CD16, which is antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumor escape exploits exactly these requirements. Loss or downregulation of MHC class I, through beta-2 microglobulin mutation or defective antigen processing with loss of TAP transporters, hides the tumor from CD8 cells. Expression of PD-L1, often induced by the IFN-gamma that infiltrating T cells themselves produce, engages PD-1 on activated T cells and delivers an inhibitory signal that blunts their receptor signaling: an adaptive resistance in which the attack triggers its own brake. Tumors also secrete TGF-beta and IL-10, recruit regulatory T cells and myeloid-derived suppressor cells, and simply stop expressing the antigens that the strongest clones recognize, which is immunoediting: selection of antigen-loss variants under immune pressure."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why a tumor that loses MHC class I is not safe","steps":["Tumor displays neoantigen peptide on MHC class I","CD8 T cells recognize it and kill through perforin and granzyme","Tumor variant loses MHC class I and escapes CD8 recognition","KIR inhibitory signal to NK cells is lost (missing self)","NKG2D sees stress ligands MICA and MICB without inhibition","NK cells kill the MHC class I negative variant"],"caption":"The opposite MHC requirements of CD8 and NK cells make escape from one arm an opening for the other."},{"type":"p","text":"Organ transplant recipients show what happens when this surveillance is removed. Lifelong immunosuppression, particularly calcineurin inhibitors and azathioprine, suppresses T cell activation and therefore the CD8 response to ultraviolet-mutated keratinocytes and to human papillomavirus-infected cells. Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma incidence rises many times over, the tumors are more numerous, arise earlier, behave more aggressively, and metastasize more often, and the usual ratio reverses so that squamous cell carcinomas outnumber basal cell carcinomas. Two mechanisms beyond immunosuppression add to the effect: azathioprine metabolites incorporate 6-thioguanine into DNA, which absorbs ultraviolet A and generates reactive oxygen species, and calcineurin inhibitors have direct effects on keratinocyte DNA repair and TGF-beta signaling. Squamous cell carcinoma is affected more than basal cell carcinoma, consistent with its stronger dependence on viral cofactors and on immune control of mutated keratinocytes."},{"type":"table","title":"CD8 T cells and NK cells compared","cols":["Feature","CD8 cytotoxic T cell","Natural killer cell"],"rows":[["Target recognition","Specific peptide on MHC class I","Balance of activating and inhibitory receptors, no peptide specificity"],["Effect of MHC class I loss on target","Escapes killing","Becomes more susceptible (missing self)"],["Priming required","Yes, by dendritic cell with costimulation","No, acts immediately; IL-12 and IL-15 enhance"],["Killing machinery","Perforin and granzyme, Fas ligand","Perforin and granzyme, Fas ligand, CD16-mediated ADCC"],["Memory","Yes, long-lived antigen-specific memory","Limited, mostly innate with some adaptive-like features"]],"caption":"The complementary MHC logic of the two cell types is the basis of most tumor immunology questions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-11-01-C07","obj":"IN-11.7","title":"Vaccine immunology: virus-like particles, live attenuated vaccines, and reactions to boosters","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A vaccine produces immunologic memory without the disease, and the response depends on where the antigen ends up inside a cell. Exogenous protein taken up by a dendritic cell loads on MHC class II and primes CD4 helper T cells that support class switching and affinity maturation, yielding high-affinity IgG and memory B cells. Protein made in the cytosol of an infected cell is processed by the proteasome, loads on MHC class I, and primes CD8 cytotoxic T cells as well. That one rule predicts what each platform yields."},{"type":"p","text":"The human papillomavirus vaccine is recombinant L1 major capsid protein made in yeast or insect cells. L1 self-assembles into virus-like particles that reproduce the geometry of the virion surface but contain no viral DNA, so they cannot replicate or transform a cell. Their repeating conformational epitopes sit in a dense ordered array, which cross-links B cell receptors strongly and, with adjuvant and CD4 help, drives durable neutralizing IgG. That antibody keeps L1 from binding heparan sulfate proteoglycans and entering basal keratinocytes, so infection is blocked at entry. The vaccine is prophylactic for that reason: it cannot clear established infection, in which E6 and E7 are expressed intracellularly and out of antibody reach."},{"type":"p","text":"Live attenuated varicella vaccine uses the Oka strain, passaged until it replicates poorly in human tissue. Because it does replicate, it makes viral protein inside host cells, so antigen reaches both the MHC class II and the MHC class I pathway. The result is antibody plus CD4 and CD8 memory, which matters for a virus held latent in dorsal root ganglia by cell-mediated immunity. Replication also explains why a mild vesicular rash can follow vaccination and why a replication-competent vaccine strain carries risk in significant immunosuppression and in pregnancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Vaccine platforms and the immunity they produce","cols":["Platform","Example","Antigen route","Immunity produced","Main limitation"],"rows":[["Virus-like particle (recombinant subunit)","HPV L1 vaccine","Exogenous, MHC class II","Strong neutralizing IgG; little CD8 response","Prophylactic only; needs adjuvant and a series"],["Live attenuated","Varicella (Oka strain), MMR","Replicates intracellularly, MHC class I and II","Antibody plus CD4 and CD8 memory; often long lasting","Replication carries risk in immunosuppression and pregnancy; mild rash possible"],["Toxoid","Tetanus and diphtheria toxoid","Exogenous, MHC class II","Neutralizing antitoxin IgG","Needs boosters; repeat doses risk local reactions"],["Inactivated whole organism","Hepatitis A, inactivated polio","Exogenous, MHC class II","IgG, no cytotoxic T cell priming","Boosters needed; weaker mucosal immunity"]],"caption":"Whether a vaccine primes CD8 cells depends on whether its antigen is made inside the host cell."},{"type":"p","text":"An Arthus reaction after a toxoid booster is the predictable consequence of giving antigen to someone who already carries a high circulating antitoxin IgG titer. Antigen injected into the dermis and subcutis meets antibody diffusing from local vessels, complexes form near equivalence in and around the vessel walls, classical complement activation produces C3a and C5a, and recruited neutrophils injure the vessel. The reaction is local, peaks at about 4 to 12 hours with painful induration and edema over a wide area of the limb, and resolves over days. It is type III rather than an allergy to the vaccine, reflecting too much pre-existing immunity rather than too little, which is why the toxoid booster interval is deliberately long. Distinguish it from type I urticaria within minutes and from a type IV adjuvant reaction at 48 to 72 hours."},{"type":"flow","title":"Arthus reaction after a booster in a highly immune person","steps":["Toxoid antigen injected into dermis and subcutis","High-titer IgG diffuses from local vessels","Immune complexes form near equivalence in and around vessel walls","Classical complement activation generates C3a and C5a","Neutrophils recruited; enzymes and reactive oxygen injure the vessel","Painful edematous, sometimes hemorrhagic swelling at 4 to 12 hours","Complexes cleared; resolution over days"],"caption":"The time course places the reaction: minutes means IgE, hours means immune complexes, days means T cells."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology decide what a cutaneous immune lesion looks like. The plane of injury is the diagnosis: separation between keratinocytes gives a flaccid intraepidermal blister, separation at the lamina lucida gives a tense subepidermal one, inflammation concentrated at the junction gives interface dermatitis with scarring only when it reaches the follicle, and fluid in the superficial dermis gives a wheal while fluid in the lower dermis and subcutis gives angioedema. Because postcapillary venules are the vessels that respond to inflammatory mediators, both edema and immune complex vasculitis are centered there, which is why palpable purpura is a small-vessel lesion of dependent skin."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, microbiology, and pathology converge on the same point: the mediator class sets the time course, and the time course is the exam's most reliable clue. Preformed mast cell granules act in minutes, complement and neutrophil recruitment in hours, and T cell recruitment and macrophage activation in days, which is why the tuberculin reaction is indurated at 48 to 72 hours while urticaria is soft at 10 minutes. The same cytokines reappear in different roles, so a mechanism learned once pays off repeatedly: IL-1 and TNF induce the endothelial adhesion molecules that make an infiltrate possible, TNF also holds a granuloma together, IFN-gamma activates macrophages and simultaneously induces tumor PD-L1, and IL-12 commits Th1 cells whose IFN-gamma feeds the loop. The limits are worth stating plainly: hypersensitivity classification is a teaching framework and real diseases often combine types, a granuloma identifies a pattern rather than a cause, antinuclear antibody positivity is common in healthy people and only the subset antibody carries diagnostic weight, and biopsy and immunofluorescence findings are probabilistic patterns read in clinical context."}],"summary":["Keratinocytes sense microbes through TLR2 and TLR4, signal through NF-kB, and release IL-1, TNF, beta-defensins, and cathelicidin LL-37; perivascular mast cells convert that alarm into vasodilation and venular leak within minutes.","Naive T cells cannot enter skin, so Langerhans cells capture antigen, upregulate CCR7, migrate to the regional node, and prime T cells with peptide on MHC plus B7 to CD28; without costimulation the T cell becomes anergic.","Leukocyte recruitment is a fixed sequence of receptor pairs: selectins and sialyl Lewis X for rolling, chemokine-activated CD18 integrins binding ICAM-1 for firm adhesion, PECAM-1 for transmigration, then chemotaxis toward C5a, LTB4, and IL-8.","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 is a CD18 defect: rolling persists but adhesion fails, so blood neutrophils are very high while tissue shows infections without pus, poor healing, and delayed umbilical cord separation.","Acute inflammation resolves when the agent is cleared and matrix is intact, suppurates into an abscess when pyogenic organisms drive liquefactive necrosis, and becomes chronic when the agent persists, with injury, repair, and fibrosis proceeding together.","Granulomas are built by a feedback loop: macrophage IL-12 commits Th1 cells, Th1 IFN-gamma converts macrophages into epithelioid and giant cells, and TNF sustains the aggregate; caseation points to tuberculosis or deep fungi, absence of caseation to sarcoidosis, Crohn disease, or foreign body.","Hypersensitivity timing names the mechanism: minutes for IgE urticaria and angioedema, 4 to 12 hours for the Arthus reaction and days to weeks for antibody-mediated blistering, 48 to 72 hours for contact dermatitis and the tuberculin reaction.","Pemphigus vulgaris targets desmoglein and gives flaccid intraepidermal bullae with net-like IgG; bullous pemphigoid targets BP180 and BP230 and gives tense subepidermal bullae with a linear junctional band.","Lupus shows interface dermatitis with a granular junctional immunofluorescence band and scars only in discoid lesions; dermatomyositis is a complement-mediated microangiopathy with Gottron papules and heliotrope rash; systemic sclerosis maps to anticentromere in limited disease and anti-Scl-70 in diffuse disease with lung fibrosis.","CD8 cells need MHC class I and NK cells are inhibited by it, so MHC loss trades one killer for the other, while PD-L1 expression brakes activated T cells; removing T cell surveillance in transplant recipients produces many aggressive cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas that outnumber basal cell carcinomas."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Inflammation and Repair; Diseases of the Immune System; Neoplasia; The Skin"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity; hypersensitivity and autoimmunity; immunity to tumors"},{"book":"Junqueira Basic Histology","where":"Skin"},{"book":"Jawetz Medical Microbiology","where":"Papillomaviruses and Polyomaviruses; Herpesviruses; immunization"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Immune System"}]},"IN-12":{"unit":"IN-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01","title":"Skin Pathogens","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Skin is a dry, salty, lipid-coated, constantly shedding surface, so the organisms that cause disease there are the ones equipped to hold on, digest keratin, hide inside cells, or arrive by injection from an arthropod. This chapter covers the bacteria (Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, Cutibacterium acnes, Pseudomonas aeruginosa), the superficial fungi (dermatophytes, Malassezia, Candida albicans), the DNA viruses (human papillomavirus, molluscum contagiosum, the herpesviruses), and the vector-borne infections that announce themselves on the skin. Board questions give a Gram stain, a culture reaction, a smear, or an exposure history and expect you to name the organism and explain how one virulence factor produces the lesion.","prerequisites":["Structure of the epidermis, stratum corneum, hair follicle, and sebaceous gland (IN-1, IN-2)","Innate skin defenses: barrier lipids, acidic pH, antimicrobial peptides, resident flora (IN-10)","Gram stain, catalase, coagulase, hemolysis, and oxidase as identification tests","Neutrophil recruitment, opsonization by IgG and C3b, and complement activation","T cell subsets, especially Th17 and IL-17, and the consequences of neutropenia"],"connections":[{"to":"Skin immunology and hypersensitivity (IN-11)","how":"Keratinocyte Toll-like receptors and Langerhans cells start the inflammatory response that turns colonization into visible disease."},{"to":"Renal pathology and glomerular disease (UR)","how":"Nephritogenic group A streptococcal skin infection seeds immune complex glomerulonephritis, which is the renal consequence of a skin event."},{"to":"Immunodeficiency (IM)","how":"The site of a Candida infection reports the defect: Th17 and IL-17 pathway failure gives mucocutaneous disease, neutropenia gives invasive disease."},{"to":"Neoplasia and cell cycle control (pathology)","how":"High-risk HPV removes p53 and Rb control through E6 and E7, which is the classic viral route to squamous carcinoma."},{"to":"Peripheral nervous system anatomy (NE)","how":"Herpesvirus latency in sensory ganglia is why recurrent lesions map onto a dermatome or a single cranial nerve territory."}],"terms":[{"term":"Virulence factor","def":"A microbial product that promotes infection by defeating a specific host defense or damaging host tissue."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A toxin that cross-links the MHC class II molecule to the T cell receptor variable beta region, activating many T cell clones without antigen processing."},{"term":"Coagulase","def":"A staphylococcal enzyme that activates prothrombin so fibrinogen becomes fibrin, which helps coat the organism and wall off an abscess."},{"term":"Keratinase","def":"A fungal protease that digests keratin, allowing growth within stratum corneum, hair, and nail."},{"term":"Dermatophyte","def":"A keratinophilic mold of the genera Trichophyton, Microsporum, or Epidermophyton that infects only keratinized, nonliving tissue."},{"term":"Pseudohypha","def":"A chain of elongated budding yeast cells that remain attached, with constrictions at the septa rather than the parallel walls of a true hypha."},{"term":"Koilocyte","def":"A papillomavirus-infected keratinocyte with a wrinkled, raisin-like nucleus surrounded by a clear perinuclear halo."},{"term":"Latency","def":"A viral state in which the genome persists in a cell with little or no protein production, so the cell is not recognized and killed."},{"term":"Tzanck smear","def":"A Giemsa- or Wright-stained scraping from a vesicle base in which multinucleated giant cells indicate a herpesvirus."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of an antibody or T cell raised against a microbial antigen with a structurally similar host antigen."},{"term":"Erythema migrans","def":"The expanding annular erythematous lesion at the site of an Ixodes bite produced by spirochetes spreading outward through dermis."},{"term":"Molluscum body","def":"A large eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion in an infected keratinocyte representing accumulated poxvirus assembly."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C01","obj":"IN-12.1","title":"Staphylococcus aureus: identification, virulence factors, and methicillin resistance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus aureus is a gram-positive coccus that divides in several planes, so it appears in grape-like clusters. It is a facultative anaerobe, catalase positive (which separates all staphylococci from streptococci), and coagulase positive, which separates it from the coagulase-negative skin residents such as Staphylococcus epidermidis. It is salt tolerant, grows on mannitol salt agar with a yellow halo from mannitol fermentation, and is beta-hemolytic. About 30% of people carry it in the anterior nares, which is the usual reservoir for later skin infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Each virulence factor defeats one defense, and the lesion follows from which factors are expressed. Coagulase activates prothrombin so that fibrinogen is converted to fibrin, which coats the organism and contributes to the fibrin wall of an abscess; this is why staphylococcal infection is typically localized and purulent rather than spreading. Protein A binds the Fc portion of IgG, so bound antibody faces outward in the wrong orientation and can neither be recognized by the neutrophil Fc receptor nor activate the classical complement pathway. Panton-Valentine leukocidin assembles pores in the membranes of neutrophils and macrophages and lyses them, which accounts for the extensive necrosis of community-associated methicillin-resistant abscesses and necrotizing pneumonia. Catalase degrades hydrogen peroxide, blunting the neutrophil oxidative burst."},{"type":"p","text":"Two exotoxins act at a distance from the organism and produce syndromes that look nothing like an abscess. Exfoliative toxins A and B are serine proteases that cleave desmoglein 1, the desmosomal cadherin holding granular layer keratinocytes together, so the epidermis splits high within itself and separates without inflammation or mucosal involvement: this is bullous impetigo when local and staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome when the toxin is blood-borne. Toxic shock syndrome toxin 1 is a superantigen that bridges MHC class II to the variable beta region of the T cell receptor, bypassing antigen processing and activating up to 20% of T cells at once, with massive interleukin-2, interferon gamma, and tumor necrosis factor release causing fever, rash, and capillary leak with shock."},{"type":"table","title":"Staphylococcus aureus virulence factors and the defense each defeats","cols":["Factor","Mechanism","Host defense defeated or tissue effect"],"rows":[["Coagulase","Activates prothrombin; fibrinogen becomes fibrin","Fibrin coat and abscess wall limit immune access"],["Protein A","Binds IgG Fc region","Opsonization and classical complement activation"],["Panton-Valentine leukocidin","Pore-forming toxin in leukocyte membranes","Kills neutrophils; tissue necrosis"],["Catalase","Degrades hydrogen peroxide","Neutrophil oxidative burst"],["Exfoliative toxin A and B","Protease cleaving desmoglein 1","Intraepidermal split: bullous impetigo, scalded skin"],["TSST-1","Superantigen bridging MHC II and T cell receptor Vbeta","Polyclonal T cell activation and cytokine storm"]],"caption":"Suppurative, localized disease comes from the surface and enzymatic factors; diffuse disease comes from the two exotoxin groups."},{"type":"p","text":"Methicillin resistance is a change in the drug target rather than drug destruction. Beta-lactams work by acylating the active site serine of penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan. The mecA gene, carried on a mobile staphylococcal cassette chromosome, encodes PBP2a, an alternative transpeptidase whose active site has very low affinity for beta-lactams. When ordinary penicillin-binding proteins are inhibited, PBP2a continues cross-linking the cell wall, so the organism keeps growing and every standard beta-lactam fails, including nafcillin and the cephalosporins that target the usual proteins. This is a different mechanism from penicillinase, the beta-lactamase that hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring and that nafcillin resists by steric hindrance."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has flaccid bullae that shear off with light pressure, no mucosal lesions, and sterile blister fluid. Which S aureus factor explains all three findings?","explain":"Exfoliative toxin. It is blood-borne, so the organism is not in the blister, it cleaves desmoglein 1 within the granular layer, so the split is superficial and the roof shears off, and desmoglein 1 is not the dominant adhesion molecule in mucosa, so mucous membranes are spared.","check":"The exotoxin paragraph and the virulence factor table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C02","obj":"IN-12.2","title":"Streptococcus pyogenes: identification, virulence, and why skin infection causes nephritis but not rheumatic fever","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes, group A Streptococcus, is a gram-positive coccus in chains and is catalase negative, which separates it from the staphylococci. On blood agar it is beta-hemolytic, producing complete clearing from hemolysins. Among beta-hemolytic streptococci it is bacitracin sensitive and PYR positive (it produces the enzyme pyrrolidonyl arylamidase), while group B Streptococcus is bacitracin resistant and hippurate positive with a positive CAMP test. Grouping rests on the Lancefield carbohydrate in the cell wall, and typing on the M protein."},{"type":"p","text":"Where S aureus walls itself off, S pyogenes spreads, and its virulence factors explain that difference. M protein, a surface fibrillar protein, binds the complement regulator factor H, which accelerates C3b breakdown on the bacterial surface, so opsonization and phagocytosis fail. The hyaluronic acid capsule is chemically the same polymer as host connective tissue ground substance, so it is poorly immunogenic and resists engulfment. Streptokinase converts plasminogen to plasmin, which lyses the fibrin that would otherwise contain the organism, and hyaluronidase and DNases loosen ground substance and liquefy the viscous DNA of lysed neutrophils. Together these let infection advance along fascial planes and dermal lymphatics, which is why erysipelas and cellulitis spread diffusely with lymphangitic streaking rather than forming a single abscess. Streptolysin O is an oxygen-labile, cholesterol-dependent pore-forming cytolysin and is strongly immunogenic, so the antistreptolysin O titer documents recent infection; streptolysin S is oxygen stable and responsible for surface hemolysis but is not immunogenic. Pyrogenic exotoxins A and C are superantigens, giving the sandpaper rash and strawberry tongue of scarlet fever and, with deeper invasion, streptococcal toxic shock."},{"type":"p","text":"The two major postinfectious syndromes follow different mechanisms, and that difference is the reason skin infection can cause one but not the other. Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is immune complex disease: antibody binds nephritogenic streptococcal antigens, complexes accumulate on the subepithelial side of the glomerular capillary wall as the subepithelial humps seen on electron microscopy, complement is activated, and a nephritic picture of hematuria, periorbital edema, hypertension, and low serum C3 appears about 1 to 3 weeks after pharyngitis or about 3 to 6 weeks after impetigo. The antigens involved belong to strains that cause either pharyngitis or impetigo, so the skin route works. Acute rheumatic fever instead depends on molecular mimicry: antibodies and T cells raised against M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin, laminin, and valve glycoproteins, and only a limited set of rheumatogenic M types, carried in the throat rather than on skin, elicit that response. The prevailing explanation is that the pharyngeal lymphoid tissue generates the systemic antibody and T cell response needed, while skin strains differ in M type and provoke a weaker systemic response, so pyoderma is followed by nephritis but not carditis."},{"type":"table","title":"Staphylococcus aureus versus Streptococcus pyogenes on the skin","cols":["Feature","S aureus","S pyogenes"],"rows":[["Microscopy and catalase","Clusters, catalase positive","Chains, catalase negative"],["Confirming tests","Coagulase positive, mannitol fermenting","Beta-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive, PYR positive"],["Antiphagocytic factor","Protein A binding IgG Fc","M protein binding factor H; hyaluronic acid capsule"],["Behavior in tissue","Walls off: abscess, furuncle, carbuncle","Spreads: erysipelas, cellulitis, lymphangitis"],["Superantigen disease","TSST-1 toxic shock","Scarlet fever, streptococcal toxic shock"],["Postinfectious sequelae","None characteristic","Glomerulonephritis after skin or throat; rheumatic fever after throat only"]],"caption":"Abscess versus spreading erythema is the bedside expression of fibrin generation versus fibrinolysis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From impetigo to acute glomerulonephritis","steps":["Nephritogenic group A strep infects broken skin","Antibody forms against streptococcal antigens","Complexes deposit subepithelially in glomerular capillary walls","Complement activation consumes C3, drawing in neutrophils","Glomerular capillary injury reduces filtration","Hematuria, periorbital edema, hypertension, low serum C3"],"caption":"An immune complex mechanism, so the kidney injury appears weeks after the skin lesion (about 3 to 6 weeks after impetigo), not during it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C03","obj":"IN-12.3","title":"Dermatophytes and Malassezia: fungi that live in keratin and sebum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dermatophytes are keratinophilic molds in three genera: Trichophyton, Microsporum, and Epidermophyton. They secrete keratinases, mostly subtilisin-like and fungalysin proteases, that cleave the disulfide-rich keratin of cornified tissue into peptides the fungus can absorb. Because their nutrient source is dead keratin and they are restricted by temperature and by serum inhibitory factors, they grow in the stratum corneum, hair shaft, and nail plate and do not invade living epidermis or dermis in an immunocompetent host. The visible lesion is therefore produced partly by the fungus and largely by the host: hyphae advance outward from the point of inoculation while the immune response and resulting inflammation are strongest at the advancing margin, giving the annular scaly plaque with a raised active border and a clearing center that is called tinea or ringworm."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on microscopy of scale, nail, or hair in 10% to 20% potassium hydroxide, which dissolves keratinocytes and leaves the alkali-resistant fungal wall visible. Dermatophytes show long, branching, septate hyphae, often with arthroconidia. Site names describe location rather than organism: tinea capitis, corporis, cruris, pedis, and unguium. The genera differ in which keratinized tissues they invade, a distinction that is commonly tested."},{"type":"table","title":"Dermatophyte genera and tissues invaded","cols":["Genus","Skin","Hair","Nail"],"rows":[["Trichophyton","Yes","Yes","Yes"],["Microsporum","Yes","Yes","No"],["Epidermophyton","Yes","No","Yes"]],"caption":"Trichophyton invades all three; Microsporum spares nail and Epidermophyton spares hair."},{"type":"p","text":"Malassezia is a different organism with a different niche and must not be grouped with the dermatophytes despite the name tinea versicolor. It is a lipophilic, lipid-dependent budding yeast that lacks the enzymes for fatty acid synthesis, so it requires exogenous lipid and lives as normal flora in sebum-rich skin of the upper trunk, shoulders, neck, and face. Overgrowth under heat, humidity, occlusion, or high sebum output produces well-demarcated macules that release fine scale when scraped and that may fluoresce yellow-green under a Wood lamp. The scrape in potassium hydroxide shows the characteristic combination of short, blunt, sometimes angular hyphal fragments mixed with clusters of round budding yeast cells, the appearance described as spaghetti and meatballs, which is distinct from the long branching septate hyphae of a dermatophyte."},{"type":"p","text":"The pigment change explains the word versicolor. Malassezia produces dicarboxylic acids, including azelaic acid, that competitively inhibit tyrosinase in neighboring melanocytes, so melanin synthesis in the involved skin falls and lesions appear hypopigmented, a difference that becomes obvious when surrounding skin tans. In other patients, or on other body sites and skin tones, lesions appear hyperpigmented, attributed to a thickened keratin layer, larger melanosomes, and the inflammatory response rather than to reduced melanin. Malassezia also contributes to Pityrosporum folliculitis and to seborrheic dermatitis, where lipase-released free fatty acids irritate the follicular and scalp epithelium."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why a dermatophyte lesion is a ring","steps":["Arthroconidium lands on skin and germinates in stratum corneum","Keratinases digest keratin and hyphae spread outward radially","Antigen reaches viable epidermis and triggers a cellular response","Inflammation and scale concentrate at the advancing hyphal front","Central corneum is shed with the fungus and heals","Annular plaque with active raised border and clearing center"],"caption":"The border is where the live fungus is, which is why scrapings for potassium hydroxide are taken from the edge."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C04","obj":"IN-12.4","title":"Candida albicans: commensal to pathogen, and what the site of disease reveals about immunity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Candida albicans is a commensal yeast of the oral cavity, gastrointestinal tract, and vagina that becomes a pathogen when local conditions or host defenses change. It is often described as dimorphic because it switches reversibly between forms depending on temperature, pH, carbon dioxide, and nutrients: a round or oval budding yeast at lower temperature, and elongated pseudohyphae and true hyphae at 37 degrees Celsius (98.6 degrees Fahrenheit) in serum or tissue. This switch matters because the yeast form is suited to adherence and dissemination while the filamentous form provides the mechanical thrust and the secreted aspartyl proteases and candidalysin that penetrate epithelium. In the laboratory, Candida albicans forms true germ tubes within a few hours in serum at 37 degrees Celsius (98.6 degrees Fahrenheit) and produces thick-walled terminal chlamydoconidia (chlamydospores) on cornmeal agar, both of which separate it from most other Candida species. In tissue or a potassium hydroxide preparation, budding yeasts together with pseudohyphae showing constrictions at the septa identify it."},{"type":"p","text":"The mucosal and cutaneous surface is defended in layers. Intact keratinized epithelium, an acidic surface, and a competing bacterial flora that occupies adherence sites and consumes nutrients hold the organism at low density. Above that, epithelial cells recognize fungal cell wall mannan and beta-glucan through C-type lectin receptors, principally dectin-1, signaling through the adaptor CARD9 to drive a Th17 response; interleukin-17 and interleukin-22 then recruit neutrophils to the epithelium and induce antimicrobial peptides and epithelial repair. Within tissue and blood, neutrophils are the decisive effector, killing hyphae by oxidative burst and by releasing neutrophil extracellular traps. Each predisposing factor removes one of these layers, so the pattern of disease identifies which layer failed."},{"type":"table","title":"Predisposing factors and the defense each removes","cols":["Factor","What it changes","Typical disease pattern"],"rows":[["Moisture, occlusion, maceration","Barrier softens and surface pH rises","Intertrigo and diaper candidiasis with satellite pustules"],["Diabetes mellitus and hyperglycemia","Abundant glucose substrate and impaired neutrophil function","Recurrent vulvovaginal, oral, and intertriginous disease"],["Broad-spectrum antibiotics","Removes competing bacterial flora","Oral thrush, vulvovaginal candidiasis"],["Glucocorticoids","Suppress phagocyte recruitment and T cell function","Thrush, esophagitis, widespread cutaneous disease"],["Th17, IL-17 receptor, or CARD9 defects; AIRE mutation","No IL-17-driven epithelial defense","Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis of skin and nails; CARD9 defects can also permit invasive disease"],["Neutropenia; indwelling catheter","No tissue and intravascular killing; direct access to blood","Candidemia with renal, hepatosplenic, retinal, and skin lesions"]],"caption":"Loss of IL-17 driven epithelial defense usually keeps disease at the surface; loss of neutrophils allows invasion."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the immune defect from the site","text":"Two patients grow Candida albicans. The first, a young adult with thickened dystrophic nails and persistent oral and esophageal plaques since childhood but no bloodstream infection, has lost IL-17-dependent epithelial defense, so the fungus is never cleared from surfaces, yet neutrophils still prevent invasion. The second, receiving chemotherapy with a neutrophil count of 0.2 times 10 to the 9th per liter and a central line, develops fever and candidemia with scattered papules and retinal lesions. Surface defense is intact, but with no neutrophils in tissue the hyphal form penetrates and disseminates. Surface disease points to the Th17 axis; invasive disease points to neutrophils."},{"type":"flow","title":"Commensal to invasive candidiasis","steps":["Yeast adheres to epithelium through Als adhesins","Loss of competing flora or barrier allows overgrowth","Hyphal switch with secreted proteases and candidalysin","Epithelial penetration causes mucocutaneous disease","With neutropenia or a catheter, organisms enter the blood","Seeding of kidney, liver, spleen, retina, and skin"],"caption":"Each step needs a specific host failure, which is why Candida is common but invasive candidiasis is not."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C05","obj":"IN-12.5","title":"Human papillomavirus and molluscum contagiosum: two DNA viruses with opposite strategies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Human papillomavirus is a small nonenveloped virus with a circular double-stranded DNA genome of about 8 kilobases in an icosahedral capsid. Lacking an envelope, it is stable on surfaces and resistant to drying and detergents, which is why transmission works by direct skin contact, autoinoculation from scratching or shaving, fomites such as shower floors, and sexual contact. It enters basal keratinocytes through microabrasions, because only the basal layer still divides and can maintain the genome as a low-copy episome. Its replication is then tied to differentiation: as the infected cell moves upward and keratinizes, viral promoters switch on, the genome amplifies, capsid proteins L1 and L2 are made, and complete virions are assembled in the upper layers and shed with the cornified cells. The cytopathic signature of that late replication is the koilocyte, a superficial keratinocyte with a shrunken wrinkled nucleus and a clear perinuclear halo, accompanied by hyperkeratosis, acanthosis, and papillomatosis, which together make the verrucous surface of a wart."},{"type":"p","text":"Oncogenic potential separates the low-risk types (1, 2, and 4 on skin; 6 and 11 in anogenital sites) from the high-risk mucosal types (16 and 18 most importantly). In low-risk infection the genome stays episomal and the regulatory protein E2 restrains E6 and E7 expression, so the lesion is a benign, self-limited proliferation. In high-risk infection the genome may integrate into host DNA, and integration commonly disrupts the E2 open reading frame, releasing E6 and E7 from control. E6 recruits the cellular ubiquitin ligase E6AP to direct p53 for proteasomal degradation, removing the G1 damage checkpoint and p53-dependent apoptosis, and E7 binds Rb and displaces it from E2F, which frees E2F to drive S phase entry. The cell divides with unrepaired damage, mutations accumulate, and progression through dysplasia to squamous cell carcinoma becomes possible over years."},{"type":"flow","title":"High-risk HPV to squamous cell carcinoma","steps":["HPV 16 or 18 enters basal keratinocytes through a microabrasion","Genome integrates and the E2 reading frame is disrupted","E6 and E7 expression rises without restraint","E6 degrades p53; E7 releases E2F from Rb","Cells pass the G1 checkpoint with unrepaired DNA damage","Mutations accumulate: dysplasia, then invasive carcinoma"],"caption":"Loss of both the checkpoint and the brake is what makes integration, not infection alone, the oncogenic event."},{"type":"p","text":"Molluscum contagiosum virus takes the opposite approach on nearly every point. It is a poxvirus: large, brick-shaped, enveloped, with a linear double-stranded DNA genome, and it carries its own DNA-dependent RNA polymerase and transcription machinery, so unlike other DNA viruses of the skin it replicates entirely in the cytoplasm and never requires the nucleus. Infected keratinocytes enlarge and accumulate assembled virus as large eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions, the molluscum bodies (Henderson-Patterson bodies), and the lesion is a firm, dome-shaped, umbilicated papule whose central plug is composed of those infected cells. The virus encodes multiple immune evasion proteins, including inhibitors of chemokine and interleukin signaling and of apoptosis, which is why lesions are strikingly free of surrounding inflammation and may persist for months before cell-mediated immunity clears them. Spread is by direct skin contact, fomites, and autoinoculation, with sexual transmission in adults; extensive or facial lesions in an adult suggest impaired cellular immunity."},{"type":"table","title":"HPV compared with molluscum contagiosum virus","cols":["Feature","HPV","Molluscum contagiosum"],"rows":[["Family and shape","Papillomavirus, small icosahedral","Poxvirus, large brick-shaped"],["Genome and envelope","Circular dsDNA, nonenveloped","Linear dsDNA, enveloped"],["Site of replication","Nucleus of differentiating keratinocytes","Cytoplasm, using its own RNA polymerase"],["Target cell at entry","Basal keratinocyte","Keratinocyte of the epidermis"],["Histologic hallmark","Koilocyte with perinuclear halo","Molluscum body, eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion"],["Lesion","Verrucous papilloma, hyperkeratotic","Smooth umbilicated dome-shaped papule"],["Oncogenic potential","Yes in high-risk types through E6 and E7","None"],["Transmission","Contact, autoinoculation, fomites, sexual","Contact, autoinoculation, fomites, sexual in adults"]],"caption":"Nuclear replication tied to differentiation versus independent cytoplasmic replication accounts for every difference in appearance and behavior."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C06","obj":"IN-12.6","title":"Herpes simplex and varicella zoster: epithelial infection, ganglionic latency, and reactivation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2 and varicella zoster virus are alphaherpesviruses: enveloped, with linear double-stranded DNA in an icosahedral capsid surrounded by a protein tegument. Envelope glycoproteins attach to heparan sulfate and then to an entry receptor (nectin-1 for herpes simplex), the envelope fuses with the plasma membrane, and the genome enters the nucleus and circularizes, where lytic genes are expressed in an ordered immediate early, early, late cascade. Replication in epithelium causes ballooning degeneration of keratinocytes, fusion into multinucleated giant cells, and intranuclear Cowdry type A inclusions; the resulting intraepidermal edema and cell lysis produce a thin-walled vesicle on an erythematous base that ruptures easily into a shallow painful erosion. A Giemsa- or Wright-stained scraping from the base of a fresh vesicle, the Tzanck smear, shows those giant cells, but it cannot distinguish herpes simplex from varicella zoster, since both fuse cells by the same glycoprotein mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"During the primary infection the virus also enters the unmyelinated terminals of sensory neurons in the skin or mucosa and travels by retrograde axonal transport, using dynein along microtubules, to the neuronal cell body in a sensory ganglion. There the lytic program shuts down, the genome persists as a circular episome, and transcription is limited largely to latency-associated transcripts that suppress apoptosis and lytic gene expression. Because almost no viral protein is made, the neuron presents little antigen on MHC class I, so cytotoxic T cells cannot clear the reservoir and latency is lifelong. Reactivation follows stress, fever, ultraviolet light, local trauma, menstruation, immunosuppression, or waning cell-mediated immunity with age."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Latency and reactivation of an alphaherpesvirus","steps":["Lytic replication in skin or mucosal epithelium forms vesicles","Virus enters sensory nerve terminals in the lesion","Retrograde axonal transport to the sensory ganglion","Latency: episomal genome, minimal protein, no immune target","Trigger lowers local cell-mediated immunity","Anterograde transport back to the same nerve territory","Recurrent lesions, then return to latency"],"caption":"The anatomy of latency explains why recurrences always return to the same dermatome or perioral site."},{"type":"p","text":"The site of latency therefore predicts the clinical pattern. Herpes simplex type 1, acquired through oral contact, is latent in the trigeminal ganglion, so recurrences appear as herpes labialis at the vermilion border, and less often as herpetic gingivostomatitis, herpetic whitlow on a finger, keratitis with dendritic corneal ulcers, or temporal lobe encephalitis. Herpes simplex type 2, acquired sexually, is latent in the sacral dorsal root ganglia, so recurrences are genital, perianal, or buttock lesions, with neonatal infection acquired during passage through an infected birth canal. Either type can infect either site, so distribution suggests but does not prove the type. Varicella zoster differs in that primary infection is systemic: the virus enters by the respiratory route, replicates in lymphoid tissue, and produces a viremia that seeds skin in successive waves, which is why chickenpox lesions appear in crops at different stages at once. It becomes latent in dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia along the whole axis, so reactivation travels down one sensory nerve and gives the painful unilateral dermatomal eruption of zoster, often preceded by neuritic pain and sometimes followed by postherpetic neuralgia from damage to the sensory neuron."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with recurrent crops of vesicles on one buttock has a Tzanck smear with multinucleated giant cells. Why does this not establish whether the cause is herpes simplex or zoster, and what feature of the history would separate them?","explain":"Both viruses fuse keratinocytes through envelope glycoproteins, so both give multinucleated giant cells, and the smear sees the cytopathic effect rather than the virus. Recurrence is the discriminator: herpes simplex type 2 reactivates repeatedly from sacral ganglia, while zoster typically occurs once because reactivation restores immunity, so repeated identical attacks in one site favor herpes simplex.","check":"The latency paragraph and the sites of latency for each virus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C07","obj":"IN-12.7","title":"Cutibacterium acnes and Pseudomonas aeruginosa: follicular infections","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cutibacterium acnes, formerly Propionibacterium acnes, is a gram-positive, non-spore-forming, anaerobic to aerotolerant rod that is normal flora of the sebaceous follicle. It ferments glycerol and other substrates to propionic acid and is slow growing, which is why it is also a classic cause of indolent prosthetic device and shunt infection. It is a commensal rather than an invader, and acne begins with changes in the follicle rather than with the organism. Androgens, principally dihydrotestosterone formed locally by 5-alpha-reductase, enlarge the sebaceous gland and raise sebum output, while abnormal keratinization of the follicular infundibulum with retained corneocytes plugs the duct. The result is a closed, lipid-rich, anaerobic microcomedone, which is exactly the environment the organism needs, so it multiplies there to high density."},{"type":"p","text":"Two activities then convert a plugged follicle into an inflammatory lesion. Bacterial lipases hydrolyze sebum triglycerides into free fatty acids, which are comedogenic and directly irritating to the follicular epithelium and which also promote further abnormal keratinization. At the same time, cell wall peptidoglycan and lipoteichoic acid engage Toll-like receptor 2 on keratinocytes and infiltrating monocytes, with signaling through MyD88 and NF-kappaB and activation of the NLRP3 inflammasome, releasing interleukin-1 beta, interleukin-8, interleukin-12, and tumor necrosis factor. Interleukin-8 recruits neutrophils, whose enzymes weaken the follicular wall, and when the wall ruptures, keratin, sebum, and bacteria spill into the dermis and provoke an intense foreign body and suppurative response. That progression accounts for the clinical sequence: open and closed comedones, then inflammatory papules and pustules, then nodules and scarring where dermal rupture has occurred."},{"type":"flow","title":"From comedone to inflammatory acne lesion","steps":["Androgens enlarge sebaceous glands and raise sebum output","Abnormal follicular keratinization plugs the duct (microcomedone)","C acnes multiplies in the anaerobic lipid-rich plug","Lipases release irritant free fatty acids","TLR2 signaling releases IL-1 beta, IL-8, IL-12","Neutrophil influx weakens the follicular wall","Rupture into dermis: papule, pustule, nodule, scar"],"caption":"The organism is a normal resident, so the pathogenesis starts with the plug and ends with the host inflammatory response."},{"type":"p","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa illustrates follicular infection from the environment rather than from flora. It is a gram-negative, non-lactose-fermenting, strictly aerobic rod, oxidase positive (which distinguishes it from the Enterobacteriaceae), motile by a single polar flagellum, and recognizable by a grape-like odor and blue-green pyocyanin and pyoverdine pigment. It needs minimal nutrients, thrives in warm water, and forms biofilms on wet surfaces, so it persists in hot tubs and whirlpools when the water is warm enough to dissipate free chlorine and chlorination is not maintained. Prolonged immersion hydrates and softens the stratum corneum and the follicular opening, and water trapped against skin by a swimsuit holds a high inoculum against follicles. The result is hot tub folliculitis: pruritic follicular papules and pustules appearing about 8 hours to 2 days after exposure, concentrated where the swimsuit held water and sparing exposed skin, and usually self-limited because infection stays within the follicle. Pyocyanin generates reactive oxygen species and, with exotoxin A and elastase, explains why the same organism destroys deeper tissue in a burn, a puncture wound, or a neutropenic patient, including the black eschar of ecthyma gangrenosum."},{"type":"table","title":"Two follicular organisms compared","cols":["Feature","Cutibacterium acnes","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"rows":[["Gram stain and oxygen","Gram-positive rod, anaerobic","Gram-negative rod, strictly aerobic"],["Key laboratory clue","Slow growing, propionic acid producer","Oxidase positive, non-lactose fermenter, pyocyanin pigment"],["Source","Resident flora of sebaceous follicles","Warm water and biofilm in the environment"],["Trigger","Androgens, sebum, follicular plugging","Immersion, macerated corneum, occlusive swimsuit"],["Inflammatory mechanism","Free fatty acids plus TLR2 cytokine release","Direct follicular invasion with endotoxin and exotoxins"],["Distribution","Face, chest, upper back: sebaceous areas","Areas covered by the swimsuit, sparing exposed skin"]],"caption":"Both inflame the follicle, but one starts from inside with a plug and the other from outside with water."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-12-01-C08","obj":"IN-12.8","title":"Vector-borne and soil-acquired infections that present on the skin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"When an arthropod or a larva breaches the epidermis it bypasses every surface defense and delivers organisms directly into dermis, where blood vessels, lymphatics, and tissue macrophages are waiting. The skin lesion that follows is the first and sometimes only clue to the organism, and board questions usually give a vector, a season, a geographic region, and a rash pattern. The pattern itself is mechanistic: it reflects whether the organism spreads outward through dermal connective tissue, infects endothelium, lives inside macrophages, or migrates through the epidermis as a parasite that cannot complete its life cycle."},{"type":"p","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi, a microaerophilic spirochete, is transmitted by the nymphal Ixodes tick, usually after about 36 to 48 hours of attachment, during which the organism changes surface proteins and migrates from the tick midgut to the salivary glands. In skin it moves outward through dermal collagen by flagellar motility and binds host plasminogen and integrins, producing erythema migrans: a slowly expanding erythematous annular lesion, often with central clearing, that is warm but typically neither painful nor pruritic and that appears about 3 to 30 days after the bite."},{"type":"p","text":"Rickettsia rickettsii, an obligate intracellular gram-negative organism, is transmitted by Dermacentor ticks and invades and replicates within vascular endothelial cells, spreading cell to cell by actin-based motility. The resulting vasculitis with endothelial injury, platelet consumption, and leakage explains why the rash of Rocky Mountain spotted fever is macular and then petechial, begins on the wrists and ankles about 2 to 5 days after fever starts, spreads centripetally to the trunk, and characteristically involves palms and soles. The same vascular injury accounts for hyponatremia, thrombocytopenia, and the risk of shock."},{"type":"p","text":"Leishmania species are transmitted by the bite of a phlebotomine sandfly as promastigotes, which are phagocytosed by dermal macrophages and survive inside the phagolysosome as amastigotes; the granulomatous response to that intracellular infection produces a painless papule that enlarges into a chronic ulcer with a raised indurated border. Cutaneous larva migrans has no vector at all: larvae of the dog and cat hookworms Ancylostoma braziliense and Ancylostoma caninum, hatched in sand or soil contaminated with animal feces, penetrate bare skin directly but lack the collagenase needed to cross the basement membrane in a human host, so they wander within the epidermis and create an intensely itchy, raised, serpiginous track that advances a few millimeters to a few centimeters a day."},{"type":"table","title":"Vectors, organisms, and the mechanism behind each skin lesion","cols":["Organism","Vector or source","Skin lesion","Mechanism of the lesion"],"rows":[["Borrelia burgdorferi","Ixodes tick nymph","Erythema migrans, expanding annular patch","Spirochete migrates outward through dermal collagen"],["Rickettsia rickettsii","Dermacentor tick","Rash from wrists and ankles to trunk, includes palms and soles","Endothelial infection with vasculitis and leakage"],["Leishmania species","Phlebotomine sandfly","Painless papule becoming an ulcer with a raised border","Amastigotes in dermal macrophages and granulomatous response"],["Ancylostoma braziliense and caninum","Larvae in sand or soil with animal feces; no vector","Itchy serpiginous advancing track","Larva trapped in epidermis, cannot cross basement membrane"]],"caption":"Centrifugal spread, centripetal spread, ulceration, and a moving track each follow from where the organism lives in the skin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two febrile patients have expanding skin lesions after outdoor exposure. One has a single warm annular patch with central clearing and no palm involvement; the other has petechiae on the wrists, ankles, palms, and soles. Which organism infects endothelium, and how does the rash tell you?","explain":"The second. Rickettsia rickettsii replicates in endothelial cells, so injury to vessel walls produces petechiae and involves acral skin including palms and soles, spreading centripetally. Borrelia burgdorferi stays in dermal connective tissue and spreads outward from the bite, so it gives one expanding annular lesion without petechiae or palmar involvement.","check":"The vector table and the paragraph on each organism's tissue niche."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Skin structure selects its pathogens. The cornified, lipid-rich, shedding surface explains why dermatophytes needed keratinases and why Malassezia became lipid dependent, why the sebaceous follicle supports an anaerobe, and why papillomavirus had to tie its replication cycle to keratinocyte differentiation. The same structure explains how each one is defeated: an intact barrier, so that breaks from maceration, shaving, scratching, eczema, a tick mouthpart, or a hookworm larva are the usual entry events. One step deeper, immunology decides the outcome. Opsonization and neutrophil killing contain the pyogenic cocci, which is why their virulence factors target IgG, complement, and neutrophils themselves. Th17 and interleukin-17 maintain epithelial surfaces, and neutrophils guard tissue, which is why the level at which Candida causes disease reads out the specific defect. Cytotoxic T cells control the herpesviruses and clear molluscum, so latency and encoded immune evasion are the viral answers, and waning cellular immunity with age or immunosuppression is what permits zoster and extensive molluscum."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and the laboratory then connect the organism to the finding on the slide, and this is where most exam reasoning happens. Epidermal splitting with intact mucosa means a toxin cleaving desmoglein 1, not infection of the blister itself. Multinucleated giant cells mean glycoprotein-mediated cell fusion by a herpesvirus, with the smear unable to name which one. Koilocytes mean papillomavirus replicating in differentiating cells, while cytoplasmic inclusions mean a poxvirus that never needed the nucleus. Limits are worth stating: potassium hydroxide, Tzanck smear, and Gram stain are insensitive and nonspecific, culture takes time and a dermatophyte may take weeks, colonization is not infection for Candida, Malassezia, and Cutibacterium acnes, which are normal residents, and a negative test never excludes any of these organisms. Reasoning from mechanism to finding, and acknowledging where a test cannot decide, is what the biomedical portion of the exam is testing."}],"summary":["Staphylococcus aureus is a catalase and coagulase positive gram-positive cluster; coagulase walls off abscesses, protein A blocks opsonization by binding IgG Fc, and Panton-Valentine leukocidin lyses neutrophils.","Exfoliative toxin cleaves desmoglein 1 for a superficial, mucosa-sparing epidermal split, and TSST-1 is a superantigen; mecA-encoded PBP2a resists beta-lactams by having a low-affinity target, not by destroying the drug.","Streptococcus pyogenes is catalase negative, beta-hemolytic, bacitracin sensitive, and PYR positive; M protein binds factor H and streptokinase dissolves fibrin, so it spreads rather than walls off.","Skin strains of group A strep cause immune complex glomerulonephritis but not rheumatic fever, which requires molecular mimicry by rheumatogenic throat M types.","Dermatophytes use keratinases to live in dead keratin and show branching septate hyphae on potassium hydroxide; Trichophyton takes skin, hair, and nail, Microsporum spares nail, Epidermophyton spares hair.","Malassezia is a lipid-dependent yeast giving spaghetti and meatballs on potassium hydroxide, with hypopigmentation from azelaic acid inhibition of tyrosinase.","Candida albicans forms budding yeast with pseudohyphae and is germ tube positive at 37 degrees Celsius (98.6 degrees Fahrenheit); Th17 and IL-17 defects give mucocutaneous disease while neutropenia allows invasion.","High-risk HPV integrates, disrupts E2, and lets E6 degrade p53 and E7 release E2F from Rb; koilocytes mark nuclear replication, while molluscum poxvirus replicates in cytoplasm and leaves molluscum bodies.","HSV-1 is latent in the trigeminal ganglion, HSV-2 in sacral ganglia, and varicella zoster in dorsal root and cranial ganglia; Tzanck multinucleated giant cells cannot separate them.","Acne starts with androgen-driven sebum and a follicular plug that lets Cutibacterium acnes release free fatty acids and trigger TLR2 inflammation, while erythema migrans, an acral-to-central petechial rash with palms and soles, a chronic ulcer with a raised border, and a moving serpiginous track point to Borrelia, Rickettsia rickettsii, Leishmania, and hookworm larvae."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci; The Streptococci; Medical Mycology; Herpesviruses; Papillomaviruses; Poxviruses; Rickettsia and related genera; Spirochetes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, infectious disorders of the skin; Infectious Diseases"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to microbes; Congenital and acquired immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin: epidermis, hair follicles, and sebaceous glands"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases; Skin disease"}]},"IN-13-A":{"unit":"IN-13-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-01","title":"Pigmentation Changes of the Skin","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Every pigment disorder in this chapter comes from one of three changes: the number of melanocytes, the amount of melanin those melanocytes make, or the transfer of melanin into keratinocytes. This chapter works through the benign melanocytic tumor (nevocellular nevus) and the autoimmune loss of melanocytes (vitiligo), then uses them as anchors for the lesions boards use as distractors: dysplastic and congenital nevi, melanoma, albinism, tinea versicolor, postinflammatory hypopigmentation, ephelides, lentigines, melasma, and the diffuse darkening of primary adrenal insufficiency. Questions usually give a description, an age, a distribution, and one bedside or microscopic finding, and expect you to name the cellular mechanism behind it.","prerequisites":["Layers of the epidermis and the position of the basal layer on the basement membrane (IN-1)","Melanin synthesis from tyrosine by tyrosinase, and melanosome transfer to keratinocytes (IN-3)","Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling through RAS, RAF, MEK, and ERK (the MAPK pathway), and cell cycle control by p16 (CDKN2A), CDK4, and Rb, including cellular senescence","CD8 T cell cytotoxicity, IFN-gamma signaling through JAK-STAT, and chemokine recruitment","Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and negative feedback by cortisol (EN-6)"],"connections":[{"to":"Skin histology (IN-1, IN-3)","how":"Where a pigment cell sits (basal layer, dermal-epidermal junction, or dermis) is what defines each lesion, so the normal layered architecture is the map for all of them."},{"to":"Neoplasia, cell cycle control, and ultraviolet damage (pathology, IN-7)","how":"A nevus is a clonal proliferation that halts itself through oncogene-induced senescence, which shows why a driver mutation alone does not make a cancer, while accumulated ultraviolet mutational load is what converts a stable nevus into melanoma."},{"to":"Autoimmunity and tolerance (IM-9, IM-13)","how":"Vitiligo is organ-specific autoimmunity against a single cell type, and it clusters with autoimmune thyroid disease and type 1 diabetes through shared susceptibility loci."},{"to":"Adrenal physiology and pathology (EN-6, EN-12)","how":"Skin color is a readout of plasma ACTH, so hyperpigmentation separates primary from secondary adrenal insufficiency without any imaging."},{"to":"Medical mycology (IN-11, microbiology)","how":"Malassezia species alter pigment by inhibiting tyrosinase, which is why a fungal infection appears in a pigment differential at all."}],"terms":[{"term":"Melanocyte","def":"A neural crest derived, dendritic cell resident in the epidermal basal layer that synthesizes melanin inside melanosomes."},{"term":"Epidermal melanin unit","def":"One melanocyte together with the roughly 30 to 40 keratinocytes to which it transfers melanosomes."},{"term":"Nevus cell","def":"A melanocyte that has lost its dendrites and grows in rounded clusters called nests rather than singly along the basal layer."},{"term":"Oncogene-induced senescence","def":"Permanent cell cycle arrest triggered by strong oncogenic signaling, enforced in melanocytes largely by p16."},{"term":"Maturation with depth","def":"The normal finding in a benign nevus that cells become smaller, less pigmented, and more spindled the deeper they lie in the dermis."},{"term":"Macule","def":"A flat, circumscribed change in skin color less than about 1 cm across, with no elevation or scale."},{"term":"Depigmentation","def":"Complete absence of melanin in a lesion, giving a milk-white color, as distinct from hypopigmentation, which is partial loss."},{"term":"Koebner phenomenon","def":"The appearance of new lesions of an existing skin disease at sites of trauma to previously normal skin."},{"term":"Wood lamp","def":"A handheld source of long-wave ultraviolet light, about 365 nm, used to compare melanin content and to detect some fungal pigments."},{"term":"Lentiginous pattern","def":"An increase in single melanocytes spaced along the basal layer, without nest formation."},{"term":"POMC","def":"Proopiomelanocortin, the pituitary precursor protein cleaved into ACTH, beta-lipotropin, and melanocyte stimulating hormone peptides."},{"term":"Melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R)","def":"A Gs-coupled melanocyte receptor that raises cAMP when bound by alpha-MSH or ACTH and shifts synthesis toward brown-black eumelanin."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-01-C01","obj":"IN-13-A.1","title":"How a nevocellular nevus forms and how nest location classifies it","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Melanocytes are neural crest derived cells that settle in the epidermal basal layer during fetal life, where they sit singly, about one for every four to ten basal keratinocytes depending on body site, and extend dendrites to supply melanosomes to roughly 30 to 40 keratinocytes each. Darker and lighter skin contain about the same number of melanocytes; the difference lies in melanosome size, melanin content, and the eumelanin to pheomelanin ratio. Any lesion that adds melanocytes, removes them, or changes their output will therefore change color without changing the thickness of the epidermis."},{"type":"p","text":"A nevocellular nevus (a common mole) is a benign neoplasm of melanocytes. It begins when a single melanocyte acquires an activating mutation in BRAF, most often the V600E substitution, or in NRAS. Both are components of the MAPK cascade, so the mutation leaves RAF and then MEK and ERK signaling switched on without a growth factor, and the cell proliferates clonally. In the process the cells lose their dendrites and round up into discrete nests, which is the architectural signature of a nevus rather than scattered individual cells along the basal layer."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the growth stops"},{"type":"p","text":"Sustained, high-level oncogenic signaling does not only drive division; it also activates a protective arrest program called oncogene-induced senescence. In melanocytes the main effector is p16, the product of CDKN2A, which inhibits CDK4 and CDK6 and so keeps Rb hypophosphorylated and the cell blocked before S phase. Accumulation of p16 is why a mole reaches a few millimeters, stabilizes, and then stays the same for decades even though it carries a bona fide oncogenic mutation. Melanoma requires additional hits that dismantle this brake, characteristically loss of CDKN2A and activation of the TERT promoter to restore telomere maintenance."},{"type":"flow","title":"Nevogenesis and its self-limiting brake","steps":["Single basal melanocyte acquires activating BRAF V600E or NRAS mutation","MAPK signaling (RAF to MEK to ERK) runs without growth factor","Clonal proliferation; cells lose dendrites and form nests","Strong oncogenic signaling induces p16 (CDKN2A)","CDK4 and CDK6 inhibited, Rb stays active, cells arrest","Lesion stabilizes as a small benign nevus"],"caption":"The same mutation that starts a nevus also triggers the senescence program that stops it, which is why common nevi are stable."},{"type":"h","text":"Classification by where the nests sit"},{"type":"p","text":"Nevus cells begin at the dermal-epidermal junction and migrate into the dermis over years, so the three named types are stages of one process rather than separate diseases, which is why the type correlates with patient age."},{"type":"table","title":"Nevus types by nest location","cols":["Type","Nest location","Clinical appearance","Typical age"],"rows":[["Junctional","Dermal-epidermal junction only","Flat, uniformly dark brown macule","Children"],["Compound","Junction and dermis","Slightly raised brown papule, often with a flat rim","Adolescents and young adults"],["Intradermal","Dermis only, no junctional component","Dome-shaped or pedunculated, often tan or skin colored","Older adults"]],"caption":"As nests descend into the dermis the lesion becomes more raised and usually less pigmented."},{"type":"p","text":"Within a benign nevus, cells also change predictably with depth, a finding called maturation. Superficial nests are larger, rounder, clearly nested, and well pigmented; deeper cells become smaller, lose pigment, take on a spindled shape, and may resemble neural or fibrous tissue. Maturation reflects progressive exit from the cell cycle as the clone descends. Loss of maturation, meaning cells that remain large, pigmented, and mitotically active deep in the dermis, is a histologic warning sign for melanoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 5 mm lesion has nests at the junction and in the superficial dermis, and deep dermal cells that are just as large, pigmented, and mitotically active as the superficial ones. What is the concern, and which brake has failed?","explain":"Answer in terms of senescence, not appearance.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on oncogene-induced senescence and on maturation with depth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-01-C02","obj":"IN-13-A.2","title":"Separating a benign nevus from melanoma, and the risk carried by congenital nevi","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The clinical features that separate a mole from a melanoma are readouts of growth behavior. A benign nevus is one clone that expanded evenly and then arrested, so it is small (usually under 6 mm), symmetric, sharply bordered, a single uniform brown or tan, and unchanged over years. A melanoma contains subclones proliferating at different rates and directions, producing asymmetry, notched or indistinct borders, mixed colors including black, red, white, and blue-gray, a larger diameter, and visible change over weeks to months."},{"type":"table","title":"Benign nevus compared with melanoma","cols":["Feature","Benign nevus","Melanoma"],"rows":[["Symmetry","Symmetric","Asymmetric"],["Border","Sharp and regular","Notched or fading into normal skin"],["Color","One uniform brown or tan","Variegated; black, red, white, blue-gray"],["Diameter","Usually under 6 mm","Often over 6 mm; early lesions can be smaller"],["Course","Stable for years","Evolving in size, shape, color, or symptoms"],["Histology","Maturation with depth, few mitoses","No maturation, deep mitoses, upward single-cell spread"]],"caption":"The ABCDE criteria are the clinical column of this table."},{"type":"p","text":"Two refinements matter. The ugly duckling sign uses the patient as their own control: because one person's nevi usually share a growth pattern and a look, the lesion that does not resemble the others deserves attention even if it meets no single ABCDE criterion. A dysplastic nevus occupies the middle ground, with architectural disorder and cytologic atypia at the junction in a lentiginous, bridging pattern of nests; dysplastic nevi mark increased melanoma risk across the whole skin surface rather than being obligate precursors at that site, and in familial atypical mole and melanoma syndrome they accompany germline CDKN2A mutations."},{"type":"h","text":"Why depth decides prognosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Melanoma grows in two phases. In the radial growth phase cells spread laterally within the epidermis and papillary dermis and have not yet acquired metastatic capacity. In the vertical growth phase a subclone grows as an expansile nodule into the reticular dermis, where it meets lymphatics and blood vessels. Depth of invasion measured from the granular layer (Breslow thickness) is therefore the strongest prognostic variable in localized melanoma, with ulceration and mitotic rate adding to it. A broad, flat, long-standing lesion can therefore carry a better prognosis than a small nodule."},{"type":"flow","title":"Progression from nevus to metastatic melanoma","steps":["Activating BRAF or NRAS mutation plus cumulative ultraviolet damage","Loss of the p16 brake (CDKN2A) and TERT promoter activation","Radial growth phase: lateral epidermal spread, no metastatic capacity","Vertical growth phase: nodular invasion of reticular dermis","Access to dermal lymphatics and vessels","Regional nodal then distant metastasis"],"caption":"Metastatic potential appears with vertical growth, which Breslow thickness measures."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital melanocytic nevi are present at birth or appear within weeks, are usually larger than acquired nevi, and often bear terminal hairs with nests extending deep around adnexa and nerves. Risk tracks with size: small and medium lesions add little lifetime melanoma risk, whereas large or giant lesions, conventionally those projected to exceed about 20 cm in adult diameter, carry clearly increased risk, and melanoma in them may arise in the dermis rather than at the junction, so it is harder to detect early. Large posterior axial lesions with many satellites also raise the possibility of neurocutaneous melanocytosis, which presents neurologically."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two changing moles","text":"Patient A, age 8, has a 4 mm flat, evenly dark brown macule on the back, unchanged for two years. Patient B, age 46, has a 9 mm back lesion with one notched edge, a black zone, a gray-white zone, and four months of enlargement. Patient A's lesion is symmetric, uniform, and stable: a clone arrested by oncogene-induced senescence, and at her age nests sit at the junction, so a junctional nevus fits. Patient B is asymmetric in color and outline and meets the border, color, diameter, and evolution criteria; the gray-white zone suggests regression and the black zone a deeper pigmented subclone. The decision rests on the rate and unevenness of growth, not on darkness."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-01-C03","obj":"IN-13-A.3","title":"Vitiligo: cytotoxic destruction of melanocytes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitiligo affects roughly 0.5 to 1 percent of people worldwide and is an organ-specific autoimmune disease in which CD8 T cells recognize melanocyte differentiation antigens, including tyrosinase, gp100, MART-1, and TRP-1, and kill the cells that express them. Because melanocytes are eliminated rather than suppressed, the affected skin cannot synthesize any melanin, so the lesions are fully depigmented and milk-white instead of merely pale. Susceptibility is polygenic, with associations at HLA class II, PTPN22, and NLRP1 among others, and oxidative stress in melanocytes is thought to expose antigen and start the process in genetically predisposed people."},{"type":"p","text":"The effector loop is driven by interferon gamma. Melanocyte-specific CD8 T cells that reach the skin release IFN-gamma, which signals through JAK1, JAK2, and STAT1 in keratinocytes and induces the chemokines CXCL9 and CXCL10. These bind CXCR3 on circulating T cells and recruit more of them to the same site, so each wave of killing attracts the next. This self-reinforcing circuit explains the progressive, patch-by-patch spread of lesions and the persistence of resident memory T cells in clinically stable skin."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The interferon gamma amplification loop in vitiligo","steps":["Oxidative stress exposes melanocyte antigens","Melanocyte-specific CD8 T cells enter the skin","T cells release IFN-gamma","Keratinocytes induce CXCL9 and CXCL10 through JAK-STAT","CXCR3 positive T cells are recruited in greater numbers","More melanocytes are killed, exposing further antigen"],"caption":"A feed-forward loop: killing releases antigen, which recruits more cytotoxic cells to the same patch."},{"type":"h","text":"Findings explained by the mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Lesions are sharply demarcated macules and patches, chalk or milk-white, favoring periorificial skin around the mouth and eyes, the dorsal hands and fingers, the genitals, and skin over bony prominences such as elbows and knees, usually in a symmetric generalized pattern. A segmental variant, confined to one area and often stable after an early phase, is thought to reflect a localized or mosaic trigger. The Koebner phenomenon, new patches at sites of friction, cuts, or burns, reflects recruitment of circulating autoreactive T cells to injured skin, which is also why pressure-bearing sites are commonly involved."},{"type":"p","text":"Under a Wood lamp, melanin in normal skin absorbs the long-wave ultraviolet light, whereas in a depigmented patch the light passes through the epidermis and excites fluorophores in dermal collagen, so the lesion glows bright blue-white with sharply visible margins. This is how borders are defined in lightly pigmented skin, where the patches are easy to miss. A biopsy shows a normal epidermis with melanocytes absent from the basal layer, often with a sparse lymphocytic infiltrate at the advancing edge, and white hairs within a patch (leukotrichia) indicate destruction of follicular melanocytes as well."},{"type":"p","text":"Because susceptibility is shared across autoimmune diseases, vitiligo clusters with autoimmune thyroid disease, which is the most common association, as well as type 1 diabetes mellitus, pernicious anemia, alopecia areata, and autoimmune adrenal insufficiency; this cluster is the reason a board question about a depigmenting rash often supplies a thyroid-stimulating hormone value or a vitamin B12 level. A halo nevus, a nevus encircled by a depigmented ring, represents the same cytotoxic response directed at nevus cells and may appear in patients with or without generalized vitiligo."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-01-C04","obj":"IN-13-A.4","title":"The white patch differential: absent melanocytes versus quiet melanocytes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four conditions account for most white lesions on examinations, and one question separates them: are the melanocytes gone, or are they present but making or transferring less melanin? Only vitiligo removes them. That single distinction predicts the color (true milk-white depigmentation in vitiligo, off-white or tan hypopigmentation in the others), the sharpness of the border, and the degree of accentuation under a Wood lamp, because accentuation depends on how completely melanin is absent from the lesion compared with the surrounding skin."},{"type":"p","text":"In oculocutaneous albinism the melanocytes are present in normal numbers and contain melanosomes, but melanin synthesis is defective, most often because of mutations in TYR (tyrosinase, type 1) or in the OCA2 gene product that regulates melanosomal pH and tyrosinase function (type 2). The deficit is generalized and present from birth rather than patchy and acquired, and it involves the eye: foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus, iris transillumination, photophobia, reduced visual acuity, and misrouting of retinal fibers at the optic chiasm. Because melanin absorbs ultraviolet photons, these patients carry a heavy burden of actinic damage and keratinocyte-derived skin cancers. Albinism also appears as one feature of syndromes with defective lysosome-related organelle trafficking, Chediak-Higashi (LYST) and Hermansky-Pudlak, where the accompanying immunodeficiency or platelet storage pool defect gives the diagnosis away."},{"type":"p","text":"Tinea versicolor is pigment change caused by infection. The lipophilic yeast Malassezia, as a commensal that overgrows in sebaceous, warm, humid conditions, releases dicarboxylic acids, including azelaic acid, that inhibit tyrosinase in the melanocytes beneath it. The melanocytes remain in place but their output falls, so the macules are hypopigmented rather than depigmented; in some patients the lesions instead appear hyperpigmented, which is why the name says versicolor. Lesional skin cannot raise its melanin output while the surrounding skin tans normally, so the patches become far more obvious after sun exposure. The fine, dry, branny scale on the upper trunk, shoulders, and neck, and a potassium hydroxide preparation showing short, curved hyphae with clusters of round yeast cells, settle the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Postinflammatory hypopigmentation follows resolution of an inflammatory dermatosis such as atopic dermatitis, psoriasis, or lichen planus. Melanocytes survive but melanosome transfer to keratinocytes is impaired and some melanin was lost with the shed epidermis, so the patch is partially pale, poorly demarcated, located exactly where the earlier rash was, and repigments slowly over months. Pityriasis alba, the pale, finely scaling patches on the cheeks of atopic children, is the common pediatric version."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing hypopigmented and depigmented lesions","cols":["Condition","Melanocytes","Distribution","Wood lamp","KOH preparation"],"rows":[["Vitiligo","Absent (CD8 T cell destruction)","Acquired, patchy, sharply bordered, often symmetric and periorificial","Bright blue-white, margins sharp","Negative"],["Oculocutaneous albinism","Normal number; melanin greatly reduced","Generalized from birth, with ocular findings","Diffusely pale, no discrete patch","Negative"],["Tinea versicolor","Present; tyrosinase inhibited","Upper trunk and shoulders; fine branny scale; pale or dark","Variable yellow-green to copper-orange","Short curved hyphae with yeast clusters"],["Postinflammatory hypopigmentation","Present; melanin and transfer reduced","At the site of a prior rash; indistinct borders","Little accentuation","Negative"]],"caption":"Scale with a positive KOH identifies the fungal cause; sharp borders with bright accentuation identify the autoimmune one."},{"type":"p","text":"Two congenital look-alikes are easily mistaken for vitiligo. Nevus depigmentosus is a stable, hypopigmented patch present from early life with normal melanocyte numbers and fewer melanosomes transferred, and the hypomelanotic (ash-leaf) macules of tuberous sclerosis are similar lesions that appear with seizures, cardiac rhabdomyoma, or renal angiomyolipoma. Both are congenital and stable, whereas vitiligo is acquired and progressive."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 22 year old has pale, slightly scaling macules across the upper back that became obvious after a sunny summer, with faint copper-orange Wood lamp fluorescence and short hyphae with yeast on KOH. Why are the lesions pale rather than milk-white, and why did sun make them conspicuous?","explain":"Account for the enzyme involved and for the surrounding skin.","check":"Compare with the tinea versicolor paragraph and the opening paragraph of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-01-C05","obj":"IN-13-A.5","title":"Brown macules: melanocyte number versus melanocyte activity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Localized brown lesions divide on the same axis as the white ones. Either the number of melanocytes along the basal layer is increased, or a normal number of melanocytes is making and exporting more melanin. Lesions in the first group persist independently of season; lesions in the second group wax and wane with whatever is stimulating the melanocyte, usually ultraviolet light or sex steroids. None of the four lesions below contains nests, which is what separates them from a junctional nevus."},{"type":"p","text":"Ephelides, or freckles, are small tan to brown macules on sun-exposed skin, typically in fair-skinned, red or blonde haired people carrying loss-of-function MC1R variants. Melanocyte number is normal; ultraviolet light increases melanin synthesis and transfer into the overlying basal keratinocytes, so freckles darken within weeks of sun exposure and fade over winter. Lentigo simplex, by contrast, is a true increase in single melanocytes spaced along an elongated basal layer, the lentiginous pattern, with no nests. It can arise at any age on any site, including covered skin and mucosa, and it does not darken with sun because the lesion is a cellular increase rather than a response to light. Multiple lentigines in characteristic sites suggest a syndrome: perioral and buccal lentigines with intestinal hamartomatous polyps in Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (germline STK11), or widespread lentigines with cardiac and growth abnormalities in the multiple lentigines form of Noonan syndrome and in Carney complex."},{"type":"p","text":"Solar lentigines develop on chronically sun-damaged skin of middle-aged and older adults, classically the face, dorsal forearms, and backs of the hands. Here chronic ultraviolet exposure has produced both an increase in basal melanocytes and increased melanin along elongated rete ridges, so unlike freckles they do not fade in winter, and unlike lentigo simplex their distribution maps onto cumulative sun exposure. Melasma is hormonally driven: symmetric brown patches on the cheeks, forehead, upper lip, and chin appearing in pregnancy or with estrogen-containing contraceptives, with melanocyte number little changed but melanogenesis upregulated by estrogen and progesterone and strongly amplified by ultraviolet light. Pigment may sit in the epidermis, the dermis within melanophages, or both; epidermal melasma accentuates under a Wood lamp while dermal melasma does not."},{"type":"table","title":"Four brown macules compared","cols":["Lesion","Melanocyte number","Melanin","Effect of sun","Typical setting"],"rows":[["Ephelis (freckle)","Normal","Increased in basal keratinocytes","Darkens in summer, fades in winter","Fair skin, MC1R variants, childhood onset"],["Lentigo simplex","Increased, single cells in lentiginous pattern","Increased","No change","Any age, any site including mucosa; syndromic when multiple"],["Solar lentigo","Increased","Increased along elongated rete ridges","Caused by chronic sun; does not fade","Older adults, face and dorsal hands"],["Melasma","Little or no increase","Increased synthesis, epidermal or dermal or both","Worsens with sun","Pregnancy, estrogen-containing contraceptives"]],"caption":"Number versus activity, and whether the lesion fades without sun, separate all four."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-01-C06","obj":"IN-13-A.6","title":"Diffuse hyperpigmentation in primary adrenal insufficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Diffuse darkening of the skin in adrenal failure is a direct consequence of pituitary feedback. Corticotrophs synthesize proopiomelanocortin (POMC), a precursor cleaved by prohormone convertase 1 into ACTH, beta-lipotropin, and an N-terminal fragment; beta-lipotropin yields beta-endorphin, and in some tissues further cleavage yields alpha and beta melanocyte stimulating hormone (MSH). Critically, the first 13 amino acids of ACTH are the alpha-MSH sequence, so ACTH itself is a melanocortin agonist. When the adrenal cortex is destroyed, cortisol falls, negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary is removed, CRH and POMC transcription rise, and ACTH concentrations climb severalfold."},{"type":"p","text":"ACTH and the MSH peptides bind melanocortin 1 receptor on melanocytes. MC1R is Gs-coupled, so occupancy raises adenylyl cyclase activity and cAMP, activates protein kinase A, phosphorylates CREB, and increases transcription of MITF, the master melanocyte transcription factor that drives tyrosinase and other melanogenic enzymes. The result is a shift toward brown-black eumelanin and more melanosome transfer to keratinocytes across the entire skin surface, which is why the pigmentation is generalized rather than patchy."},{"type":"flow","title":"From adrenal destruction to hyperpigmentation","steps":["Adrenal cortex destroyed, most often by autoimmune adrenalitis; cortisol falls","Loss of negative feedback raises CRH and pituitary POMC transcription","POMC cleaved to ACTH, whose first 13 residues are the alpha-MSH sequence","ACTH and MSH peptides occupy MC1R on melanocytes","Gs raises cAMP, PKA phosphorylates CREB, MITF and tyrosinase rise","Eumelanin synthesis and transfer increase across all skin","Generalized darkening, accentuated at creases, mucosa, scars, and sun-exposed skin"],"caption":"Skin color here is a bioassay for plasma ACTH, which is why the distribution of darkening is diagnostic."},{"type":"p","text":"The accentuated sites follow regions where melanocytes are already most responsive or where friction and light add stimulus: palmar and other flexural creases, the gingival and buccal mucosa, the areolae, pressure points such as knuckles, elbows, and knees, scars that formed after the onset of disease, and sun-exposed areas. Pigmentation of a scar is useful because it dates the process. In secondary adrenal insufficiency the lesion is pituitary or hypothalamic, so ACTH is low rather than high, no melanocortin drive reaches the skin, and these patients are pale rather than dark; they also retain mineralocorticoid production because aldosterone is regulated chiefly by the renin-angiotensin system and potassium, which is why hyperkalemia and salt craving belong to the primary form."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of diffuse darkening and how to tell them apart","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Primary adrenal insufficiency","High ACTH and POMC peptides stimulate MC1R","Creases, buccal mucosa, scars; hyperkalemia, hyponatremia, low cortisol with high ACTH"],["Secondary adrenal insufficiency","ACTH low, no melanocortin drive","Pale skin; normal potassium; low cortisol with low ACTH"],["Ectopic ACTH syndrome (for example small cell lung carcinoma)","Tumor-derived ACTH and POMC peptides","Hyperpigmentation with cortisol excess, hypokalemic alkalosis, rapid onset"],["Hereditary hemochromatosis","Dermal iron deposition plus increased melanin","Bronze or slate-gray color with diabetes, arthropathy, cirrhosis, high ferritin and transferrin saturation"],["Acanthosis nigricans","Insulin and IGF-1 drive keratinocyte and fibroblast growth","Velvety thickened plaques in axillae and neck, not simple melanin excess; insulin resistance or visceral malignancy"]],"caption":"Only the first and third raise melanin through melanocortin signaling; the others darken skin by different mechanisms."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has low morning cortisol, generalized darkening with pigmented gums, and sodium 128 mmol/L with potassium 5.8 mmol/L. A second patient has low morning cortisol, pale skin, and normal electrolytes. Where is the lesion in each, and what is the expected plasma ACTH in each?","explain":"Justify the electrolyte difference from the way aldosterone is regulated.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on accentuated sites and secondary adrenal insufficiency."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"This chapter is a single mechanism applied repeatedly: pigment reflects melanocyte number, melanocyte output, and melanosome transfer, and each disease changes one of the three. Pathology supplies the clonal logic, since a nevus demonstrates that an activating BRAF or NRAS mutation plus an intact senescence program yields a stable benign tumor, while loss of CDKN2A and TERT activation yield melanoma; the same p16 and Rb circuit taught in general neoplasia appears here at a size a patient can see. Immunology supplies vitiligo, where antigen-specific CD8 killing plus an IFN-gamma, JAK-STAT, CXCL10 recruitment loop explains both progression and the clustering with thyroid autoimmunity and type 1 diabetes. Endocrinology supplies the melanocortin axis, where skin color becomes a readout of plasma ACTH and therefore separates primary from secondary adrenal insufficiency. Microbiology supplies Malassezia, which changes pigment not by killing cells but by inhibiting their key enzyme."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits are worth stating. Clinical criteria are probabilistic: ABCDE criteria are sensitive for larger melanomas but miss small nodular and amelanotic lesions, so histology with Breslow thickness remains the reference standard. Wood lamp examination compares melanin content and does not identify a cause; it cannot distinguish vitiligo from a congenital hypomelanotic macule, and dermal pigment such as dermal melasma does not accentuate at all. Two pigment disorders frequently coexist, since autoimmune adrenal insufficiency and vitiligo belong to the same polyglandular cluster, which is why a patient can present with depigmented patches on diffusely darkened skin, a combination that makes sense only when the mechanisms are kept separate."}],"summary":["Melanocyte number is roughly equal across skin tones; color differences come from melanosome size, melanin content, and the eumelanin to pheomelanin ratio.","A nevocellular nevus starts with activating BRAF V600E or NRAS and stops through oncogene-induced senescence enforced by p16, which is why common nevi are small and stable.","Nevi are classified by nest location and move with age: junctional in children, compound in young adults, intradermal in older adults, with cells maturing (smaller, less pigmented, spindled) as they go deeper.","Benign nevi are symmetric, uniform, sharply bordered, and stable; melanoma shows asymmetry, border irregularity, color variation, diameter over 6 mm, and evolution, and loses maturation with depth.","Metastatic potential appears in the vertical growth phase, so Breslow thickness is the strongest prognostic measure in localized melanoma; large congenital nevi carry increased risk, small ones little.","Vitiligo is CD8 T cell destruction of melanocytes amplified by IFN-gamma, JAK-STAT, and CXCL10: milk-white sharply bordered macules, bright Wood lamp accentuation, Koebner phenomenon, and melanocytes absent on biopsy.","In vitiligo melanocytes are gone; in albinism, tinea versicolor, and postinflammatory hypopigmentation they are present but make or transfer less melanin, and scale with a positive KOH identifies the fungal cause.","Ephelides have normal melanocyte numbers and darken with sun; lentigines have increased basal melanocytes and persist; melasma reflects estrogen-driven melanogenesis amplified by ultraviolet light.","High ACTH in primary adrenal insufficiency activates MC1R through its alpha-MSH sequence, raising cAMP, MITF, and tyrosinase, which darkens creases, mucosa, and scars; secondary insufficiency has low ACTH and pale skin.","Vitiligo and autoimmune adrenal insufficiency belong to the same polyglandular cluster, so depigmented patches on diffusely darkened skin is a coherent presentation."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin: disorders of pigmentation and melanocytes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia: oncogenes, cell cycle control, and cellular senescence"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin: epidermis and melanocytes"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Basic Immunology (Abbas)","where":"Immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Pathophysiology of Disease: An Introduction to Clinical Medicine (Lange)","where":"Disorders of the adrenal cortex; Diseases of the skin"}]},"IN-13-B":{"unit":"IN-13-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02","title":"Acute Inflammatory Skin Conditions","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the acute inflammatory rashes: atopic dermatitis, allergic and irritant contact dermatitis, erythema multiforme, urticaria and angioedema, Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, pityriasis rosea, seborrheic dermatitis, and aphthous ulcers. Each one is an immune or barrier problem in a specific layer of the skin, and the layer involved predicts what the lesion looks like, how long it lasts, and what the biopsy shows. Board questions give a distribution, a time course, or a histologic description and expect you to name the mechanism. The most useful habit is to ask which cell is doing the damage and at what depth.","prerequisites":["Epidermal layers, keratinocyte differentiation, and the cornified envelope (IN-1, IN-5)","Dermal vasculature, postcapillary venules, and the mechanism of edema formation","Hypersensitivity types I through IV, including mast cell and cytotoxic T cell effector pathways (IM-15-C, IM-5)","Antigen presentation by dendritic cells, MHC class I and II, and T cell priming (IM-5, IM-3)","Complement activation and its regulators, including C1 inhibitor (IM-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Diseases of hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","how":"These rashes are the visible form of type I (urticaria), type IV (contact dermatitis), and cytotoxic T cell reactions (erythema multiforme, toxic epidermal necrolysis), so the skin becomes a readable model of each mechanism."},{"to":"Allergic airway disease (PU-14-B, PU-14-C)","how":"Atopic dermatitis shares its Th2 bias with allergic rhinitis and asthma, which is why sensitization through damaged skin can be followed by airway disease."},{"to":"Skin pathogens and infectious skin disease (IN-12, IN-13-H)","how":"Barrier failure invites Staphylococcus aureus and herpes simplex virus, and reactivation of HSV, HHV-6, HHV-7, and Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection triggers several of these eruptions."},{"to":"Inflammatory and malabsorptive bowel disease (GI-13-G, GI-13-E)","how":"Recurrent aphthous ulcers can be the first visible sign of Behcet disease, inflammatory bowel disease, or celiac disease."},{"to":"Fluid, electrolyte, and thermoregulatory physiology (IN-4)","how":"Widespread epidermal loss in toxic epidermal necrolysis removes the water barrier, so the physiology of the burn patient explains the hypovolemia, heat loss, and sepsis risk."}],"terms":[{"term":"Spongiosis","def":"Accumulation of fluid between epidermal keratinocytes that widens intercellular spaces and stretches desmosomal attachments."},{"term":"Interface dermatitis","def":"An inflammatory pattern in which lymphocytes gather at the dermoepidermal junction and damage the basal keratinocytes there."},{"term":"Lichenification","def":"Thickening of the skin with exaggerated surface markings caused by repeated rubbing and scratching, with epidermal acanthosis and hyperkeratosis on biopsy."},{"term":"Wheal","def":"A transient, raised, edematous plaque produced by plasma leaking into the superficial dermis."},{"term":"Angioedema","def":"Nonpitting swelling from plasma leakage into the deep dermis and subcutaneous or submucosal tissue."},{"term":"Hapten","def":"A molecule too small to be immunogenic on its own that becomes immunogenic after binding a carrier protein."},{"term":"Nikolsky sign","def":"Separation of the epidermis from the dermis when lateral pressure is applied to apparently normal skin, indicating loss of epidermal cohesion or basal attachment."},{"term":"Filaggrin","def":"A protein that aggregates keratin filaments during cornification and is broken down into the natural moisturizing factor that holds water in the stratum corneum."},{"term":"Atopy","def":"A genetic tendency to mount IgE responses to common environmental antigens, expressed as eczema, allergic rhinitis, and asthma."},{"term":"Herald patch","def":"The single larger scaly plaque that precedes the generalized eruption of pityriasis rosea by one to two weeks."},{"term":"Collarette scale","def":"A fine rim of scale attached at the periphery of a plaque and free at its inner edge, characteristic of pityriasis rosea."},{"term":"Nonkeratinized oral mucosa","def":"The movable lining of the buccal and labial surfaces, floor of mouth, and ventral tongue, which lacks a cornified layer and is the site of aphthous ulceration."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C01","obj":"IN-13-B.1","title":"Atopic dermatitis: barrier failure and Th2 inflammation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Atopic dermatitis is a chronic, relapsing, intensely itchy dermatitis that begins in most patients before the age of five. Two defects drive it and each worsens the other: a leaky epidermal barrier and an immune system biased toward type 2 responses."},{"type":"p","text":"The barrier side begins in the stratum corneum. Filaggrin, encoded by FLG, aggregates keratin filaments into the dense bundles of the cornified cell and is then degraded into the small hydrophilic molecules known as natural moisturizing factor. Loss of function mutations in FLG, which are common in populations of European ancestry, therefore produce a cornified layer with disorganized keratin bundles and little natural moisturizing factor, so it holds water poorly. Transepidermal water loss rises, surface pH rises, and allergens, haptens, and microbes penetrate to reach the Langerhans cells of the epidermis."},{"type":"p","text":"The immune side amplifies the defect. Injured keratinocytes release thymic stromal lymphopoietin, IL-25, and IL-33, which push dendritic cells to prime Th2 cells. IL-4 and IL-13 drive B cell class switching to IgE, which explains the raised serum IgE and eosinophilia, and they also suppress transcription of filaggrin, loricrin, and the antimicrobial peptides, so the barrier worsens because of the inflammation it caused. IL-31, also a Th2 product, acts on receptors on sensory neurons and is a principal pruritogen, and IL-4 and IL-13 sensitize those neurons as well. This matters because the itch responds poorly to antihistamines: histamine is not the main mediator here."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The itch and barrier cycle in atopic dermatitis","steps":["Filaggrin loss raises water loss and lets allergens enter","Keratinocytes release TSLP, IL-25, and IL-33","Th2 cells make IL-4, IL-13, and IL-31","IL-4 and IL-13 lower filaggrin and antimicrobial peptides","IL-31 drives itch; scratching breaches the barrier further"],"caption":"Barrier failure and Th2 inflammation reinforce each other, so the disease is self sustaining until the loop is interrupted."},{"type":"p","text":"Distribution changes with age, and two points explain the pattern. The infant diaper area is characteristically spared because it stays moist and is not scratched, and the move from face and extensors into the flexures occurs from about age two. Adult lesions are often thick plaques with exaggerated skin lines, the lichenification that follows years of rubbing."},{"type":"table","title":"Age-related features of atopic dermatitis","cols":["Stage","Distribution","Lesion character","Histology"],"rows":[["Infantile (2 months to 2 years)","Cheeks, scalp, extensor limbs; diaper area spared","Erythematous, weeping, crusted patches","Marked spongiosis with vesicles"],["Childhood (2 years to puberty)","Antecubital and popliteal fossae, neck, wrists, ankles","Drier papules and plaques, excoriations","Spongiosis with early acanthosis"],["Adult","Flexures, hands, eyelids, periorbital skin","Lichenified plaques, dyspigmentation","Acanthosis and hyperkeratosis, less spongiosis"]],"caption":"The same disease looks different by age because the lesion reflects how long the skin has been scratched."},{"type":"p","text":"Atopic dermatitis is one third of the atopic triad with allergic rhinitis and asthma, and in many children it comes first, so sensitization through inflamed skin precedes airway disease. Two complications follow directly from the loss of antimicrobial peptides. Staphylococcus aureus colonizes lesional skin in most patients and its superantigens worsen inflammation, producing weeping, honey colored crusting, and flares. Eczema herpeticum is widespread herpes simplex infection of eczematous skin, recognized by monomorphic punched out erosions with scalloped borders, fever, and malaise, and it can disseminate."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with severe eczema is exposed to a parent with a cold sore and develops hundreds of uniform, punched out erosions with fever. Why does atopic skin permit this when normal skin does not?","explain":"Name the two defects that allow viral spread and say which cytokines created the second one.","check":"Compare your answer with the paragraph on IL-4 and IL-13 suppression of antimicrobial peptides and the complications paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C02","obj":"IN-13-B.2","title":"Allergic versus irritant contact dermatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Contact dermatitis is inflammation caused by something that touched the skin. It comes in two forms that look similar at the bedside and are separated by whether the immune system learned the antigen first."},{"type":"p","text":"Allergic contact dermatitis is a delayed type (type IV) hypersensitivity reaction and requires two phases. In the sensitization phase, a small lipophilic chemical crosses the stratum corneum and acts as a hapten: it is too small to be recognized alone, so it binds covalently to self proteins. Langerhans cells, the dendritic cells of the epidermis, carry the modified protein to the regional lymph node and present hapten-modified peptides to naive T cells, generating memory T cells over about one to two weeks. Nothing is visible during this phase. On re-exposure, skin homing memory CD4 and CD8 effectors return to the site and release interferon gamma and other cytokines that recruit mononuclear cells. Because this depends on cell recruitment rather than preformed mediators, the eruption appears about 48 to 72 hours after contact rather than within minutes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Allergic contact dermatitis: sensitization then elicitation","steps":["Hapten such as nickel or urushiol penetrates the stratum corneum","Hapten binds self proteins, creating a new antigen","Langerhans cells carry it to the regional lymph node","Naive T cells are primed; memory T cells form over 1 to 2 weeks","Re-exposure recruits skin homing memory T cells","Cytokines recruit inflammatory cells; spongiosis and vesicles appear","Itchy vesicular plaques 48 to 72 hours after contact"],"caption":"The first exposure teaches and the later exposure inflames, which is why a patient can wear an earring for years and then react to it."},{"type":"p","text":"The common allergens are worth knowing by name. Nickel is the most frequent, released from jewelry, buckles, and snaps when sweat corrodes the metal. Urushiol is the catechol oil of poison ivy, poison oak, and poison sumac, and it cross reacts among these plants and with mango skin and cashew shell. Other frequent sensitizers are fragrance mixes, preservatives, rubber accelerators, and topical neomycin. The patch test confirms the diagnosis: allergens are applied under occlusion and read at 48 hours and again at 72 to 96 hours, and a positive result is an itchy, indurated reaction that persists or increases at the later reading."},{"type":"p","text":"Irritant contact dermatitis is more common overall and involves no immune memory. The agent injures keratinocytes directly, releasing IL-1 and other preformed cytokines, so it can occur on first exposure, it is dose and duration dependent, and anyone exposed to enough of the agent will react. Patients report burning and stinging more than itch, the skin looks dry, glazed, and fissured rather than vesicular, the margins are sharp at the limits of exposure, and the patch test is negative. Soaps, detergents, solvents, acids and alkalis, and repeated wetting are the usual causes."},{"type":"table","title":"Allergic versus irritant contact dermatitis","cols":["Feature","Allergic","Irritant"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Type IV hypersensitivity, hapten-specific T cells","Direct keratinocyte injury, innate cytokine release"],["Prior exposure needed","Yes, sensitization phase of 1 to 2 weeks","No"],["Timing after exposure","48 to 72 hours","Minutes to hours"],["Who reacts","Only sensitized individuals, any dose","Anyone, dose and duration dependent"],["Symptom","Itch predominates","Burning and stinging predominate"],["Appearance","Vesicles, edema, may spread beyond contact site","Dry, glazed, fissured, sharply limited to contact site"],["Patch test","Positive","Negative"]],"caption":"Timing, itch versus burn, and the patch test separate the two forms; the lesion itself can be identical."},{"type":"p","text":"Two points prevent common errors. The eruption of allergic contact dermatitis can extend slightly beyond the contact site and occasionally becomes generalized because sensitized T cells circulate, while irritant dermatitis stays where the chemical was. Blister fluid in poison ivy dermatitis contains no urushiol, so the rash does not spread from scratching open vesicles; what spreads it is unwashed oil on hands, clothing, pets, or tools."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C03","obj":"IN-13-B.3","title":"Reading the exposure from the distribution, and the histology of eczema","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Contact dermatitis is the one rash whose shape records what touched the skin. The reaction occurs where antigen or irritant penetrated, so the eruption reproduces the geometry of the exposure, and the relationship works in both directions: a described pattern suggests an agent, and a named exposure predicts a pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting distribution from exposure","cols":["Exposure","Expected distribution","Reasoning"],"rows":[["Nickel in earrings, buckles, snaps, watch backs","Earlobes, periumbilical skin, wrist, waistline","Sweat corrodes the metal and releases nickel ions only where it rests on skin"],["Urushiol from poison ivy or oak","Linear and streaked vesicles on exposed forearms and legs","The leaf or stem drags across skin, depositing oil in a line"],["Wet work with soaps and detergents","Dorsal hands and finger webs, sparing palms","Thin dorsal skin is injured first; thick palmar stratum corneum resists"],["Rubber accelerators in gloves or elastic","Dorsal hands and wrists, or waistband and sock lines","The reaction follows the garment margin where contact is firm and occlusive"],["Topical neomycin or a preservative in a cream","Exactly the treated area, often an eczematous or ulcerated site","Barrier loss at the treated site increases hapten penetration"],["Airborne plant or fragrance particles","Eyelids, face, and neck, including under the chin","Particles settle on exposed skin, so shadowed areas are involved unlike photodermatitis"]],"caption":"A contact eruption maps the exposure, which is why distribution carries more diagnostic weight here than lesion morphology."},{"type":"p","text":"Two patterns are easy to confuse with contact dermatitis. A photoallergic or phototoxic reaction also involves exposed skin but spares shadowed sites, so the upper eyelids, the submental area, and the skin behind the ears are clear, and the eruption stops at the collar and sleeve lines. Airborne contact dermatitis involves those same shadowed areas. Dermatitis limited to the dorsa of the hands with sparing of the palms suggests an external agent, while symmetric involvement of palms and soles with deep itchy vesicles suggests dyshidrotic eczema instead."},{"type":"p","text":"The histologic hallmark of all eczematous dermatitis, allergic and irritant contact dermatitis included, is spongiosis. Inflammatory edema moves from the dermis into the epidermis and collects between keratinocytes, so the cells appear separated by clear fluid and connected only by stretched desmosomes. When enough fluid accumulates the desmosomes give way and the spaces coalesce into intraepidermal vesicles, which is why acute lesions weep. Lymphocytes migrate in with the fluid, and a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate with papillary dermal edema sits beneath."},{"type":"p","text":"The picture changes with duration, and the sequence explains the clinical appearance at each stage. Acute lesions are dominated by spongiosis and vesiculation and look red and weeping. Subacute lesions add parakeratosis, the retention of nuclei in the cornified layer that corresponds to visible scale and crust. Chronic lesions show acanthosis, hyperkeratosis, and hypergranulosis with little remaining spongiosis, the microscopic basis of lichenification. Spongiosis is not specific to contact dermatitis; it appears in atopic dermatitis, dyshidrotic eczema, and stasis dermatitis, so the diagnosis still depends on distribution and history."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C04","obj":"IN-13-B.4","title":"Erythema multiforme as a cytotoxic T cell reaction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Erythema multiforme is an acute, self limited, often recurrent eruption in which cytotoxic T cells kill keratinocytes at the dermoepidermal junction. In the great majority of cases it follows an infection rather than a drug, which is the most useful fact separating it from Stevens-Johnson syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Herpes simplex virus, usually HSV-1, causes most cases. Circulating mononuclear cells carry viral DNA fragments to the skin, keratinocytes there express viral antigen, and HSV-specific CD4 cells release interferon gamma that recruits CD8 cells to kill those keratinocytes. The lesions therefore appear about one to two weeks after the cold sore, at sites distant from it, and recur with each reactivation; the virus is not replicating in the target lesions. Mycoplasma pneumoniae is the second major trigger, mainly in children, in whom it produces more mucosal disease and follows a cough and fever."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why target lesions follow a cold sore","steps":["HSV reactivates at the lip and replicates","Mononuclear cells carry HSV DNA fragments to distant skin","Keratinocytes express viral antigen","HSV-specific CD4 cells release interferon gamma","CD8 cells kill antigen-bearing basal keratinocytes","Target lesions appear 1 to 2 weeks later on hands and forearms"],"caption":"The rash is an immune response to viral antigen carried into the skin, so it is remote from the cold sore and recurs with it."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion is the diagnosis. A typical target lesion is a round papule or plaque under about 3 cm across with three concentric zones: a dusky or blistered center where keratinocytes have died, a pale ring of edema, and an outer ring of erythema. Lesions appear abruptly over a few days, stay fixed for at least seven days, and favor acral and extensor skin: the dorsal hands, palms, forearms, elbows, and knees. They are symmetric and burn or itch mildly rather than being painful. Mucosal disease is limited: erythema multiforme minor has none, and erythema multiforme major has erosions usually confined to the oral mucosa and lips with the patient otherwise well."},{"type":"p","text":"Histology shows interface dermatitis: lymphocytes line up along the dermoepidermal junction, the basal layer vacuolizes, and scattered necrotic keratinocytes appear, each sometimes ringed by lymphocytes, which is satellite cell necrosis. Papillary dermal edema beneath the dying basal cells lifts the epidermis and creates the pale middle ring of the target, and when the whole junction fails a subepidermal blister forms at the center. Because the necrosis is scattered and partial, the epidermis stays attached, the Nikolsky sign is negative, and lesions heal without scarring over two to four weeks."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from a target lesion","text":"A healthy 24 year old reports a cold sore that healed ten days ago. He now has about forty symmetric, 1 to 2 cm round plaques on the dorsal hands, forearms, and knees, each with a dusky center, a pale ring, and a red outer ring, plus two small oral erosions. He feels well and takes no medication. Work backward: three concentric zones and acral distribution identify a target lesion; the dusky center means keratinocytes killed by cytotoxic T cells; the interval after HSV identifies the trigger; limited mucosal disease with no drug and no systemic illness argues against Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Expect resolution in weeks and recurrence at the next reactivation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C05","obj":"IN-13-B.5","title":"Urticaria, angioedema, and the two mediators of swelling","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Urticaria is transient edema of the superficial dermis caused by mast cell degranulation. Histamine binds H1 receptors on the endothelium of postcapillary venules, which contract their endothelial cells and open interendothelial gaps, so plasma leaks into the dermis. The result is a wheal: a raised, pink, intensely itchy plaque with a pale center and a surrounding flare from axon reflex vasodilation. Because histamine is cleared within minutes and the fluid is reabsorbed, any individual wheal lasts less than 24 hours and leaves no mark, while new wheals appear elsewhere. A lesion that persists for days in one place, or that leaves bruising or scale, is not urticaria and suggests urticarial vasculitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Mast cells can be triggered three ways, and only the first is an allergy. IgE-mediated degranulation is type I hypersensitivity: antigen cross links IgE bound to FcepsilonRI, as with foods, beta lactams, and insect stings. Direct, non-IgE degranulation occurs when a substance activates the mast cell without antibody, as with opioids, vancomycin, and radiocontrast, so a reaction can occur on first exposure and skin testing is uninformative. Physical urticarias are reproducible responses to a stimulus: dermographism, cold, delayed pressure, solar, and cholinergic urticaria, in which a rise in core temperature produces small wheals with large flares. Aspirin and other nonselective cyclooxygenase inhibitors worsen urticaria by shifting arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes, not by an immune mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Duration divides urticaria into acute, lasting under six weeks and usually following an infection, drug, or food, and chronic, lasting over six weeks. Chronic spontaneous urticaria has no external trigger in most patients, and many have IgG autoantibodies against IgE or FcepsilonRI itself that crosslink the receptor and keep mast cells releasing mediators; it is associated with autoimmune thyroid disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Angioedema is the same leak occurring deeper, in the lower dermis, subcutis, or submucosa, so the swelling is nonpitting, poorly demarcated, and tense rather than itchy, and it favors the eyelids, lips, tongue, genitals, hands, and feet. When it accompanies hives it is histamine driven, up to and including anaphylaxis. When it occurs without hives or itch it is usually bradykinin driven. C1 inhibitor restrains plasma kallikrein and factor XIIa as well as C1, so hereditary deficiency (low level in type 1, dysfunctional protein in type 2) lets kallikrein cleave high molecular weight kininogen into bradykinin, which raises permeability through endothelial B2 receptors. Attacks involve the face, larynx, and bowel wall, causing colic and vomiting, and antihistamines do not help. Unopposed C1 activity consumes C4, so a low C4 between attacks is the screening clue. Angiotensin converting enzyme is also kininase II, the main enzyme degrading bradykinin, so ACE inhibitors cause the same swelling by blocking its breakdown."},{"type":"table","title":"Histamine versus bradykinin swelling","cols":["Feature","Histamine (mast cell)","Bradykinin"],"rows":[["Hives present","Yes","No"],["Itch","Prominent","Little or none"],["Onset and course","Minutes, resolves in hours","Hours to a peak, resolves over 2 to 5 days"],["Typical causes","IgE allergy, direct degranulators, physical stimuli, autoantibodies","C1 inhibitor deficiency, ACE inhibitors"],["Laboratory clue","None specific; tryptase rises in anaphylaxis","Low C4; low C1 inhibitor level or function"],["Response to antihistamine","Good","None"]],"caption":"Whether hives accompany the swelling is the fastest way to decide which mediator is responsible."},{"type":"flow","title":"Bradykinin angioedema","steps":["C1 inhibitor is deficient, dysfunctional, or bradykinin breakdown is blocked","Kallikrein cleaves high molecular weight kininogen","Bradykinin accumulates and binds endothelial B2 receptors","Permeability rises in deep dermis, subcutis, and submucosa","Nonpitting swelling of face, larynx, or bowel wall without hives"],"caption":"The pathway explains the absence of itch, the low C4, and the failure of antihistamines."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has recurrent lip and tongue swelling without hives or itch, with normal C4 and normal C1 inhibitor level and function. Which medication would you ask about, and why does that fit the mechanism?","explain":"Name the enzyme the drug inhibits and what it normally does to bradykinin.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on kininase II."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C06","obj":"IN-13-B.6","title":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis are two ends of one disease: widespread, full thickness necrosis of the epidermis caused by a drug-specific cytotoxic T cell response. They are separated only by how much skin detaches."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism is cytotoxic rather than inflammatory in the ordinary sense. A drug or its reactive metabolite is presented on MHC class I, so drug-specific CD8 T cells and natural killer cells accumulate in the epidermis. They kill keratinocytes by releasing granulysin, which is the most abundant cytotoxic protein in blister fluid, along with perforin and granzyme B, and by engaging Fas on keratinocytes with Fas ligand. Killing is diffuse and complete rather than scattered, so the entire epidermis dies and separates from the dermis at the level of the basement membrane. Susceptibility is partly genetic and HLA restricted, which explains why only a small fraction of exposed patients react; well established examples are HLA-B 15:02 with carbamazepine and HLA-B 58:01 with allopurinol."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical course follows the mechanism. One to four weeks after a new drug is started, the patient develops fever, malaise, and sore eyes and mouth, then painful dusky macules and flat atypical targets that begin on the face and trunk rather than acrally. Pain out of proportion to the visible rash, and erosions at two or more mucosal sites, are the early warning signs. The epidermis then detaches in sheets, and the Nikolsky sign is positive because basal attachment, not just the intercellular junction, has failed. Classic triggers are sulfonamides, anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine, lamotrigine, and phenytoin, allopurinol, nevirapine, and oxicam nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Mycoplasma pneumoniae can produce a similar picture with prominent mucositis in children."},{"type":"p","text":"Losing the epidermis is physiologically equivalent to a large burn, which is where the mortality comes from. Water evaporates freely, so the patient becomes hypovolemic and loses heat, protein is lost in the exudate, and the denuded surface is invaded by Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas, so sepsis and multiorgan failure follow. Mucosal necrosis extends to the conjunctiva, where healing can leave symblepharon and corneal scarring, and to the respiratory and genitourinary epithelium."},{"type":"table","title":"Erythema multiforme, SJS, and TEN","cols":["Feature","Erythema multiforme","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis"],"rows":[["Usual trigger","HSV, Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Drugs","Drugs"],["Body surface detached","Essentially none","Under 10 percent","Over 30 percent (10 to 30 percent is overlap)"],["Lesions","Raised typical targets, acral and extensor","Dusky macules and flat atypical targets, face and trunk","Confluent sheets of detaching epidermis"],["Mucosa","None or oral only, patient well","Two or more sites, severe","Two or more sites, severe"],["Pain","Mild burning or itch","Marked, skin and mucosa","Severe"],["Nikolsky sign","Negative","Positive","Positive"],["Histology","Interface dermatitis, scattered necrotic keratinocytes","Full thickness necrosis, subepidermal split, sparse infiltrate","Full thickness necrosis, subepidermal split, sparse infiltrate"],["Outcome","Resolves in 2 to 4 weeks, recurs with HSV","Mortality about 10 percent","Mortality about 30 percent"]],"caption":"Extent of detachment, site of onset, degree of pain, and the sparseness of the infiltrate relative to the amount of necrosis separate these conditions."},{"type":"p","text":"Two look-alikes are distinguished by the plane of separation. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome results from exotoxins that cleave desmoglein 1, so the split is high in the epidermis beneath the stratum corneum, the mucosa is spared, and the patient is usually an infant or young child. Pemphigus vulgaris also splits within the epidermis, just above the basal layer, and shows acantholysis with intercellular IgG on immunofluorescence. Only Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis show necrosis of the whole epidermal thickness with separation at the dermoepidermal junction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C07","obj":"IN-13-B.7","title":"Pityriasis rosea","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pityriasis rosea is an acute, self limited papulosquamous eruption of adolescents and young adults, most common in spring and autumn. It is associated with systemic reactivation of human herpesvirus 6 and human herpesvirus 7, which accounts for the occasional prodrome of malaise, headache, mild fever, and sore throat in the week before the rash, and for the fact that it almost never recurs."},{"type":"p","text":"The eruption unfolds in two stages. First a herald patch appears, a single oval salmon pink to brown plaque about 2 to 10 cm across with a fine collarette of scale, usually on the trunk, neck, or proximal limb. Because it is solitary and annular it is commonly mistaken for tinea corporis, and a potassium hydroxide preparation that shows no hyphae settles the question. One to two weeks later the generalized eruption follows as crops of smaller oval papules and plaques with the same collarette scale, which is attached peripherally and free at its inner edge."},{"type":"p","text":"The arrangement is the examinable detail. The long axis of each oval lesion parallels the skin cleavage lines, the lines of Langer that reflect the predominant orientation of dermal collagen bundles. On the back the cleavage lines slope downward and laterally from the spine, so the lesions form the drooping branches described as a Christmas tree or fir tree pattern. The eruption concentrates on the trunk and proximal limbs and generally spares the face, palms, and soles. Itch is mild or absent, the patient feels well, and the lesions fade spontaneously over about six to eight weeks, sometimes leaving temporary postinflammatory hypopigmentation or hyperpigmentation in darker skin. Histology is nonspecific, showing focal spongiosis, mounds of parakeratosis, a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate, and often extravasated red cells in the papillary dermis."},{"type":"p","text":"The distinction that matters most is from secondary syphilis, which can produce a similar scaly trunk eruption. Secondary syphilis characteristically involves the palms and soles, lacks a herald patch, and comes with generalized lymphadenopathy, mucous patches, or condylomata lata, and those features, or an eruption that persists for months, point away from pityriasis rosea. Guttate psoriasis has thicker, silvery, non collarette scale and tends to follow streptococcal pharyngitis, and tinea corporis has an active scaling advancing border and hyphae on potassium hydroxide examination."},{"type":"table","title":"Scaly trunk eruptions compared","cols":["Condition","Key clue","Palms and soles","Course"],"rows":[["Pityriasis rosea","Herald patch, collarette scale, cleavage line orientation","Spared","Resolves in 6 to 8 weeks"],["Secondary syphilis","Lymphadenopathy, mucous patches, no herald patch","Involved","Resolves but disease persists"],["Guttate psoriasis","Follows streptococcal pharyngitis, silvery adherent scale","Usually spared","Weeks to months, may become chronic"],["Tinea corporis","Advancing scaling border with central clearing, hyphae on KOH","Variable","Persists until treated"]],"caption":"A herald patch and the collarette scale are specific; palmoplantar involvement should redirect attention to syphilis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C08","obj":"IN-13-B.8","title":"Seborrheic dermatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Seborrheic dermatitis is a chronic, relapsing dermatitis confined to skin rich in sebaceous glands. It is not an infection and it is not caused by poor hygiene; it is an inflammatory response to commensal Malassezia yeasts, chiefly Malassezia globosa and Malassezia restricta, living in sebum."},{"type":"p","text":"Malassezia species are lipid dependent and cannot synthesize their own fatty acids, so they secrete lipases that hydrolyze sebum triglycerides and take up the saturated fatty acids they need. The unsaturated fatty acids left behind, including oleic acid, are irritating to the stratum corneum: they disrupt the barrier, provoke keratinocyte cytokine release, and cause abnormal, hurried keratinization that appears as scale. Susceptibility depends more on the host response than on the yeast count, since Malassezia is present on everyone, which is why the disease tracks sebaceous activity and immune status rather than colonization alone."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why sebaceous areas scale","steps":["Sebaceous glands secrete triglyceride-rich sebum","Malassezia lipases split sebum into free fatty acids","Unsaturated fatty acids remain and disrupt the barrier","Keratinocytes release inflammatory cytokines","Hurried keratinization produces greasy yellow scale on erythema"],"caption":"The lesion appears only where sebum is plentiful, which is why distribution maps sebaceous gland density."},{"type":"p","text":"Distribution therefore follows the sebaceous map: the scalp, eyebrows and glabella, nasolabial folds, external ear canals and retroauricular skin, beard area, central chest, and the intertriginous folds. The lesions are poorly defined pink to salmon patches with greasy yellow scale, in contrast to the sharply marginated plaques and dry silvery scale of psoriasis, and itch is mild. Dandruff is the same process limited to scalp scaling without obvious inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"The incidence is bimodal, and both peaks follow androgen-driven sebum production. In infants, maternal and neonatal androgens keep sebaceous glands active for the first months, producing cradle cap, which is thick adherent yellow scale on the vertex with little itch, often with flexural and diaper area involvement, and which resolves as sebum output falls. Sebum output falls through childhood, which is when the disease is least common, then rises again after puberty, and the adult form persists through life with remissions and flares."},{"type":"p","text":"Two associations are regularly tested because they are diagnostic signals. Severe, extensive, or persistent seborrheic dermatitis is common in advanced HIV infection, where falling CD4 counts weaken T cell control of Malassezia, so a florid eruption in a young adult is a recognized marker of immunosuppression. Parkinson disease and other parkinsonism, including the drug induced form, is associated with facial seborrhea and an oily facies, attributed to autonomic dysfunction and increased sebum output. Seborrheic dermatitis is also more frequent and more severe after stroke and in other conditions that limit facial movement."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-02-C09","obj":"IN-13-B.9","title":"Recurrent aphthous ulcers and their systemic associations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aphthous ulcers, called canker sores, are painful shallow ulcers of the oral mucosa produced by a T cell mediated attack on the epithelium rather than by any infectious agent. They are the most common ulcerative condition of the mouth."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion begins as a tender red macule that breaks down within a day into a round or oval ulcer with a shallow crater, a yellow gray fibrinous base, and a sharply defined erythematous halo. Pain is out of proportion to size because the exposed submucosa carries dense sensory innervation. Histology shows a nonspecific ulcer with fibrinopurulent exudate and a mononuclear infiltrate, with CD8 cells predominating at the ulcer base, and the absence of viral cytopathic change or acantholysis distinguishes it from herpetic and autoimmune blistering lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Location is the most reliable discriminator on an examination. Aphthous ulcers occur only on nonkeratinized, movable mucosa: the buccal and labial mucosa, the floor of the mouth, the ventral and lateral tongue, and the soft palate. They spare the keratinized surfaces, meaning the hard palate, the attached gingiva, and the dorsal tongue. Intraoral recurrent herpes simplex does the reverse, favoring keratinized hard palate and gingiva, and herpes labialis sits on the vermilion border. Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis presents with fever, diffuse gingival swelling, and clustered vesicles that coalesce, which aphthae never do."},{"type":"table","title":"Forms of recurrent aphthous stomatitis","cols":["Form","Size and number","Healing","Notes"],"rows":[["Minor (most cases)","Under 1 cm, 1 to 5 ulcers","7 to 14 days, no scar","Confined to nonkeratinized mucosa"],["Major","Over 1 cm, 1 to 3 deep ulcers","Weeks, often with scarring","May involve soft palate and pharynx; raises concern for systemic disease"],["Herpetiform","1 to 3 mm, tens to over 100 in crops","7 to 30 days","Clusters may merge; not caused by herpesvirus despite the name"]],"caption":"Size, number, and whether healing scars separate the three patterns and signal how aggressively to look for an underlying disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Isolated minor aphthae in an otherwise well patient are usually idiopathic, with precipitants including local trauma, stress, menstrual hormonal change, sodium lauryl sulfate in toothpaste, and stopping smoking. Frequent, severe, or major ulcers, or ulcers with systemic symptoms, point to an underlying cause. Behcet disease is the classic association: recurrent oral and genital ulceration, uveitis, skin lesions such as pseudofolliculitis and erythema nodosum, and a positive pathergy test, driven by a vasculitis of vessels of all sizes and linked to HLA-B51. Crohn disease produces aphthous ulcers both as an extraintestinal manifestation and through malabsorption, and celiac disease does the same, sometimes before any gastrointestinal symptom. What the malabsorptive causes share is deficiency of iron, folate, vitamin B12, or zinc, which impairs epithelial turnover. Other causes include cyclic neutropenia, with ulcers recurring at the neutrophil nadir about every three weeks, HIV infection, and the childhood syndrome of periodic fever with aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis, and adenitis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 30 year old has had painful oral ulcers every few weeks for two years, now with painful scrotal ulcers that scar and two episodes of a red painful eye. Which diagnosis unifies these findings, and what single mechanism explains ulcers at such different sites?","explain":"Name the vessel process involved and the associated HLA allele.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on systemic associations."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These conditions are best held together by depth and effector cell. In the superficial dermis, mast cell histamine opens venular gaps and produces a wheal that resolves in hours, while the same leak in deep dermis and subcutis is angioedema; replace the mediator with bradykinin and the itch and the hives disappear, which is immunology read directly off the skin. Within the epidermis, fluid between keratinocytes is spongiosis, the common histology of atopic and contact dermatitis, and it explains weeping vesicles. At the dermoepidermal junction, cytotoxic T cells produce interface dermatitis: scattered keratinocyte death gives the fixed target lesion of erythema multiforme, and confluent full thickness necrosis gives the detaching sheets of toxic epidermal necrolysis. Pathology supplies the plane of separation that names a blistering disease, immunology supplies the cell, and anatomy supplies the layer."},{"type":"p","text":"Several threads run outward into other disciplines. Barrier biochemistry links filaggrin and the cornified envelope to transepidermal water loss, and the loss of antimicrobial peptides under IL-4 and IL-13 connects a genetic barrier defect to Staphylococcus aureus colonization and eczema herpeticum, which is microbiology following from physiology. Microbial triggers run through the whole chapter: HSV in erythema multiforme, HHV-6 and HHV-7 in pityriasis rosea, Malassezia in seborrheic dermatitis, and Mycoplasma pneumoniae in both erythema multiforme and Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and in each case the organism is not destroying tissue but provoking an immune response, so cultures of the rash are unhelpful. Where lesions in the mouth are concerned, gastroenterology and rheumatology matter as much as dermatology. The limits are worth stating plainly: histology in several of these conditions, including pityriasis rosea and aphthous ulceration, is nonspecific, so the diagnosis rests on distribution and time course, and immunologic classification is imperfect because chronic spontaneous urticaria and erythema multiforme both involve mechanisms that do not fit neatly into one hypersensitivity type."}],"summary":["Atopic dermatitis is filaggrin-related barrier failure plus Th2 inflammation; IL-4 and IL-13 lower filaggrin and antimicrobial peptides, and IL-31 drives itch, so antihistamines help little.","Atopic dermatitis affects the face and extensors in infants and the flexures later, with lichenification from chronic scratching, and it predisposes to Staphylococcus aureus infection and eczema herpeticum.","Allergic contact dermatitis is type IV hypersensitivity requiring hapten sensitization through Langerhans cells and appearing 48 to 72 hours after re-exposure with a positive patch test; irritant dermatitis is direct keratinocyte injury with no sensitization and a negative patch test.","Contact dermatitis reproduces the shape of the exposure, and spongiosis, which is intercellular epidermal edema, is the histologic hallmark of all eczematous dermatitis.","Erythema multiforme is cytotoxic T cell killing of keratinocytes, usually after HSV and sometimes after Mycoplasma pneumoniae, giving fixed acral three-zone target lesions, limited mucosal disease, and interface dermatitis with scattered necrotic keratinocytes.","Each urticarial wheal lasts under 24 hours because histamine opens venular gaps transiently; chronic urticaria lasts over 6 weeks and often involves IgG autoantibodies to IgE or its receptor.","Angioedema without hives or itch is bradykinin mediated: C1 inhibitor deficiency with a low C4, or ACE inhibition blocking bradykinin breakdown, and antihistamines do not work.","Stevens-Johnson syndrome (under 10 percent detached) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (over 30 percent) are drug-triggered CD8 and granulysin-mediated full thickness epidermal necrosis with severe mucosal disease and a positive Nikolsky sign; losing the epidermis causes burn-like fluid loss and sepsis.","Pityriasis rosea follows HHV-6 and HHV-7 reactivation: a herald patch, then oval collarette-scaled patches along cleavage lines in a Christmas tree pattern, resolving in 6 to 8 weeks; palmoplantar involvement suggests secondary syphilis instead.","Seborrheic dermatitis is inflammation from Malassezia lipase products in sebaceous skin, with greasy yellow scale and infant cradle cap, and severe disease points to HIV infection or Parkinson disease; aphthous ulcers occur only on nonkeratinized mucosa and recur in Behcet disease, inflammatory bowel disease, and celiac disease."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, acute inflammatory dermatoses and blistering disorders"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immediate hypersensitivity; T cell-mediated hypersensitivity"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Skin and the immune system chapters"},{"book":"Junqueira Basic Histology","where":"Skin: epidermis, dermis, and cutaneous glands"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Microcirculation and transcapillary fluid exchange"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses; medically important fungi (Malassezia)"}]},"IN-13-C":{"unit":"IN-13-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03","title":"Chronic Inflammatory Skin Conditions","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Chronic inflammatory skin disease is where immunology becomes visible. This chapter works through rosacea, lichen planus, and psoriasis, then adds pyoderma gangrenosum, hidradenitis suppurativa, and the alopecias, because the exam groups them by mechanism rather than by appearance. For each one, follow the chain from the immune cell and cytokine to the histologic change to the physical finding, since the question usually gives you a lesion and a location and asks for the mechanism, the biopsy, or the systemic disease it accompanies. The distinctions that earn points are mechanistic: innate neurovascular inflammation versus a cytotoxic T cell interface attack versus a Th17 proliferative loop versus pure neutrophil activation.","prerequisites":["Layers of the epidermis, keratinocyte maturation, and the dermal-epidermal junction (IN-1)","Structure of the hair follicle, sebaceous gland, and apocrine gland, and the hair cycle (IN-2)","Innate immunity: antimicrobial peptides, neutrophil chemotaxis, IL-1, IL-8, and TNF","Helper T cell subsets and their cytokines, especially Th17, plus CD8 cytotoxic killing by perforin, granzyme, and Fas","Basic patterns of inflammation and repair: acanthosis, apoptosis, granulation tissue, and fibrosis"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology (IM-9, IM-10)","how":"Psoriasis is the clearest human example of an IL-23 and Th17 axis disease, and lichen planus is a model of CD8 mediated interface injury, so the same cytokine maps are tested in both subjects."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal system (GI-13)","how":"Pyoderma gangrenosum and erythema nodosum are extraintestinal manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease, and lichen planus is associated with hepatitis C infection."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal system (MS-13)","how":"Psoriatic arthritis is a seronegative spondyloarthropathy with distal interphalangeal and enthesis involvement, and pyoderma gangrenosum travels with rheumatoid arthritis."},{"to":"Endocrine and reproductive physiology (EN-12, RE-13)","how":"Androgenetic alopecia and hidradenitis suppurativa both depend on postpubertal androgen signaling, including conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone by 5-alpha reductase."},{"to":"Hematology and oncology (HE-9)","how":"Pyoderma gangrenosum can accompany myelodysplastic syndrome and other myeloid disorders, so a neutrophilic dermatosis can be the presenting sign of marrow disease."}],"terms":[{"term":"Interface dermatitis","def":"A pattern in which lymphocytes mass along the dermal-epidermal junction and injure the basal keratinocyte layer."},{"term":"Acanthosis","def":"Thickening of the viable epidermis, usually from keratinocyte hyperproliferation."},{"term":"Parakeratosis","def":"Retention of keratinocyte nuclei in the stratum corneum, indicating that cells reached the surface before completing maturation."},{"term":"Hypergranulosis","def":"Thickening of the stratum granulosum, the opposite of the granular layer loss seen in psoriasis."},{"term":"Civatte body","def":"An apoptotic basal keratinocyte seen as a rounded eosinophilic (colloid) body at or below the junction."},{"term":"Munro microabscess","def":"A collection of neutrophils within the parakeratotic stratum corneum, characteristic of psoriasis."},{"term":"Suprapapillary plate","def":"The thin shelf of epidermis lying over a dermal papilla, thinned in psoriasis so that papillary capillaries bleed easily."},{"term":"Koebner phenomenon","def":"Appearance of new lesions of an existing skin disease at a site of trauma, seen in psoriasis, lichen planus, and vitiligo."},{"term":"Pathergy","def":"Worsening or new ulceration of a neutrophilic dermatosis after minor trauma, needle puncture, or debridement."},{"term":"Comedo","def":"A plugged follicular orifice, open (blackhead) or closed (whitehead), present in acne vulgaris and absent in rosacea."},{"term":"Anagen","def":"The actively growing phase of the hair cycle, followed by catagen involution and telogen rest."},{"term":"Follicular immune privilege","def":"The normally low antigen presentation and reduced MHC class I expression of the anagen hair bulb, lost in alopecia areata."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C01","obj":"IN-13-C.1","title":"Rosacea: neurovascular and innate immune inflammation of central facial skin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory disorder confined almost entirely to the convexities of the central face: cheeks, nose, chin, glabella, and forehead. It typically begins in adults between about 30 and 50 years of age and is most common in fair-skinned people. The distribution is a clue to the mechanism, because central facial skin carries a dense superficial vascular plexus, abundant sebaceous glands, and a rich supply of sensory nerve endings."},{"type":"p","text":"Two processes run together. The first is neurovascular dysregulation. Sensory nerve endings in facial skin express transient receptor potential channels, including TRPV1, which respond to heat, capsaicin, ethanol, and inflammatory mediators. When these channels open, the nerve releases vasoactive neuropeptides such as substance P and calcitonin gene related peptide, which dilate the superficial plexus and increase permeability. Early on this produces transient flushing. With repeated episodes the vessels remain dilated and elongated, so the erythema becomes fixed and visible telangiectasias appear."},{"type":"p","text":"The second process is innate immune overactivation. Rosacea skin contains increased cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide and increased activity of the serine protease kallikrein 5, which cleaves cathelicidin into fragments such as LL-37. These fragments are chemotactic for neutrophils, stimulate keratinocyte cytokine release, and promote angiogenesis, which is why papules and sterile pustules can sit on a background of erythema. Toll-like receptor 2 signaling, ultraviolet damage, and increased density of the follicular mite Demodex folliculorum are all thought to feed this loop, although Demodex is a correlation rather than an established cause."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Trigger to fixed erythema in rosacea","steps":["Trigger: heat, sunlight, alcohol, spicy food, hot drinks, stress","TRPV1 and related channels open on facial sensory nerves","Substance P and CGRP released; superficial plexus dilates","Flushing with plasma leakage and mediator delivery","Cathelicidin and kallikrein 5 activity rise; LL-37 recruits neutrophils","Vessels remodel and stay dilated: fixed erythema and telangiectasias","Remodeled, inflamed skin responds to the next trigger more easily"],"caption":"Repeated flushing becomes persistent erythema because vascular remodeling and innate inflammation reinforce each other."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the inflammation centers on vessels and the perifollicular dermis rather than on a plugged follicular orifice, comedones never form. Biopsy, which is rarely needed, shows dilated dermal vessels, lymphohistiocytic perivascular and perifollicular inflammation, dermal edema, and solar elastosis, with neutrophils in and around follicles when pustules are present."},{"type":"table","title":"Rosacea phenotypes and the mechanism behind each","cols":["Phenotype","Findings","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Erythematotelangiectatic","Flushing, persistent central erythema, telangiectasias, stinging","Neurovascular hyperreactivity with vascular remodeling"],["Papulopustular","Inflammatory papules and sterile pustules on erythema, no comedones","Cathelicidin and LL-37 driven neutrophil recruitment"],["Phymatous","Rhinophyma: bulbous, thickened nose with patulous follicles","Years of inflammation drive sebaceous hyperplasia and dermal fibrosis"],["Ocular","Blepharitis, conjunctival injection, gritty or foreign body sensation, chalazia","Meibomian gland inflammation and obstruction with tear film instability"]],"caption":"Phenotypes overlap in the same patient; rhinophyma and ocular disease are the complications to predict."},{"type":"p","text":"Two complications are heavily tested. Rhinophyma is sebaceous and fibrous overgrowth of the nose, produced by sustained inflammation and lymphatic obstruction, and it occurs mostly in men. Ocular rosacea affects a large minority of patients and can precede the skin findings, so eye symptoms in an adult with facial flushing belong to the same diagnosis rather than to a separate problem."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C02","obj":"IN-13-C.2","title":"Separating rosacea from acne vulgaris, seborrheic dermatitis, and the malar rash of lupus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four conditions produce central facial redness, and each has one mechanistic feature that settles the question. Acne vulgaris begins with follicular hyperkeratinization and sebum retention, so the primary lesion is a comedo. Rosacea begins in vessels and nerves, so there is no comedo. Seborrheic dermatitis is an inflammatory response to Malassezia yeast in sebum-rich skin, so it produces greasy scale and it concentrates in the folds and creases. Acute cutaneous lupus erythematosus begins with ultraviolet-induced apoptosis of keratinocytes, exposure of nuclear antigens such as Ro (SSA), autoantibody binding, and a resulting interface dermatitis, so it spares the shaded nasolabial folds and comes with systemic findings."},{"type":"table","title":"Central facial eruptions compared","cols":["Feature","Rosacea","Acne vulgaris","Seborrheic dermatitis","Acute lupus malar rash"],"rows":[["Typical age","Adults, about 30 to 50","Adolescents and young adults","Infants, then adults","Young to middle-aged adults, mostly women"],["Primary lesion","Erythema, telangiectasias, papules, pustules","Open and closed comedones, then papules, pustules, nodules","Erythematous patches with greasy yellow scale","Confluent erythematous to edematous malar patch"],["Comedones","Absent","Present; the defining primary lesion","Absent","Absent"],["Scale","None or minimal","None","Greasy, yellow, flaking","None to fine"],["Nasolabial folds","Spared or mildly involved","Variable","Characteristically involved","Characteristically spared"],["Other sites","Central face and eyes","Face, chest, upper back","Scalp, brows, ears, presternal skin","Photodistributed arms and chest"],["Mechanism","Neurovascular plus innate immune","Follicular plugging, Cutibacterium acnes, androgen-driven sebum","Inflammation to Malassezia in sebum","Ultraviolet-induced keratinocyte apoptosis with autoimmune interface injury"],["Pointer to systemic disease","Ocular rosacea, rhinophyma","None usually; consider hyperandrogenism if severe","Severe or refractory disease with HIV or Parkinson disease","Arthralgia, cytopenias, nephritis, positive ANA and anti-dsDNA"]],"caption":"Comedones, scale, and the nasolabial folds separate these four faster than overall redness does."},{"type":"p","text":"The nasolabial fold rule is worth understanding rather than memorizing. The fold shades itself from direct sunlight, so a photosensitive eruption such as acute cutaneous lupus tends to stop at it. Seborrheic dermatitis follows sebaceous gland density and the retention of yeast and sebum within creases, so the same fold is a favored site. Rosacea follows the convex, vessel-rich central face and so sits between the two patterns."},{"type":"p","text":"Two further distinctions appear on examinations. Perioral dermatitis presents as small papules and pustules around the mouth with a clear rim of skin at the vermilion border, often after topical corticosteroid use, and it lacks both telangiectasias and comedones. Steroid-induced rosacea-like eruptions worsen after each withdrawal of a topical corticosteroid, because the corticosteroid suppresses inflammation while thinning the dermis and dilating superficial vessels, so erythema rebounds when it is stopped. The ordering question is which structure the eruption begins in: the follicular orifice in acne, the sebaceous crease in seborrheic dermatitis, the superficial vascular plexus and sensory nerves in rosacea, and the basal layer of sun-exposed skin in acute cutaneous lupus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An adult reports that wine and hot soup turn the cheeks and nose red within minutes, and you see fine dilated vessels but no blackheads or whiteheads. Which one finding rules out acne vulgaris, and which rules out the lupus malar rash?","explain":"Name the structure each disease starts in, then say which physical finding reports on that structure.","check":"Compare your answer against the comedones row and the nasolabial folds row of the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C03","obj":"IN-13-C.3","title":"Lichen planus: cytotoxic interface attack on basal keratinocytes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lichen planus is the prototype of interface dermatitis caused by CD8 cytotoxic T lymphocytes. The cells recognize an antigen presented on basal keratinocytes, accumulate in a dense band immediately beneath and within the basal layer, and kill those keratinocytes by apoptosis using perforin, granzyme, Fas ligand, and TNF. Every classic finding of the disease follows from that single event."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical lesion is summarized by six words beginning with the letter P: pruritic, purple, polygonal, planar papules and plaques. They favor the flexor wrists and forearms, the shins, the ankles, and the genitals, and they can appear along a scratch or scar, which is the Koebner phenomenon. Fine white reticulated lines called Wickham striae cross the surface. In the mouth, lichen planus produces a white lacy network on the buccal mucosa, and erosive oral and genital forms are painful rather than itchy. Nail involvement can produce longitudinal ridging, thinning, and in severe cases a dorsal pterygium from scarring of the matrix."},{"type":"flow","title":"From T cell attack to the visible papule","steps":["CD8 T cells recognize antigen on basal keratinocytes","Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate forms at the dermal-epidermal junction","Basal keratinocytes undergo apoptosis and become Civatte bodies","Basal layer damage produces a sawtooth contour of the rete ridges","Melanin released from dying cells is taken up by dermal macrophages","Surviving epidermis thickens its granular layer (hypergranulosis)","Result: flat violaceous polygonal papules with Wickham striae"],"caption":"One cytotoxic event at the junction explains the color, the shape, the surface lines, and the biopsy."},{"type":"p","text":"Biopsy findings map one to one onto the mechanism. The band-like lymphocytic infiltrate hugs the junction. Apoptotic basal keratinocytes appear as rounded eosinophilic Civatte bodies, also called colloid bodies. Loss of the normal basal layer leaves the rete ridges with a pointed, sawtooth outline. Hypergranulosis thickens the stratum granulosum, and because that layer holds the keratohyalin granules, the thickening shows through as the white Wickham striae. The violaceous color has two sources: the dense infiltrate itself, and pigment incontinence, meaning melanin dropped from dying basal cells into the papillary dermis. Compact orthokeratosis, not parakeratosis, covers the surface, which is the opposite of psoriasis."},{"type":"p","text":"Lichen planus is associated with chronic hepatitis C virus infection, particularly the oral form and particularly in certain populations, and the association is strong enough that board questions pair lacy buccal lesions with abnormal liver enzymes. Lichenoid drug eruptions look similar but tend to be more photodistributed and to contain eosinophils in the infiltrate, and graft versus host disease produces the same interface pattern through the same cytotoxic mechanism in an allogeneic setting. Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation lasting months after the papules flatten is expected, again from pigment incontinence. Long-standing erosive oral lichen planus carries a small risk of oral squamous cell carcinoma, on the order of one percent, which is why it is treated as a lesion to follow rather than a purely cosmetic finding."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C04","obj":"IN-13-C.4","title":"Psoriasis: the IL-23 and Th17 loop and its clinical signature","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Psoriasis is an immune-driven disorder of keratinocyte proliferation. Genetic susceptibility is strong, with concordance in monozygotic twins of roughly 60 to 70 percent and with HLA-Cw6 (HLA-C*06:02) as the classic associated allele, strongest in early-onset plaque disease. A trigger such as mechanical trauma, streptococcal pharyngitis, certain drugs including lithium and beta blockers, or withdrawal of systemic corticosteroids activates dermal dendritic cells in susceptible skin."},{"type":"p","text":"Activated dendritic cells release IL-23 and TNF. IL-23 expands and sustains Th17 lymphocytes, which secrete IL-17A and IL-22. IL-17 acts directly on keratinocytes, driving them to proliferate and to release chemokines such as CXCL8 (IL-8) along with antimicrobial peptides. Those chemokines recruit neutrophils and more T cells into the lesion, and the recruited cells supply more cytokine, so the loop sustains itself. TNF amplifies each step. Epidermal transit time, normally about four weeks from basal layer to shed corneocyte, falls to a few days."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The self-sustaining psoriatic loop","steps":["Genetic risk plus trigger activates dermal dendritic cells","Dendritic cells secrete IL-23 and TNF","IL-23 expands Th17 cells","Th17 cells release IL-17A and IL-22","Keratinocytes proliferate and release chemokines and antimicrobial peptides","Neutrophils and T cells are recruited into epidermis and dermis","Recruited cells deliver more IL-23 and TNF, restarting the loop"],"caption":"Because the output of the loop recruits cells that feed its input, psoriatic plaques persist without any ongoing external trigger."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically this produces sharply demarcated erythematous plaques with adherent silvery-white scale. Plaques favor extensor surfaces, especially elbows and knees, along with the scalp, the lumbosacral area and gluteal cleft, and the umbilicus. The sharp border reflects the abrupt edge of the proliferating population, and the silvery scale is thick stratum corneum made of immature corneocytes. New plaques appearing in a scratch, a surgical scar, or a sunburn line is the Koebner phenomenon, explained by trauma-released antimicrobial peptides and cytokines activating the same loop in previously normal skin."},{"type":"table","title":"Psoriasis variants and features to recognize","cols":["Variant or feature","Presentation","Mechanistic note"],"rows":[["Chronic plaque (psoriasis vulgaris)","Extensor plaques with silvery scale, most common form","Established IL-23 and Th17 loop in chronic lesions"],["Guttate","Abrupt small drop-like papules in children or young adults","Follows streptococcal pharyngitis, with molecular mimicry between M protein and keratin"],["Inverse","Shiny erythema in axillae, groin, and inframammary folds with little scale","Moisture and occlusion in folds prevent the usual dry scale"],["Pustular and erythrodermic","Sterile pustules or near-total body erythema with systemic illness","Massive neutrophil recruitment, with fluid, protein, and heat loss across a failed barrier"],["Nail disease","Pitting, onycholysis, oil-drop discoloration, subungual hyperkeratosis","Inflammation in the nail matrix (pitting) or the nail bed (oil drop, onycholysis)"],["Psoriatic arthritis","Distal interphalangeal arthritis, dactylitis, enthesitis, pencil-in-cup erosions","Seronegative spondyloarthropathy sharing the IL-23 and IL-17 axis; nail disease raises the risk"]],"caption":"Nail findings matter because they predict joint disease: pitting comes from matrix inflammation, oil-drop change from nail bed inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Psoriasis also behaves as a systemic inflammatory disease. Moderate to severe disease is independently associated with metabolic syndrome and with cardiovascular events, which is attributed to the circulating burden of TNF and IL-17 driven inflammation and its effect on insulin signaling and the endothelium."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a patient's dendritic cells still produced normal IL-23 but keratinocytes could not respond to IL-17A, which features of a psoriatic plaque would fail to develop?","explain":"Work through the loop one arrow at a time and ask what each arrow is responsible for producing.","check":"Compare your reasoning against the cycle figure in this section and the histology section that follows."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C05","obj":"IN-13-C.5","title":"Psoriasis histology and the mechanical basis of the Auspitz sign","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every histologic feature of a psoriatic plaque is a consequence of accelerated keratinocyte turnover plus neutrophil recruitment, so the slide can be reconstructed from the mechanism rather than memorized as a list."},{"type":"table","title":"Psoriasis histology: finding, cause, and clinical correlate","cols":["Histologic finding","Why it occurs","What it produces clinically"],"rows":[["Acanthosis with regular, uniform elongation of the rete ridges","Sustained IL-17 driven proliferation of the basal compartment","Palpable, thickened, well-demarcated plaque"],["Parakeratosis (retained nuclei in the stratum corneum)","Transit time falls from about four weeks to a few days, so cells reach the surface immature","Loose, friable, silvery scale rather than normal invisible shedding"],["Thin or absent stratum granulosum","Cells pass through the granular stage too quickly to accumulate keratohyalin granules","Scale that detaches in sheets instead of being tightly bound"],["Thinned suprapapillary plates","Rete ridges elongate downward while the epidermis over each papilla stays thin","Mechanical basis of the Auspitz sign"],["Dilated, tortuous papillary dermal capillaries","Angiogenesis driven by VEGF released from activated keratinocytes","Bright erythema of the plaque and the bleeding source for the Auspitz sign"],["Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum)","Keratinocyte chemokines such as CXCL8 draw neutrophils all the way through the epidermis","Sterile pustules in pustular variants"],["Spongiform pustule of Kogoj (neutrophils in the spinous layer)","The same chemotaxis caught at an earlier level of the epidermis","Supports the diagnosis, especially in pustular psoriasis"]],"caption":"Two processes, fast turnover and neutrophil chemotaxis, generate the entire histologic picture."},{"type":"p","text":"The Auspitz sign deserves a careful mechanical explanation because it is a favorite question. In normal skin the epidermis over each dermal papilla is a reasonably thick shelf, and the capillary loop inside the papilla is protected. In psoriasis the rete ridges elongate downward between the papillae while the epidermis directly above each papilla is stretched and thinned, so the papillae rise into a shallow space covered only by a thin suprapapillary plate. The capillary loops inside those papillae are simultaneously dilated and more fragile. Removing the adherent scale shears away that thin plate, the exposed capillary loops rupture, and pinpoint droplets of blood appear across the surface."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading one biopsy two ways","text":"A specimen shows a thickened epidermis, no granular layer, nuclei retained in the stratum corneum, and neutrophils sitting in that corneum. A second specimen shows a thickened granular layer, compact keratin without retained nuclei, a dense band of lymphocytes at the junction, and scattered apoptotic basal cells. The first is psoriasis: proliferation outruns maturation, so maturation markers (granular layer, enucleated corneocytes) are lost, and chemokines pull neutrophils to the surface. The second is lichen planus: proliferation is normal but the basal layer is under cytotoxic attack, so maturation is intact or exaggerated and the infiltrate is lymphocytic at the junction. Granular layer status alone separates them: lost in psoriasis, thickened in lichen planus."},{"type":"p","text":"Grattage, the gentle scraping used to elicit these signs, first lifts fine silvery flakes (the candle wax sign) and then produces the pinpoint bleeding of the Auspitz sign. Because trauma can seed new plaques through the Koebner phenomenon, the maneuver can also extend the disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C06","obj":"IN-13-C.6","title":"Pyoderma gangrenosum: a sterile neutrophilic dermatosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pyoderma gangrenosum is named misleadingly. It is neither pyogenic in the infectious sense nor gangrenous: it is a neutrophilic dermatosis, meaning a sterile inflammatory disease in which activated neutrophils accumulate in tissue and destroy it. No organism is found on special stains, and cultures of an untreated lesion yield no causative pathogen, although an open ulcer surface can be secondarily colonized."},{"type":"p","text":"The driving abnormality is dysregulated innate immunity. Overproduction of IL-1 beta, together with IL-8 (CXCL8), IL-17, and TNF, recruits neutrophils and prolongs their survival, and neutrophil function itself appears abnormal in many patients. Released proteases, reactive oxygen species, and matrix metalloproteinases digest dermal collagen, so the lesion becomes a deep ulcer rather than a superficial erosion. Several monogenic autoinflammatory syndromes that cause pyoderma gangrenosum are driven by inflammasome dysregulation, which supports the IL-1 mechanism."},{"type":"flow","title":"Natural history of a pyoderma gangrenosum lesion","steps":["Small tender pustule, nodule, or papule appears, often on the lower leg","Lesion breaks down centrally","Neutrophil proteases digest dermal collagen and the ulcer deepens","Border becomes violaceous and undermined, with epidermis overhanging the base","Ulcer expands centrifugally with a boggy, purulent-appearing base that is sterile","Healing leaves a characteristic thin cribriform (sieve-like) scar"],"caption":"The undermined violaceous border and the cribriform scar are the two most recognizable features."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathergy is the behavior that identifies the disease. Minor trauma, a needle puncture, a biopsy site, or surgical debridement delivers fresh chemotactic signals into already primed tissue, so the lesion enlarges instead of healing. The clinical consequence is diagnostically useful: an ulcer that grows after debridement is behaving like a neutrophilic dermatosis, not like an infected wound. Biopsy shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate with abscess formation, necrosis, and often a secondary leukocytoclastic vasculitis, with no organisms on special stains and no growth on culture. Because the histology is suggestive rather than specific, diagnosis requires excluding infection, venous and arterial ulceration, vasculitis, and malignancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Systemic associations and why they travel together","cols":["Association","Shared mechanism"],"rows":[["Inflammatory bowel disease, especially ulcerative colitis","Shared neutrophil-driven mucocutaneous inflammation; one of several extraintestinal manifestations"],["Rheumatoid arthritis and seronegative arthritis","Chronic TNF and IL-1 driven inflammation with neutrophil recruitment into tissue"],["Myelodysplastic syndrome, acute and chronic myeloid leukemia, monoclonal gammopathy (IgA)","Abnormal myeloid clones produce functionally abnormal neutrophils and excess myeloid cytokines"],["Autoinflammatory syndromes with pyoderma gangrenosum","Inflammasome dysregulation with excess IL-1 beta signaling"]],"caption":"In roughly half of cases a systemic disease is present, so the ulcer can be the presenting sign of bowel or marrow disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Distinguish pyoderma gangrenosum from two mimics. Sweet syndrome is also a neutrophilic dermatosis and shares the malignancy associations, but it produces tender edematous plaques with fever rather than deep ulcers. Necrotizing infection produces rapidly spreading pain, systemic toxicity, and positive cultures, and organisms are demonstrable on Gram stain and tissue culture."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C07","obj":"IN-13-C.7","title":"Hidradenitis suppurativa: follicular occlusion in apocrine-bearing skin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hidradenitis suppurativa was once attributed to primary apocrine gland infection, and the name still reflects that idea. Current understanding places the initiating lesion in the hair follicle. Hyperkeratosis and infundibular plugging obstruct the terminal follicle in intertriginous skin, the occluded follicle dilates under retained keratin and sebum, and it eventually ruptures into the dermis. Apocrine glands are involved secondarily because they drain into the same follicular unit, which explains the distribution without making the gland the cause."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of hidradenitis suppurativa","steps":["Follicular hyperkeratosis plugs terminal follicles in axilla, groin, perineum, inframammary skin","Occluded follicle dilates as keratin and sebum accumulate","Follicular wall ruptures, spilling keratin, sebum, and commensal bacteria into dermis","Keratin and bacterial products trigger IL-1, TNF, and IL-17 driven neutrophilic inflammation","Painful deep nodules and sterile or secondarily infected abscesses form","Repeated rupture and re-epithelialization create epithelium-lined sinus tracts","Chronic healing produces rope-like fibrotic bands and contracture"],"caption":"Occlusion, rupture, inflammation, and tract formation, in that order, explain every physical finding."},{"type":"p","text":"Onset is after puberty, usually in the second or third decade, and women are affected more often than men. The disease is restricted to flexural, apocrine-bearing skin: axillae, inguinal folds, perineum and perianal skin, buttocks, and inframammary skin. Lesions are deep, painful, and recurrent, and they differ from a simple furuncle in three ways: they recur in the same sites, they interconnect through sinus tracts that drain foul-smelling material, and they heal with hypertrophic and rope-like scars. Double-ended (tombstone) comedones with two or more orifices sharing a dilated follicular space are a hallmark of chronic disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors and the mechanism each contributes","cols":["Risk factor","Contribution"],"rows":[["Obesity","Increases skin-on-skin friction, moisture, and occlusion in folds, and adds adipose-derived inflammatory cytokines"],["Smoking","Nicotine promotes follicular hyperkeratosis and plugging, and alters neutrophil and keratinocyte signaling"],["Postpubertal androgen exposure","Androgen-dependent follicular and glandular activity explains onset after puberty"],["Family history","A minority of cases carry gamma-secretase (nicastrin, PSEN1, PSENEN) variants affecting follicular differentiation"],["Mechanical friction and tight clothing","Repeated trauma to the follicular infundibulum promotes occlusion and rupture"]],"caption":"Obesity and smoking are the two risk factors most often tested, and both act through follicular occlusion."},{"type":"p","text":"Long-standing disease carries structural consequences: lymphedema from scarring of lymphatics, anal and urethral fistulas in perineal disease, restricted limb movement from contracture, secondary amyloidosis, and squamous cell carcinoma arising in chronically inflamed, scarred perineal or buttock skin. The follicular occlusion tetrad (hidradenitis suppurativa, acne conglobata, dissecting cellulitis of the scalp, and pilonidal sinus) groups the diseases that share this mechanism in different sites."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why do recurrent axillary abscesses that connect by draining tracts and heal with rope-like scars point away from a staphylococcal furuncle, even when a swab grows Staphylococcus aureus?","explain":"Identify the primary lesion in each process and say what each one does to the follicular unit over time.","check":"Compare your answer against the pathogenesis flow in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-03-C08","obj":"IN-13-C.8","title":"Alopecias: which part of the hair cycle fails, and whether the follicle survives","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hair grows in cycles. Anagen is the growth phase and lasts about two to six years on the scalp, catagen is a brief involution phase of about two to three weeks, and telogen is a resting phase of about three months that ends when the club hair is shed. At any moment roughly 85 to 90 percent of scalp follicles are in anagen, and shedding of up to about 100 hairs daily is normal. The follicle is rebuilt each cycle from stem cells in the bulge, a niche in the outer root sheath at the level of the arrector pili insertion. Place any alopecia by asking which phase is disturbed and whether those bulge stem cells survive."},{"type":"p","text":"The second question separates the two major categories. Nonscarring alopecia leaves follicular ostia visible on the scalp surface, so regrowth remains possible. Scarring (cicatricial) alopecia destroys the bulge stem cells and replaces the follicle with fibrous tissue, so the scalp becomes smooth and shiny with no visible openings and the loss is permanent. Looking for follicular openings is the single most informative step in examining hair loss."},{"type":"table","title":"Alopecias by mechanism","cols":["Type","Phase or target affected","Appearance","Scarring"],"rows":[["Alopecia areata","CD8 T cell attack on the anagen bulb after loss of follicular immune privilege","Round or oval smooth patches, exclamation point hairs, nail pitting","No"],["Androgenetic alopecia","Dihydrotestosterone shortens anagen and miniaturizes androgen-sensitive follicles","Vertex and frontotemporal recession in men, widened central part with retained frontal line in women","No"],["Telogen effluvium","A stressor synchronously shifts many anagen follicles into telogen","Diffuse shedding of club hairs about two to four months after the event, positive gentle pull test","No"],["Anagen effluvium","Abrupt arrest of mitosis in the matrix, as with cytotoxic chemotherapy","Rapid diffuse loss within days to weeks, broken tapered shafts","No"],["Traction alopecia","Sustained mechanical tension on follicles","Marginal thinning, early nonscarring and late scarring","Late"],["Lichen planopilaris and discoid lupus erythematosus","Lymphocytic attack on the follicular bulge and infundibulum","Perifollicular erythema and scale, then smooth patches without ostia","Yes"]],"caption":"Place any case by naming the disturbed phase or target, then checking whether follicular ostia remain."},{"type":"p","text":"Three mechanisms deserve a fuller explanation. In alopecia areata the anagen bulb normally enjoys immune privilege through low MHC class I expression and local immunosuppressive signals; when that privilege is lost, autoreactive CD8 T cells attack the bulb. The shaft narrows where growth was disrupted and breaks near the surface as the exclamation point hair, but the bulge stem cells survive, so regrowth can occur. In androgenetic alopecia, 5-alpha reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, which shortens each successive anagen phase in genetically sensitive scalp follicles, so every cycle yields a shorter, finer, less pigmented shaft until a terminal hair becomes a vellus hair; occipital follicles lack this sensitivity, which is why the occipital fringe persists. In telogen effluvium an insult such as childbirth, major surgery, high fever, iron deficiency, thyroid disease, or rapid weight loss pushes many follicles into telogen together, and because telogen lasts about three months the shedding appears two to four months later."},{"type":"example","title":"Three patterns of diffuse or patchy loss","text":"Heavy shedding four months after a complicated delivery, with normal scalp skin and visible ostia: telogen effluvium, expected to recover. Two sharply round smooth patches with short tapered marginal hairs and fine nail pitting: alopecia areata, ostia preserved. Smooth shiny vertex patches with perifollicular scale, no visible ostia, and violaceous papules on the wrists: lichen planopilaris, the scarring scalp counterpart of lichen planus, permanent because the bulge is destroyed."},{"type":"p","text":"Tinea capitis and trichotillomania both produce patchy loss with broken hairs: tinea adds scale, inflammation, and sometimes a boggy kerion, while trichotillomania shows hairs broken at varying lengths in an irregular pattern. Alopecia areata associates with autoimmune thyroid disease, vitiligo, and type 1 diabetes."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These diseases are best organized by immune mechanism rather than by appearance, because the mechanism predicts the histology, the associated systemic disease, and the behavior of the lesion. Innate neurovascular inflammation with cathelicidin activation produces rosacea. A CD8 cytotoxic attack at the dermal-epidermal junction produces lichen planus, so maturation is intact or exaggerated and the infiltrate is lymphocytic at the junction. An IL-23 and Th17 loop produces psoriasis, so proliferation outruns maturation and chemokines pull neutrophils into the corneum. Pure neutrophil activation without a microbe produces the sterile, protease-driven, pathergic ulcer of pyoderma gangrenosum. Follicular occlusion with rupture produces the tracts and scars of hidradenitis suppurativa. Loss of follicular immune privilege, androgen sensitivity, cycle synchronization, and stem cell destruction account for the alopecias."},{"type":"p","text":"Skin findings also function as a window on other organ systems, which is why these conditions appear in integration questions. Lacy oral lichen planus raises the question of hepatitis C. A pathergic leg ulcer points at ulcerative colitis, rheumatoid arthritis, or a myeloid neoplasm. Nail pitting in psoriasis predicts a seronegative arthritis of the distal interphalangeal joints, and severe psoriasis tracks with metabolic and cardiovascular risk. Scarring alopecia on the scalp with violaceous papules on the wrists is one disease in two locations. The limits are worth stating plainly: these associations are statistical rather than universal, histology in rosacea, pyoderma gangrenosum, and hidradenitis suppurativa is supportive rather than diagnostic, and pyoderma gangrenosum in particular remains a diagnosis of exclusion that requires ruling out infection, vascular ulceration, vasculitis, and malignancy."}],"summary":["Rosacea is adult central facial flushing, fixed erythema, telangiectasias, and pustules without comedones, from TRPV1 driven neurovascular reactivity plus kallikrein 5 and cathelicidin activation; predict rhinophyma and ocular rosacea.","Comedones mean acne vulgaris, greasy scale in the nasolabial folds means seborrheic dermatitis, and sparing of the nasolabial folds with systemic findings means the acute lupus malar rash.","Lichen planus is a CD8 interface dermatitis: pruritic purple polygonal planar papules with Wickham striae and lacy buccal lesions, and an association with hepatitis C.","Lichen planus histology follows from basal keratinocyte apoptosis: band-like junctional lymphocytes, Civatte bodies, sawtooth rete ridges, hypergranulosis, and pigment incontinence.","Psoriasis is a self-sustaining loop in which dendritic cell IL-23 expands Th17 cells whose IL-17 and IL-22, with TNF, drive keratinocyte proliferation and neutrophil recruitment; HLA-Cw6 is the classic allele.","Psoriasis plaques are sharply demarcated with silvery scale on extensors, scalp, and lumbosacral skin, with Koebner phenomenon, nail pitting that predicts psoriatic arthritis, and guttate disease after streptococcal pharyngitis.","Psoriasis histology is fast turnover plus chemotaxis: acanthosis with regularly elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, lost granular layer, and Munro microabscesses; thin suprapapillary plates over dilated capillaries explain the Auspitz sign.","Granular layer status separates the two papulosquamous patterns: lost in psoriasis, thickened in lichen planus.","Pyoderma gangrenosum is a sterile neutrophilic dermatosis forming a painful ulcer with a violaceous undermined border that worsens after trauma (pathergy), linked to inflammatory bowel disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and myeloid neoplasms.","Hidradenitis suppurativa is follicular occlusion and rupture in axillary, inguinal, and perineal skin producing nodules, abscesses, sinus tracts, and rope-like scars, with obesity and smoking as the main risk factors; alopecias are classified by the hair cycle phase affected and by whether follicular ostia survive."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin: acute and chronic inflammatory dermatoses"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Helper T cell subsets and effector mechanisms of cell-mediated immunity"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Skin Disease: inflammatory and papulosquamous disorders"},{"book":"Junqueira Basic Histology","where":"Skin: epidermis, dermis, hair follicles, and glands"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Skin blood flow and the control of cutaneous circulation"}]},"IN-13-D":{"unit":"IN-13-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-04","title":"Blistering Diseases","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"A blister forms wherever the proteins that hold skin together fail, and the depth of that failure decides everything a board question can show you. This chapter works through the adhesion apparatus of the epidermis and the dermal-epidermal junction, then uses it to explain the inherited blistering diseases (epidermolysis bullosa) and the autoantibody-mediated ones (pemphigus vulgaris, pemphigus foliaceus, bullous pemphigoid, dermatitis herpetiformis). Questions give you an age, a blister that is tense or flaccid, the presence or absence of oral erosions, a histologic split level, and an immunofluorescence pattern. Each of those findings follows from one target protein and its position in the skin.","prerequisites":["Layers of the epidermis and the normal keratinocyte differentiation sequence (IN-1)","Structure of desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, and the basement membrane zone, including cadherins and integrins","Collagen types and their tissue distribution, especially types IV and VII","Antibody classes and effector functions, including complement activation and Fc receptor recruitment of granulocytes","Type II hypersensitivity and the genetics of HLA-linked autoimmunity"],"connections":[{"to":"Skin structure and histology (IN-1)","how":"The adhesion proteins taught as normal histology are the exact antigens and gene products that fail in every disease in this chapter."},{"to":"Immunology of hypersensitivity (IM chapters)","how":"Pemphigus is a type II reaction in which antibody binding alone disrupts function, while pemphigoid and dermatitis herpetiformis add complement and granulocyte recruitment."},{"to":"Celiac disease and malabsorption (GI chapters)","how":"Dermatitis herpetiformis is the cutaneous expression of gluten-sensitive enteropathy, sharing HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 and the transglutaminase antigen family."},{"to":"Skin infections and toxin-mediated disease (IN-13-H and microbiology)","how":"Staphylococcal exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1, producing the same subcorneal split as pemphigus foliaceus by a nonimmune route."},{"to":"Neoplasia and wound biology (IN-13 neoplasia sections)","how":"Chronic wounding and scarring in recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa drive aggressive squamous cell carcinoma in young adults."}],"terms":[{"term":"Acantholysis","def":"Loss of adhesion between keratinocytes, so the cells round up and separate within the epidermis."},{"term":"Desmoglein","def":"A desmosomal cadherin, present as desmoglein 1 and desmoglein 3, that binds keratinocytes to one another across the intercellular space."},{"term":"Hemidesmosome","def":"The junction anchoring a basal keratinocyte to the basement membrane, built on integrin alpha-6 beta-4, BP180 (collagen XVII), and BP230."},{"term":"Lamina lucida","def":"The electron-lucent layer of the basement membrane zone just beneath the basal cell, crossed by laminin 332."},{"term":"Anchoring fibrils","def":"Type VII collagen loops that extend from the lamina densa into the papillary dermis and tie the basement membrane to the dermal matrix."},{"term":"Nikolsky sign","def":"Shearing of the epidermis from clinically normal skin under lateral finger pressure, indicating that cohesion has already been lost in skin that still looks intact."},{"term":"Flaccid bulla","def":"A blister whose roof is only part of the epidermis, so it is thin walled, easily ruptured, and often seen already eroded."},{"term":"Tense bulla","def":"A blister roofed by the full thickness of the epidermis, which resists rupture and stays dome shaped."},{"term":"Direct immunofluorescence","def":"Staining of the patient's own skin biopsy with labeled anti-human immunoglobulin and anti-complement to show where antibody is deposited."},{"term":"Salt-split skin","def":"Normal skin separated in the lamina lucida with 1 M sodium chloride, used to place subepidermal antibody on the epidermal roof or the dermal floor."},{"term":"Desmoglein compensation","def":"The principle that a site blisters only when its dominant desmoglein is lost, because the other desmoglein can carry adhesion where both are expressed."},{"term":"Epidermal transglutaminase (TG3)","def":"A keratinocyte enzyme that cross-links proteins during cornification and is the IgA target in dermatitis herpetiformis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-04-C01","obj":"IN-13-D.1","title":"Adhesion architecture of the skin and the types of epidermolysis bullosa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Skin resists shear through two sets of junctions arranged in series. Keratinocytes bind to one another at desmosomes, whose transmembrane cadherins are desmoglein 1 and desmoglein 3 linked inside the cell to plakoglobin, plakophilin, desmoplakin, and the keratin intermediate filament network. Basal keratinocytes bind downward to the basement membrane at hemidesmosomes, built on integrin alpha-6 beta-4 with the transmembrane protein BP180 (also called collagen XVII) and the intracellular plakin BP230. Beneath the basal cell lies the lamina lucida, crossed by laminin 332, then the lamina densa of type IV collagen, and finally the anchoring fibrils of type VII collagen looping into the papillary dermis."},{"type":"p","text":"Because these elements sit at fixed depths, the identity of the broken protein fixes the level of the split, and the level of the split fixes the clinical appearance. A split high in the epidermis leaves a thin roof that tears almost immediately, so the lesion is a flaccid blister or a bare erosion. A split beneath the whole epidermis leaves a full-thickness roof and a tense dome. A split at or below the lamina densa injures dermal matrix, so healing proceeds through fibrosis and leaves scars, milia, and contractures."},{"type":"flow","title":"From broken protein to clinical lesion","steps":["Adhesion protein absent (mutation) or blocked (autoantibody)","Junction at that protein's depth fails under shear","Fluid enters the plane of separation","Split level sets blister tension and scarring risk","Clinical lesion and histologic split level match"],"caption":"The reasoning chain used for every disease in this chapter, in both the inherited and the autoimmune groups."},{"type":"p","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa is a group of inherited diseases in which one of these proteins is absent or defective from birth. In simplex disease the keratin 5 and keratin 14 filament scaffold of basal keratinocytes collapses under friction and those cells cytolyse, so the split is intraepidermal and healing is usually scar free. In junctional disease, loss of laminin 332 or of collagen XVII removes the link that crosses the lamina lucida, so the skin separates in that layer. In dystrophic disease, reduced or absent type VII collagen leaves no anchoring fibrils, so the split falls below the lamina densa within the uppermost dermis and every blister is a dermal wound."},{"type":"table","title":"Major types of epidermolysis bullosa","cols":["Type","Defective protein (gene)","Structure lost","Split level","Usual inheritance"],"rows":[["Simplex","Keratin 5 or keratin 14 (KRT5, KRT14)","Intermediate filament scaffold of basal cells","Within basal keratinocytes (intraepidermal)","Autosomal dominant"],["Junctional","Laminin 332 or collagen XVII (LAMA3, LAMB3, LAMC2, COL17A1)","Hemidesmosome anchor across the lamina lucida","Lamina lucida","Autosomal recessive"],["Dystrophic","Type VII collagen (COL7A1)","Anchoring fibrils into the papillary dermis","Below the lamina densa (sublamina densa)","Dominant or recessive"]],"caption":"The split descends as the defective protein sits deeper, which is why scarring is a feature of dystrophic disease and not of simplex disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Two points guard against common errors. First, a split within basal cells is still intraepidermal, so simplex disease is not a subepidermal blistering disease despite being so fragile. Second, type VII collagen is the shared antigen of dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa and of epidermolysis bullosa acquisita; the first is a mutation in the gene, the second an IgG autoantibody against the protein in an adult, which is why the acquired form has no family history and shows immunoglobulin on biopsy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has widespread blisters, and electron microscopy places the plane of separation in the lamina lucida. Which proteins are candidates, and would you expect scarring?","explain":"Laminin 332 and collagen XVII cross the lamina lucida, so either can be defective. The split lies above the lamina densa, so dermal matrix is largely spared and scarring is less prominent than in dystrophic disease, although erosions in severe generalized junctional disease still carry high infant mortality.","check":"Compare the split level column of the epidermolysis bullosa table with the layers described in the first paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-04-C02","obj":"IN-13-D.2","title":"Clinical course of epidermolysis bullosa and the consequences of dermal splitting","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The defect is present in every keratinocyte from conception, so disease appears with the first mechanical stress: blisters and erosions at birth or in early infancy, at sites of handling, rubbing of clothing, crawling, or the pressure of nursing. The distribution follows friction rather than any immunologic pattern, favoring hands, feet, knees, elbows, and the occiput, and new lesions can be produced deliberately by rubbing. Because the same proteins are expressed in all stratified squamous epithelium and in nail and tooth structures, mucosal erosions, nail dystrophy or loss, and enamel defects accompany the skin disease in the more severe forms."},{"type":"p","text":"Severity tracks the split level and the amount of residual protein. In simplex disease, blisters are localized or generalized but intraepidermal, so re-epithelialization from intact basal cells and adnexa is efficient and scarring is unusual; localized disease in particular tends to become less troublesome after childhood. In severe generalized junctional disease, loss of laminin 332 leaves extensive nonhealing erosions, exuberant granulation tissue around the nares and mouth, laryngeal and tracheal involvement, protein and fluid loss, failure to thrive, and sepsis, which together account for death in infancy in the most severe cases."},{"type":"p","text":"Recessive dystrophic disease is where the consequences of splitting below the lamina densa become visible over years. Each blister is a dermal wound, and repeated wounding in the absence of anchoring fibrils heals by fibrosis. Scar contracture fuses the fingers into a mitten deformity (pseudosyndactyly) and flexes the joints, nails are lost, and milia appear where sweat ducts and follicles are occluded by scar. Mucosal involvement scars as well, producing esophageal strictures with dysphagia and secondary malnutrition, microstomia, and ankyloglossia, while corneal erosions scar the eye. Anemia, growth failure, and dilated cardiomyopathy follow from chronic inflammation and nutritional deficiency."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why recessive dystrophic disease ends in carcinoma","steps":["Absent type VII collagen, sublamina densa split","Repeated blistering wounds the papillary dermis","Chronic nonhealing wounds with fibrosis and inflammation","Sustained keratinocyte proliferation and genomic damage","Aggressive, often metastatic squamous cell carcinoma in young adults"],"caption":"Carcinoma arises in chronic wounds rather than at random, which is why risk rises steeply from the second and third decades."},{"type":"p","text":"The squamous cell carcinoma of recessive dystrophic disease behaves unlike the sun-induced squamous cell carcinoma of elderly skin. It arises in chronically wounded, scarred, usually sun-protected sites on the hands, arms, and legs, often before age 30, and metastasizes early despite being well differentiated histologically, so it is the leading cause of death in these patients by middle adulthood. Dominant dystrophic disease is milder, with blistering and nail dystrophy over bony prominences, albopapuloid lesions (pale firm papules over the trunk), and much less risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical course by type","cols":["Feature","Simplex","Junctional (severe generalized)","Recessive dystrophic"],"rows":[["Onset","Birth to infancy, friction sites","Birth, widespread","Birth, widespread"],["Scarring and milia","Absent or minimal","Variable, atrophic healing","Prominent"],["Mucosa and nails","Mild or spared","Oral, airway, enamel defects","Oral and esophageal strictures, nail loss"],["Hand deformity","No","Uncommon","Pseudosyndactyly (mitten hands)"],["Major cause of death","None attributable","Sepsis and failure to thrive in infancy","Metastatic squamous cell carcinoma in young adults"]],"caption":"Course, not initial appearance, separates the types at the bedside; all three blister from minor trauma at birth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-04-C03","obj":"IN-13-D.3","title":"Bullous pemphigoid: hemidesmosomal antigens, complement, and the tense subepidermal blister","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bullous pemphigoid is the most common autoimmune subepidermal blistering disease and a disease of later life, with most patients older than about 60 years and incidence rising steeply with age. The autoantibodies are IgG against two hemidesmosomal proteins: BP180 (collagen XVII), a transmembrane protein whose extracellular NC16A domain is the dominant pathogenic epitope, and BP230, an intracellular plakin that links the hemidesmosome plaque to keratin filaments. Antibody against the NC16A domain of BP180 correlates with disease activity, which fits the fact that only this epitope is accessible to circulating antibody from outside the cell."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Pathogenesis of bullous pemphigoid","steps":["IgG binds the NC16A domain of BP180 at the hemidesmosome","Classical complement activation deposits C3 along the basement membrane","C5a and mast cell degranulation recruit eosinophils and neutrophils","Granulocyte elastase, gelatinase B, and tryptase degrade BP180 and matrix","Basal cells detach from the basement membrane, producing a subepidermal split","Exposed antigen and continued inflammation sustain further antibody binding"],"caption":"An antibody plus complement plus granulocyte protease mechanism, which is why eosinophils are conspicuous in the blister and the dermal infiltrate."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical picture follows directly from the depth of this split. Separation occurs beneath the entire epidermis, so the roof of the blister is full thickness and mechanically strong: bullae are tense, dome shaped, up to several centimeters, and persist for days before eroding, and lateral pressure on normal-looking adjacent skin does not shear the epidermis, so the Nikolsky sign is negative. Lesions favor the flexures, lower abdomen, inner thighs, groin, and forearms, and are intensely pruritic. Many patients pass through a prodromal phase of weeks to months with urticarial plaques, eczematous patches, or pruritus alone before any blister forms, which is explained by inflammation at the junction preceding frank separation. Oral involvement occurs in a minority, roughly 10 to 20 percent of patients, and when it does the erosions are limited and rarely the presenting complaint. Healing is without scarring, because the split lies above the dermal matrix, although postinflammatory pigment change is common."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on three levels of evidence that each reflect the mechanism. Light microscopy of a fresh blister shows a subepidermal blister with an intact epidermal roof and no acantholysis, with eosinophils in the blister cavity and a perivascular dermal infiltrate rich in eosinophils. Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin shows a smooth linear band of IgG and C3 along the basement membrane zone, the morphologic signature of antibody bound to a single continuous structure. On salt-split normal skin the patient's serum labels the epidermal roof, because BP180 and BP230 lie above the artificial plane of cleavage in the lamina lucida; this separates bullous pemphigoid from epidermolysis bullosa acquisita and from anti-laminin 332 disease, whose antigens lie below that plane and label the dermal floor."},{"type":"p","text":"Two variants reuse the same antigen and are worth recognizing. Pemphigoid gestationis presents with periumbilical urticarial lesions and blisters in the second or third trimester, driven by anti-BP180 generated against placental collagen XVII. Mucous membrane pemphigoid targets BP180 or laminin 332 and produces scarring erosions of oral, ocular, and genital mucosa, with conjunctival fibrosis and symblepharon; mucosal healing proceeds by fibrosis of the lamina propria, so scarring is the rule at these sites even though the split level is the same."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-04-C04","obj":"IN-13-D.4","title":"Pemphigus vulgaris: desmoglein autoantibodies, acantholysis, and the tombstone basal layer","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pemphigus vulgaris is a type II hypersensitivity disease in which IgG, predominantly the IgG4 subclass, binds the extracellular domain of desmoglein 3, frequently with additional antibody against desmoglein 1. The antibody is pathogenic by itself: it does not need complement or granulocytes, and neonatal disease from transplacental IgG, which resolves as maternal antibody clears, demonstrates the point. Bound antibody interferes with desmoglein trans-interaction directly and also triggers signaling, including p38 mitogen-activated protein kinase activation, that causes keratin filament retraction and internalization of desmoglein from the cell surface. Adhesion between keratinocytes therefore fails, the cells round up and float apart, and this separation of cell from cell within the epidermis is acantholysis. The disease associates with the HLA class II alleles DRB1*04:02 and DQB1*05:03, and onset is typically in the fourth to sixth decades."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pemphigus vulgaris from antibody to erosion","steps":["IgG4 against desmoglein 3, often also desmoglein 1","Steric blockade plus p38 MAPK signaling and desmoglein internalization","Desmosomes disassemble; keratinocytes separate (acantholysis)","Suprabasal split; hemidesmosomes keep basal cells on the membrane","Thin-roofed flaccid bulla ruptures into a painful erosion"],"caption":"No complement step is required, which distinguishes this mechanism from that of bullous pemphigoid."},{"type":"p","text":"The split sits immediately above the basal layer because desmoglein 3 is expressed most heavily in the lower epidermis, while the basal cells remain anchored downward by hemidesmosomes, which the antibody does not touch. Histology therefore shows a suprabasal blister whose floor is a single row of basal keratinocytes still attached to the basement membrane but separated from one another, each left standing on its narrow base: the row of tombstones. Rounded acantholytic keratinocytes float in the cavity, and a cytologic smear from the base of a fresh lesion (Tzanck preparation) shows these cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically, mucosal surfaces declare the disease first in the great majority of patients. Oral erosions, painful enough to limit eating, precede skin lesions by weeks to months, and because mucosa depends on desmoglein 3 and blister roofs there are rubbed off at once, intact blisters are almost never seen in the mouth. Skin lesions are flaccid, easily ruptured bullae on the scalp, face, trunk, axillae, and groin that leave weeping, crusted, slow-healing erosions. The Nikolsky sign is positive: lateral pressure on uninvolved skin shears the epidermis, since the acantholytic plane is present in skin that still looks normal. Pressure on an intact bulla spreads it into adjacent skin (the Asboe-Hansen sign). Extensive erosion causes fluid and protein loss and allows bacterial invasion, which made untreated disease highly lethal before immunosuppression was available."},{"type":"p","text":"Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin shows IgG, often with C3, outlining the surface of every keratinocyte through the epidermis, producing the net-like or fishnet pattern; the antibody is deposited on cell membranes rather than along one line, which is exactly what the net pattern means. Indirect immunofluorescence on monkey esophagus substrate detects circulating antibody, and titers track activity. Paraneoplastic pemphigus is the exception to remember: antibodies against plakins (envoplakin and periplakin) as well as desmogleins, severe intractable stomatitis, lichenoid skin lesions, and sometimes bronchiolitis obliterans, occurring with Castleman disease or non-Hodgkin lymphoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If antibody bound only desmoglein 1 and spared desmoglein 3, where would the split fall and would the mouth be involved?","explain":"Desmoglein 1 is concentrated in the superficial epidermis and is not the dominant desmoglein in mucosa, so the split would be subcorneal and the mouth spared. That is pemphigus foliaceus, covered in the next section.","check":"Compare the desmoglein distribution stated here with the comparison table in the following section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-04-C05","obj":"IN-13-D.5","title":"Distinguishing pemphigus vulgaris, pemphigus foliaceus, and bullous pemphigoid","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"All three diseases are IgG mediated, and all three can present with erosions in an adult, so they are separated by one question asked four ways: which protein is the target, and therefore at what depth does the skin split, how tense is the blister, is mucosa involved, and what shape does the deposited antibody make. Desmoglein compensation explains the mucosal column. Mucosa expresses desmoglein 3 with little desmoglein 1, so losing desmoglein 3 blisters the mouth; the superficial epidermis expresses mainly desmoglein 1, so losing desmoglein 1 blisters superficial skin but leaves mucosa adequately held by its abundant desmoglein 3."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparison of the three IgG-mediated blistering diseases","cols":["Feature","Pemphigus vulgaris","Pemphigus foliaceus","Bullous pemphigoid"],"rows":[["Target antigen","Desmoglein 3, often also desmoglein 1","Desmoglein 1 only","BP180 (collagen XVII) and BP230"],["Structure attacked","Desmosome, lower epidermis and mucosa","Desmosome, superficial epidermis","Hemidesmosome at the dermal-epidermal junction"],["Split level","Suprabasal, intraepidermal, tombstone basal row","Subcorneal, at or just below the granular layer","Subepidermal, full-thickness epidermal roof"],["Blister character","Flaccid, ruptures early","Rarely intact; scaly crusted erosions","Tense, dome shaped, persists"],["Nikolsky sign","Positive","Positive","Negative"],["Mucosal involvement","Painful oral erosions, usually first","Spared","Infrequent and limited (about 10 to 20 percent)"],["Typical age and sites","Fourth to sixth decades; scalp, face, trunk, flexures","Middle age; seborrheic areas of face, scalp, upper trunk","Usually over 60 years; flexures, abdomen, thighs"],["Histologic infiltrate","Acantholytic cells, sparse inflammation","Acantholytic cells superficially, sparse inflammation","Eosinophils in blister and dermis, no acantholysis"],["Direct immunofluorescence","Net-like intercellular IgG through the epidermis","Intercellular IgG, strongest superficially","Linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane"]],"caption":"Target antigen determines every other row; the immunofluorescence shape simply reports whether antibody lies around cells or along one line."},{"type":"p","text":"Pemphigus foliaceus deserves its own description, since its appearance misleads. The split is so superficial that intact blisters are seldom seen; patients present with scaly, crusted, sometimes exfoliative erosions in a seborrheic distribution on the face, scalp, and upper trunk, and the disease can be mistaken for seborrheic dermatitis or widespread impetigo. There is no oral disease. An endemic form, fogo selvagem, occurs in clusters in rural Brazil, with anti-desmoglein 1 antibody arising after insect bite exposure, which illustrates environmental triggering of autoimmunity."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two subcorneal splits with different causes","text":"A three-year-old has fever and diffuse tender erythema, then superficial sheets of skin peel with gentle pressure. A forty-five-year-old has months of scaly crusted erosions on the face and upper back with the same subcorneal split on biopsy. Both lose desmoglein 1, so both split at the same level and both shear under pressure. In the child, staphylococcal exfoliative toxin A or B is a protease that cleaves desmoglein 1 directly, so onset is acute, there is systemic toxicity, and direct immunofluorescence is negative. In the adult, IgG binds desmoglein 1, so the course is chronic and immunofluorescence shows intercellular IgG. Same protein, same plane, different agent: enzyme versus antibody."},{"type":"p","text":"Two further distinctions avoid errors. A positive Nikolsky sign is not specific to pemphigus; it is also positive in staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome and in Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, where keratinocyte necrosis rather than acantholysis destroys cohesion, and immunofluorescence there is negative. Among the subepidermal diseases, immunofluorescence shape separates the group: linear IgG and C3 indicates bullous pemphigoid or epidermolysis bullosa acquisita (separated by salt-split skin, epidermal roof versus dermal floor), linear IgA alone indicates linear IgA bullous dermatosis, and granular IgA in the papillae indicates dermatitis herpetiformis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-04-C06","obj":"IN-13-D.6","title":"Dermatitis herpetiformis: IgA against epidermal transglutaminase and the celiac connection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dermatitis herpetiformis is the skin expression of gluten-sensitive enteropathy. In people carrying HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8, deamidated gliadin peptides presented by those class II molecules drive a T cell dependent IgA response against tissue transglutaminase (TG2) in the intestine. The response broadens to the closely homologous epidermal transglutaminase (TG3), a keratinocyte enzyme that cross-links proteins during cornification, and high-avidity IgA against TG3 is the serologic marker of the skin disease. Nearly all patients have celiac-type changes on small-bowel biopsy, ranging from an increase in intraepithelial lymphocytes to villous atrophy, yet most have no bowel symptoms, which is why the rash can be the only evidence of celiac disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"Gut to skin in dermatitis herpetiformis","steps":["Gluten peptides deamidated by tissue transglutaminase in the gut","HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 presentation drives IgA against TG2, then TG3","Circulating IgA and TG3 complexes deposit in dermal papillary tips","Neutrophils recruited, forming papillary microabscesses","Protease release splits the epidermis at the papillary tips","Grouped subepidermal vesicles with intense pruritus"],"caption":"A single dietary antigen in the gut produces lesions in the skin through a shared enzyme family."},{"type":"p","text":"The eruption is intensely pruritic and burning, often enough that excoriations and crusts are all that remain by the time the patient is seen. Lesions are small, grouped (herpetiform) vesicles and urticarial papules distributed symmetrically over extensor surfaces: elbows, knees, buttocks, shoulders, sacrum, and the posterior scalp. Mucosal lesions are rare. The disease is chronic and relapsing, more common in adults of northern European ancestry, and iodine exposure can provoke a flare. Associated autoimmune disease, especially autoimmune thyroid disease, and the small-bowel lymphoma risk of celiac disease both travel with it."},{"type":"p","text":"Histology and immunofluorescence follow the deposition pattern. Early lesional skin shows collections of neutrophils with fibrin at the tips of the dermal papillae, the papillary microabscesses, with vacuolar change and then small subepidermal vesicles as the papillary tips separate; eosinophils may be present but are not the dominant cell, which distinguishes this from bullous pemphigoid. Direct immunofluorescence of normal-appearing perilesional skin, the preferred biopsy site, shows granular IgA in the dermal papillae, sometimes with C3. The deposits are granular rather than linear because IgA sits in clumps within the papillary tips instead of along a continuous basement membrane, and they persist for months after gluten is withdrawn, then fade, confirming that dietary antigen sustains the process."},{"type":"table","title":"Subepidermal blistering diseases by immunofluorescence pattern","cols":["Disease","Immunoreactant and pattern","Antigen","Dominant infiltrate"],"rows":[["Bullous pemphigoid","Linear IgG and C3 at the basement membrane","BP180 and BP230","Eosinophils"],["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Granular IgA in dermal papillae","Epidermal transglutaminase (TG3)","Neutrophils at papillary tips"],["Linear IgA bullous dermatosis","Linear IgA at the basement membrane","LAD-1, a shed fragment of BP180","Neutrophils in a line along the junction"],["Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Linear IgG, dermal floor on salt-split skin","Type VII collagen","Variable, often neutrophils"]],"caption":"Antibody class and the shape of the deposit separate four diseases that all split beneath the epidermis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has itchy grouped vesicles on the elbows and knees, and direct immunofluorescence shows linear IgA along the basement membrane rather than granular IgA in the papillae. Is this dermatitis herpetiformis?","explain":"No. Linear IgA indicates linear IgA bullous dermatosis, whose antigen is the LAD-1 fragment of BP180 and which has no gluten relationship; several cases are drug associated. The distribution can mimic dermatitis herpetiformis, so the immunofluorescence pattern, not the rash, decides.","check":"Compare the rows for dermatitis herpetiformis and linear IgA bullous dermatosis in the table above."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"This chapter is histology turned into pathology. Each junction described in normal skin, the desmosome with its desmogleins, the hemidesmosome with BP180 and BP230, the laminin 332 of the lamina lucida, and the type VII collagen anchoring fibrils, is the target of a specific disease, and the depth of the target predicts blister tension, the Nikolsky sign, scarring, and the mucosal pattern. Genetics and immunology reach the same proteins by different routes: a mutation removes the protein from birth and gives a lifelong mechanical fragility, while an autoantibody blocks it in adulthood and gives an inflammatory disease with deposited immunoglobulin on biopsy. Type VII collagen illustrates both routes in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa and epidermolysis bullosa acquisita, and desmoglein 1 illustrates a third, enzymatic route when staphylococcal exfoliative toxin cleaves it in scalded skin syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"The immunologic mechanisms are themselves worth contrasting, because they explain the histology. Pemphigus is antibody acting alone, a type II reaction in which binding and signaling disassemble a junction, so the infiltrate is sparse and acantholytic cells dominate. Bullous pemphigoid adds complement and granulocyte proteases, so eosinophils fill the blister. Dermatitis herpetiformis works through immune complexes of IgA and transglutaminase that recruit neutrophils, which is why its lesion begins as a papillary microabscess and why the deposits depend on continued exposure to a dietary antigen rather than on anything local to the skin. The biomedical limits are worth stating: immunofluorescence localizes antibody but does not prove it is pathogenic, antibody titers correlate imperfectly with activity, and reported frequencies such as mucosal involvement in bullous pemphigoid vary between series."}],"summary":["The depth of the defective adhesion protein sets the split level, and the split level sets blister tension, scarring, and the Nikolsky sign.","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex breaks keratins 5 and 14 within basal cells, junctional disease breaks laminin 332 or collagen XVII in the lamina lucida, and dystrophic disease breaks type VII collagen below the lamina densa.","Epidermolysis bullosa blisters from minor friction at birth, with nail, enamel, and mucosal involvement in severe forms; recessive dystrophic disease scars into mitten hands and esophageal strictures and leads to aggressive squamous cell carcinoma in young adults.","Bullous pemphigoid is IgG against BP180 (collagen XVII) and BP230 with complement activation and eosinophil recruitment, giving tense subepidermal bullae in older adults, a negative Nikolsky sign, and linear IgG and C3 at the basement membrane.","Pemphigus vulgaris is IgG against desmoglein 3, often also desmoglein 1, acting without complement to cause suprabasal acantholysis with a tombstone basal row, flaccid bullae, early painful oral erosions, a positive Nikolsky sign, and net-like intercellular IgG.","Desmoglein compensation explains site: mucosa depends on desmoglein 3, superficial epidermis on desmoglein 1, so pemphigus foliaceus splits subcorneally and spares the mouth.","Salt-split skin separates linear IgG diseases: bullous pemphigoid labels the epidermal roof, epidermolysis bullosa acquisita the dermal floor.","Dermatitis herpetiformis is IgA against epidermal transglutaminase in gluten-sensitive enteropathy, with grouped pruritic vesicles on extensor surfaces, neutrophilic microabscesses at papillary tips, and granular IgA in the dermal papillae.","A positive Nikolsky sign also occurs in staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome and in Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, where immunofluorescence is negative.","Acantholysis means separation of keratinocyte from keratinocyte and is intraepidermal; subepidermal diseases show no acantholysis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, blistering (bullous) diseases"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin, epidermis and dermal-epidermal junction"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity, antibody-mediated (type II) diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Skin disease, immunologically mediated blistering disorders"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Organization of cells into tissues, cell junctions and the extracellular matrix"}]},"IN-13-E":{"unit":"IN-13-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-05","title":"Genetic Skin Conditions: Albinism and Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter takes two inherited skin disorders down to the molecule. In albinism the melanocytes are present in normal numbers but cannot finish making melanin, so the skin, hair, and eyes lack their pigment shield and the visual pathway does not develop normally. In Ehlers-Danlos syndrome the collagen fibril is built or processed incorrectly, so skin, joints, vessels, and hollow organs lose tensile strength. Board questions usually give a child or young adult with a physical finding and ask for the missing enzyme, the collagen type, the mode of inheritance, or the complication that follows. The reasoning runs from the defective protein to the tissue that depends on it.","prerequisites":["Epidermal layers and the location of melanocytes in the basal layer (IN-1)","Neural crest origin and migration of melanocytes (IN-2)","Dermal matrix composition: collagen, elastin, and ground substance (IN-1)","Basic Mendelian inheritance patterns and the idea of a dominant negative allele","Enzyme cofactor chemistry: copper, iron, vitamin C, and alpha-ketoglutarate dependent hydroxylation"],"connections":[{"to":"Pigmentation changes of the skin (IN-13-A)","how":"Albinism is a failure of melanin synthesis with melanocytes present, while vitiligo is loss of the melanocytes themselves, so the two sit at opposite ends of the same pigment pathway."},{"to":"Skin biochemistry and nutrition (IN-5)","how":"Vitamin C and copper deficiency strike the same hydroxylation and cross-linking steps that are mutated in kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, so scurvy and Menkes disease mimic parts of it."},{"to":"Malignant skin neoplasms (IN-13-G) and skin genetics (IN-7)","how":"Melanin absorbs ultraviolet photons and limits pyrimidine dimer formation, so the unpigmented epidermis of albinism accumulates the mutations that drive squamous and basal cell carcinoma early."},{"to":"Immune responses and infection in skin disease (IN-9, IN-11)","how":"Chediak-Higashi syndrome couples partial albinism to failed lysosomal delivery in neutrophils and cytotoxic lymphocytes, which explains its recurrent pyogenic infections."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal connective tissue disorders (MS-13-D) and wound healing (IN-10)","how":"Marfan syndrome and osteogenesis imperfecta share joint laxity with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome but differ in the defective protein, and scar strength in all of them depends on type I fibril assembly and cross-linking."}],"terms":[{"term":"Melanosome","def":"The lysosome-related organelle inside a melanocyte in which tyrosine is converted to melanin and from which pigment is transferred to keratinocytes."},{"term":"Tyrosinase","def":"The copper-containing melanosomal enzyme that hydroxylates tyrosine to DOPA and oxidizes DOPA to dopaquinone, the committed steps of melanin synthesis."},{"term":"Eumelanin and pheomelanin","def":"The brown to black and the red to yellow melanin polymers made downstream of dopaquinone, with eumelanin giving most of the ultraviolet protection."},{"term":"Foveal hypoplasia","def":"Incomplete development of the central retina, seen when retinal pigment epithelium melanin is absent, and the main reason acuity is low in albinism."},{"term":"Iris transillumination","def":"Light reflected from the fundus passing outward through an iris that lacks pigment, seen as a glowing iris on slit lamp retroillumination."},{"term":"Gly-X-Y repeat","def":"The repeating collagen sequence in which glycine occupies every third position because only glycine fits the crowded axis of the triple helix."},{"term":"Procollagen","def":"The secreted triple helical precursor with N and C propeptides still attached, which cannot assemble into fibrils until the propeptides are cleaved."},{"term":"Lysyl oxidase","def":"The copper-dependent extracellular enzyme that converts lysine to allysine so adjacent collagen molecules can form covalent cross-links."},{"term":"Hyperextensibility","def":"Skin that can be lifted well away from the underlying tissue and then snaps back, reflecting disordered dermal collagen fibrils with intact elastin."},{"term":"Cigarette-paper scar","def":"A thin, wide, atrophic, wrinkled scar produced when a healing wound cannot build normal tensile strength and stretches under skin tension."},{"term":"Molluscoid pseudotumor","def":"A soft fleshy nodule of piled scar tissue and fat over a repeatedly traumatized pressure point such as the knee or elbow."},{"term":"Beighton score","def":"A nine point bedside scale of joint hypermobility using the fifth fingers, thumbs, elbows, knees, and forward trunk flexion."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-05-C01","obj":"IN-13-E.1","title":"Oculocutaneous albinism: a synthetic block with melanocytes intact","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Melanocytes are neural crest derived cells that settle in the basal layer of the epidermis and in the hair bulb, where roughly one melanocyte serves about ten basal keratinocytes. Inside each melanocyte, melanin is built within melanosomes, lysosome-related organelles that import tyrosine and the enzymes that act on it. Tyrosinase, a copper-containing enzyme, hydroxylates tyrosine to DOPA and then oxidizes DOPA to dopaquinone; everything downstream of dopaquinone proceeds largely without enzymatic help toward eumelanin or pheomelanin. Mature melanosomes travel out along melanocyte dendrites and are handed to surrounding keratinocytes, where the pigment forms a supranuclear cap that intercepts ultraviolet photons before they reach DNA."},{"type":"flow","title":"Melanin synthesis and transfer","steps":["Tyrosine is taken into the melanosome of the melanocyte","Tyrosinase hydroxylates tyrosine to DOPA","Tyrosinase oxidizes DOPA to dopaquinone","Dopaquinone polymerizes to eumelanin or pheomelanin","Mature melanosomes move along dendrites to keratinocytes","Melanin caps keratinocyte nuclei and absorbs ultraviolet light"],"caption":"Albinism interrupts this chain at the enzyme or at melanosome function; the melanocytes themselves remain in place."},{"type":"p","text":"Oculocutaneous albinism is a group of autosomal recessive disorders of this pathway. The key teaching point is that melanocyte number and distribution are normal, and so is their position in the basal layer on biopsy; what fails is pigment production. In the classic form, OCA1, both copies of the tyrosinase gene are defective, so the committed step cannot run. A complete loss of activity gives white hair, milk-white skin, and blue-gray translucent irides that never darken, while partial activity allows some pigment to accumulate over the first years of life. Other forms lose melanosomal membrane proteins rather than the enzyme: OCA2 affects the P protein and is the form most often seen in populations of African ancestry, OCA3 affects tyrosinase-related protein 1 and often produces reddish-brown pigment, and OCA4 affects a melanosomal transporter."},{"type":"table","title":"Common forms of oculocutaneous albinism","cols":["Form","Defective product","Pigment pattern","Point to remember"],"rows":[["OCA1A","Tyrosinase, no residual activity","White hair and skin, no pigment accumulation with age","The textbook tyrosinase-negative case"],["OCA1B","Tyrosinase, residual activity","Pigment accumulates with age; a temperature sensitive variant pigments only cooler regions such as the limbs","Explains a child who darkens slightly over years"],["OCA2","P protein of the melanosomal membrane","Variable cream to light brown pigment, often pigmented nevi","Most common form worldwide and in African populations"],["OCA3","Tyrosinase-related protein 1","Reddish-brown skin and ginger hair","Reported mainly in African populations"],["OCA4","SLC45A2 melanosomal transporter","Resembles OCA2 clinically","Relatively frequent in Japanese populations"]],"caption":"All forms are autosomal recessive, and in all of them melanocyte numbers are normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Two sets of consequences follow. In the skin, melanin is the main physical filter for ultraviolet light and also quenches the radicals that ultraviolet exposure generates, so without it keratinocytes accumulate cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers and other photoproducts with every exposure. Sunburn occurs at trivial doses, solar elastosis and actinic keratoses appear in childhood or early adult life, and squamous cell and basal cell carcinoma arise decades earlier than expected; melanoma also occurs and is often amelanotic, which makes it harder to recognize. In the eye, melanin within the retinal pigment epithelium is required for normal central retinal development and for the correct routing of retinal ganglion cell axons at the optic chiasm. Its absence produces foveal hypoplasia with acuity commonly in the 20/100 to 20/400 range, infantile nystagmus, photophobia, iris transillumination, strabismus, and poor stereopsis from axonal misrouting."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has white hair and skin. Would you expect abnormal catecholamine levels, since both melanin and catecholamines start from tyrosine?","explain":"No. Catecholamine synthesis begins with tyrosine hydroxylase in neurons and chromaffin cells, a different enzyme in a different compartment. Tyrosinase works only inside melanosomes, so its loss removes pigment without touching dopamine, norepinephrine, or epinephrine.","check":"Compare the first paragraph of this section with the catecholamine pathway you learned in endocrine biochemistry."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-05-C02","obj":"IN-13-E.2","title":"Separating oculocutaneous albinism from ocular albinism and the trafficking disorders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypopigmentation has several mechanisms, and the exam separates them by asking which tissues are involved, how the trait is inherited, and whether melanocytes are present. Ocular albinism is X-linked and results from loss of GPR143, a melanosomal membrane protein needed chiefly in the eye and in the retinal pigment epithelium. Affected boys have the full ocular picture of nystagmus, low acuity, foveal hypoplasia, and iris transillumination with skin and hair pigment that falls within or close to the family range. Because X inactivation is mosaic, carrier mothers are usually asymptomatic but show patchy fundus pigmentation on examination, a finding that confirms the inheritance pattern when a pedigree is ambiguous."},{"type":"p","text":"Chediak-Higashi syndrome is autosomal recessive and caused by defects in LYST, a regulator of the fission and fusion of lysosomes and other lysosome-related organelles. The pigment defect is partial rather than complete: melanosomes clump into giant macromelanosomes that are poorly transferred, giving silvery hair, pale skin, and ocular findings milder than in oculocutaneous albinism."},{"type":"p","text":"The same trafficking failure produces the rest of the syndrome. Neutrophils and monocytes contain giant granules on a peripheral smear but cannot deliver them into the phagosome, so patients have recurrent pyogenic skin and respiratory infections, typically with Staphylococcus aureus and streptococci. Natural killer cells and cytotoxic T lymphocytes cannot release their lytic granules, which allows an accelerated phase resembling hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis. Platelet dense granules are deficient, so platelet aggregation is impaired and mucocutaneous bleeding occurs with a normal platelet count, and progressive peripheral neuropathy develops as abnormal organelles accumulate in long axons."},{"type":"table","title":"Disorders of reduced skin and ocular pigment","cols":["Disorder","Inheritance","Molecular defect","Melanocytes","Discriminating findings"],"rows":[["Oculocutaneous albinism","Autosomal recessive","Tyrosinase or a melanosomal protein","Normal number, pigment poor","Generalized pale skin and hair plus nystagmus and low acuity"],["Ocular albinism","X-linked","GPR143 melanosomal protein","Normal number","Ocular findings in boys, near normal skin, patchy fundus in carriers"],["Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Autosomal recessive","LYST organelle trafficking","Normal number, giant melanosomes","Silvery hair, giant neutrophil granules, pyogenic infections, neuropathy, bleeding"],["Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome","Autosomal recessive","Melanosome and granule biogenesis complexes","Normal number","Albinism with platelet storage pool bleeding, pulmonary fibrosis, granulomatous colitis"],["Vitiligo","Acquired autoimmune","T cell killing of melanocytes","Absent within patches","Sharply marginated depigmented macules on previously normal skin"],["Piebaldism","Autosomal dominant","KIT, failed melanoblast migration","Absent within patches","Stable white forelock and ventral patches present from birth"],["Phenylketonuria","Autosomal recessive","Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Normal number","Diffuse light pigment with intellectual disability and a musty odor"]],"caption":"Melanocytes present but unproductive points to a synthetic or trafficking defect; melanocytes absent points to destruction or failed migration."},{"type":"p","text":"Two entries on that table reward a second look. Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome belongs with Chediak-Higashi as a disorder of lysosome-related organelle biogenesis, and it is recognized by albinism plus a platelet storage pool defect with mucosal bleeding, with pulmonary fibrosis and granulomatous colitis developing in some subtypes. Phenylketonuria dilutes pigment for a biochemical reason worth naming: phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency blocks the conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, so tyrosine becomes a dietary requirement, and the accumulated phenylalanine also inhibits tyrosinase directly. The result is light hair and skin in a child whose dominant problem is neurological rather than cutaneous."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A boy has nystagmus and a translucent iris but hair and skin that match his siblings. His mother's fundus shows patchy pigment. Which diagnosis fits, and why is the skin spared?","explain":"X-linked ocular albinism. GPR143 is required mainly for melanosome maturation in the eye and retinal pigment epithelium, so cutaneous melanocytes pigment adequately. The mother's mottled fundus reflects random X inactivation in a carrier.","check":"Compare the inheritance and tissue columns of the pigment table in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-05-C03","obj":"IN-13-E.3","title":"Collagen synthesis and the molecular basis of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fibrillar collagen is a rope of three polypeptide chains wound into a right-handed triple helix. Each chain repeats the sequence glycine, X, Y, with proline or hydroxyproline common at X and Y, because glycine is the only residue small enough to pack along the crowded helical axis. Inside the cell, prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases add hydroxyl groups using molecular oxygen, alpha-ketoglutarate, ferrous iron, and vitamin C as the reducing agent that keeps the iron active. Hydroxyproline stabilizes the helix by hydrogen bonding, and hydroxylysine provides sites for sugars and for later covalent cross-links."},{"type":"flow","title":"Collagen assembly from gene to fiber","steps":["Pro-alpha chains with Gly-X-Y repeats are translated into the rough endoplasmic reticulum","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases hydroxylate residues, requiring vitamin C","Selected hydroxylysines are glycosylated","Three chains wind into a procollagen triple helix and are secreted","N and C procollagen peptidases cleave the propeptides to give tropocollagen","Tropocollagen self-assembles into staggered fibrils","Copper-dependent lysyl oxidase cross-links fibrils into mature fibers"],"caption":"Ehlers-Danlos subtypes, scurvy, Menkes disease, and osteogenesis imperfecta each interrupt a different step of this one pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is not one disease but a family of defects in collagen structure or processing, and the protein involved predicts which tissues fail. The classic type is autosomal dominant and usually follows mutations in COL5A1 or COL5A2, which encode type V collagen. Type V is quantitatively minor, but it nucleates type I fibrils and limits their diameter, so when it is reduced or abnormal the dermis fills with irregular, oversized, poorly organized fibrils. The skin stretches and the joints are lax while elastin remains normal, which is why the skin recoils rather than hanging loosely."},{"type":"p","text":"The vascular type, also autosomal dominant, arises from mutations in COL3A1. Type III collagen is a major fibrillar collagen of the media and adventitia of arteries, of bowel wall, of uterus, and of early granulation tissue, so these are the structures that tear. The kyphoscoliotic type is autosomal recessive and reflects deficiency of lysyl hydroxylase 1, encoded by PLOD1; underhydroxylated lysine cannot form stable cross-links, so collagen in sclera, ligament, and paraspinal tissue stays weak."},{"type":"table","title":"Where the pathway breaks and what results","cols":["Step","Protein or cofactor","Disorder when it fails","Tissue consequence"],"rows":[["Chain sequence and helix formation","Type I collagen chains","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Bone fragility, blue sclerae, hearing loss"],["Fibril nucleation and diameter control","Type V collagen","Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Hyperextensible fragile skin, lax joints, atrophic scars"],["Vessel and viscus fibril content","Type III collagen","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture"],["Lysine hydroxylation","Lysyl hydroxylase 1 (PLOD1)","Kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Scleral fragility, hypotonia, progressive scoliosis"],["Proline and lysine hydroxylation","Vitamin C","Scurvy","Perifollicular hemorrhage, bleeding gums, poor wound healing"],["Cross-linking","Copper-dependent lysyl oxidase","Menkes disease","Kinky hair, arterial tortuosity, hypopigmentation"]],"caption":"Nutritional and genetic lesions converge on the same steps, which is why scurvy and Menkes disease resemble parts of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the subtype from one laboratory clue","text":"A boy has severe hypotonia at birth, progressive scoliosis, hyperextensible skin, and a scleral rupture after minor trauma. Urine collagen cross-link analysis shows a high ratio of lysyl pyridinoline to hydroxylysyl pyridinoline. Work backward: the cross-links present are the ones made from lysine that was never hydroxylated, so the missing activity is lysyl hydroxylase 1, the product of PLOD1. That places the case in the kyphoscoliotic type, explains why both parents are unaffected carriers in an autosomal recessive trait, and predicts fragile sclera and ligament rather than the arterial rupture of the type III defect."},{"type":"p","text":"Two further subtypes are worth keeping straight. The hypermobile type is the most common clinically and is recognized by joint hypermobility, soft skin, and chronic pain with no consistently identified gene, so a question that gives hypermobility and no molecular clue usually points here. The arthrochalasia type removes the N-propeptide cleavage site of type I collagen and presents with congenital bilateral hip dislocation and extreme laxity, which shows that one gene gives a joint phenotype or a fracture phenotype depending on the domain affected."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-05-C04","obj":"IN-13-E.4","title":"Skin, joint, and vascular findings of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cutaneous examination follows directly from disorganized dermal fibrils. Skin feels velvety and doughy and lifts several centimeters off the forearm or neck, then snaps back cleanly because elastin is intact; this is hyperextensibility rather than the loose redundant skin of an elastin disorder. The dermis tears under shear that normal skin tolerates, so minor trauma produces gaping wounds and bruising appears after contact the patient cannot recall. Scar tensile strength depends on new type I fibrils being assembled and cross-linked, so healing is slow and scars widen under resting skin tension into thin, wrinkled, atrophic cigarette-paper plaques. Over repeatedly injured pressure points, piled scar tissue and fat form soft molluscoid pseudotumors, and mobile calcified subcutaneous spheroids may be felt along the shins and forearms."},{"type":"p","text":"Joints are hypermobile because ligament and capsule collagen is weak, and the degree is graded with the Beighton score. Recurrent subluxation and dislocation of shoulders, patellae, and digits, chronic joint and muscle pain, flat feet, early osteoarthritis from repeated microtrauma, and gingival and periodontal fragility are all common. Mitral valve prolapse appears more often than in the general population because valve leaflets also depend on fibrillar collagen."},{"type":"p","text":"The vascular type differs in both the character of the skin and the organs at risk. Rather than markedly hyperextensible skin, these patients have thin translucent skin over the chest and abdomen with an unusually visible venous pattern, aged-looking hands with prominent tendons, called acrogeria, and a characteristic face with thin lips, a pinched nose, hollow cheeks, and prominent eyes. The mechanical problem is that type III collagen bears much of the circumferential load in the media and adventitia of medium caliber arteries and in the walls of bowel and uterus. Wall stress equals transmural pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so a wall that cannot carry that stress dissects or ruptures without any preceding aneurysm, often in the second to fourth decade. The characteristic events are rupture or dissection of mesenteric, splenic, renal, or iliac arteries, sigmoid perforation without diverticulitis, uterine rupture at or near delivery, carotid-cavernous fistula, and intracranial berry aneurysms."},{"type":"flow","title":"From a COL3A1 mutation to arterial rupture","steps":["Abnormal or reduced type III collagen chains are made","Media and adventitia of medium arteries lose tensile reinforcement","Wall stress from pressure and radius exceeds wall strength","Intimal tear or full thickness medial disruption occurs","Dissection or free rupture with hemorrhage into a body cavity"],"caption":"Rupture can occur in a vessel of normal caliber, so arterial dilation need not precede it."},{"type":"table","title":"Skin and systemic findings by Ehlers-Danlos subtype","cols":["Subtype","Inheritance","Skin","Joints","Systemic risk"],"rows":[["Classic (COL5A1, COL5A2)","Autosomal dominant","Markedly hyperextensible, soft, fragile; wide atrophic scars; molluscoid pseudotumors","Generalized hypermobility with dislocations","Easy bruising, mitral valve prolapse, hernia"],["Hypermobile","Autosomal dominant, gene usually not identified","Soft and mildly stretchy, scars near normal","Hypermobility with chronic pain and instability","Functional gastrointestinal complaints and orthostatic intolerance"],["Vascular (COL3A1)","Autosomal dominant","Thin and translucent with visible veins, acrogeria, bruising","Hypermobility often limited to digits","Arterial dissection and rupture, sigmoid perforation, uterine rupture, berry aneurysm"],["Kyphoscoliotic (PLOD1)","Autosomal recessive","Hyperextensible and fragile","Marked laxity with congenital hypotonia","Progressive scoliosis, scleral fragility and globe rupture, restrictive lung disease"],["Dermatosparaxis (ADAMTS2)","Autosomal recessive","Extremely fragile, sagging, redundant skin","Laxity","Severe bruising, short limbs, delayed closure of fontanelles"]],"caption":"The subtype is reached by matching the skin texture and the organ at risk, not by hypermobility alone."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two young adults both have hypermobile fingers and easy bruising. One has velvety skin that lifts far from the forearm with wide scars on both knees; the other has thin skin with visible chest veins and no unusual scars. Which one carries the risk of arterial rupture, and which collagen explains it?","explain":"The second. Thin translucent skin with a visible venous pattern and relatively unremarkable scarring points to a COL3A1 defect, and type III collagen is the collagen that reinforces arterial media, bowel wall, and uterus. The first picture, dominated by stretchy skin and wide atrophic scars, fits the classic type V defect, whose consequences are cutaneous and articular.","check":"Compare the skin column with the systemic risk column of the subtype table above."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-05-C05","obj":"IN-13-E.5","title":"Distinguishing Ehlers-Danlos syndrome from Marfan syndrome and osteogenesis imperfecta","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"All three disorders can present with lax joints and a family history, so they are separated by the defective protein and by the organ that fails first. Marfan syndrome is autosomal dominant and follows mutations in FBN1, which encodes fibrillin-1, the scaffold protein of elastic microfibrils. Fibrillin also binds latent transforming growth factor beta and keeps it sequestered, so when fibrillin is abnormal excess signaling by that growth factor degrades the aortic media, and the aorta dilates at the root and may dissect. The skeleton is tall and slender with arm span exceeding height, a reduced upper to lower segment ratio, arachnodactyly, pectus deformity, scoliosis, and joint laxity. The lens subluxates upward because the zonular fibers are rich in fibrillin, and mitral valve prolapse, dural ectasia, and spontaneous pneumothorax complete the picture. The skin shows striae but is neither hyperextensible nor fragile."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteogenesis imperfecta is most often autosomal dominant and arises from mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2. A mutation that produces an abnormal chain acts in a dominant negative way, spoiling every helix that contains it, which is why structural mutations are generally more severe than those that merely halve the amount of normal collagen. Because type I collagen dominates bone matrix, dentin, sclera, and the ossicular chain, the phenotype is recurrent fractures with minimal trauma, bowing and short stature, blue or gray sclerae from a thin sclera that lets choroidal pigment show through, conductive or mixed hearing loss, and opalescent fragile teeth. Joint laxity occurs, but fractures dominate the history."},{"type":"table","title":"Four heritable connective tissue disorders compared","cols":["Disorder","Defective protein and gene","Inheritance","Skin and joints","Decisive findings elsewhere"],"rows":[["Ehlers-Danlos, classic","Type V collagen (COL5A1, COL5A2)","Autosomal dominant","Hyperextensible fragile skin, wide atrophic scars, hypermobility","Easy bruising, hernia, mitral valve prolapse"],["Ehlers-Danlos, vascular","Type III collagen (COL3A1)","Autosomal dominant","Thin translucent skin, bruising, digital hypermobility","Arterial and bowel rupture, uterine rupture, berry aneurysm"],["Marfan syndrome","Fibrillin-1 (FBN1)","Autosomal dominant","Striae and joint laxity, skin not fragile","Aortic root dilation and dissection, upward lens subluxation, tall slender build"],["Osteogenesis imperfecta","Type I collagen (COL1A1, COL1A2)","Usually autosomal dominant","Joint laxity, thin skin","Recurrent fractures, blue sclerae, hearing loss, opalescent teeth"],["Homocystinuria","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Autosomal recessive","Marfanoid build with joint laxity","Downward lens subluxation, thrombosis, intellectual disability, high homocysteine"]],"caption":"Homocystinuria is included because its marfanoid habitus is a standard distractor; the direction of lens displacement and the inheritance pattern separate it."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: one finding at a time","text":"A 19 year old is 193 cm tall with long fingers, lax joints, and a dislocated lens. First ask the direction of lens displacement. Upward points to fibrillin-1 and Marfan syndrome, so the aortic root is the organ at risk. Downward points to homocystinuria, an autosomal recessive enzyme defect with thromboembolic rather than aneurysmal risk, confirmed biochemically by elevated plasma homocysteine. If instead the lens is normal but the skin lifts far off the forearm and old scars are wide and papery, the answer moves to classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. If the history is of several fractures in childhood with gray-blue sclerae, it moves to osteogenesis imperfecta."},{"type":"p","text":"A practical sequence helps with a mixed picture. Ask first whether bones fracture, which isolates osteogenesis imperfecta. Ask next whether the aorta dilates and the lens is displaced, which isolates Marfan syndrome and its mimics. Ask then whether the skin itself is abnormal, stretchy and fragile with atrophic scars or thin and translucent, which isolates Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and names the subtype. Two caveats keep the reasoning honest: aortic root dilation also occurs in some Ehlers-Danlos and related conditions, so it is suggestive rather than diagnostic, and joint hypermobility alone is far too common to decide anything."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These two disorders show how one molecular lesion reaches several disciplines at once. In albinism, embryology supplies neural crest derived melanocytes that migrate normally, histology confirms them present in the basal layer, biochemistry names the copper-dependent tyrosinase step that fails, neuroscience explains why absent retinal pigment epithelium melanin leaves a hypoplastic fovea and misrouted chiasmal axons, and pathology supplies the ultraviolet mutagenesis that brings keratinocyte carcinoma forward by decades. The trafficking disorders add immunology: when LYST fails, the same organelle defect that clumps melanosomes also keeps neutrophil granules out of phagosomes and lytic granules inside cytotoxic lymphocytes, so partial albinism travels with pyogenic infection and an uncontrolled inflammatory phase."},{"type":"p","text":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome makes the same point along the collagen pathway. Biochemistry gives the steps, nutrition gives the cofactors so that vitamin C and copper deficiency reproduce parts of the phenotype, anatomy explains why type III collagen loss endangers arteries, sigmoid colon, and uterus, and pathology of wound healing explains the widened atrophic scar. The limits are worth stating plainly. Subtype classification has been revised repeatedly and more than a dozen types are now recognized, genotype does not fully predict severity, the hypermobile type has no reliable molecular marker, and a mutation in the same gene can give different diseases depending on the domain affected, as type I collagen mutations produce either fractures or congenital hip dislocation. For board purposes, learn the four or five subtypes with a named protein and reason from the protein to the tissue."}],"summary":["In oculocutaneous albinism, melanocyte number is normal and pigment production fails; in vitiligo and piebaldism the melanocytes are absent from affected skin.","Tyrosinase, a copper-containing melanosomal enzyme, converts tyrosine to DOPA and DOPA to dopaquinone; catecholamine synthesis uses tyrosine hydroxylase instead and is unaffected.","Without melanin, ultraviolet light reaches keratinocyte DNA, so sunburn, actinic damage, and squamous and basal cell carcinoma appear decades early.","Retinal pigment epithelium melanin guides foveal development and chiasmal axon routing, which is why albinism causes foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus, low acuity, and poor stereopsis.","Ocular albinism is X-linked with near normal skin; Chediak-Higashi syndrome is an autosomal recessive LYST trafficking defect with partial albinism, giant granules, pyogenic infections, bleeding, and neuropathy.","Collagen requires glycine every third residue, vitamin C dependent hydroxylation, propeptide cleavage, and copper-dependent lysyl oxidase cross-linking; a lesion at each step names a different disease.","Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome reflects type V collagen (COL5A1), which controls type I fibril diameter, and gives hyperextensible fragile skin, hypermobility, and wide atrophic scars.","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome reflects type III collagen (COL3A1) and predicts arterial dissection or rupture, sigmoid perforation, uterine rupture, and berry aneurysms, often without prior aneurysm.","The kyphoscoliotic type is autosomal recessive lysyl hydroxylase 1 deficiency, with congenital hypotonia, progressive scoliosis, and fragile sclerae.","Marfan syndrome is fibrillin-1 with aortic root dilation and upward lens subluxation; osteogenesis imperfecta is type I collagen with fractures, blue sclerae, and hearing loss."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders, section on diseases of the extracellular matrix; The Skin, disorders of pigmentation and melanocytes"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous proteins: collagen structure, synthesis, and degradation; amino acid metabolism and tyrosine"},{"book":"Junqueira Basic Histology","where":"Connective tissue, collagen fibers; Skin, epidermis and melanocytes"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Congenital and acquired immunodeficiencies, defects of leukocyte granule function"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Genetic Disease; Diseases of the Skin"}]},"IN-13-F":{"unit":"IN-13-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06","title":"Benign and Premalignant Skin Lesions","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the common growths of skin and oral mucosa and sorts them by what drives them and what they can become. Some are purely benign clonal proliferations (seborrheic keratosis, lipoma, cysts, cherry hemangioma), some are dysplastic lesions that sit on the path to carcinoma (actinic keratosis, oral leukoplakia), some mark systemic risk rather than local danger (dysplastic nevi), and a few announce an internal cancer through paraneoplastic mechanisms. Board questions describe a lesion by site, age, texture, and course, then ask for the mechanism, the histology, or the risk. The reasoning is the same each time: which cell is proliferating, what started it, and whether its nuclei are atypical.","prerequisites":["Normal epidermal layers, keratinocyte maturation, and the granular layer (IN-1)","Melanocyte biology, the nevus growth sequence, and nest location (IN-13-A)","Cell cycle control through p53, Rb, p16, CDK4 and CDK6, and oncogene-induced senescence","Difference between hyperplasia, dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, and invasive carcinoma","Granulomatous and foreign body inflammation, including giant cell formation"],"connections":[{"to":"Malignant skin tumors (IN-13-G)","how":"Actinic keratosis and oral leukoplakia are the dysplastic stage of the same squamous pathway that ends in squamous cell carcinoma, and dysplastic nevi mark the host at risk for melanoma."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasia (GI chapters)","how":"Leser-Trelat sign and malignant acanthosis nigricans point to gastric adenocarcinoma, so a skin finding becomes a reason to look at the stomach."},{"to":"Endocrine and metabolic regulation (EN chapters)","how":"Most acanthosis nigricans is driven by hyperinsulinemia acting through IGF-1 receptors on keratinocytes, which links the lesion to insulin resistance rather than cancer."},{"to":"Microbiology and immunology (IM chapters)","how":"Oral hairy leukoplakia is EBV replication in tongue epithelium under immunosuppression, and thrush is Candida overgrowth, so both read as host defense failures."},{"to":"Embryology and vascular development","how":"Vascular birthmarks separate into true endothelial tumors and developmental malformations, and a somatic GNAQ mutation in one embryonic field explains the face, meninges, and eye findings of Sturge-Weber syndrome."}],"terms":[{"term":"Dysplasia","def":"Disordered growth with nuclear atypia and loss of normal maturation that involves part, but not the full thickness, of an epithelium."},{"term":"Carcinoma in situ","def":"Atypia through the entire epithelial thickness with the basement membrane still intact."},{"term":"Parakeratosis","def":"Retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum because keratinocyte maturation is faulty, which gives the cornified layer a rough adherent scale."},{"term":"Solar elastosis","def":"Accumulation of degenerated, blue-gray elastic and collagen material in the upper dermis from chronic ultraviolet exposure."},{"term":"Pyrimidine dimer","def":"A covalent link between adjacent pyrimidine bases formed when DNA absorbs UVB directly, repaired by nucleotide excision repair."},{"term":"Oncogene-induced senescence","def":"A permanent growth arrest triggered when a strong proliferative signal such as mutant BRAF drives expression of p16 and related cell cycle inhibitors, which is why most nevi stay small."},{"term":"Architectural atypia","def":"Disordered arrangement of melanocytic nests, including bridging between adjacent rete ridges and lentiginous single-cell growth along the basal layer."},{"term":"Horn cyst","def":"A round intraepidermal collection of laminated keratin seen within a seborrheic keratosis, also called a pseudo-horn cyst when it is a surface invagination."},{"term":"Paraneoplastic sign","def":"A finding caused by tumor-derived mediators or tumor-driven immunity rather than by local tumor growth or metastasis."},{"term":"Acanthosis nigricans","def":"Velvety hyperpigmented papillomatous plaques of the neck, axillae, and flexures from keratinocyte and fibroblast growth signaling."},{"term":"Vascular malformation","def":"A developmental error in vessel formation that is present at birth, grows only with the child, and does not involute."},{"term":"Leukoplakia","def":"A white mucosal plaque that cannot be scraped off and has no other specific diagnosis, which may harbor dysplasia detectable only on histology."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06-C01","obj":"IN-13-F.1","title":"Actinic keratosis: ultraviolet injury, keratinocyte dysplasia, and progression","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Actinic keratosis is a focus of dysplastic keratinocytes produced by cumulative ultraviolet exposure. It appears on chronically sun-exposed skin: the face, bald scalp, ears, lateral neck, forearms, and dorsal hands. Risk rises with age, fair skin (Fitzpatrick types I and II), outdoor work, and immunosuppression, and the lesions tend to be multiple because the whole field has been irradiated for decades."},{"type":"p","text":"UVB (about 280 to 320 nm) is absorbed directly by DNA and joins adjacent pyrimidines into cyclobutane dimers. Nucleotide excision repair removes most of them, but repair is imperfect. A cytosine held inside a dimer deaminates to uracil much faster than normal, and replication across the unrepaired lesion places adenine opposite it, so a cytosine and guanine pair becomes a thymine and adenine pair. This is why the ultraviolet signature mutation is a C to T transition at dipyrimidine sites. When this hits TP53, the keratinocyte loses the p53-dependent arrest and apoptosis that normally eliminate UV-damaged cells. The mutant clone survives further exposure, expands from the basal layer upward, and acquires additional changes in pathways such as NOTCH and CDKN2A. UVA contributes indirectly through reactive oxygen species. Chronic UV also suppresses local immune surveillance, which is why transplant recipients accumulate these lesions rapidly."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical findings follow the biology. Because the atypical cells mature poorly, nuclei persist in the stratum corneum (parakeratosis) and the cornified layer becomes adherent and gritty, so the lesion is often easier to feel than to see: a rough, sandpaper-like, erythematous scaly papule or plaque a few millimeters across. Histology shows atypia confined to the lower epidermis with alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis, often sparing the follicular and sweat duct openings, together with solar elastosis in the dermis as evidence of the cumulative dose. A hypertrophic lesion may build a cutaneous horn."},{"type":"p","text":"Progression is a matter of degree, not of kind. Partial-thickness atypia is dysplasia; full-thickness atypia with an intact basement membrane is squamous cell carcinoma in situ (Bowen disease); breach of the basement membrane is invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Any single actinic keratosis has a low annual probability of progressing, on the order of a fraction of one percent per lesion per year, but a patient with many lesions over many years carries a substantial cumulative risk. Induration, tenderness, rapid growth, bleeding, or a diameter over about 1 cm raises concern that invasion has already occurred."},{"type":"flow","title":"From photon to dysplastic clone","steps":["UVB absorbed by keratinocyte DNA","Cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers form","Nucleotide excision repair incomplete","C to T mutation, often in TP53","Loss of UV-induced arrest and apoptosis","Basal clone expands with poor maturation","Partial-thickness atypia: actinic keratosis"],"caption":"Each step explains a finding: basal clonal expansion puts the atypia low in the epidermis, and faulty maturation gives parakeratosis and rough scale."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of the squamous pathway in sun-damaged skin","cols":["Stage","Depth of atypia","Basement membrane","Typical appearance"],"rows":[["Photodamage without dysplasia","None","Intact","Mottled pigment, wrinkling, solar elastosis"],["Actinic keratosis","Lower epidermis, partial thickness","Intact","Rough scaly erythematous papule"],["Squamous cell carcinoma in situ","Full thickness","Intact","Sharply marginated scaly erythematous plaque"],["Invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Invades dermis","Breached","Firm indurated nodule, may ulcerate"]],"caption":"One continuum of accumulating mutations; the stage is defined by how far atypia extends and whether the basement membrane is crossed."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A transplant recipient on long-term immunosuppression develops dozens of actinic keratoses within three years, while an age-matched neighbor with the same sun history has four. What accounts for the difference?","explain":"Both have similar mutational burden from accumulated UV, but immunosuppression removes the T cell surveillance that normally eliminates antigenic dysplastic clones, so more clones persist and expand.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on UV-induced mutation and local immune suppression in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06-C02","obj":"IN-13-F.2","title":"Dysplastic nevi: atypia, the senescence brake, and melanoma risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A common acquired nevus starts when a melanocyte acquires an activating mutation in BRAF (most often V600E) or NRAS. The mutant cell proliferates in a burst, then the same strong MAP kinase signal drives expression of p16, the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor encoded by CDKN2A, and the clone enters oncogene-induced senescence. This brake is why an ordinary nevus is small, symmetric, uniform in color, and stable for decades."},{"type":"p","text":"Dysplastic nevi are melanocytic proliferations in which the brake holds only partly. They are usually larger than common nevi, commonly over 5 mm, with irregular or ill-defined borders, variable tan to dark brown pigment, and a flat peripheral rim around a slightly raised center. They occur on sun-exposed and covered skin, including the scalp, buttocks, and breasts, which tells you that UV is a contributor but not the whole story."},{"type":"p","text":"Biopsy shows two kinds of disorder. Cytologic atypia means enlarged, hyperchromatic, irregular nuclei in melanocytes that are larger than their neighbors. Architectural atypia means disordered placement: elongation of rete ridges with lentiginous single-cell growth along the basal layer, nests that fuse and bridge between adjacent rete ridges, and, in the dermal papillae, lamellar and concentric eosinophilic fibrosis with a patchy lymphocytic infiltrate and melanophages."},{"type":"p","text":"The risk message is epidemiologic, not local. Any individual dysplastic nevus very rarely becomes melanoma, and most melanomas arise in previously normal skin. What matters is the phenotype: having many dysplastic nevi identifies a person whose melanocytes carry a heavy mutational load and weakened senescence, so the raised risk is spread across all of the skin rather than concentrated in the atypical moles themselves. In familial atypical multiple mole melanoma, germline loss of CDKN2A removes p16, so CDK4 and CDK6 phosphorylate Rb without restraint and both the G1 checkpoint and the senescence response weaken. These families show many atypical nevi, melanoma at a younger age, multiple primaries, and in some kindreds pancreatic adenocarcinoma. A smaller number carry activating CDK4 mutations, which disable the same checkpoint from the other side."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The senescence brake and how it is lost","steps":["Activating BRAF or NRAS mutation","Strong MAP kinase proliferative signal","p16 (CDKN2A) induced","CDK4 and CDK6 inhibited, Rb stays active","Growth arrest: stable common nevus","Loss of p16 (CDKN2A) weakens the loop","Continued proliferation with atypia"],"caption":"A nevus is normally self-limiting because its own driver mutation triggers the brake; losing p16 removes the brake and allows atypical growth."},{"type":"table","title":"Common nevus, dysplastic nevus, and melanoma","cols":["Feature","Common nevus","Dysplastic nevus","Melanoma"],"rows":[["Size","Usually under 5 mm","Often over 5 mm","Often over 6 mm and enlarging"],["Border and color","Sharp, uniform","Irregular, variegated","Notched, multiple colors, changing"],["Cytology","Bland, uniform nuclei","Nuclear enlargement and hyperchromasia","Marked atypia, mitoses, prominent nucleoli"],["Architecture","Even nests, no bridging","Bridging nests, lentiginous growth, papillary dermal fibrosis","Asymmetry, poor circumscription, upward (pagetoid) spread"],["Significance","Benign","Marker of host melanoma risk","Malignant, metastasizes"]],"caption":"A graded series. Dysplastic nevi overlap both neighbors, which is why the clinical count and family history matter as much as any one biopsy."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why counting moles beats watching one mole","text":"A 28-year-old woman has about 60 nevi, 12 of them larger than 5 mm with irregular borders, and a mother and maternal uncle who had melanoma before age 45. One atypical nevus is removed and reported as a dysplastic nevus with moderate atypia, fully excised. Does that excision address her risk? No. The removed lesion itself had a very small chance of becoming melanoma, and her danger comes from germline CDKN2A-type loss of the senescence checkpoint in every melanocyte she has. Her future melanoma is more likely to appear in skin that looks normal today, including the scalp and covered sites, because the weakened checkpoint is systemic while the excision was local."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06-C03","obj":"IN-13-F.3","title":"Seborrheic keratosis: a benign clonal epidermal proliferation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Seborrheic keratosis is the most common benign epidermal tumor of older adults. Lesions begin in middle age, increase in number with time, and favor the trunk, face, and neck, although they appear almost anywhere except palms, soles, and mucosa. They are sharply demarcated, tan to dark brown or black, waxy or verrucous plaques that look pasted or stuck onto the surface rather than growing into it. The surface often shows plugged follicular openings, which appear on dermoscopy as comedo-like openings and milia-like cysts."},{"type":"p","text":"The appearance follows directly from the architecture. These are clonal proliferations of small, uniform basaloid keratinocytes that grow upward and outward from the level of the surrounding basal layer, so the lesion sits above the skin line with a flat base and a sharp lateral edge. Acanthosis (thickened epidermis) and hyperkeratosis give the waxy bulk, invaginations of the surface and intraepidermal keratin collections form horn cysts and pseudo-horn cysts, and melanin within the basaloid cells accounts for the color. There is hyperpigmentation without melanocytic proliferation, so a dark seborrheic keratosis is still an epidermal, not a melanocytic, lesion. Recognized variants include the stucco keratosis of the lower legs, dermatosis papulosa nigra on the face in darkly pigmented skin, and the irritated or inflamed keratosis with squamous eddies after friction or trauma."},{"type":"p","text":"Many lesions carry activating mutations in FGFR3 or in PIK3CA, which drive keratinocyte proliferation through the MAP kinase and PI3 kinase pathways. The important point for the exam is that these are activating mutations in a growth pathway without the loss of checkpoint control seen in dysplasia: nuclei stay uniform, maturation remains orderly, there is no significant atypia, and the lesions do not become malignant."},{"type":"p","text":"Number and tempo carry the clinical signal. Slow accumulation over years is ordinary aging skin. The sudden eruption of many seborrheic keratoses over weeks to months, particularly with pruritus, is the Leser-Trelat sign and raises concern for an internal malignancy, classically gastric adenocarcinoma, with lymphoma and other adenocarcinomas also reported. The proposed mechanism is tumor-derived growth factor activity, such as transforming growth factor alpha acting on the epidermal growth factor receptor of keratinocytes, which is the same signaling logic behind malignant acanthosis nigricans and explains why the two signs often appear together. The association is debated because seborrheic keratoses are so common in the age group that also develops cancer, so the finding is a prompt to evaluate rather than proof of a tumor."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing pigmented lesions in older adults","cols":["Lesion","Proliferating cell","Key clinical clue","Histologic signature","Malignant potential"],"rows":[["Seborrheic keratosis","Basaloid keratinocyte","Stuck-on, waxy, sharp edge, plugged follicles","Acanthosis, horn cysts, bland basaloid cells","None"],["Actinic keratosis","Dysplastic keratinocyte","Rough gritty scale on sun-exposed skin","Lower epidermal atypia, parakeratosis, solar elastosis","Can progress to squamous cell carcinoma"],["Solar lentigo","Normal melanocyte, increased melanin","Flat uniform tan macule, sun-exposed","Basal hyperpigmentation, elongated rete, no nests","None"],["Melanoma","Atypical melanocyte","Asymmetric, changing, multiple colors","Atypical melanocytes, pagetoid spread, mitoses","High"]],"caption":"Ask which cell is proliferating and whether its nuclei are atypical; the clinical surface texture usually gives the answer before the biopsy does."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06-C04","obj":"IN-13-F.4","title":"Mucocutaneous paraneoplastic signs and their tumor associations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A paraneoplastic sign is produced by mediators the tumor releases or by immunity the tumor provokes, not by local invasion or metastasis. In the skin this happens through two mechanisms: tumor-derived growth factors that drive keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation, and tumor antigens that elicit antibodies or T cells which cross-react with normal skin and muscle. Both can appear before the cancer is found, which is why the skin finding sometimes directs the search."},{"type":"p","text":"In the growth factor group, eruptive seborrheic keratoses (Leser-Trelat sign) and malignant acanthosis nigricans share a proposed pathway: transforming growth factor alpha from the tumor, classically a gastric adenocarcinoma, stimulates the epidermal growth factor receptor on keratinocytes, which produces papillomatosis, hyperkeratosis, and epidermal thickening. Acanthosis nigricans gives velvety, hyperpigmented, thickened plaques in the axillae, posterior neck, and other flexures. The great majority of cases are not paraneoplastic at all: hyperinsulinemia from obesity, type 2 diabetes, or polycystic ovary syndrome spills over onto IGF-1 receptors on keratinocytes and fibroblasts and produces the identical lesion. The features that shift suspicion toward cancer are abrupt onset, a thin or weight-losing older adult, extensive or unusual sites including the palms and oral mucosa, and associated pruritus."},{"type":"p","text":"In the immune group, dermatomyositis in an adult carries a meaningful risk of underlying malignancy, especially ovarian, lung, gastric, colorectal, and pancreatic cancer and non-Hodgkin lymphoma; the risk is highest within the first few years of diagnosis and is lower in children. Its skin findings reflect interface injury and vasculopathy: a heliotrope violaceous periorbital rash, Gottron papules over the knuckles, a shawl or V-sign photodistributed erythema, and dilated nailfold capillaries, with proximal muscle weakness from perifascicular muscle injury. Paraneoplastic pemphigus occurs with lymphoid neoplasms, most often chronic lymphocytic leukemia and non-Hodgkin lymphoma and Castleman disease, and is driven by autoantibodies against desmogleins together with plakin proteins such as envoplakin and periplakin; the broader antigen target explains its severe, treatment-resistant oral erosions and its lichenoid as well as blistering features."},{"type":"table","title":"Mucocutaneous paraneoplastic signs","cols":["Sign","Appearance","Tumor association","Mechanism","Common nonmalignant cause"],"rows":[["Leser-Trelat sign","Sudden eruption of many seborrheic keratoses, often itchy","Gastric adenocarcinoma, other adenocarcinomas, lymphoma","Tumor growth factors acting on keratinocyte EGF receptor","Ordinary age-related accumulation over years"],["Acanthosis nigricans","Velvety dark thickened flexural plaques","Gastric adenocarcinoma (minority of cases)","Growth factor signaling, or insulin acting on IGF-1 receptors","Insulin resistance: obesity, type 2 diabetes, polycystic ovary syndrome"],["Dermatomyositis (adult)","Heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, shawl sign, proximal weakness","Ovarian, lung, gastric, colorectal, pancreatic cancer, lymphoma","Tumor antigen-directed immunity with interface dermatitis and vasculopathy","Idiopathic autoimmune disease, drug-induced forms"],["Paraneoplastic pemphigus","Severe refractory oral erosions with polymorphous skin lesions","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia, non-Hodgkin lymphoma, Castleman disease","Antibodies to desmogleins plus plakin proteins","None; it is tumor-associated by definition"]],"caption":"Read the tempo and the body habitus along with the lesion: most acanthosis nigricans is metabolic, while abrupt onset in a thin older adult points toward a tumor."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why a stomach tumor changes the skin","steps":["Gastric adenocarcinoma secretes TGF-alpha","Circulates to skin","Binds keratinocyte EGF receptor","Keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation","Papillomatosis and hyperkeratosis","Acanthosis nigricans and eruptive seborrheic keratoses"],"caption":"The proposed growth factor route that makes two different-looking signs appear together in the same patient."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06-C05","obj":"IN-13-F.5","title":"Epidermal inclusion cyst, trichilemmal cyst, and lipoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An epidermal inclusion cyst is a dermal cavity lined by stratified squamous epithelium that matures the way surface epidermis does, including a granular layer. It arises when surface or follicular infundibular epithelium is displaced into the dermis, by trauma or by plugging of the follicular opening, and then continues its normal program: it keratinizes through a granular layer and sheds soft, flaky, laminated keratin into the lumen. Clinically it is a mobile, dome-shaped dermal or subcutaneous nodule, often with a central punctum, that discharges cheesy, malodorous material. The common name sebaceous cyst is a misnomer because the contents are keratin, not sebum."},{"type":"p","text":"Keratin is inert while it stays inside an epithelial lining and strongly irritating once it escapes. Rupture, usually after squeezing or trauma, spills keratin into the dermis, where macrophages fuse into foreign body giant cells around the keratin flakes and a brisk neutrophilic and granulomatous reaction follows. The result is a red, hot, tender, swollen nodule that mimics an abscess but is usually sterile at first, which is why the exam stem describes an inflamed cyst with giant cells engulfing keratin rather than a bacterial infection. Multiple epidermal cysts in unusual numbers or sites, especially with osteomas and desmoid tumors, should raise the question of Gardner syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"A trichilemmal or pilar cyst comes from the outer root sheath of the follicular isthmus, which keratinizes abruptly without forming a granular layer. Its lining shows cells that enlarge and then transition sharply to dense, compact, homogeneous keratin, and the contents are firm rather than flaky; calcification is common. About 90 percent occur on the scalp, they are frequently multiple, and familial cases with autosomal dominant inheritance are described. The single histologic discriminator worth memorizing is the granular layer: present in the epidermal inclusion cyst, absent in the trichilemmal cyst."},{"type":"p","text":"Lipoma is a benign tumor of mature adipocytes and the most common soft tissue tumor in adults. It presents as a soft, doughy, mobile, painless subcutaneous mass, usually on the trunk, shoulders, neck, or proximal limbs, with a thin fibrous capsule that lets it slide under the examining finger. Histology shows lobules of mature fat cells indistinguishable from normal adipose tissue apart from the capsule, and many lipomas carry rearrangements of chromosome 12q involving HMGA2. Because the proliferating cells are mature and nonatypical, lipomas do not transform; features that argue instead for liposarcoma are large size, deep or retroperitoneal location, firmness, fixation, rapid growth, and pain."},{"type":"table","title":"Cutaneous and subcutaneous lumps compared","cols":["Lesion","Origin","Lining or cell","Contents or texture","Typical site"],"rows":[["Epidermal inclusion cyst","Displaced surface or infundibular epidermis","Stratified squamous with granular layer","Soft laminated flaky keratin, central punctum","Face, neck, trunk, scrotum"],["Trichilemmal (pilar) cyst","Outer root sheath of follicular isthmus","Squamous without granular layer","Dense compact keratin, often calcified","Scalp, often multiple"],["Lipoma","Mature adipocyte clone","Mature adipocytes in a thin capsule","Soft, doughy, freely mobile","Trunk, shoulders, neck, proximal limbs"]],"caption":"Two keratin-filled cysts separated by the granular layer, and one fat tumor that feels like normal fat because that is what it is."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ruptured cyst: why it looks infected","steps":["Pressure or trauma tears the cyst wall","Laminated keratin enters the dermis","Keratin recognized as foreign material","Neutrophils then macrophages recruited","Macrophages fuse into foreign body giant cells","Painful red swollen nodule, usually sterile"],"caption":"A foreign body reaction to the cyst's own keratin, which explains the abscess-like picture without an organism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06-C06","obj":"IN-13-F.6","title":"Vascular lesions: tumors that proliferate and malformations that persist","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vascular lesions split into two categories with different natural histories. A vascular tumor grows by endothelial proliferation, so it has a growth phase that outpaces the child and may regress. A vascular malformation is an error of vessel development that is present at birth, is lined by quiescent endothelium, and enlarges only in proportion to body growth, so it never involutes. Deciding which category a lesion belongs to answers most questions about its course."},{"type":"p","text":"Infantile hemangioma is the prototypical tumor. It is usually absent or only faintly visible at birth, appears in the first weeks of life, proliferates rapidly over roughly the first 6 to 12 months, then involutes slowly, with about half of lesions largely involuted by age 5 and most by age 9. It is more common in preterm and low birth weight infants and in girls. The endothelium is GLUT1 positive, which distinguishes it from other vascular lesions, and it is histologically a dense proliferation of plump capillaries that gradually fibroses and fattens as it involutes. Because involution is the rule, the consequences that matter are local and depend on site: ulceration of the surface, periocular lesions that block the visual axis and cause deprivation amblyopia, and subglottic lesions that narrow the airway."},{"type":"p","text":"Cherry hemangioma (cherry angioma) is an acquired benign capillary proliferation of adult life: small, bright red, dome-shaped papules a few millimeters across on the trunk and proximal limbs that increase in number with age and do not regress. Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma) is reactive rather than neoplastic. After minor trauma or during pregnancy, a lobular mass of capillaries grows over days to weeks into a friable, exophytic red nodule that bleeds profusely with slight contact and often has a collarette of scale at its base. Pregnancy-associated gingival lesions, called granuloma gravidarum, typically regress after delivery, which fits a hormone-driven reactive process. The name is a double misnomer: the lesion is neither pyogenic nor granulomatous."},{"type":"p","text":"Port-wine stain (nevus flammeus) is a capillary malformation: a flat pink to red patch present at birth that persists, deepens in color, and may thicken with age. A lesion in the V1 trigeminal distribution raises the question of Sturge-Weber syndrome, in which a somatic activating GNAQ mutation affects one embryonic vascular field, so the same abnormal capillary development appears in facial skin, leptomeninges, and eye. The leptomeningeal angioma produces seizures, contralateral hemiparesis, and cognitive impairment, and abnormal ocular vasculature raises intraocular pressure and causes glaucoma. Nevus simplex (salmon patch or stork bite) on the glabella and nape is a separate, common finding that usually fades."},{"type":"table","title":"Vascular lesions by category and course","cols":["Lesion","Category","Onset","Course","Distinguishing clue"],"rows":[["Infantile hemangioma","Tumor","First weeks of life","Proliferates months, involutes over years","GLUT1 positive endothelium"],["Cherry hemangioma","Tumor, acquired benign","Adulthood, increases with age","Persists","Multiple small bright red trunk papules"],["Pyogenic granuloma","Reactive proliferation","Days to weeks after trauma or in pregnancy","May regress after delivery or recur","Friable, bleeds readily, often gingival"],["Port-wine stain","Capillary malformation","Present at birth","Persists, darkens and thickens","V1 distribution suggests Sturge-Weber syndrome"]],"caption":"Tumors have a growth phase and may involute; malformations are present at birth and stay. Onset and course identify the category before histology does."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 3-week-old infant has a 1 cm bright red raised forearm lesion that was absent at discharge and has doubled in size. Another infant has a flat pink patch over the cheek and upper eyelid present from birth. Which lesion will be gone by school age, and which child needs brain and eye evaluation?","explain":"The raised, late-appearing, growing lesion is an infantile hemangioma, a tumor that involutes. The flat patch present at birth in a V1 distribution is a capillary malformation that persists and carries the Sturge-Weber association, so leptomeningeal and ocular assessment belongs to that child.","check":"Compare with the tumor versus malformation distinction and the table in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-06-C07","obj":"IN-13-F.7","title":"White oral lesions: leukoplakia, oral hairy leukoplakia, and thrush","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A white mucosal patch is white for one of two reasons: a thickened, keratinized epithelium scatters light, or a layer of organisms and debris sits on the mucosal surface. The bedside scrape separates the two, since material that wipes away was never part of the epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"Leukoplakia is defined by exclusion: a white plaque that cannot be scraped off and has no other specific diagnosis. Tobacco in all forms and alcohol are the main drivers, with betel quid in some populations, and together they act more than additively. Carcinogen exposure thickens and hyperkeratinizes the epithelium and, in a proportion of lesions, produces dysplasia, so leukoplakia is the oral counterpart of actinic keratosis: a mutated field that can progress through dysplasia and carcinoma in situ to invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Transformation is low in absolute terms, but risk rises with nonhomogeneous or speckled lesions, high-risk sites (floor of the mouth, lateral and ventral tongue, soft palate), larger size, and dysplasia on histology. Erythroplakia, a velvety red patch, is less common but carries higher rates of dysplasia and carcinoma because the atrophic epithelium reveals the vessels beneath."},{"type":"p","text":"Oral hairy leukoplakia is a corrugated, shaggy white plaque on the lateral tongue borders that also fails to scrape off, but its mechanism differs. It is Epstein-Barr virus replication in the upper layers of the squamous epithelium, with ballooned cells containing viral inclusions and marked hyperkeratosis but no dysplasia. It occurs in immunosuppression, is a classic marker of advanced HIV infection with a low CD4 count, resolves as immune function improves, and is not premalignant."},{"type":"p","text":"Oral candidiasis (thrush) is superficial overgrowth of Candida albicans on the buccal mucosa, tongue, or palate, seen in infants, denture wearers, users of inhaled or systemic corticosteroids, after broad-spectrum antibiotics, and in diabetes or immunosuppression. The pseudomembrane is a mat of pseudohyphae, yeast, fibrin, neutrophils, and desquamated cells lying on the surface, so it scrapes off and leaves erythematous, sometimes bleeding mucosa beneath. Potassium hydroxide or periodic acid-Schiff preparations show pseudohyphae confined to the most superficial epithelial layers, and thrush without a local explanation points to an immune or endocrine defect behind it."},{"type":"table","title":"White oral lesions compared","cols":["Feature","Leukoplakia","Oral hairy leukoplakia","Thrush"],"rows":[["Cause","Tobacco, alcohol, betel quid","Epstein-Barr virus replication","Candida albicans overgrowth"],["Site","Buccal mucosa, floor of mouth, tongue, soft palate","Lateral borders of the tongue","Buccal mucosa, tongue, palate"],["Scrapes off","No","No","Yes, leaving red base"],["Histology","Hyperkeratosis, acanthosis, dysplasia in a subset","Hyperkeratosis with ballooned cells, no dysplasia","Superficial pseudohyphae and neutrophils on epithelium"],["Setting","Older adult smoker or drinker","HIV with low CD4 count, transplant recipient","Infant, denture wearer, inhaled or systemic steroids, diabetes"],["Premalignant","Yes, dysplasia graded on histology","No","No"]],"caption":"One change in the epithelium, one virus inside the epithelium, one organism on top of it. The scrape test and the site narrow the field quickly."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: three white tongues","text":"Three adults have white oral patches. A 62-year-old smoker has a thick white plaque on the ventral tongue and floor of the mouth that a tongue blade does not move. A 34-year-old with a CD4 count of 90 cells per microliter has corrugated white streaks on both lateral tongue borders, also fixed. A 70-year-old using inhaled corticosteroids has curd-like buccal patches that wipe away and leave red mucosa. The third is superficial Candida. Carcinogen exposure at high-risk sites makes the first leukoplakia, with its dysplasia risk, while bilateral lateral tongue corrugation in immunosuppression makes the second Epstein-Barr virus driven oral hairy leukoplakia, which carries none."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The organizing question in this chapter is which cell proliferates and whether its checkpoints still work. Seborrheic keratosis, lipoma, cherry hemangioma, and the keratin cysts are clonal or reactive proliferations of mature, nonatypical cells, often with an activating mutation or rearrangement in a growth pathway (FGFR3 or PIK3CA in seborrheic keratosis, HMGA2 rearrangement in lipoma), but with intact p53 and Rb control, so they enlarge and stop. Actinic keratosis, dysplastic nevi, and oral leukoplakia involve the same proliferative drive plus loss of checkpoint function, through TP53 mutation from UV photoproducts, loss of p16 encoded by CDKN2A, or carcinogen-induced mutation in oral epithelium, which is why atypia appears and progression becomes possible. This explains the grading language shared with every other epithelium on the exam: hyperplasia, dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, invasive carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Each lesion also connects outward. Anatomy and embryology explain the cysts and the vascular birthmarks: a cyst keeps the keratinization program of the epithelium it came from, and a single somatic GNAQ mutation in one embryonic field gives the face, meninges, and eye findings of Sturge-Weber syndrome. Immunology explains why immunosuppression multiplies actinic keratoses, allows EBV to replicate in tongue epithelium, and permits Candida overgrowth, and why paraneoplastic pemphigus and dermatomyositis injure skin through tumor-directed immunity. Endocrinology explains the acanthosis nigricans a learner will actually see, since hyperinsulinemia acting on IGF-1 receptors is far more common than a gastric tumor acting through the EGF receptor. The limits are worth stating plainly: the Leser-Trelat association rests on case series in an age group where both lesions are common, progression rates for individual actinic keratoses and leukoplakias vary widely between studies, and the diagnosis of a dysplastic nevus has meaningful interobserver variability."}],"summary":["UVB forms pyrimidine dimers that become C to T mutations, often in TP53; the surviving basal clone matures poorly, which gives lower epidermal atypia, parakeratosis, and the rough scale of actinic keratosis over a dermis showing solar elastosis.","Partial-thickness epidermal atypia is actinic keratosis, full-thickness atypia with an intact basement membrane is squamous cell carcinoma in situ, and a breached basement membrane is invasive carcinoma.","A common nevus stops growing because its own BRAF or NRAS mutation triggers p16-dependent senescence; germline CDKN2A loss removes that brake, so many dysplastic nevi mark whole-body melanoma risk rather than acting as the usual precursor lesion.","Seborrheic keratosis is a bland basaloid epidermal proliferation with horn cysts and FGFR3 or PIK3CA activating mutations; it is benign at any color, but sudden eruption of many lesions is the Leser-Trelat sign.","Paraneoplastic skin signs work either through tumor growth factors (Leser-Trelat sign, malignant acanthosis nigricans, classically gastric adenocarcinoma) or tumor-directed immunity (dermatomyositis with ovarian, lung, and GI cancers; paraneoplastic pemphigus with lymphoid neoplasms); most acanthosis nigricans is simply insulin resistance.","An epidermal inclusion cyst has a granular layer and flaky laminated keratin, a trichilemmal cyst of the scalp has no granular layer and dense compact keratin, and rupture of either provokes a foreign body giant cell reaction that mimics an abscess.","Lipoma is an encapsulated mass of mature adipocytes, the most common adult soft tissue tumor, and does not transform; size, depth, firmness, fixation, and pain argue for liposarcoma instead.","Vascular tumors proliferate and may involute (infantile hemangioma appears in the first weeks, grows, then regresses; pyogenic granuloma grows in days and bleeds), while vascular malformations such as port-wine stain are present at birth and persist, with V1 involvement raising Sturge-Weber syndrome.","A white oral plaque that scrapes off is Candida sitting on the mucosa; one that does not is epithelial, with leukoplakia from tobacco and alcohol carrying dysplasia risk and oral hairy leukoplakia from EBV on the lateral tongue in immunosuppression carrying none."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, benign and premalignant epidermal and melanocytic tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, carcinogenesis and paraneoplastic syndromes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, vascular tumors and malformations"},{"book":"Junqueira Basic Histology","where":"Skin, epidermis and epidermal appendages"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to tumors"}]},"IN-13-G":{"unit":"IN-13-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-07","title":"Malignant Skin Neoplasms","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how ultraviolet radiation and loss of immune surveillance turn normal keratinocytes and melanocytes into the three common skin cancers, and how a clonal T cell population produces cutaneous lymphoma. It covers the hedgehog pathway in basal cell carcinoma, ultraviolet mutagenesis and repair failure in squamous cell carcinoma, the growth phases and depth of melanoma, and the staging of mycosis fungoides. Board questions give a lesion description, a biopsy finding, or a risk history and ask for the tumor, its molecular driver, or its likelihood of spreading. Reasoning from mechanism to appearance, rather than memorizing lesion lists, is what makes those questions answerable.","prerequisites":["Normal epidermal layers, keratinocyte maturation, desmosomes, and the basement membrane (IN-1, IN-2)","Melanocyte biology: melanosome transfer, tyrosinase, and eumelanin versus pheomelanin (IN-3)","DNA damage and repair, especially nucleotide excision repair and the p53 damage response","Core oncogenesis concepts: proto-oncogene activation, tumor suppressor loss, clonal evolution, invasion, and metastasis","T cell subsets, memory T cell homing, and tumor immune surveillance (IM-4, IM-9)"],"connections":[{"to":"Benign and premalignant skin lesions (IN-13-F)","how":"Actinic keratosis, seborrheic keratosis, and benign nevi are the lesions these cancers must be distinguished from, and actinic keratosis is the direct precursor of squamous cell carcinoma."},{"to":"Immunology and transplant medicine (IM-9, IM-13)","how":"T cell immune surveillance normally clears mutant keratinocyte clones, so pharmacologic immunosuppression produces a sharp, dose-related rise in squamous cell carcinoma."},{"to":"Microbiology and virology","how":"Human papillomavirus E6 and E7 proteins inactivate p53 and Rb, giving a viral route to the same cell cycle escape that ultraviolet mutation causes."},{"to":"Genetics and inherited cancer syndromes","how":"Germline PTCH1 loss (Gorlin syndrome), CDKN2A loss (familial melanoma), and nucleotide excision repair defects (xeroderma pigmentosum) each compress decades of sporadic risk into childhood or early adult life."},{"to":"Hematopathology (HE-9)","how":"Mycosis fungoides is a peripheral T cell lymphoma that happens to home to skin, so its staging, transformation, and leukemic phase follow lymphoma rules rather than carcinoma rules."}],"terms":[{"term":"Peripheral palisading","def":"Alignment of tumor cells into an orderly row at the outer edge of a basaloid nest, imitating the normal epidermal basal layer."},{"term":"Retraction cleft","def":"A gap between a basaloid tumor nest and its surrounding stroma, produced during tissue processing because the tumor and its specialized stroma separate readily."},{"term":"Cyclobutane pyrimidine dimer","def":"A covalent link between two adjacent pyrimidine bases formed directly by ultraviolet B photons, the principal premutagenic lesion in sun-exposed skin."},{"term":"Keratin pearl","def":"A rounded whorl of concentrically layered keratinized cells within a squamous tumor, evidence that the tumor retains squamous differentiation."},{"term":"Intercellular bridge","def":"A light-microscopic image of desmosomal attachment between adjacent squamous cells, seen as fine spines across the intercellular space."},{"term":"Carcinoma in situ","def":"Full-thickness epithelial atypia that has not breached the basement membrane, so it has no access to dermal vessels or lymphatics."},{"term":"Marjolin ulcer","def":"Squamous cell carcinoma arising in a chronic burn scar, long-standing ulcer, or draining sinus tract."},{"term":"Radial growth phase","def":"The stage in which melanoma cells spread horizontally within the epidermis and papillary dermis without forming an expansile dermal mass."},{"term":"Vertical growth phase","def":"The stage in which a melanoma clone grows as an expanding nodule into the dermis, gaining access to lymphatics and blood vessels."},{"term":"Breslow thickness","def":"The depth in millimeters from the top of the granular layer to the deepest invasive tumor cell, the strongest prognostic measure in localized melanoma."},{"term":"Epidermotropism","def":"The tendency of malignant skin-homing T cells to migrate into and persist within the epidermis."},{"term":"Pautrier microabscess","def":"A discrete intraepidermal cluster of atypical lymphocytes with folded (cerebriform) nuclei, characteristic of mycosis fungoides."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-07-C01","obj":"IN-13-G.1","title":"Basal cell carcinoma: hedgehog activation, appearance, and local behavior","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Basal cell carcinoma is the most common cancer in humans, and it is a disease of chronically sun-exposed skin in lightly pigmented people. About four out of five arise on the head and neck, with the nose, inner canthus, and upper lip favored. The tumor cells resemble immature basal keratinocytes and arise from basal cells and from stem cells of the hair follicle, which is why the lesion is essentially never seen on true mucosa or on palms and soles, where follicles are absent."},{"type":"p","text":"The driving lesion is constitutive activation of the hedgehog signaling pathway, present in nearly every case. PTCH1, on chromosome 9q, encodes the hedgehog receptor, and its signaling role is inhibitory: in the absence of hedgehog ligand, PTCH1 holds the transmembrane protein smoothened (SMO) silent. Ultraviolet mutation that inactivates PTCH1, or an activating mutation in SMO, releases that restraint, so GLI transcription factors move to the nucleus and drive proliferation genes continuously without any ligand. Germline loss of one PTCH1 allele produces Gorlin syndrome (nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome), in which numerous basal cell carcinomas appear in the second and third decades alongside odontogenic keratocysts, palmar and plantar pits, and medulloblastoma. TP53 mutation is a frequent second hit."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hedgehog pathway release in basal cell carcinoma","steps":["Normally PTCH1 holds SMO inactive without hedgehog ligand","UVB mutation inactivates PTCH1, or SMO is activated","SMO signals with no ligand present","GLI transcription factors enter the nucleus","Continuous proliferation of basal-type keratinocytes","Basaloid nests expand and invade adjacent tissue"],"caption":"Loss of an inhibitor, not gain of a growth signal, is what switches this pathway on. Germline PTCH1 loss (Gorlin syndrome) starts the sequence one step ahead."},{"type":"p","text":"The nodular form, about two thirds of cases, is a translucent or pearly papule crossed by fine dilated vessels (telangiectasias) with a rolled, elevated border. Central necrosis and ulceration leave a crater with a raised rim, historically called a rodent ulcer. Superficial basal cell carcinoma appears as a thin erythematous scaly patch on the trunk and can be mistaken for eczema or psoriasis. The morpheaform or infiltrative pattern is a firm indurated plaque whose true edges extend well beyond what is visible, so it recurs more often and may track along nerves."},{"type":"p","text":"Histologically, nests and cords of small, uniform, dark basaloid cells descend from the epidermis; the outermost cells line up in a palisade, imitating the normal basal layer, and retraction clefts separate the nest from its mucinous stroma. Mitoses are present but the cells show little pleomorphism."},{"type":"p","text":"Basal cell carcinoma invades and destroys locally, eroding cartilage, bone, and orbital structures when neglected, yet metastasis is exceptional, usually quoted at well under 0.1 percent of cases. The usual explanation is dependence on its induced stroma: the tumor requires the specialized fibrovascular stroma it recruits in order to grow, so cells that reach blood or lymph rarely establish elsewhere. Morbidity therefore comes from local destruction at anatomically delicate sites rather than from distant disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has dozens of basal cell carcinomas by age 25, plus jaw cysts and small pits on the palms. Which gene is mutated in the germline, and why do the tumors appear so early?","explain":"Every cell already carries one inactivated copy of the pathway's inhibitor, so a single somatic hit, rather than two, is enough to release signaling in any sun-exposed keratinocyte.","check":"Compare your answer with the paragraph on PTCH1 and the hedgehog flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-07-C02","obj":"IN-13-G.2","title":"Squamous cell carcinoma: ultraviolet mutagenesis, immune escape, and site selection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common skin cancer, and its risk tracks cumulative lifetime ultraviolet dose rather than the episodic sunburn pattern that matters for melanoma. The mechanism starts with photochemistry. Ultraviolet B (about 290 to 320 nm) is absorbed directly by DNA and joins adjacent pyrimidines into cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers. If nucleotide excision repair removes them before replication, nothing follows; if replication comes first, the polymerase misreads the lesion and inserts the characteristic C to T and CC to TT substitutions at dipyrimidine sites. Ultraviolet A penetrates deeper and acts mainly through reactive oxygen species."},{"type":"p","text":"Mutations accumulate in a defined order. TP53 is hit early, which is why actinic keratosis, the partial-thickness dysplastic lesion that precedes invasive carcinoma, already carries p53 mutant clones; NOTCH and CDKN2A loss follow. Losing p53 removes the damage checkpoint, so the next ultraviolet injury is replicated instead of repaired or deleted by apoptosis. That stepwise logic explains the clinical sequence of sun damage, actinic keratosis, carcinoma in situ, and invasive carcinoma over years to decades."},{"type":"flow","title":"From photon to invasive squamous cell carcinoma","steps":["UVB forms pyrimidine dimers in keratinocyte DNA","Nucleotide excision repair fails or is overwhelmed","Replication makes the C to T mutation permanent, TP53 hit early","Checkpoint and apoptosis lost, dysplastic clone expands (actinic keratosis)","Further hits, NOTCH and CDKN2A loss, full-thickness atypia in situ","Basement membrane breached, invasion into dermis"],"caption":"Each step needs the one before it, so interrupting repair, checkpoints, or immune clearance accelerates the whole chain."},{"type":"p","text":"Anything that raises the mutational load, blocks repair, or removes immune clearance multiplies risk. Xeroderma pigmentosum, an autosomal recessive failure of nucleotide excision repair, leaves dimers unrepaired, so affected children develop multiple skin cancers, melanoma among them, in the first two decades. Solid organ transplant recipients lose the T cell surveillance that normally eliminates mutant and virus-infected keratinocytes: their squamous cell carcinoma incidence rises many times over, the tumor becomes more common than basal cell carcinoma, and lesions behave more aggressively. Beta human papillomavirus types persist under that same immunosuppression, while mucosal types including HPV 16 and 18 drive anogenital and periungual carcinoma, where E6 degrades p53 and E7 inactivates Rb."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma and the mechanism of each","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism","Clinical signature"],"rows":[["Cumulative ultraviolet exposure","Pyrimidine dimers, early TP53 mutation","Bald scalp, dorsal hands, lower lip"],["Actinic keratosis","Established p53 mutant dysplastic clone","Rough scaly macule, direct precursor"],["Transplant immunosuppression","Loss of T cell surveillance and viral control","Multiple fast tumors, squamous exceeds basal cell"],["Human papillomavirus","E6 degrades p53, E7 inactivates Rb","Anogenital, periungual, verrucous lesions"],["Chronic arsenic exposure","Oxidative damage, impaired repair","Well water, palmar keratoses, in situ lesions"],["Xeroderma pigmentosum","Defective nucleotide excision repair","Photosensitivity, multiple cancers in childhood"],["Chronic scars, ulcers, sinus tracts","Decades of inflammation-driven proliferation","Marjolin ulcer in burn scar or sinus"],["Ionizing radiation, tar and soot, smoking","Direct and chemical mutagenesis","Radiation fields, occupational exposure, lip"]],"caption":"Every entry acts by one of three routes: more DNA damage, less repair, or less immune clearance."},{"type":"p","text":"Site selection follows ultraviolet dose and anatomy. The lower lip projects forward and is held nearly horizontal to the sun, its vermilion epithelium is thin and lightly pigmented, and the upper lip is shaded by the nose. Add tobacco contact and chronic actinic cheilitis, and the lower lip becomes the classic squamous site while the shaded upper lip and inner canthus favor basal cell carcinoma. The scalp vertex, helix of the ear, and dorsal forearms follow the same logic of maximum exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"Marjolin ulcer deserves separate attention because its stimulus is not ultraviolet light but decades of wound repair: persistent inflammation, repeated keratinocyte proliferation, scar lymphatic obliteration, and relative local immune isolation. A long-stable burn scar or chronic ulcer that begins to grow, indurate, or exceed its old margins should be read as carcinoma arising in the scar."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-07-C03","obj":"IN-13-G.3","title":"Recognizing squamous cell carcinoma and separating it from Bowen disease and keratoacanthoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma presents as a firm, scaly, crusted, or ulcerated nodule or plaque on sun-damaged skin, often tender, with an indurated base that feels fixed to surrounding tissue. The usual history is rapid enlargement or a nonhealing ulcer in a field of actinic damage. A thick conical keratin spike (cutaneous horn) may sit on top, and since about a quarter of horns overlie a carcinoma, the base rather than the horn is diagnostic."},{"type":"p","text":"Histology reflects the tumor's attempt to continue squamous differentiation. Irregular nests and tongues of atypical keratinocytes extend through the basement membrane into the dermis, and within them cells enlarge, acquire abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, and layer concentrically around central keratin to form keratin pearls. The fine spines crossing the space between adjacent cells, the intercellular bridges, are desmosomes seen at the light microscope and confirm squamous lineage. Poorly differentiated tumors lose keratinization, become spindled, and may need cytokeratin staining to separate them from sarcoma or melanoma."},{"type":"table","title":"Squamous proliferations compared","cols":["Lesion","Depth of atypia","Appearance","Key distinguishing feature"],"rows":[["Actinic keratosis","Partial thickness, lower epidermis","Rough gritty macule, better felt than seen","Parakeratosis alternating with orthokeratosis"],["Bowen disease (in situ)","Full thickness, basement membrane intact","Sharply marginated red scaly plaque","No dermal invasion, so no metastatic capacity"],["Keratoacanthoma","Atypia present, architecture symmetric","Dome nodule with central keratin plug","Weeks of growth then involution, symmetric cup"],["Invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Through basement membrane into dermis","Indurated scaly or ulcerated nodule, fixed base","Keratin pearls, intercellular bridges, invasive nests"]],"caption":"Invasion through the basement membrane, not the degree of cytologic atypia, is what separates in situ from invasive disease and creates metastatic potential."},{"type":"p","text":"Bowen disease is squamous cell carcinoma in situ: full-thickness atypia with disordered maturation and mitoses at all levels, but an intact basement membrane, so the lesion has no access to dermal lymphatics or vessels until it invades. It arises on sun-exposed skin and also on covered skin, where arsenic exposure and human papillomavirus are the more likely causes. On the glans penis the same in situ lesion is called erythroplasia of Queyrat and appears as a velvety red plaque."},{"type":"p","text":"Keratoacanthoma is the classic look-alike, because its cells can be indistinguishable from well-differentiated carcinoma on a small sample. The distinction rests on architecture and tempo rather than cytology, and a shave biopsy may capture neither. Since some lesions diagnosed this way prove to be carcinoma, it is now widely regarded as a well-differentiated squamous variant rather than a reliably benign entity."},{"type":"p","text":"Metastatic risk is low overall but higher than for basal cell carcinoma: low single digits of percent for ordinary sun-induced tumors on the trunk and limbs, and substantially higher for tumors of the lip and ear, those over about 2 cm wide or about 2 mm deep, poorly differentiated or perineurally invasive tumors, recurrent tumors, carcinoma in a scar or chronic ulcer, and any tumor in an immunosuppressed patient. Spread is lymphatic to regional nodes first, then hematogenous. Squamous cell carcinoma is far less dependent than basal cell carcinoma on a recruited stroma, so detached cells survive at distant sites."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading two lip lesions","text":"Two patients present with lip lesions. The first is a pearly papule with fine telangiectasias and a rolled border on the upper lip of a 70 year old gardener. The second is a 1.5 cm indurated ulcer with a keratotic rim on the lower lip of a 68 year old former pipe smoker, with a firm submental node. The upper lip sits in the shadow of the nose and favors basal cell carcinoma, whose pearly translucency comes from thin tumor over an induced vascular stroma and whose nodal spread would be very unusual. The lower lip takes direct sun on thin vermilion epithelium, so the second lesion is squamous cell carcinoma, with keratin production explaining the crust and dermal lymphatic access explaining the node."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-07-C04","obj":"IN-13-G.4","title":"Melanoma: risk factors and the clinicopathologic subtypes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Melanoma is a malignancy of melanocytes, the neural crest derived pigment cells of the epidermal basal layer, mucosa, uvea, and meninges. It accounts for a small minority of skin cancers but most skin cancer deaths, because unlike the keratinocyte cancers it metastasizes readily once it acquires the capacity. Its risk profile differs from squamous cell carcinoma in one way worth holding onto: what matters most is intense intermittent exposure, meaning blistering sunburns, especially in childhood and adolescence, rather than total lifetime dose."},{"type":"p","text":"Host factors multiply that exposure risk. Fair skin that burns rather than tans (Fitzpatrick types I and II), red or blond hair, light eyes, and freckling all reflect a pheomelanin-predominant pigment phenotype that absorbs ultraviolet poorly and itself generates reactive oxygen species; melanin at low concentration gives little photoprotection. A high total nevus count and the presence of clinically atypical or dysplastic nevi mark both melanocyte proliferative tendency and past exposure, and dysplastic nevi are markers of risk far more often than they are direct precursors. A first degree family history raises risk roughly twofold. Germline CDKN2A mutation, which eliminates the p16 brake on CDK4 and cyclin D, causes familial melanoma with multiple primaries and an associated risk of pancreatic cancer, and xeroderma pigmentosum raises melanoma risk alongside the keratinocyte cancers."},{"type":"p","text":"Somatic drivers sort by site and exposure pattern. Melanomas on intermittently exposed trunk and limb skin, in younger patients and sometimes arising in a nevus, frequently carry BRAF V600E, which activates the MAP kinase pathway; about half of cutaneous melanomas are BRAF mutant and roughly another quarter carry NRAS mutations. Tumors on chronically sun-damaged head and neck skin in older patients more often carry NF1 or other non-V600E drivers and show the highest mutational burden of any common tumor. Acral and mucosal melanomas arise where ultraviolet barely reaches, show few ultraviolet signature mutations, and are enriched for KIT alterations, which is why their incidence is similar across skin colors while cutaneous melanoma incidence is not."},{"type":"table","title":"Melanoma subtypes by site, exposure pattern, and behavior","cols":["Subtype","Typical site and patient","Ultraviolet relationship","Growth behavior"],"rows":[["Superficial spreading","Trunk in men, legs in women, middle age; most common","Intermittent intense","Long radial phase, often thin if caught early"],["Nodular","Any site, often trunk or head and neck","Intermittent","Vertical growth from the outset, thick at diagnosis"],["Lentigo maligna","Cheek, nose, temple of older sun-damaged adults","Chronic cumulative","Very long in situ phase; invasive form is lentigo maligna melanoma"],["Acral lentiginous","Palms, soles, nail beds; all skin colors","Not ultraviolet driven","Long radial phase but diagnosed late, so thick"],["Mucosal, including genital","Oral, sinonasal, anorectal, vulvar, penile mucosa","Not ultraviolet driven","Often multifocal and advanced, poorest prognosis"]],"caption":"Subtype predicts site and tempo, not inherent lethality: once thickness and ulceration are accounted for, outcome follows depth rather than subtype name."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinical recognition uses the ABCDE features: asymmetry, border irregularity or notching, color variation within one lesion, diameter greater than about 6 mm, and evolution, meaning documented change in size, shape, color, or symptoms. Evolution carries the most weight because it reports active clonal growth, and the ugly duckling observation, a lesion that does not resemble the patient's other nevi, catches tumors that fail the other criteria. Subungual melanoma presents as a longitudinal pigmented nail band with pigment extending onto the adjacent cuticle and proximal nail fold, which distinguishes it from trauma or benign melanonychia. Amelanotic melanoma, which has lost pigment production, is a pink or skin-colored nodule and is a classic missed diagnosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why do acral and mucosal melanoma occur at similar rates in people with deeply and lightly pigmented skin, while superficial spreading melanoma does not?","explain":"Trace which risk factor each subtype depends on, and ask whether epidermal melanin can modify that factor at the site in question.","check":"Compare your reasoning with the paragraph on somatic drivers by site and the subtype table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-07-C05","obj":"IN-13-G.5","title":"Growth phases, Breslow thickness, and melanocytic immunostains","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Melanoma progression is best understood as two biologically different phases rather than a steady increase in size. In the radial growth phase, malignant melanocytes proliferate within the epidermis and spread laterally, with single cells or small nests dropping into the papillary dermis but forming no expansile mass. These cells remain dependent on keratinocyte-derived signals, so they have essentially no metastatic capacity, and a melanoma still confined to this phase has not yet left its site of origin."},{"type":"p","text":"The vertical growth phase begins when one clone acquires the ability to proliferate as a mass within the dermis. This requires loss of keratinocyte dependence, a switch from E-cadherin to N-cadherin that lets the cell engage dermal fibroblasts and endothelium, matrix metalloproteinase secretion, and recruitment of new vessels. The clinical correlate is a nodule arising within a previously flat pigmented lesion. Because the dermis holds the lymphatic plexuses and vessels the epidermis lacks, a tumor reaching it gains both routes of spread: nodal spread usually comes first, which is why the regional nodal basin is sampled for staging, and hematogenous spread reaches lung, liver, brain, bone, and, characteristically, skin and small bowel."},{"type":"flow","title":"Radial to vertical growth and the acquisition of metastatic capacity","steps":["Atypical melanocytes proliferate along the basal layer, in situ","Radial phase: lateral spread, single cells in papillary dermis, no mass","One clone loses keratinocyte dependence, E-cadherin to N-cadherin switch","Vertical phase: expansile dermal nodule with its own stroma and vessels","Tumor reaches dermal lymphatics and blood vessels","Regional nodal spread, then hematogenous spread"],"caption":"Metastatic capacity is acquired at a specific step, not accumulated gradually with lesion diameter."},{"type":"p","text":"Breslow thickness quantifies how far the vertical phase has progressed. It is measured in millimeters from the top of the granular layer to the deepest invasive tumor cell, and from the base of an ulcer when the epidermis is lost. It is the strongest prognostic variable in localized melanoma because it is a direct proxy for how much contact the tumor has had with dermal lymphatics and vessels, and it sets T classification at 1.0, 2.0, and 4.0 mm. Ulceration independently worsens prognosis at every thickness, and a high dermal mitotic rate also predicts worse outcome. Clark level, the deepest anatomic layer reached, correlates with outcome but is less reproducible."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two lesions of the same diameter","text":"Two pigmented lesions are each 9 mm across. Lesion A is a flat, irregularly pigmented macule with atypical melanocytes along the basal layer and a few single cells in the papillary dermis, Breslow 0.3 mm, no ulceration. Lesion B is a smooth dark nodule, Breslow 3.2 mm, with surface ulceration. Diameter is identical, so it carries no prognostic information. Lesion A is still in radial growth, with no expansile dermal mass and no meaningful access to lymphatics, so the tumor is almost certainly still confined to the site of origin. Lesion B is well into vertical growth, and ulceration marks outgrowth of its blood supply, so nodal and distant spread are both substantial possibilities."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunohistochemistry confirms melanocytic lineage when a tumor is amelanotic, spindled, or poorly differentiated. The principle is to pair a sensitive marker with a specific one: S-100 is highly sensitive but also stains Schwann and Langerhans cells, so a negative result argues strongly against melanoma while a positive one is not diagnostic by itself, whereas HMB-45 and Melan-A detect melanosomal proteins and are more specific."},{"type":"table","title":"Melanocytic immunostains","cols":["Stain","Target","Strength","Limitation"],"rows":[["S-100","Calcium-binding protein family","High sensitivity, useful screen","Also positive in nerve sheath and Langerhans cell tumors"],["HMB-45","gp100 premelanosomal glycoprotein","Good specificity for melanocytes","Often negative in desmoplastic melanoma"],["Melan-A (MART-1)","Melanosome membrane protein","Good specificity, stains most melanomas","Also marks steroidogenic cells"],["SOX10","Neural crest transcription factor","Nuclear, retained in spindled tumors","Also positive in nerve sheath tumors"]],"caption":"Pair a sensitive marker with a specific one when pigment is absent."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-07-C06","obj":"IN-13-G.6","title":"Mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome: cutaneous T cell lymphoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mycosis fungoides is the most common cutaneous T cell lymphoma and the one malignant skin tumor in this chapter that arises from an immune cell rather than from a keratinocyte or a melanocyte. The malignant population is a clone of mature CD4 positive memory helper T cells that carry skin-homing receptors, chiefly cutaneous lymphocyte antigen and the chemokine receptors CCR4 and CCR10, whose ligands are produced constitutively by dermal endothelium and keratinocytes. That receptor profile explains the disease's defining behavior: the clone keeps returning to and persisting in the skin rather than filling nodes and marrow, which is the reverse of most lymphomas. It presents in older adults, is somewhat more common in men, and is frequently mistaken for eczema, psoriasis, or tinea for years before diagnosis, partly because early biopsies are often nonspecific."},{"type":"p","text":"Progression occurs in three clinical stages over years to decades. Patch stage lesions are flat, scaly, often itchy erythematous patches with a predilection for sun-protected areas such as the buttocks, lower trunk, and upper thighs, a distribution that distinguishes them from the photodistributed keratinocyte cancers. Plaque stage lesions become infiltrated, well demarcated, and sometimes annular. Tumor stage brings nodules that may ulcerate, and at this point extracutaneous spread to nodes, liver, spleen, and lung becomes likely. Large cell transformation, in which the small cerebriform clone converts to a high-grade large cell lymphoma, marks a sharp change in behavior."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stages of mycosis fungoides","steps":["Clonal skin-homing CD4 T cells enter the epidermis","Patch stage: scaly itchy patches on sun-protected skin","Plaque stage: infiltrated demarcated plaques, Pautrier microabscesses","Tumor stage: ulcerating nodules, nodal and visceral spread","Large cell transformation or leukemic Sézary phase"],"caption":"Stage reflects how far the clone has escaped its dependence on the epidermal environment."},{"type":"p","text":"The histologic hallmarks follow directly from epidermotropism. Atypical lymphocytes with enlarged, hyperchromatic, deeply folded nuclei, described as cerebriform, form a band-like infiltrate in the upper dermis and migrate into the epidermis as single cells and as discrete intraepidermal clusters, the Pautrier microabscesses. Unlike the spongiotic dermatitis it mimics, there is little intercellular edema around these cells. Immunophenotype is typically CD3 positive, CD4 positive, CD8 negative, with characteristic loss of CD7 and often CD26; clonality is confirmed by T cell receptor gene rearrangement studies, which matter because reactive dermatoses can look similar."},{"type":"p","text":"Sézary syndrome is the leukemic, erythrodermic variant, best regarded as a related entity rather than simply the end stage of mycosis fungoides. It presents with generalized exfoliative erythroderma involving nearly the whole skin surface, intense pruritus, lymphadenopathy, often palmoplantar hyperkeratosis and nail dystrophy, and circulating malignant cells with the same cerebriform nuclei, the Sézary cells, usually defined as at least 1,000 per microliter with a demonstrable clone. Confluent erythroderma has systemic consequences that are pure physiology: cutaneous vasodilation across the entire surface increases insensible water loss and radiant and convective heat loss, so these patients feel cold and shiver even when core temperature is normal or slightly raised, high-output cardiac failure can follow the large shunt, and transepidermal protein and fluid loss plus barrier breakdown allows hypoalbuminemia and Staphylococcus aureus sepsis."},{"type":"table","title":"Mycosis fungoides compared with Sézary syndrome","cols":["Feature","Mycosis fungoides","Sézary syndrome"],"rows":[["Skin involvement","Discrete patches, plaques, then tumors","Generalized erythroderma of nearly the whole surface"],["Blood involvement","Absent or minimal","At least about 1,000 Sézary cells per microliter with a clone"],["Nodes","Late, in tumor stage","Lymphadenopathy typical at presentation"],["Cell","Cerebriform CD4 T cell, often CD7 negative","Same cerebriform CD4 phenotype, circulating"],["Course","Indolent for years in early stages","Aggressive, with systemic consequences of erythroderma"]],"caption":"Both share one malignant cell type; what differs is whether the clone stays confined to skin or circulates."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These four tumors make one coherent argument about carcinogenesis in an organ that is deliberately exposed to a mutagen. Anatomy and histology supply the cells of origin and predict the lesion: basal keratinocytes and follicular stem cells produce palisaded basaloid nests and spare palms, soles, and mucosa; differentiating keratinocytes produce keratin pearls and intercellular bridges; neural crest derived melanocytes produce pigmented lesions wherever they reside, including mucosa and nail beds; and skin-homing memory T cells produce an epidermotropic lymphoma. Biochemistry and genetics supply the drivers, and each follows a different logic: loss of an inhibitor in the hedgehog pathway, stepwise accumulation of ultraviolet signature mutations beginning with TP53, and MAP kinase activation with loss of the p16 brake on the cell cycle. Immunology supplies the third variable, since T cell surveillance normally eliminates mutant and virus-infected keratinocyte clones, which is why transplant immunosuppression raises squamous cell carcinoma incidence sharply and changes its behavior."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathophysiology then explains why three tumors with the same ultraviolet cause behave so differently. Metastatic potential is not proportional to how abnormal cells look but to whether the tumor can grow independently of its original environment: basal cell carcinoma remains stroma-dependent and almost never metastasizes, squamous cell carcinoma is less dependent and spreads through lymphatics at a rate set by site, size, depth, differentiation, and host immunity, and melanoma acquires independence at an identifiable step, the vertical growth phase, which is why a millimeter measurement outperforms every descriptive feature in localized disease. The limits of these rules are worth stating plainly. Risk factors give probabilities, not diagnoses, so biopsy decides; small samples can be genuinely ambiguous, particularly keratoacanthoma against well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma and early mycosis fungoides against a reactive dermatitis; and a single immunostain never establishes lineage on its own."}],"summary":["Basal cell carcinoma is the most common human cancer: ultraviolet damage releases hedgehog signaling by inactivating PTCH1 or activating SMO, giving pearly telangiectatic papules with rolled borders and palisaded basaloid nests that invade locally but almost never metastasize.","Germline PTCH1 loss causes Gorlin syndrome, with numerous early basal cell carcinomas, odontogenic keratocysts, palmar and plantar pits, and medulloblastoma.","Squamous cell carcinoma risk follows cumulative ultraviolet dose: pyrimidine dimers that escape nucleotide excision repair fix C to T mutations, TP53 is hit early, and actinic keratosis is the dysplastic precursor.","Defective repair (xeroderma pigmentosum), lost immune surveillance (transplant immunosuppression), persistent human papillomavirus, arsenic, and chronic scars or ulcers (Marjolin ulcer) all accelerate that same sequence.","The lower lip takes direct sun on thin vermilion epithelium and favors squamous cell carcinoma, while the nose-shaded upper lip and inner canthus favor basal cell carcinoma.","Keratin pearls and intercellular bridges identify squamous differentiation; Bowen disease is full-thickness atypia with an intact basement membrane and no metastatic capacity, and keratoacanthoma is a symmetric crateriform nodule that grows in weeks and may regress.","Melanoma risk is driven by intense intermittent ultraviolet exposure, fair pheomelanin-predominant skin, many or dysplastic nevi, family history, CDKN2A loss, and xeroderma pigmentosum; BRAF V600E predominates on intermittently exposed skin while acral and mucosal tumors are not ultraviolet driven and favor KIT alterations.","Radial growth spreads laterally and cannot metastasize; vertical growth creates an expansile dermal nodule that reaches lymphatics and vessels, so Breslow thickness, with ulceration and mitotic rate, carries the prognosis in localized disease.","S-100 is the sensitive melanocytic marker, while HMB-45 and Melan-A are the specific ones, with SOX10 useful in spindled and desmoplastic tumors.","Mycosis fungoides is a clonal skin-homing CD4 T cell lymphoma progressing from patch to plaque to tumor with cerebriform cells in Pautrier microabscesses, and Sézary syndrome is its erythrodermic leukemic form with circulating Sézary cells."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, tumors of the epidermis and melanocytic tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, carcinogenesis and the molecular basis of cancer"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia; and the section on skin disorders"},{"book":"Junqueira Basic Histology","where":"Skin, epidermis and its cells"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to tumors; leukocyte circulation and migration into tissues"}]},"IN-13-H":{"unit":"IN-13-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08","title":"Infectious Skin Diseases","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the infections and infection-linked reactions of the skin: bacterial, fungal, viral, mycobacterial, toxin mediated, and parasitic. The organizing idea is depth and barrier. Each organism reaches a particular layer, and the layer it reaches sets the appearance of the lesion, the organism you should name, and the complication that follows. Board questions give a setting (a child with crusted lesions, a wrestler with recurrent abscesses, a gardener pricked by a thorn) and ask for the organism, its virulence factor, the histologic level of the split, or the sequela to expect. Work from exposure to barrier breach to layer involved, and most of these questions answer themselves.","prerequisites":["Layers of the epidermis and the structure of the dermis, hypodermis, and pilosebaceous unit (IN-1, IN-2)","Desmosomes, desmoglein isoforms, and keratinocyte adhesion (IN-1)","Innate skin defenses: stratum corneum barrier, acid mantle, defensins, Langerhans cells (IN-4)","Gram stain morphology and virulence factors of staphylococci, streptococci, and clostridia (IN-12)","Th1 versus Th2 polarization, Th17 and IL-17, and delayed type hypersensitivity (IM-6, IM-10)"],"connections":[{"to":"Skin immunology and barrier function (IN-4)","how":"Every infection in this chapter begins with a breach in the stratum corneum or a follicular opening, so the barrier defenses determine who gets infected and how deeply."},{"to":"Immunodeficiency (IM-15)","how":"The pattern of skin infection reports the arm of immunity that failed: Th17 defects give chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, T cell defects give extensive warts and molluscum, and neutrophil defects give recurrent abscesses."},{"to":"Renal pathology (UR-12)","how":"Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis follows impetigo as well as pharyngitis, so a skin infection can present weeks later as hematuria."},{"to":"Immune mediated blistering disease (IN-13-C)","how":"Staphylococcal exfoliative toxin cleaves desmoglein 1, the same target as the autoantibody of pemphigus foliaceus, so toxin and autoimmunity produce the same split level."},{"to":"Nervous system infection (NE-20)","how":"Tetanospasmin, herpes zoster latency in the dorsal root ganglion, and leprous nerve damage all enter through the skin and act on the nervous system."}],"terms":[{"term":"Comedo","def":"A noninflamed plug of keratin and sebum in a follicle, open (blackhead) when the orifice is dilated and closed (whitehead) when it is not."},{"term":"Intertriginous","def":"Involving skin folds where two surfaces touch, such as the groin, axilla, inframammary crease, and neck folds."},{"term":"Satellite pustule","def":"A small pustule just outside the main plaque, produced by local spread of Candida and highly suggestive of candidal infection."},{"term":"Panniculitis","def":"Inflammation of subcutaneous fat, septal when centered on the connective tissue septa and lobular when centered on the fat lobules."},{"term":"Furuncle","def":"A deep abscess of a single hair follicle, extending into the dermis and subcutaneous tissue (a boil)."},{"term":"Carbuncle","def":"Several adjacent furuncles coalescing into one inflammatory mass that drains through multiple sinus tracts."},{"term":"Exfoliative toxin","def":"A staphylococcal serine protease that cleaves desmoglein 1 and separates keratinocytes within the granular layer."},{"term":"Dermatophyte","def":"A keratinophilic mold of the genera Trichophyton, Microsporum, or Epidermophyton that infects only keratinized tissue: stratum corneum, hair, and nail."},{"term":"Koilocyte","def":"A squamous cell with a raisin like, wrinkled hyperchromatic nucleus and a clear perinuclear halo, the cytopathic signature of human papillomavirus."},{"term":"Kerion","def":"A boggy, tender, pus filled inflammatory mass of the scalp in tinea capitis, representing a vigorous host response to the dermatophyte."},{"term":"Crepitus","def":"A crackling sensation on palpation produced by gas in soft tissue, typical of gas forming infection such as clostridial myonecrosis."},{"term":"Lymphangitis","def":"Infection tracking up dermal lymphatic channels, seen as tender red streaks running proximally from a wound toward regional nodes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C01","obj":"IN-13-H.1","title":"Acne vulgaris: four steps in the pilosebaceous unit","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acne is a disease of the pilosebaceous unit: a hair follicle, its sebaceous gland, and the short duct joining them. The unit is dense on the face, chest, upper back, and shoulders, which is exactly where acne appears. Sebaceous glands are holocrine, meaning the whole cell disintegrates to release sebum, a mixture of triglycerides, wax esters, and squalene. Gland size and sebum output are under androgen control, which is why acne begins at adrenarche and puberty and why it tracks androgen excess at any age."},{"type":"p","text":"Four events must combine, and no single one produces the disease. First, keratinocytes lining the follicular infundibulum proliferate and fail to shed, so corneocytes stay stuck together and obstruct the duct; this microcomedone is the invisible primary lesion. Second, androgens, with testosterone converted to the more potent dihydrotestosterone by 5-alpha reductase in the gland, enlarge the gland and raise sebum output, so material accumulates behind the plug. Third, the sealed, lipid rich, oxygen poor follicle is an ideal niche for Cutibacterium acnes, an anaerobic gram positive commensal rod whose density rises sharply inside the comedone. Fourth, inflammation follows: bacterial lipases release irritating free fatty acids from sebum triglycerides, bacterial components signal through toll-like receptor 2 to release interleukin 1 beta, interleukin 8, and tumor necrosis factor alpha, and neutrophils arrive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of acne vulgaris","steps":["Androgens enlarge sebaceous glands and raise sebum output","Follicular keratinocytes retain and plug the infundibulum (microcomedone)","Sebum and keratin distend the follicle into an open or closed comedo","Cutibacterium acnes proliferates in the anaerobic, lipid rich lumen","Lipases release free fatty acids and TLR2 signaling recruits neutrophils","The follicle wall ruptures into the dermis, producing papules, pustules, and nodules"],"caption":"Obstruction comes first and inflammation last, so comedones and inflamed lesions coexist."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion type reports how far the sequence has gone. Comedones are noninflamed plugs, while rupture of the distended follicle spills keratin, sebum, and bacteria into the dermis, where they behave as foreign material; superficial rupture gives an inflamed papule or pustule and deep rupture a tender nodule or draining sinus. Because nodular lesions destroy dermal collagen and the follicle itself, they heal with atrophic or hypertrophic scars, while comedones do not scar."},{"type":"table","title":"Acne lesions by stage","cols":["Lesion","What it is","Inflamed","Scars"],"rows":[["Microcomedone","Keratin plug in the infundibulum, not visible clinically","No","No"],["Open comedo (blackhead)","Dilated orifice with oxidized keratin and melanin","No","No"],["Closed comedo (whitehead)","Plugged follicle under intact epithelium","No","No"],["Papule","Superficial follicular rupture into the dermis","Yes","Rarely"],["Pustule","Neutrophil rich papule with visible purulence","Yes","Rarely"],["Nodule or cyst","Deep rupture with dermal destruction and sinus tracts","Yes","Yes"]],"caption":"Obstruction alone is noninflamed and reversible; rupture into the dermis inflames and scars."},{"type":"p","text":"Anything that raises androgen effect or follicular keratinization aggravates acne. Polycystic ovary syndrome raises ovarian androgen output and lowers sex hormone binding globulin, so free testosterone rises and acne appears with hirsutism and irregular cycles; congenital adrenal hyperplasia and androgen secreting tumors do the same more abruptly. Glucocorticoids, endogenous in Cushing syndrome or exogenous, produce a monomorphic eruption of follicular papules and pustules, often truncal and lacking comedones, reflecting direct steroid induced follicular inflammation rather than the full four step sequence. Acne beginning abruptly in an adult woman, or with virilization, points to an androgen source needing evaluation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 23 year old woman has new jawline acne with hirsutism and cycles every 45 days. Which of the four steps is being driven, and why does free androgen rise more than total testosterone suggests?","explain":"The androgen driven sebum step. In polycystic ovary syndrome, hyperinsulinemia stimulates ovarian androgen production and suppresses hepatic sex hormone binding globulin, so more of the total testosterone circulates free and reaches the 5-alpha reductase of the gland.","check":"The paragraph on aggravating causes and the androgen control of gland size in the opening paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C02","obj":"IN-13-H.2","title":"Cutaneous and mucosal candidiasis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Candida albicans is a dimorphic commensal yeast of the gastrointestinal tract, mouth, and vagina that becomes pathogenic when the environment or the host changes. It is not a dermatophyte and does not digest keratin; it needs moisture, warmth, and a macerated or breached stratum corneum. That requirement explains the distribution. Candidal intertrigo occupies the skin folds, where apposed surfaces trap sweat, raise local pH, and soften the barrier: the inguinal and gluteal creases, axillae, inframammary folds, abdominal pannus, interdigital web spaces, and the angles of the mouth. The plaque is described as beefy red, moist, and sharply bordered with a fringe of loose scale, and the finding that makes the diagnosis is a ring of small satellite pustules just beyond the plaque edge, produced as yeast spreads onto adjacent skin."},{"type":"p","text":"In the diaper area this anatomy separates two diseases. Candidal diaper dermatitis involves the depths of the inguinal and gluteal folds, because that is where moisture collects, and it carries satellite pustules. Irritant (contact) diaper dermatitis is caused by urine, feces, fecal proteases and lipases, and friction acting on skin that touches the diaper, so it involves the convex surfaces of the buttocks, lower abdomen, and thighs and characteristically spares the creases, which the diaper does not contact. Candidal involvement often follows irritant dermatitis, since a damaged barrier invites colonizing yeast."},{"type":"table","title":"Diaper dermatitis: candidal versus irritant","cols":["Feature","Candidal","Irritant contact"],"rows":[["Folds","Involved, often most intense","Spared"],["Convex surfaces","May be involved","Involved, where the diaper touches"],["Border","Sharp with a scaly fringe","Ill defined, glazed erythema"],["Satellite pustules","Present","Absent"],["Main driver","Candida albicans in a moist fold","Urine, feces, proteases, friction"]],"caption":"The folds answer the question: moisture collects in them, and irritants cannot reach them."},{"type":"p","text":"On mucosa the same organism produces thrush: adherent white plaques on the buccal mucosa, palate, and tongue that scrape off to leave an erythematous, sometimes bleeding base, which distinguishes them from the fixed white plaques of leukoplakia or oral hairy leukoplakia. Scraping a lesion and treating it with potassium hydroxide shows budding yeast with pseudohyphae; germ tube formation in serum is the classic identifying feature of C albicans in the laboratory."},{"type":"p","text":"Because Candida is already present, candidiasis is a report on host defense. Neutrophils and macrophages handle invasive candidiasis, while mucosal and cutaneous control depends on Th17 cells and interleukin 17, which drive epithelial antimicrobial peptide production and neutrophil recruitment. Defects along the interleukin 17 axis, including autosomal dominant hyper IgE (STAT3) syndrome, STAT1 gain of function mutations, and autoimmune polyendocrinopathy with autoantibodies to interleukin 17, produce chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis: persistent, recurring, treatment resistant candidal infection of skin, nails, and mucosa without disseminated disease. Thrush in an adult who has no local explanation, or esophageal candidiasis, points to T cell deficiency, most importantly advanced HIV infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for candidiasis and the defect each creates","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Diabetes mellitus","Glucose rich secretions, impaired neutrophil function, moist folds in obesity"],["Broad spectrum antibiotics","Loss of competing bacterial flora removes colonization resistance"],["Glucocorticoids, including inhaled","Suppressed local cell mediated immunity; inhaled drug deposits in the mouth"],["Advanced HIV infection","Loss of CD4 T cell help, including Th17 function, giving thrush and esophagitis"],["Infancy and old age","Immature or waning immunity, occlusion by diapers, dentures, or saliva pooling"],["Th17 axis defects","Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis with intact control of deep infection"]],"caption":"Local moisture and sugar explain intertrigo; T cell and interleukin 17 failure explains persistent mucosal disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C03","obj":"IN-13-H.3","title":"Erysipelas versus cellulitis: depth sets the border","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Erysipelas and cellulitis are both spreading bacterial infections of the skin, and the single fact that organizes every difference between them is the depth of tissue involved. Erysipelas occupies the upper dermis and the superficial dermal lymphatics. Because the dermis is a dense, relatively confined compartment and because infection travels along discrete lymphatic channels, the edematous inflamed tissue is elevated above the surrounding skin and its edge is sharply demarcated, so the border can be traced with a pen. The plaque is bright red, hot, tense, and tender, classically on the face in a butterfly distribution or on the lower leg, and systemic symptoms (abrupt fever, chills) often precede the skin findings by hours. Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococcus) is the usual organism, with other beta hemolytic streptococci next."},{"type":"p","text":"Cellulitis involves the deep dermis and the subcutaneous fat. The hypodermis is a loose, septate, fatty compartment through which edema diffuses without boundary, so cellulitis is warm, red, swollen, and tender with borders that fade gradually into normal skin and cannot be outlined. It is deeper and flatter, less often elevated, and usually more indolent in onset. Both S pyogenes and Staphylococcus aureus cause cellulitis, with staphylococci favored when there is a focal purulent center, an abscess, or a penetrating wound, and streptococci favored in diffuse nonpurulent disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Erysipelas versus cellulitis","cols":["Feature","Erysipelas","Cellulitis"],"rows":[["Depth","Upper dermis and superficial lymphatics","Deep dermis and subcutaneous fat"],["Border","Raised, sharply demarcated","Flat, ill defined, fades out"],["Color and onset","Bright red, abrupt, fever often first","Pink to red, more gradual"],["Usual organism","Streptococcus pyogenes","S pyogenes or Staphylococcus aureus"],["Typical sites","Face, lower leg","Lower leg, any breached site"],["Lymphatic signs","Lymphangitic streaks and node tenderness common","Possible but less prominent"]],"caption":"A tissue plane difference produces every clinical difference, including which organism is more likely."},{"type":"p","text":"Neither infection occurs through intact skin, so the portal of entry is part of the diagnosis and is where board questions hide the answer. Interdigital fissures from tinea pedis are the classic portal for recurrent leg cellulitis, and the bacterial episodes keep recurring for as long as that fissure stays open. Other portals are traumatic wounds and surgical incisions, venous stasis and diabetic ulcers, insect and animal bites, intravenous catheter sites and injection drug use, eczema or psoriasis with excoriation, and toe web maceration. Anything that impairs lymphatic drainage, including prior erysipelas, lymph node dissection, chronic lymphedema, and venous insufficiency, both predisposes to infection and is made worse by it: each episode scars lymphatics, raises local protein rich edema, and sets up the next episode."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Why erysipelas and cellulitis recur in the same leg","steps":["Barrier breach (tinea pedis fissure, ulcer, bite)","Streptococci enter dermis and dermal lymphatics","Inflammation and fibrosis obliterate lymphatic channels","Protein rich lymphedema accumulates in the limb","Stagnant fluid and stretched skin impair local defense","Lower threshold for the next infection at the same site"],"caption":"Each episode damages the drainage that would have prevented it, which is why recurrence clusters in one limb."},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes spreads through tissue rather than forming abscesses because of its enzymes: hyaluronidase degrades the hyaluronic acid of the dermal matrix, streptokinase dissolves fibrin that would wall off the infection, and DNases break down the neutrophil extracellular traps that bind bacteria. Its M protein resists phagocytosis and binds fibrinogen, and its hyaluronic acid capsule mimics host matrix. Staphylococcus aureus, by contrast, makes coagulase and clumping factor, which promote local fibrin deposition and walling off, which is the mechanistic reason staphylococcal skin infection tends toward discrete abscesses while streptococcal infection spreads diffusely."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C04","obj":"IN-13-H.4","title":"Complications of cellulitis, including necrotizing fasciitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cellulitis can extend in three directions, and each gives a recognizable complication. Extending and coalescing, it liquefies tissue into an abscess: a fluctuant collection of neutrophils, necrotic debris, and organisms, usually Staphylococcus aureus, walled off by granulation tissue. Because an avascular pus filled cavity is cut off from circulating defenses and its acidic, hypoxic interior impairs neutrophil killing, the collection persists on its own, so cellulitis that does not resolve raises the question of an occult abscess. Extending along dermal lymphatics it produces lymphangitis, with tender red streaks running proximally and node tenderness. Extending into vessels it produces bacteremia, with fever, rigors, and risk of seeding endocarditis, vertebral osteomyelitis, or septic arthritis, and in severe disease the superantigen mediated toxic shock syndromes."},{"type":"p","text":"The complication that must be recognized immediately is necrotizing fasciitis, which spreads along the superficial fascia rather than through fat. Fascial planes are avascular, loose, and continuous over long distances, so organisms and toxins travel rapidly with little resistance. The infection thromboses the perforating vessels that cross the fascia to supply the overlying skin and the nerves running with them, and two consequences follow directly. Deep tissue is dying while the surface still looks mildly involved, so pain is out of proportion to the physical findings, the single most useful early clue. Then, as cutaneous nerves infarct, the excruciating area becomes anesthetic, and skin that was red turns dusky, then bullous, then frankly necrotic."},{"type":"flow","title":"From breach to necrotizing fasciitis","steps":["Organisms enter through a wound, ulcer, or surgical site","Spread along the avascular superficial fascia, not through fat","Toxins and inflammation thrombose perforating vessels","Fat and fascia necrose while overlying skin still looks mild","Pain out of proportion, then cutaneous anesthesia","Dusky skin, hemorrhagic bullae, crepitus, systemic toxicity"],"caption":"The gap between severe pain and unimpressive skin is created by the anatomy of the fascial plane."},{"type":"table","title":"Cellulitis versus necrotizing fasciitis","cols":["Feature","Cellulitis","Necrotizing fasciitis"],"rows":[["Plane","Deep dermis and subcutaneous fat","Superficial fascia, with secondary skin necrosis"],["Pain","Proportionate to redness","Out of proportion, later anesthetic"],["Rate of change","Hours to days","Hours, with visible advance"],["Skin","Red, warm, swollen","Dusky, bullous, hemorrhagic, crepitus if gas forming"],["Systemic state","Fever, otherwise well","Hypotension, confusion, organ failure"],["Organisms","S pyogenes or S aureus","Type I polymicrobial (diabetes, abdomen) or type II S pyogenes, with or without S aureus; Clostridium species when gas is present"]],"caption":"Severity that outruns appearance is the discriminating feature, not the degree of redness."},{"type":"p","text":"Two forms are distinguished. Type I is polymicrobial, mixing aerobes with anaerobes, and arises in older adults with diabetes, peripheral vascular disease, or recent abdominal or perineal surgery; Fournier gangrene of the perineum is this form. Type II is monomicrobial Streptococcus pyogenes, sometimes with S aureus, and can strike healthy young people after minor trauma, often with streptococcal toxic shock from superantigen release. Crepitus and gas on imaging mean gas forming organisms, classically Clostridium perfringens or mixed anaerobes. Laboratory clues reflect tissue destruction rather than the organism: marked leukocytosis with a left shift, creatine kinase release from involved muscle, hyponatremia, acidosis, and a rising lactate."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two red legs","text":"Patient A has a hot, bright red, sharply bordered plaque on the shin, fever of 38.6 C (101.5 F), and tenderness that matches the redness. Patient B has a dull red, slightly swollen thigh, pain scored 10 of 10 that required escalating analgesia overnight, a heart rate of 130, blood pressure of 85 over 50, and a patch that is now numb to light touch. Patient A has erysipelas: the sharp raised border places the infection in the upper dermis, and pain proportional to findings means the process is where it looks. Patient B has necrotizing fasciitis: pain and shock exceeding visible disease, with developing anesthesia, indicate fascial spread with thrombosis of perforating vessels and cutaneous nerves, so the surface understates what has died beneath it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C05","obj":"IN-13-H.5","title":"Erythema nodosum as septal panniculitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Erythema nodosum is not an infection of the skin; it is a delayed hypersensitivity reaction in subcutaneous fat to an antigen located elsewhere. Subcutaneous fat is organized into lobules of adipocytes separated by fibrous septa that carry the arterioles, venules, and lymphatics supplying the fat. Inflammation confined to those septa is a septal panniculitis, and erythema nodosum is the prototype. Early lesions show neutrophils and edema in widened septa; established lesions show lymphocytes, histiocytes, and small histiocytic granulomas within the widened septa. Critically, there is no vasculitis: vessel walls are not destroyed by fibrinoid necrosis, and the fat lobules themselves are spared."},{"type":"p","text":"That histology predicts the clinical picture exactly. Because the inflammation is deep, the lesions are palpable nodules felt more easily than seen, with ill defined edges and overlying erythema rather than a sharp epidermal border. Because septal vessels are inflamed but not necrosed and the lobules survive, the lesions do not ulcerate, do not suppurate, and resolve over two to eight weeks like a deep bruise, leaving transient yellow brown discoloration but no scar and no atrophy. They are strikingly tender and warm, usually 1 to 5 cm, bilateral, and distributed over the pretibial surface of the shins, where fat is thin over bone and septal anatomy is prominent. Fever, malaise, and arthralgias commonly accompany the eruption."},{"type":"table","title":"Erythema nodosum versus mimics","cols":["Feature","Erythema nodosum","Lobular panniculitis or vasculitic nodule"],"rows":[["Site of inflammation","Fat septa, no vasculitis","Fat lobules, or vessel wall destruction"],["Course of the lesion","Resolves without ulcer or scar","Often ulcerates, drains, or scars"],["Distribution","Bilateral anterior shins","Calves, thighs, or scattered"],["Typical setting","Reaction to infection or systemic disease","Erythema induratum (tuberculosis), pancreatic panniculitis, polyarteritis"]],"caption":"A nodule on the shin that ulcerates or scars is not erythema nodosum, and that single observation redirects the differential."},{"type":"p","text":"The value of recognizing erythema nodosum is that it is a signal of something systemic. The antigen may be microbial, from a granulomatous disease, from bowel inflammation, or hormonal. Streptococcal pharyngitis is the most common identifiable trigger overall, particularly in children, with the eruption appearing two to three weeks after the sore throat. In regions where they are endemic, coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis are frequent causes, and in coccidioidomycosis erythema nodosum actually marks a vigorous cell mediated response and a favorable prognosis. Sarcoidosis is a major cause, and the combination of erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, fever, and arthritis is named Lofgren syndrome. Tuberculosis, inflammatory bowel disease (both Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis, often tracking bowel activity), pregnancy and exogenous estrogen or progestin, Yersinia and other enteric infections, and Behcet disease round out the list; a substantial fraction remains idiopathic."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a distant antigen inflames the fat septa","steps":["Antigen from infection, granulomatous disease, or bowel inflammation","Immune complexes and sensitized T cells circulate","Deposition and T cell recruitment in septal vessels of the hypodermis","Neutrophils then granulomatous inflammation widen the septa","Tender erythematous nodules over the anterior shins","Resolution without lobular necrosis, so no ulcer or scar"],"caption":"The eruption is an immune response to an antigen elsewhere, which is why the search is for the trigger rather than for an organism in the skin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C06","obj":"IN-13-H.6","title":"Folliculitis, furuncles, carbuncles, and abscesses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The follicular ostium is a funnel of epidermis that opens the barrier, and Staphylococcus aureus, which colonizes the anterior nares and moist skin of a large minority of people, enters through it. The resulting lesions form one family graded only by depth and lateral extent, so the naming follows the anatomy. Folliculitis is suppuration confined to the ostium and upper follicle: small dome shaped pustules each pierced by a hair, superficial and nonscarring. A furuncle (boil) occurs when infection penetrates the full length of the follicle into dermis and subcutaneous tissue, forming a deep tender fluctuant nodule that points and drains a necrotic core, and because the follicle is destroyed it can scar. A carbuncle is several adjacent furuncles coalescing into one indurated plaque draining through multiple ostia, typically on the thick skin of the posterior neck, upper back, or thighs, with fever and malaise because the tissue volume involved is large."},{"type":"flow","title":"Depth of follicular infection","steps":["S aureus enters the follicular ostium","Folliculitis: pustule in the upper follicle, hair in the center","Extension down the follicle into dermis and fat","Furuncle: deep fluctuant nodule with a necrotic core","Coalescence of adjacent involved follicles","Carbuncle: indurated plaque draining through several openings, with fever"],"caption":"One organism and one anatomic route produce three named lesions; only the depth and number of follicles differ."},{"type":"p","text":"Related suppurative lesions sit outside the follicle but behave the same way. A cutaneous abscess is a walled off collection of pus in the dermis and deeper tissue, most often from S aureus, arising from an infected follicle, a puncture, or an inoculated foreign body; it is fluctuant, exquisitely tender, and persists on its own because the avascular, acidic, hypoxic cavity is cut off from circulating defenses and does not support neutrophil killing. A pilonidal abscess arises in the sacrococcygeal cleft, where hair fragments are driven into the midline skin by friction and sitting; the hairs act as a foreign body and a conduit, a sinus forms, and the cavity becomes infected with skin and enteric flora. Hidradenitis suppurativa produces recurrent painful nodules, sinus tracts, and scarring in the axillae and groin and is primarily a disease of follicular occlusion rather than a primary infection, which is why it recurs despite drainage."},{"type":"table","title":"Follicular and suppurative skin lesions","cols":["Lesion","Anatomic level","Usual organism","Distinguishing clue"],"rows":[["Folliculitis","Follicular ostium and upper follicle","S aureus","Pustule with a central hair, no induration"],["Hot tub folliculitis","Upper follicle, occluded skin","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Itchy pustules under where the swimsuit sat, 1 to 2 days after a poorly chlorinated tub"],["Furuncle","Entire follicle into dermis and fat","S aureus","Deep tender nodule that points and drains a core"],["Carbuncle","Several contiguous follicles","S aureus","Multiple draining openings, nape of neck, systemic symptoms"],["Cutaneous abscess","Dermis and subcutis, not follicle bound","S aureus, including MRSA","Fluctuance; pus sealed off from the blood supply"],["Pilonidal abscess","Sacrococcygeal midline sinus","Mixed skin and enteric flora","Driven in hair acting as a foreign body"]],"caption":"Depth, follicular centering, and exposure history separate lesions that otherwise look alike."},{"type":"p","text":"Exposure can override the usual organism. Hot tub folliculitis follows immersion in inadequately chlorinated hot tubs and whirlpools; Pseudomonas aeruginosa tolerates warm water in which chlorine dissipates, and swimsuit occlusion macerates the stratum corneum, so itchy follicular pustules appear one to two days later in the distribution the suit covered and are usually self limited. Gram negative folliculitis of the face follows prolonged antibiotic suppression of normal flora, and Malassezia folliculitis gives monomorphic itchy truncal papules in humid conditions. Host factors matter as well: diabetes, obesity with intertriginous friction, nasal carriage of S aureus, and neutrophil disorders such as chronic granulomatous disease or leukocyte adhesion deficiency all produce recurrent furuncles, so recurrent abscesses in a child should prompt evaluation of neutrophil function."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C07","obj":"IN-13-H.7","title":"Nonbullous and bullous impetigo, and poststreptococcal sequelae","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Impetigo is the most superficial bacterial skin infection, confined to the epidermis above the basal layer, which is why it heals without scarring. It is predominantly a disease of preschool and school age children, favored by warm humid weather, crowding, and any breach that lets surface flora in: insect bites, scratches, scabies, eczema, and varicella lesions. Two forms exist, and they are separated not by severity but by whether a toxin is involved."},{"type":"p","text":"Nonbullous impetigo accounts for the great majority. Bacteria colonize a minor abrasion, a vesicle or pustule forms and ruptures within hours, and the exuded serum and neutrophils dry into a thick adherent golden crust described as honey colored, classically around the nose and mouth and on exposed limbs. Lesions spread by autoinoculation from scratching, and regional lymphadenopathy is common while fever is not. Staphylococcus aureus is now the most frequent isolate in most settings, with Streptococcus pyogenes also common and often found alongside it."},{"type":"p","text":"Bullous impetigo is toxin mediated and caused by S aureus alone. Certain strains secrete exfoliative (epidermolytic) toxins A and B, serine proteases with a single substrate: desmoglein 1, the desmosomal cadherin holding keratinocytes together in the superficial epidermis. Cleaving it separates cells within the granular layer, so intact fluid filled bullae form very high in the epidermis. Their thin roofs rupture easily and leave a shiny, moist, varnished erosion with a collarette of scale. In bullous impetigo the toxin acts where it is made, so bullae are limited to infected skin; when it reaches the circulation from a distant focus it splits the epidermis everywhere, which is staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome. Desmoglein 1 is largely replaced by desmoglein 3 in mucosa, which is why mucosa is spared."},{"type":"flow","title":"Exfoliative toxin to superficial split","steps":["S aureus strain secretes exfoliative toxin A or B","Toxin acts locally in skin or spreads through blood","Serine protease cleaves desmoglein 1","Keratinocytes separate within the granular layer","Flaccid superficial bullae and varnished erosions, mucosa spared"],"caption":"Local toxin gives bullous impetigo; circulating toxin gives scalded skin syndrome. The molecular lesion is identical."},{"type":"table","title":"Nonbullous versus bullous impetigo","cols":["Feature","Nonbullous","Bullous"],"rows":[["Organism","S aureus or S pyogenes","S aureus only"],["Mechanism","Direct superficial infection","Exfoliative toxin cleaving desmoglein 1"],["Lesion","Vesicopustule then honey colored crust","Flaccid bulla then varnished erosion with collarette"],["Split level","Subcorneal pustule","Granular layer"],["Typical age and site","Children, face around nose and mouth, limbs","Infants and young children, trunk, folds, diaper area"],["Poststreptococcal risk","Yes, when S pyogenes is the cause","No"]],"caption":"Finding an intact bulla rather than a crust names the organism as S aureus and implies a toxin."},{"type":"p","text":"The important sequela belongs to the streptococcal form. Nephritogenic M types of S pyogenes, including skin associated types, elicit antibodies whose immune complexes deposit in the glomerulus and activate complement, so about three to six weeks after impetigo, a longer latency than the one to two weeks that follows pharyngitis, a child develops acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis: cola colored urine, periorbital edema, hypertension, a low serum C3, and subepithelial humps on electron microscopy. Antistreptolysin O titers rise poorly after skin infection because skin lipids inhibit streptolysin O, so anti DNase B is the more useful marker. Acute rheumatic fever, in contrast, follows pharyngeal infection and has not been shown to follow impetigo, a distinction examiners test directly."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 6 year old had crusted lesions around the nose five weeks ago and now has dark urine, puffy eyelids, and a blood pressure at the 98th percentile. Will the antistreptolysin O titer be the best confirmation, and could this child develop rheumatic fever from the same episode?","explain":"No to both. Skin lipids inhibit streptolysin O, so anti DNase B is the better serologic marker after impetigo, and acute rheumatic fever follows pharyngeal, not cutaneous, streptococcal infection. Glomerulonephritis can follow either site.","check":"The last paragraph of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C08","obj":"IN-13-H.8","title":"MRSA: mecA, SCCmec, and two epidemiologic patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Beta-lactam antibiotics work by binding the transpeptidase domain of penicillin binding proteins, the enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan, so the wall cannot be completed and the dividing cell lyses. Staphylococcus aureus acquired two successive defenses. Penicillinase (a beta-lactamase) hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring and defeats penicillin, which is why penicillins carrying a bulky side chain that the enzyme cannot reach remain active. Methicillin resistance is a different and more complete mechanism: the organism acquires mecA, a gene encoding PBP2a, an alternative penicillin binding protein whose active site has very low affinity for essentially all beta-lactams. Wall synthesis therefore continues while the drug is present, so resistance extends to the entire beta-lactam class rather than to one agent."},{"type":"p","text":"mecA does not sit on the native chromosome. It is carried on the staphylococcal cassette chromosome mec (SCCmec), a mobile genomic island integrated at a fixed site near the origin of replication and mobilized by its own recombinase genes. SCCmec types differ in size and content, and this difference underlies the two clinical patterns. The large types (I, II, III) found in healthcare associated strains carry additional resistance determinants, so hospital associated MRSA is typically resistant to multiple non beta-lactam classes as well. The small types (IV, V) carried by community strains impose less fitness cost and transfer more readily, so community strains spread efficiently among healthy people while remaining susceptible to several non beta-lactam agents."},{"type":"flow","title":"How mecA defeats a whole drug class","steps":["SCCmec island integrates into the S aureus chromosome","mecA is expressed, producing PBP2a","Beta-lactam binds native PBPs but has very low affinity for PBP2a","PBP2a continues peptidoglycan cross-linking","Wall synthesis proceeds despite drug, so all beta-lactams fail"],"caption":"Resistance here is substitution of the target, not destruction of the drug, which is why it is class wide."},{"type":"p","text":"Community acquired MRSA has a distinct virulence profile. Most strains carry Panton-Valentine leukocidin, a two component pore forming toxin that inserts into neutrophil and monocyte membranes and lyses them. Loss of the cells that would contain the infection explains the lesions: recurrent furuncles and spontaneous abscesses with central necrosis in people with no healthcare exposure, and in a minority a severe necrotizing pneumonia, often following influenza. Transmission requires skin contact or shared items, so outbreaks cluster among contact athletes, military recruits, correctional and child care facilities, households, and people who inject drugs. Healthcare associated MRSA instead reflects selection pressure and devices: recent hospitalization or surgery, indwelling catheters and prosthetic material, hemodialysis, long term care, and prolonged antibiotic exposure, with bacteremia, surgical site infection, and ventilator associated pneumonia rather than isolated boils."},{"type":"table","title":"Community versus healthcare associated MRSA","cols":["Feature","Community acquired","Healthcare associated"],"rows":[["SCCmec type","Type IV or V, small and mobile","Type I, II, or III, large"],["Panton-Valentine leukocidin","Usually present","Usually absent"],["Host","Healthy, often young","Recently hospitalized, instrumented, or institutionalized"],["Typical disease","Recurrent abscesses and furuncles, necrotizing pneumonia after influenza","Bacteremia, line and surgical site infection, pneumonia"],["Other resistance","Often susceptible to non beta-lactam agents","Frequently multidrug resistant"],["Spread","Skin to skin contact, shared equipment","Hands of personnel, devices, environment"]],"caption":"The size and content of the resistance island predicts the host, the lesion, and the resistance pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"Reservoir matters as much as resistance. Roughly a quarter to a third of people carry S aureus in the anterior nares at any time, where it adheres to squamous epithelium through wall teichoic acid and clumping factor B. Carriers autoinoculate breaks in their own skin, which is why one patient has repeated boils with an identical strain, and they shed organisms to others, so the nares rather than the lesion is the reservoir that sustains recurrent disease and perioperative transmission. The laboratory detects resistance using cefoxitin as a surrogate marker for mecA or by detecting mecA directly; S aureus itself is a catalase positive, coagulase positive, beta-hemolytic, mannitol fermenting gram positive coccus in clusters."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C09","obj":"IN-13-H.9","title":"Molluscum contagiosum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Molluscum contagiosum virus is a large double stranded DNA poxvirus that infects humans only and has no animal reservoir. Like all poxviruses it replicates entirely in the cytoplasm using its own transcription machinery, and it infects only keratinocytes. Infection is restricted to the epidermis, where it drives proliferation without inducing the destructive inflammation of a herpesvirus, so the lesion is a growth rather than a vesicle or ulcer."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion is a firm, flesh colored to pearly, dome shaped papule, typically 2 to 5 mm, with a central dell or umbilication from which a white keratinous core can be expressed. Lesions are usually multiple, grouped where skin has been rubbed or scratched, and asymptomatic, although a surrounding eczematous reaction (molluscum dermatitis) may itch. Histology is distinctive and often tested: the epidermis forms a lobulated, cup shaped downgrowth into the dermis, and infected keratinocytes contain large eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies (Henderson-Paterson bodies) that displace the nucleus; the central umbilication corresponds to the plugged opening of that crater."},{"type":"table","title":"Umbilicated papules: separating the causes","cols":["Diagnosis","Clue","Microscopic finding"],"rows":[["Molluscum contagiosum","Firm pearly dome, expressible core, healthy child or HIV","Cytoplasmic inclusion bodies in a cup shaped lesion"],["Disseminated cryptococcosis","Advanced HIV, systemic illness, rapid appearance","Encapsulated yeast on mucicarmine or India ink"],["Disseminated histoplasmosis","Endemic exposure, systemic illness","Small intracellular yeast in macrophages"],["Basal cell carcinoma","Single pearly papule with telangiectasia in sun damaged adult","Nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading"],["Herpes simplex","Painful grouped vesicles, not firm papules","Multinucleated cells with intranuclear inclusions"]],"caption":"In advanced immunosuppression, umbilicated papules can be fungal, so the clue to weigh is how sick the patient is."},{"type":"p","text":"Transmission requires direct contact with infected keratinocytes and occurs three ways. Skin to skin contact spreads it among children, in wrestling and gymnastics, and through shared towels or mats; autoinoculation by scratching explains linear or clustered lesions and spread to adjacent sites; and sexual contact explains lesions confined to the genitals, lower abdomen, and inner thighs in adults, which is why genital molluscum in an adult is treated as a sexually transmitted infection and why the finding in a child prompts careful but neutral consideration of how contact occurred. Incubation is roughly two weeks to six months."},{"type":"p","text":"Course is determined by cell mediated immunity. In an immunocompetent child the virus is eventually recognized and cleared, often after a period of lesion inflammation and redness that precedes resolution, so lesions are self limited over months up to about two years and heal without scarring unless traumatized or secondarily infected. With impaired T cell function the same virus produces far more and far larger lesions: in advanced HIV infection, particularly with low CD4 counts, molluscum becomes extensive and facial, with giant lesions exceeding 1 cm that are difficult to clear, and widespread molluscum in an adult is an indication to test for HIV. Lesions also become numerous where the barrier and local immunity are altered, as in atopic dermatitis and in skin under topical steroids."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C10","obj":"IN-13-H.10","title":"Dermatophytosis by site, and why tinea versicolor is different","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dermatophytes are keratinophilic molds of three genera, Trichophyton, Microsporum, and Epidermophyton, that secrete keratinases and therefore live only in dead keratinized tissue: the stratum corneum, the hair shaft, and the nail plate. They do not invade living epidermis or dermis in a normal host, which explains both the superficial scaling of the lesions and the fact that most of the inflammation is a host response to fungal antigen diffusing inward rather than to tissue invasion. On the trunk this produces the classic configuration: the fungus advances outward through the stratum corneum while the immune response clears the older central zone, leaving an annular plaque with a raised, scaly, sometimes vesicular active border and relative central clearing. Scale scraped from that advancing border, dissolved in potassium hydroxide, shows branching septate hyphae, and hyphae fluoresce with a chitin binding stain."},{"type":"table","title":"Tinea by site","cols":["Name","Site","Presentation and clue"],"rows":[["Tinea capitis","Scalp hair","Children; scaly patches with broken hairs and alopecia; kerion is a boggy tender inflammatory mass; Microsporum species may fluoresce under Wood lamp"],["Tinea corporis","Trunk and limbs","Annular plaque, raised scaly advancing border, central clearing"],["Tinea cruris","Groin and medial thighs","Scaly bordered plaque in the crural folds that characteristically spares the scrotum"],["Tinea pedis","Feet and toe webs","Interdigital maceration and fissuring, or moccasin pattern scaling; fissures are a portal for cellulitis"],["Tinea barbae","Beard area of adult men","Follicular pustules and inflamed plaques, often from animal contact"],["Tinea unguium (onychomycosis)","Nail plate","Thick, yellow, crumbling, distally detached nail with subungual debris"],["Tinea manuum","Palm","Often unilateral with concurrent tinea pedis (two feet, one hand pattern)"]],"caption":"One group of organisms, named by the keratin it occupies; the site determines the appearance."},{"type":"p","text":"Two site specific points carry mechanism. Tinea cruris spares the scrotum while candidal intertrigo of the groin involves it, because dermatophytes need keratinized skin and thin, sebum rich scrotal skin is a poor substrate, whereas Candida thrives on moist apposed surfaces. Tinea capitis matters because the organism occupies the hair shaft: endothrix invasion breaks hairs at the surface leaving black dots, ectothrix invasion breaks them a few millimeters out, and a vigorous host response produces a kerion, which is sterile pus from immune attack on the fungus rather than bacterial superinfection. Because the fungus lies inside the hair shaft and the nail plate rather than on the surface, scalp and nail disease persist far longer than body disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Tinea versicolor is named by tradition rather than by cause. It is produced by Malassezia species, lipophilic commensal yeasts of sebaceous follicles that lack fatty acid synthase and so depend on sebum rather than on keratin; they are not dermatophytes. In hot humid conditions and on sebum rich skin the organism converts to a mycelial form and proliferates in the stratum corneum of the upper trunk, neck, and shoulders, producing well demarcated macules with fine branny scale. The macules may be lighter or darker than surrounding skin because Malassezia produces dicarboxylic acids, chiefly azelaic acid, that inhibit tyrosinase in melanocytes, so pigmentation falls in affected areas and the contrast becomes obvious after sun exposure tans the surrounding skin. Potassium hydroxide preparation shows the spaghetti and meatballs pattern of short hyphae with clusters of round yeast, which distinguishes it at a glance from the long branching hyphae of a dermatophyte."},{"type":"table","title":"Dermatophyte versus Malassezia","cols":["Feature","Dermatophyte (tinea corporis)","Malassezia (tinea versicolor)"],"rows":[["Organism type","Keratinophilic mold","Lipophilic yeast, part of normal flora"],["Nutrient required","Keratin","Skin lipid, so sebaceous areas"],["Lesion","Annular plaque, scaly advancing edge, central clearing","Hypopigmented or hyperpigmented macules with fine scale"],["Potassium hydroxide","Long branching septate hyphae","Short hyphae with round yeast clusters"],["Pigment change","Postinflammatory, nonspecific","Azelaic acid inhibition of tyrosinase"],["Contagion","Person, animal, or soil contact","Not contagious; overgrowth of resident flora"]],"caption":"The word tinea in versicolor is historical; organism, nutrient, and microscopic appearance all differ."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C11","obj":"IN-13-H.11","title":"Human papillomavirus warts and their histology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Human papillomaviruses are small nonenveloped double stranded DNA viruses that infect basal keratinocytes reached through a microabrasion. The viral life cycle is tied to epithelial maturation: the episomal genome is maintained at low copy number in the dividing basal layer, early genes drive proliferation as the cell moves upward, and the capsid proteins L1 and L2 are expressed only in the uppermost differentiated layers, where fully assembled virions are shed in desquamating squames. The virus therefore never lyses a cell, causes no viremia, and presents little antigen to the immune system, which is why warts persist for months to years and why clearance, when it comes, depends on a cell mediated response."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinical type reflects anatomy and HPV type. Verruca vulgaris, the common wart, is a firm hyperkeratotic papule with a rough papillated surface on the hands, fingers, and periungual skin, and the thrombosed dermal capillaries at its tips appear as the black dots that distinguish it from a callus. Verruca plantaris, the plantar wart, is pressed inward by weight bearing into a flat, tender, keratotic plaque that interrupts the skin lines, separating it from a corn. Verruca plana, the flat wart, is a smooth, slightly elevated, skin colored papule in clusters on the face, dorsal hands, and shins, where linear arrays betray autoinoculation by shaving. Condyloma acuminatum, the anogenital wart, is a soft, moist, cauliflower like exophytic growth of the vulva, penis, perineum, and perianal skin, and about 90 percent are caused by the low risk types HPV 6 and 11, which do not integrate and rarely progress to cancer. The high risk types, chiefly HPV 16 and 18, integrate their genome, overexpress E6 and E7, which degrade p53 and inactivate the retinoblastoma protein, and cause squamous dysplasia and carcinoma rather than ordinary warts."},{"type":"table","title":"Wart types","cols":["Type","Site","Appearance","Common HPV types"],"rows":[["Verruca vulgaris","Hands, fingers, periungual","Rough papillated papule with black dots","HPV 1, 2, 4"],["Verruca plantaris","Soles, pressure points","Inward growing keratotic plaque, interrupts skin lines","HPV 1, 2, 4"],["Verruca plana","Face, dorsal hands, shins","Smooth flat papules, often in a shaved line","HPV 3, 10"],["Condyloma acuminatum","Anogenital skin and mucosa","Soft moist cauliflower like growth","HPV 6 and 11"]],"caption":"Low risk types cause warts; the oncogenic types cause dysplasia and are a different clinical problem."},{"type":"p","text":"Histology is diagnostic and follows directly from the life cycle. Driving keratinocyte proliferation produces acanthosis (thickened epidermis) and papillomatosis (the epidermis thrown into finger like projections over dermal papillae containing dilated capillaries), with overlying hyperkeratosis and often parakeratosis. The hallmark cytopathic change is the koilocyte: a squamous cell of the upper layers with a shrunken, wrinkled, hyperchromatic nucleus surrounded by a clear perinuclear halo produced by collapse of the cytokeratin cytoskeleton, usually accompanied by coarse keratohyalin granules. Inward curving of the epidermal rete at the margins of the lesion completes the picture."},{"type":"p","text":"Because clearance requires T cell mediated recognition of infected keratinocytes rather than antibody, impaired cellular immunity produces extensive, confluent, and treatment refractory warts. This is seen in advanced HIV infection, in organ transplant recipients, in combined and T cell immunodeficiencies, and in two single gene diseases: epidermodysplasia verruciformis, in which EVER1 or EVER2 (TMC6, TMC8) mutations permit widespread infection by otherwise harmless beta HPV types with later squamous cell carcinoma on sun exposed skin, and WHIM syndrome, caused by gain of function CXCR4 mutations, whose acronym names warts, hypogammaglobulinemia, infections, and myelokathexis. Extensive warts are therefore a reason to evaluate T cell function."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C12","obj":"IN-13-H.12","title":"Bloodborne pathogen risk after a needle stick","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A percutaneous needle injury inoculates a small volume of blood under the skin, so the probability that infection follows is governed by how much infectious virus that volume contains and how efficiently that virus establishes infection. The three pathogens of concern rank in a fixed order that tracks circulating titer. Hepatitis B virus circulates at very high levels in untreated chronic infection, often 100 million to a billion virions per milliliter, and has a very low infectious dose, giving a risk of about 30 percent from a hepatitis B e antigen positive source and about 1 to 6 percent when the source is e antigen negative, which marks lower replication. Hepatitis C circulates at intermediate titers and carries a risk of about 1.8 percent, usually rounded to 2 percent. HIV circulates at the lowest titers in untreated infection and is a fragile enveloped virus, giving about 0.3 percent after a percutaneous injury and about 0.09 percent after a mucous membrane splash."},{"type":"table","title":"Approximate risk per percutaneous exposure","cols":["Pathogen","Risk per needle stick","Why","Preventable by vaccine"],"rows":[["Hepatitis B virus","About 30% if source is HBeAg positive; about 1 to 6% if HBeAg negative","Very high circulating titer, very low infectious dose, stable particle","Yes"],["Hepatitis C virus","About 2% (about 1.8%)","Intermediate titer","No"],["HIV","About 0.3% percutaneous; about 0.09% mucosal","Lowest titer, fragile enveloped virion","No"]],"caption":"The roughly tenfold steps between the three, and the order, are what examiners expect; each figure varies with source viral load."},{"type":"p","text":"Four features of the injury modify that baseline, and all four work through inoculum volume or through viral load. A hollow-bore needle, especially one used for venipuncture or arterial sampling, carries residual blood inside its lumen, so it delivers far more blood than a solid suture needle or scalpel edge, which carries only a surface film; this is the single most important device factor. A deep puncture deposits that blood into vascularized tissue instead of the superficial dermis. Visible blood on the device, and a needle taken directly from the source patient's vein or artery, both indicate fresh, undiluted blood. A high source viral load raises the number of infectious particles per unit volume, which is why a source with untreated acute or advanced HIV infection, or a hepatitis B e antigen positive source, carries much greater risk than a source with suppressed or low level viremia; conversely, a source on effective therapy with undetectable virus transmits far less efficiently."},{"type":"flow","title":"What determines transmission after a stick","steps":["Source viral titer in blood (HBV highest, then HCV, then HIV)","Volume of blood delivered: hollow-bore lumen versus solid needle film","Depth of inoculation into vascularized tissue","Freshness of blood: visibly bloody or straight from a vessel","Recipient immunity: hepatitis B vaccination and anti-HBs status","Resulting probability of established infection"],"caption":"Every risk factor on the list acts by changing the infectious dose or the host's starting defense."},{"type":"p","text":"Recipient factors complete the picture. Hepatitis B is the one of the three with an effective vaccine, and a documented anti-HBs response of at least 10 milli-international units per milliliter makes transmission very unlikely, so the highest risk pathogen is also the one whose outcome depends most on the recipient's prior immunity. There is no vaccine for hepatitis C or HIV. Intact skin is an effective barrier, so a splash onto unbroken skin carries negligible risk, while exposure of mucous membranes or nonintact skin is measurable but lower risk than a percutaneous injury."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C13","obj":"IN-13-H.13","title":"Predicting the organism from the wound exposure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Wound infections are inoculations: the organism comes from the flora of whatever broke the skin or from the environment the wound met, so an exposure in a question is asking for an organism. Dog and cat bites inoculate oral flora, in which Pasteurella multocida is prominent. Cat teeth are slender and drive bacteria deep into joints and tendon sheaths through a small wound, and Pasteurella divides rapidly, so cellulitis already intense within 12 to 24 hours is its signature, unlike the slower staphylococcal or streptococcal infection of two to three days. Capnocytophaga canimorsus, also from canine saliva, is cleared by splenic macrophages and opsonizing antibody, so in an asplenic or cirrhotic patient it causes fulminant sepsis with purpura fulminans from a trivial bite."},{"type":"p","text":"Human bites and clenched fist injuries inoculate human oral flora, including Eikenella corrodens, a fastidious gram negative rod. The clenched fist injury over the metacarpophalangeal joint is dangerous out of proportion to its size because the extensor tendon and joint capsule are inoculated while the fist is closed, and when the hand opens the tendon slides proximally, carrying contamination into deep spaces no longer reachable from the surface."},{"type":"p","text":"Water exposure splits by salinity and temperature. Vibrio vulnificus is a halophilic gram negative rod of warm brackish and coastal water acquired through a seawater exposed wound or raw oysters. Its growth is iron dependent, so iron overload states and chronic liver disease, where transferrin saturation is high and macrophage function impaired, permit rapid bacteremia with hemorrhagic bullae and necrotizing fasciitis, while a healthy host usually has only gastroenteritis or local infection. Mycobacterium marinum is a slow growing nontuberculous mycobacterium of fresh and salt water that grows best at about 30 C (86 F), well below core temperature, so it causes indolent granulomatous nodules on the cooler hands and forearms of people who clean fish tanks, sometimes ascending the lymphatics, and its culture must be requested at low temperature or it will be missed."},{"type":"p","text":"Plant and soil exposures give Sporothrix schenckii, a dimorphic fungus on rose thorns, sphagnum moss, hay, and soil. Inoculated by a thorn prick, it forms a painless papule or ulcer at the puncture site, then spreads up the draining lymphatic as a chain of subcutaneous nodules that ulcerate in sequence, the lymphocutaneous or sporotrichoid pattern. It grows as a mold in the environment and as cigar shaped budding yeast in tissue at 37 C (98.6 F)."},{"type":"table","title":"Exposure to organism","cols":["Exposure","Organism","Mechanistic clue"],"rows":[["Cat or dog bite","Pasteurella multocida","Oral flora; intense cellulitis within about 24 hours"],["Dog bite in an asplenic or cirrhotic patient","Capnocytophaga canimorsus","Normally cleared by the spleen; fulminant sepsis with purpura"],["Human bite or clenched fist injury","Eikenella corrodens","Oral flora driven into the joint and tendon sheath"],["Seawater wound or raw oysters","Vibrio vulnificus","Halophilic, iron dependent; bullae in liver disease or iron overload"],["Fish tank, fish handling, pool","Mycobacterium marinum","Grows at about 30 C (86 F), so cool distal skin; indolent granulomas"],["Rose thorn, moss, hay, soil","Sporothrix schenckii","Dimorphic fungus; nodules ascending the lymphatics"],["Soil contaminated deep crush wound","Clostridium perfringens","Anaerobic necrotic muscle; gas and crepitus"],["Puncture wound, unimmunized","Clostridium tetani","Spore germination in anaerobic tissue; tetanospasmin"]],"caption":"Each pairing follows from where the organism lives and which host defense contains it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A fish store employee has three painless nodules marching up the forearm from a small hand ulcer that began six weeks ago; cultures at 37 C (98.6 F) are negative. What two organisms produce this pattern, and what single laboratory change raises the yield for one of them?","explain":"Sporothrix schenckii and Mycobacterium marinum both give sporotrichoid lymphocutaneous nodules. Mycobacterium marinum grows best at about 30 C (86 F), so culture must be incubated at that lower temperature; routine incubation at core temperature misses it.","check":"The water exposure and plant exposure paragraphs and the exposure table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C14","obj":"IN-13-H.14","title":"Clostridial wound disease: gas gangrene and tetanus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Clostridia are anaerobic, spore forming, gram positive rods of soil and of the human colon. Spores germinate only where the redox potential is low, so clostridial disease requires a wound that excludes oxygen: crushed devitalized muscle, a deep puncture that closes over itself, a wound contaminated with soil or feces, or ischemic tissue. The two diseases that follow are purely toxin mediated, and the toxins act in different compartments, which is why one destroys tissue locally and the other leaves the wound unimpressive while paralyzing the patient."},{"type":"p","text":"Clostridium perfringens causes clostridial myonecrosis, or gas gangrene. Its principal virulence factor is alpha toxin, a phospholipase C also called lecithinase, which hydrolyzes lecithin (phosphatidylcholine) and sphingomyelin in cell membranes. Lysing the membranes of myocytes, endothelium, erythrocytes, platelets, and leukocytes produces immediate myonecrosis, intravascular hemolysis, microvascular thrombosis, and destruction of the neutrophils that would contain the infection. Thrombosis extends the anaerobic zone, so the organism creates its own habitat and advances rapidly, while fermentation of muscle glycogen releases gas into tissue and produces crepitus. Clinically there is severe pain, tense edema, thin brown malodorous discharge, dusky skin, hemolysis with hemoglobinuria, and strikingly few neutrophils on Gram stain of an exudate full of large gram positive rods. The laboratory shows a double zone of hemolysis on blood agar and a positive lecithinase (Nagler) reaction on egg yolk agar."},{"type":"flow","title":"Alpha toxin to gas gangrene","steps":["Spores enter devitalized, hypoxic muscle and germinate","Alpha toxin (lecithinase) hydrolyzes membrane phospholipid","Myocyte, endothelial, red cell, and neutrophil lysis","Microvascular thrombosis extends the anaerobic zone","Fermentation of muscle glycogen releases gas into tissue","Rapidly advancing myonecrosis with crepitus, hemolysis, and shock"],"caption":"The toxin destroys tissue and removes the defenses and oxygen that would limit the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"Clostridium tetani does the opposite: it remains confined to a wound, a splinter, nail puncture, burn, or neonatal umbilical stump, and causes little local inflammation. Its toxin, tetanospasmin, is a zinc dependent metalloprotease cleaved into a heavy chain that binds and delivers and a light chain that cuts. The heavy chain binds gangliosides at the presynaptic membrane of the alpha motor neuron, and the internalized toxin travels by retrograde axonal transport to the anterior horn. Rather than acting there, it crosses the synapse into the terminals of the inhibitory interneurons of the spinal cord and brainstem, where the light chain cleaves synaptobrevin (VAMP), a SNARE protein required for vesicle fusion, so glycine and GABA cannot be released."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of inhibition, not stimulation, produces every sign of tetanus. Alpha motor neurons fire without restraint and reciprocal inhibition of antagonists fails, so muscles contract simultaneously and rigidly: trismus (lockjaw), risus sardonicus, opisthotonos, a board like abdomen, and laryngeal and respiratory spasm that causes death. The patient remains fully conscious and the spasms are exquisitely painful and triggered by minor stimuli, because sensory pathways are untouched, and loss of inhibition in preganglionic sympathetic neurons adds labile blood pressure, tachycardia, and sweating. Recovery requires growth of new nerve terminals, which is why tetanus lasts weeks. Botulinum toxin is the instructive contrast: the same class of SNARE cleaving metalloprotease, but acting peripherally on cholinergic terminals at the neuromuscular junction, so acetylcholine release fails and paralysis is flaccid and descending. The toxin class is identical; the synapse targeted decides whether the patient is stiff or limp."},{"type":"table","title":"Tetanus versus botulism versus gas gangrene","cols":["Feature","Tetanus","Botulism","Gas gangrene"],"rows":[["Organism","Clostridium tetani","Clostridium botulinum","Clostridium perfringens"],["Toxin","Tetanospasmin","Botulinum toxin","Alpha toxin (lecithinase)"],["Molecular action","Cleaves synaptobrevin in inhibitory interneurons","Cleaves SNARE proteins in cholinergic terminals","Hydrolyzes membrane lecithin"],["Site of action","Spinal cord and brainstem","Neuromuscular junction","Local tissue, not neural"],["Result","Spastic paralysis, trismus, autonomic instability","Flaccid descending paralysis, dilated pupils","Myonecrosis, crepitus, hemolysis, shock"],["Local wound","Often trivial and noninflamed","Wound botulism or ingested toxin","Devitalized, foul, gas filled"]],"caption":"Three clostridial diseases separated by what the toxin cleaves and where."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C15","obj":"IN-13-H.15","title":"Herpes simplex and herpes zoster in the skin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2 and varicella-zoster virus are enveloped double stranded DNA alphaherpesviruses sharing a two phase life history. Primary infection occurs in epithelium, where the virus replicates and lyses keratinocytes, and lysis with intraepidermal edema produces the intraepidermal vesicle. A Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant keratinocytes with ballooning degeneration and intranuclear inclusions, which does not distinguish the three viruses. The virus then enters sensory nerve endings in the lesion, travels by retrograde axonal transport to the sensory ganglion, and establishes latency as an episome with minimal gene expression, invisible to cytotoxic T cells because almost no viral peptide reaches the cell surface. Reactivation sends virus anterograde down the same axon, which is why recurrences return to the territory of the latently infected neuron."},{"type":"p","text":"That rule explains the clinical patterns of herpes simplex. Herpes labialis recurs at the vermilion border because the trigeminal ganglion was seeded, and burning or tingling precedes it as virus travels down the nerve. Genital herpes, more often HSV-2, recurs in sacral dermatomes. Primary gingivostomatitis in a child is far more extensive than any recurrence, with fever, painful oral ulcers, and adenopathy, because no memory exists yet. Herpetic whitlow is inoculation of a finger, classically in dentists and thumb sucking children, giving deep painful vesicles mistaken for a bacterial felon. Eczema herpeticum occurs when HSV lands on skin whose barrier defenses are impaired by atopic dermatitis, so it disseminates across eczematous skin as monomorphic punched out erosions with fever."},{"type":"p","text":"Varicella-zoster virus follows the same rules with different geography. Primary infection is varicella, which is viremic and therefore seeds many sensory ganglia at once. Decades later, as virus specific T cell surveillance wanes with age, immunosuppression, or malignancy, the virus reactivates in a single ganglion and spreads down all the axons of that neuron population, producing herpes zoster: grouped vesicles on an erythematous base confined to one dermatome, stopping at the midline, usually thoracic or trigeminal, preceded for several days by pain or dysesthesia before any rash. Involvement of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve threatens the eye, and lesions on the nasal tip (the nasociliary branch) predict it. Reactivation in the geniculate ganglion of the facial nerve produces Ramsay Hunt syndrome: vesicles in the ear canal and on the auricle, ipsilateral peripheral facial palsy, and often vertigo and hearing loss, because that ganglion lies beside the facial motor fibers and the vestibulocochlear nerve. Lesions crossing dermatomes indicate immunosuppression."},{"type":"flow","title":"Latency and reactivation","steps":["Epithelial replication produces vesicles","Virus enters sensory nerve endings in the lesion","Retrograde transport to the sensory ganglion","Latency as an episome with minimal gene expression","Waning T cell surveillance or a local trigger","Anterograde transport back to that neuron's skin territory"],"caption":"Recurrence is confined to one nerve's territory because latency sits in that nerve's ganglion."},{"type":"p","text":"Postherpetic neuralgia is the main sequela of zoster and follows neural injury rather than ongoing infection. Inflammation within the ganglion and nerve destroys neurons and produces fibrosis and demyelination, with preferential loss of large myelinated fibers; surviving small nociceptive fibers become spontaneously active and centrally sensitized, so pain persists after the rash heals. It is defined as dermatomal pain beyond about three months, often with allodynia, and its strongest risk factor is age, with incidence rising steeply after 60 years."},{"type":"table","title":"Herpes simplex versus herpes zoster","cols":["Feature","Herpes simplex recurrence","Herpes zoster"],"rows":[["Latent site","Trigeminal or sacral ganglion","Any dorsal root or cranial sensory ganglion seeded during varicella"],["Distribution","Small cluster at the same spot each time","One whole dermatome, unilateral, stops at the midline"],["Recurrences","Many, over years","Usually once; repeated episodes suggest immunosuppression"],["Prodrome","Hours of tingling","Days of dermatomal pain, often before any rash"],["Sequela","Recurrence; erythema multiforme as a reaction","Postherpetic neuralgia, ocular disease, Ramsay Hunt palsy"]],"caption":"Identical cellular behavior; the extent of dermatome reflects how many neurons carry latent virus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C16","obj":"IN-13-H.16","title":"Viral exanthems: measles, rubella, roseola, erythema infectiosum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A viral exanthem is a rash produced by a systemic viral infection, and in most cases it is an immune response to virus in dermal capillaries rather than viral destruction of the epidermis. That is why these rashes are maculopapular rather than vesicular, why they appear several days into the illness as the adaptive response matures, and why their timing relative to fever is itself diagnostic."},{"type":"p","text":"Measles, caused by a negative sense RNA paramyxovirus, is the most severe of the four. About 10 days after exposure, a prodrome of high fever with cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis reflects respiratory epithelial infection and viremia. Late in the prodrome, Koplik spots appear on the buccal mucosa opposite the molars as tiny blue white papules on a red base, an enanthem specific for measles that precedes the rash by a day or two. The exanthem begins behind the ears and at the hairline and spreads downward, becoming confluent, with fever peaking as the rash peaks, then fading in the same order with fine desquamation. Complications follow its suppression of lymphocyte function and its tropism: otitis media and bacterial pneumonia (the commonest cause of death), giant cell pneumonia in the immunocompromised, acute postinfectious encephalitis, and, years later, subacute sclerosing panencephalitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Rubella, caused by a positive sense RNA togavirus, is milder but has a more useful sign: tender postauricular, suboccipital, and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy that often precedes and outlasts the rash. The exanthem is a fine pink maculopapular eruption that also begins on the face and spreads downward but never becomes confluent and clears in about three days, with arthralgias common in adolescent and adult women. Its importance lies in congenital rubella syndrome: first trimester infection crosses the placenta and causes sensorineural deafness, cataracts, and cardiac defects, most characteristically patent ductus arteriosus."},{"type":"p","text":"Roseola infantum (exanthem subitum, sixth disease) is caused by human herpesvirus 6 and affects infants roughly 6 months to 2 years, the window after maternal antibody wanes. The pattern is three to five days of high fever, often 39.5 to 40.5 C (103 to 105 F), in a child who looks surprisingly well, and then, as the fever breaks, a rose pink macular rash on the trunk spreading to the neck and proximal limbs and sparing the face. Fever this high makes it the classic cause of a first febrile seizure, and the illness is otherwise benign."},{"type":"p","text":"Erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) is caused by parvovirus B19, a single stranded DNA virus that infects erythroid progenitors through the P antigen receptor and halts erythropoiesis. A mild febrile illness during viremia is followed, as antibody appears, by bright confluent erythema of both cheeks with circumoral pallor (slapped cheek) and then a reticulated lacy rash on the limbs and trunk that can recur for weeks with heat or sun. Because the rash is immune mediated and arrives after viremia, the child is typically no longer contagious once it is visible. The same arrest of erythropoiesis explains transient aplastic crisis in anyone dependent on rapid red cell turnover, chronic anemia in immunodeficiency, and hydrops fetalis after maternal infection."},{"type":"table","title":"The four classic exanthems","cols":["Disease","Agent","Before the rash","Rash pattern","Hallmark or complication"],"rows":[["Measles","Measles virus (paramyxovirus)","High fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis","Hairline and behind ears, spreads down, confluent","Koplik spots; pneumonia, encephalitis, panencephalitis"],["Rubella","Rubella virus (togavirus)","Mild fever, tender postauricular nodes","Face then down, fine, nonconfluent, about 3 days","Congenital rubella: deafness, cataracts, patent ductus"],["Roseola","Human herpesvirus 6","3 to 5 days of fever to about 40 C (104 F), well infant","As fever breaks: trunk then neck, face spared","Febrile seizure"],["Erythema infectiosum","Parvovirus B19","Mild febrile illness during viremia","Slapped cheeks, then lacy rash on limbs","Aplastic crisis, hydrops fetalis, adult arthritis"]],"caption":"Timing relative to fever, direction of spread, and the associated finding separate these four."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C17","obj":"IN-13-H.17","title":"Varicella, enteroviral eruptions, and scarlet fever","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three more childhood eruptions are separated by the kind of lesion each produces, which in turn reflects what the organism does to the epidermis. Varicella and the enteroviral eruptions kill keratinocytes and therefore blister; scarlet fever is toxin mediated vasodilation and therefore produces erythema with a texture change but no vesicle."},{"type":"p","text":"Varicella (chickenpox) is primary infection with varicella-zoster virus, acquired by respiratory droplets and contact with vesicle fluid. The virus replicates in respiratory mucosa and regional nodes, and primary and secondary viremias carry it to the skin after about 14 days; because seeding occurs in successive waves, new lesions keep appearing for several days. That wave pattern produces the most useful finding: lesions in different stages side by side in the same area, macules, papules, clear vesicles, pustules, and crusts at once. The eruption is centripetal, beginning on the trunk, face, and scalp with mucosal involvement and fewer lesions distally, and is intensely pruritic. Lesions heal without scarring unless scratched and secondarily infected with S aureus or S pyogenes, the commonest complication. In adults and in immunocompromised or pregnant patients, varicella pneumonia and visceral dissemination make it far more severe, and the virus then lies latent in sensory ganglia. Smallpox is the contrast examiners use: deep umbilicated lesions, centrifugal distribution, and all lesions at the same stage, because seeding was synchronous."},{"type":"p","text":"Hand-foot-and-mouth disease and herpangina are caused by enteroviruses, principally coxsackievirus A16 and enterovirus 71 for the former and coxsackievirus A for the latter: nonenveloped positive sense RNA viruses spread by the fecal-oral and respiratory routes, peaking in late summer. In hand-foot-and-mouth disease, fever and oral pain are followed by shallow painful ulcers of the anterior mouth and by oval gray vesicles on the palms, soles, and often buttocks, oriented along skin lines, with nail shedding sometimes weeks later. Herpangina gives abrupt fever with vesicles and ulcers restricted to the posterior pharynx, soft palate, and tonsillar pillars, sparing the anterior mouth, which separates it from hand-foot-and-mouth disease and from herpetic gingivostomatitis. Enterovirus 71 is associated with brainstem encephalitis and aseptic meningitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Vesicular eruptions compared","cols":["Disease","Agent","Distribution","Discriminating feature"],"rows":[["Varicella","Varicella-zoster virus","Trunk and face first, centripetal, mucosa involved","Lesions in several different stages at once"],["Smallpox (historical contrast)","Variola virus","Face and limbs, centrifugal","All lesions at the same stage, deep and umbilicated"],["Hand-foot-and-mouth disease","Coxsackievirus A16, enterovirus 71","Anterior mouth, palms, soles, buttocks","Oval vesicles along skin lines on palms and soles"],["Herpangina","Coxsackievirus A","Posterior palate, pharynx, tonsillar pillars","Posterior mouth only, anterior mouth spared"],["Herpetic gingivostomatitis","Herpes simplex virus 1","Anterior mouth, gingivae, perioral skin","Bleeding gingivitis with perioral vesicles"]],"caption":"Location and whether lesions are synchronous answer these without a laboratory test."},{"type":"p","text":"Scarlet fever is not a viral exanthem. It accompanies Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis in a patient lacking antibody to that strain's streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin. These exotoxins are superantigens: they bind simultaneously to the outside of the major histocompatibility complex class II molecule and to the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain, bypassing antigen processing and activating a large fraction of T cells at once. The resulting flood of interleukin 1, interleukin 2, interferon gamma, and tumor necrosis factor produces fever, cutaneous vasodilation, and endothelial activation. One to two days after the sore throat, a fine blanching punctate erythema starts on the neck and upper trunk and spreads outward, and because each papule is centered on a follicle it feels like sandpaper, its most characteristic quality. Flexural accentuation gives linear petechial streaks (Pastia lines), the cheeks flush while perioral skin stays pale, and the tongue is first white coated with projecting inflamed papillae, then a beefy red strawberry tongue. The rash resolves with desquamation of the hands and feet one to two weeks later, and this illness, unlike the others here, carries risk of rheumatic fever and glomerulonephritis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C18","obj":"IN-13-H.18","title":"Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome versus toxic epidermal necrolysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Two diseases make large sheets of epidermis separate, and they are distinguished by the level at which the epidermis splits and the mechanism that split it. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome is toxin mediated separation high in a living epidermis; toxic epidermal necrolysis is immune mediated death of the entire epidermis. That difference determines the age group, mucosal involvement, the appearance of the denuded base, and how dangerous the illness is."},{"type":"p","text":"In staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, a focus of Staphylococcus aureus elsewhere than the skin that peels, often the nasopharynx, conjunctiva, umbilicus, or a minor wound, secretes exfoliative toxin A or B into the blood. The toxin cleaves desmoglein 1, so keratinocytes separate within the granular layer: they are not killed, they are unstuck. Fever and tender erythroderma are followed by flaccid bullae and sheets of epidermis sliding off with gentle lateral pressure (a positive Nikolsky sign), leaving a moist, bright red base. Because the split lies just beneath the stratum corneum, little tissue is lost and the base re-epithelializes from intact keratinocytes in one to two weeks without scarring. It occurs almost entirely in children under about 5 years, because renal clearance of the toxin is immature and antitoxin antibody has not developed, and mucosa is spared because desmoglein 3 compensates there. Blood cultures are usually negative, since the toxin acts at a distance, so the organism is cultured from the original focus."},{"type":"p","text":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis is a severe cutaneous adverse drug reaction, continuous with Stevens-Johnson syndrome and separated from it only by the surface area detached (under 10 percent there, over 30 percent here). A drug, most often a sulfonamide, an aromatic anticonvulsant such as carbamazepine or phenytoin, allopurinol, or a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory agent, generates a drug specific CD8 cytotoxic T cell response against keratinocytes; granulysin, perforin, and Fas ligand drive apoptosis, so the epidermis dies full thickness and separates at the dermoepidermal junction. Prodromal fever is followed by painful dusky macules with targetoid centers that coalesce, then detachment of gray full thickness epidermis, leaving a raw dermis. Two or more mucosal surfaces are involved in most cases: hemorrhagic crusting of the lips, conjunctivitis risking corneal scarring, and genital erosions. Losing the whole epidermis means losing the barrier, so fluid and protein loss, hypothermia, and sepsis follow as in a large burn, and mortality is high."},{"type":"table","title":"Scalded skin syndrome versus toxic epidermal necrolysis","cols":["Feature","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis"],"rows":[["Cause","Exfoliative toxin from a distant S aureus focus","Drug specific cytotoxic T cell attack on keratinocytes"],["Molecular lesion","Cleavage of desmoglein 1","Apoptosis via granulysin, perforin, Fas ligand"],["Split level","Within the granular layer","Dermoepidermal junction, full thickness necrosis"],["Age group","Neonates and young children","Any age; increased with HIV and certain HLA types"],["Mucosa","Spared","Two or more surfaces, including the eyes"],["Denuded surface","Moist, bright red, superficial","Dark red, raw, full thickness"],["Healing","1 to 2 weeks, no scarring","Slow, with scarring and ocular sequelae"]],"caption":"A frozen section of the blister roof separates them: granular layer only, or full epidermis."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two children with peeling skin","text":"Child A is 3, febrile, with crusting around the nares, tender red skin, and flaccid blisters peeling from the neck and groin to leave a moist pink base; the mouth is normal. Child B is 11, began carbamazepine three weeks ago, and has dusky patches that became gray sheets lifting off the chest, with eroded crusted lips and conjunctivitis. Child A has staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome: the nasal focus produces exfoliative toxin, desmoglein 1 is cleaved in the granular layer, and spared mucosa plus a superficial moist base confirm an intraepidermal split. Child B has toxic epidermal necrolysis: the drug timing, mucosal involvement at two sites, and full thickness gray detachment indicate cytotoxic T cell killing of the whole epidermis, with the fluid loss and sepsis risk of a major burn."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C19","obj":"IN-13-H.19","title":"Leprosy: how the host response decides the disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium leprae is an acid fast bacillus with two properties that shape the disease. First, it is an obligate intracellular organism with a massively decayed genome, having lost the genes for many biosynthetic and energy generating pathways, so it depends on the host cell for metabolites and has never been grown in artificial culture; it is propagated in the armadillo or the mouse footpad. Second, it grows optimally at about 30 C (86 F), well below the core temperature of 37 C (98.6 F), so it colonizes the coolest tissues: skin of the face, earlobes, nose, and distal limbs, superficial peripheral nerves such as the ulnar nerve at the elbow, the eye, and the upper respiratory mucosa, while sparing the warm axillae, groin, scalp, and viscera. Within nerves it invades Schwann cells by binding laminin 2 in the basal lamina, which is why sensory loss is an early and defining feature rather than a late complication."},{"type":"p","text":"The infection itself has a very long incubation, often 5 to 20 years, and the clinical form is determined almost entirely by the host T cell response rather than by the organism. A vigorous Th1 response, with interleukin 12 driving interferon gamma production, activates macrophages to kill intracellular bacilli and organize them into granulomas, so bacilli are scarce and lesions are few and sharply defined: tuberculoid leprosy presents as one or a few well demarcated, hypopigmented, dry, hairless plaques with raised borders that are strikingly anesthetic to light touch, pinprick, and temperature, together with asymmetric thickening and dysfunction of one or two named nerve trunks. Skin biopsy shows well formed granulomas around nerves with very few or no acid fast bacilli, and the lepromin skin test is positive because cell mediated reactivity is intact."},{"type":"p","text":"A Th2 polarized response, with interleukin 4 and interleukin 10 suppressing macrophage activation, fails to kill the organism; bacilli multiply unchecked inside foamy macrophages and are present in enormous numbers. Lepromatous leprosy therefore presents with widespread, poorly demarcated, symmetric infiltrated plaques and nodules, diffuse thickening of facial skin with loss of eyebrows and eyelashes, producing the leonine facies, nasal mucosal involvement with obstruction, epistaxis, and eventual septal collapse (saddle nose), and symmetric glove and stocking peripheral neuropathy from diffuse nerve invasion. Biopsy shows sheets of foamy macrophages packed with acid fast bacilli (lepra cells) and a negative lepromin test. Borderline forms occupy the spectrum between the two poles and are unstable, shifting toward either pole as immunity changes. The neurologic consequences are what disable the patient: anesthetic hands and feet accumulate unnoticed trauma, burns, and ulcers, and motor nerve damage produces claw hand and foot drop, so the deformities of leprosy are largely the result of denervation rather than of direct bacterial destruction."},{"type":"table","title":"Tuberculoid versus lepromatous leprosy","cols":["Feature","Tuberculoid","Lepromatous"],"rows":[["T helper polarization","Th1, interleukin 12 and interferon gamma","Th2, interleukin 4 and interleukin 10"],["Macrophage activation","Effective, granulomas form","Ineffective, foamy macrophages fill with bacilli"],["Bacillary load","Paucibacillary, few or none on biopsy","Multibacillary, abundant acid fast bacilli"],["Skin lesions","Few, sharply demarcated, hypopigmented, anesthetic plaques","Many, symmetric, diffuse nodules and infiltration, leonine facies"],["Nerve involvement","Asymmetric, one or a few large nerve trunks, early severe anesthesia","Symmetric glove and stocking neuropathy"],["Lepromin test","Positive","Negative"],["Contagiousness","Low","Higher, nasal secretions carry many bacilli"]],"caption":"One organism, two diseases: the Th1 to Th2 balance sets bacillary load, lesion number, and symmetry."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the cool tissues and the nerves","steps":["Inhaled or contact exposure to Mycobacterium leprae","Optimal growth at about 30 C (86 F) limits it to cool tissue","Skin of face and distal limbs, superficial nerves, nasal mucosa, eye","Invasion of Schwann cells by binding laminin 2","Demyelination and axonal loss with sensory then motor deficit","Unperceived trauma, ulcers, and contracture deformity"],"caption":"Temperature preference explains the distribution, and Schwann cell tropism explains why anesthesia comes first."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C20","obj":"IN-13-H.20","title":"Cutaneous anthrax and the anthrax toxins","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bacillus anthracis is a large, aerobic, nonmotile, spore forming gram positive rod appearing in boxcar shaped chains. Spores persist in soil for decades and infect grazing herbivores, so cutaneous anthrax is classically occupational, in people handling hides, wool, hair, bone meal, or carcasses, and it also follows deliberate release. Spores enter through a cut, are taken up by macrophages, germinate, and the vegetative bacilli multiply locally. Cutaneous disease accounts for roughly 95 percent of naturally occurring anthrax; inhaled spores instead produce hemorrhagic mediastinitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Virulence rests on two plasmid encoded systems. The capsule, encoded on pXO2, is made of poly D glutamic acid rather than polysaccharide; because it is a polymer of the D isomer it is poorly immunogenic and resists host enzymes, and its negative charge repels phagocytes, so the organism evades ingestion and opsonization. The toxins, encoded on pXO1, follow the AB model with one shared B subunit and two alternative A subunits. Protective antigen is the B component: it binds the host anthrax toxin receptors, is cleaved by a host protease, oligomerizes into a ring, and that ring is endocytosed and forms a pore through which an A subunit is translocated into the cytosol. Its name reflects the fact that antibody against it is protective and it is the basis of the vaccine; without it neither A subunit can enter a cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Edema factor is a calmodulin dependent adenylate cyclase. Inside the cytosol it borrows host calmodulin and converts ATP to cyclic AMP far above physiologic regulation. Unopposed cyclic AMP in endothelium and surrounding cells disturbs ion and water handling and raises vascular permeability, so protein rich fluid pours into the interstitium, producing the massive, nonpitting, strikingly painless gelatinous edema that extends well beyond the lesion and gives the toxin its name; the same rise impairs neutrophil chemotaxis. Lethal factor is a zinc dependent metalloprotease that cleaves and inactivates mitogen activated protein kinase kinases, shutting down MAP kinase signaling in macrophages and dendritic cells. That blocks innate immune activation and cytokine responses, impairs endothelial integrity, and at high doses kills macrophages, and lethal toxin is the principal cause of the shock and death of systemic anthrax."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the anthrax toxins enter and act","steps":["Protective antigen binds anthrax toxin receptor on the host cell","Host protease cleaves it and the fragments oligomerize into a ring","Ring is endocytosed and forms a translocation pore","Edema factor enters: calmodulin dependent adenylate cyclase raises cyclic AMP","Lethal factor enters: protease cleaves MAP kinase kinases","Massive painless edema, impaired phagocytes, macrophage dysfunction and shock"],"caption":"One shared delivery subunit carries two enzymes, so antibody to protective antigen neutralizes both."},{"type":"p","text":"The cutaneous lesion follows the biology. Two to seven days after inoculation, a small painless, often pruritic papule appears on an exposed site, usually the hand, forearm, face, or neck. It becomes a vesicle that ulcerates centrally, and over several days the base necroses and dries into a depressed, coal black, adherent eschar, which gives the organism its name (anthrakis, Greek for coal). The eschar is surrounded by brawny nonpitting edema and often regional lymphadenopathy. The most useful discriminating feature is that the lesion is painless, because the toxins do not provoke the suppurative, nerve irritating inflammation of an abscess; a tender, purulent, fluctuant lesion is a staphylococcal abscess or ecthyma, not anthrax."},{"type":"table","title":"Black eschar: narrowing the cause","cols":["Cause","Setting","Discriminating feature"],"rows":[["Cutaneous anthrax","Animal hides, wool, carcasses, deliberate release","Painless papule to vesicle to black eschar with massive nonpitting edema"],["Ecthyma gangrenosum","Neutropenia, Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia","Painful necrotic ulcer with erythematous halo in a severely ill patient"],["Rickettsial or scrub typhus tache noire","Tick or mite bite, with fever and rash","Small painless eschar at the bite plus a systemic febrile rash"],["Spider bite or vasculitic necrosis","Variable","Pain, no spreading gelatinous edema, no exposure history"]],"caption":"Painlessness with disproportionate edema points to toxins rather than suppuration."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-13-08-C21","obj":"IN-13-H.21","title":"Scabies, lice, and bedbugs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ectoparasitic skin disease is largely an immune response to the parasite rather than injury by it, so the timing and distribution of the itch matter more than seeing the organism. Scabies is caused by Sarcoptes scabiei variety hominis, a mite about 0.4 mm long. The fertilized female burrows into the stratum corneum at roughly 2 mm per day, laying two or three eggs daily and leaving feces (scybala) behind her. Only about 10 to 15 mites are present in ordinary scabies, so symptoms cannot be explained by mite burden: the itch is a type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction to mite proteins, eggs, and feces. That is why a first infestation itches only after three to six weeks of sensitization while reinfestation itches within a day or two, and why inflammatory papules and nodules appear at sites where no mite is present."},{"type":"p","text":"The distribution follows where the stratum corneum is thin and warm: finger web spaces, flexor wrists, elbows, axillary folds, periumbilical skin, belt line, buttocks, areolae in women, and the penis and scrotum in men, where firm pruritic nodules are characteristic. The head and neck are spared in adults but commonly involved in infants, who also show palmoplantar vesicles and pustules. The pathognomonic lesion is the burrow, a short scaly serpiginous grey line a few millimeters long, and itch is classically worse at night as mite activity rises with skin warmth. Transmission needs about 15 to 20 minutes of skin to skin contact, so cases cluster in households, sexual partners, and long term care facilities; diagnosis is confirmed by scraping a burrow in mineral oil and finding mites, eggs, or scybala."},{"type":"p","text":"Crusted (Norwegian) scabies is the same mite in a host whose cell mediated immunity cannot mount the hypersensitivity response: advanced HIV infection, human T lymphotropic virus 1 infection, immunosuppressive therapy, severe malnutrition, or impairment that prevents scratching. Without an effective response the mites multiply into the thousands or millions, producing thick, hyperkeratotic, fissured, warty crusts on the hands, feet, elbows, and scalp with surprisingly little itch, enormous mite counts, and extreme contagiousness, so it is a common source of institutional outbreaks."},{"type":"table","title":"Ectoparasites compared","cols":["Parasite","Where it lives","Lesion and itch","Clinical significance"],"rows":[["Sarcoptes scabiei","Burrows in the stratum corneum","Burrows plus widespread papules, nocturnal itch","Crusted scabies with immunosuppression; household spread"],["Pediculus humanus capitis","Scalp hair, nits cemented near the scalp","Occipital and retroauricular itch, excoriation","School age children; direct head contact"],["Pthirus pubis","Coarse pubic, perianal, axillary, eyelash hair","Pruritus, blue grey macules from feeding","Sexually transmitted; evaluate for other infections"],["Pediculus humanus corporis","Seams of clothing, not on the body","Excoriated truncal papules, postinflammatory pigment","Vector for Rickettsia prowazekii, Bartonella quintana, Borrelia recurrentis"],["Cimex lectularius","Mattress seams and crevices, feeds at night","Pruritic urticarial papules in linear clusters","Not a disease vector"]],"caption":"Where a parasite lives between meals predicts lesion distribution and vector potential."},{"type":"p","text":"Lice are wingless blood feeding insects, and their epidemiology follows where they live between meals. Head lice stay on the scalp and transmit by direct head to head contact or shared headgear; nits are cemented to the hair shaft and cannot be slid off, which distinguishes them from hair casts and dandruff. Pubic lice are transmitted sexually, so their presence warrants evaluation for other sexually transmitted infections. Body lice are the one important vector, because they live in clothing seams rather than on the host and travel with the clothing; they thrive in crowding, cold, and situations where clothes cannot be changed, and their feces scratched into the bite transmit Rickettsia prowazekii (epidemic typhus), Bartonella quintana (trench fever), and Borrelia recurrentis (louse borne relapsing fever). Bedbugs feed at night and retreat to crevices by day, so they are rarely seen on the patient, and they are not known to transmit human disease."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The organizing principle across this chapter is anatomy. The level a pathogen reaches determines the lesion, so the lesion names the level. A subcorneal pustule is impetigo, a split in the granular layer means desmoglein 1 has been cleaved by a staphylococcal protease or by a pemphigus foliaceus autoantibody, separation at the dermoepidermal junction with full thickness necrosis means cytotoxic T cells have killed the epidermis, infection of the upper dermis and its lymphatics gives the raised sharp border of erysipelas, infection of the deep dermis and fat gives the fading border of cellulitis, inflammation of the fat septa gives the nonulcerating nodules of erythema nodosum, and spread along the superficial fascia gives pain out of proportion to the skin. Microbiology then explains who gets where: keratinases let dermatophytes occupy only dead keratin, lipid dependence confines Malassezia to sebaceous skin, follicular anatomy admits Staphylococcus aureus, and barrier breaches such as a tinea pedis fissure admit streptococci."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology explains severity, and biochemistry explains the toxins. The same organism produces opposite diseases depending on the host response, most clearly in leprosy, where Th1 polarization gives paucibacillary tuberculoid disease and Th2 polarization gives multibacillary lepromatous disease, and in candidiasis, where an intact interleukin 17 axis confines Candida to a fold and its failure produces chronic mucocutaneous disease. Extensive warts or molluscum reports T cell failure, recurrent furuncles report neutrophil failure, and the pattern of skin infection is therefore a readable immunologic assay. The toxins are enzymes with identifiable substrates: a serine protease cutting desmoglein 1, a phospholipase C hydrolyzing membrane lecithin, metalloproteases cleaving synaptobrevin centrally in tetanus and peripherally in botulism, an adenylate cyclase raising cyclic AMP in anthrax, and superantigens cross-linking major histocompatibility complex class II to the T cell receptor in scarlet fever and toxic shock. Two cautions limit this reasoning: clinical appearance overlaps considerably, so cellulitis is mimicked by stasis dermatitis, contact dermatitis, deep vein thrombosis, and gout, and every per exposure transmission figure and prevalence estimate quoted here is an average that varies with source viral load, region, and host factors."}],"summary":["Depth determines appearance: impetigo is subcorneal, bullous impetigo splits the granular layer, erysipelas is upper dermis with a raised sharp border, cellulitis is deep dermis and fat with a fading border, and necrotizing fasciitis travels the superficial fascia.","Acne needs all four steps: follicular hyperkeratinization, androgen driven sebum, Cutibacterium acnes growth, and inflammation after follicular rupture; comedones do not scar and nodules do.","Candidal rashes occupy moist folds with satellite pustules, irritant diaper dermatitis spares the folds, and persistent mucocutaneous candidiasis points to a Th17 or interleukin 17 defect.","Exfoliative toxin is a serine protease that cleaves desmoglein 1: locally it gives bullous impetigo, in the circulation it gives scalded skin syndrome, and mucosa is spared because desmoglein 3 covers mucosal adhesion.","Toxic epidermal necrolysis is drug triggered cytotoxic T cell killing of the full epidermis with two or more mucosal surfaces involved, which is why it scars and kills while scalded skin syndrome heals in two weeks.","Pain out of proportion to the skin, crepitus, and rapid advance mean necrotizing fasciitis, because fascial spread thromboses the vessels and nerves supplying the overlying skin.","Erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis without vasculitis, so shin nodules are tender but never ulcerate or scar, and the task is to find the trigger: streptococcus, sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, endemic fungi, inflammatory bowel disease, or pregnancy.","Impetigo from Streptococcus pyogenes can be followed by glomerulonephritis but not by rheumatic fever, and anti DNase B is the better marker after skin infection because skin lipids inhibit streptolysin O.","mecA on SCCmec encodes PBP2a with low beta-lactam affinity, so resistance covers the whole class; community MRSA carries Panton-Valentine leukocidin and the anterior nares are the reservoir.","Needle stick risk falls roughly tenfold at each step, hepatitis B about 30 percent from an e antigen positive source, hepatitis C about 2 percent, HIV about 0.3 percent, tracking circulating viral titer, and only hepatitis B is vaccine preventable."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Streptococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Mycology"},{"book":"Cellular and Molecular Immunology (Abbas)","where":"Immunity to Microbes"},{"book":"Pathophysiology of Disease: An Introduction to Clinical Medicine (Lange)","where":"Skin Disease"}]},"IN-2":{"unit":"IN-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01","title":"Ectoderm Embryology and Skin Development","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the ectoderm as it splits into surface ectoderm, neuroectoderm, and neural crest, then traces how the skin, its appendages, and its pigment cells form before birth. Board questions usually name a structure and ask for its germ layer, or describe a newborn skin finding or pigment disorder and ask where development went wrong. The unifying idea is that melanocytes are migrants: they start at the neural tube and must travel, survive, and then make melanin. Knowing which of those steps failed explains a blue sacral patch, a white forelock, and generalized albinism.","prerequisites":["The three germ layers and gastrulation","Somite organization into sclerotome and dermomyotome","Epidermal layers, melanocytes, and adnexa of adult skin (IN-1)","Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling","Autosomal dominant and recessive inheritance"],"connections":[{"to":"Neural tube embryology (NE-1)","how":"Neurulation creates the neural tube and releases the neural crest, so neural tube and crest defects share their origin in the same event."},{"to":"Skin layers and adnexa (IN-1)","how":"The adult epidermis, follicle, and glands described there are built by the developmental steps in this chapter."},{"to":"Pigmentation changes of the skin (IN-13-A)","how":"Vitiligo, albinism, and nevi are classified by whether melanocytes are missing, present but inactive, or proliferating."},{"to":"Endocrine embryology (EN-1)","how":"The anterior and posterior pituitary, adrenal medulla, and thyroid C cells come from three different ectodermal lineages."},{"to":"Congenital gastrointestinal disease (GI-13-H)","how":"Hirschsprung disease is a failure of neural crest migration to the distal bowel, the enteric counterpart of piebaldism."}],"terms":[{"term":"Surface ectoderm","def":"Ectoderm that remains outside the neural plate and forms the epidermis, its appendages, and several sensory and oral epithelia."},{"term":"Neuroectoderm","def":"Ectoderm of the neural plate that folds into the neural tube and forms the central nervous system, retina, and posterior pituitary."},{"term":"Neural crest","def":"Cells from the border of the neural plate that undergo an epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition and migrate to form many peripheral tissues."},{"term":"Epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition","def":"The loss of cell junctions and polarity that lets an epithelial cell become a motile mesenchymal cell."},{"term":"Dermomyotome","def":"The dorsal part of a somite, which gives rise to the dermis of the back (dermatome) and to skeletal muscle (myotome)."},{"term":"Periderm","def":"A temporary superficial layer of flattened fetal epidermal cells that covers the developing epidermis and is later shed."},{"term":"Melanoblast","def":"A neural crest-derived precursor of the melanocyte that is still migrating and does not yet make melanin."},{"term":"Dermal papilla","def":"A condensation of mesenchyme at the base of the hair bud that signals to the epithelium to form and grow the hair."},{"term":"Lanugo","def":"Fine, unpigmented first fetal hair, appearing around the fifth month and mostly shed before or around birth."},{"term":"Vernix caseosa","def":"A whitish fetal skin coating of sebum, shed periderm and epidermal cells, and water that protects the skin in amniotic fluid."},{"term":"Tyndall effect","def":"Scattering of shorter blue wavelengths by tissue, which makes melanin deep in the dermis appear blue-gray instead of brown."},{"term":"KIT","def":"The receptor tyrosine kinase for stem cell factor, required for melanoblast survival, proliferation, and migration."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C01","obj":"IN-2.1","title":"Surface ectoderm, neuroectoderm, and neural crest","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"After gastrulation, the ectoderm is a single sheet on the dorsal surface of the embryo. Around day 18 to 19, the notochord and prechordal mesoderm secrete noggin, chordin, and follistatin, which block bone morphogenetic protein 4 (BMP4) in the overlying ectoderm. Ectoderm with low BMP signaling thickens into the neural plate (neuroectoderm); ectoderm that keeps high BMP signaling becomes surface ectoderm. The neural plate folds, and its edges fuse in the midline starting in the future cervical region, forming the neural tube."},{"type":"p","text":"Cells at the border between the plate and surface ectoderm receive intermediate BMP signals together with Wnt and fibroblast growth factor signals. They express transcription factors such as SNAI2 (Slug), SOX10, and PAX3, lose their epithelial junctions, and migrate away as neural crest. Cranial crest begins migrating before the folds fuse; trunk crest leaves as the tube closes. Because all three lineages arise from one sheet in one event, a single developmental error can affect the brain, the skin, and crest derivatives together."},{"type":"flow","title":"One ectoderm, three lineages","steps":["Notochord secretes noggin and chordin","BMP4 blocked in midline ectoderm","Neural plate forms and folds","Folds fuse into the neural tube (neuroectoderm)","Border cells lose their junctions and leave as crest","Lateral ectoderm stays as surface ectoderm"],"caption":"Low BMP gives neural plate, high BMP gives surface ectoderm, and the border between them gives neural crest."},{"type":"table","title":"Major ectodermal derivatives","cols":["Lineage","Skin and appendages","Head and sense organs","Other"],"rows":[["Surface ectoderm","Epidermis, hair, nails, sweat and sebaceous glands, mammary glands","Lens, corneal epithelium, inner ear epithelium (otic placode), olfactory epithelium","Anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), tooth enamel (ameloblasts), oral epithelium anterior to the oropharyngeal membrane"],["Neuroectoderm","None","Retina including pigment epithelium, optic nerve, iris sphincter and dilator muscles","CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependyma, posterior pituitary, pineal gland"],["Neural crest","Melanocytes, dermis of the face","Corneal stroma and endothelium, facial bones and cartilage, pia and arachnoid over the forebrain","Peripheral ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, odontoblasts, thyroid C cells"]],"caption":"Microglia are the exception in the CNS: they come from yolk sac macrophage precursors (mesoderm), not neuroectoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"Several pairs of adjacent structures have different origins. The anterior pituitary grows up from the roof of the primitive mouth (Rathke pouch, surface ectoderm), while the posterior pituitary grows down from the diencephalon (neuroectoderm); remnants of Rathke pouch give rise to craniopharyngioma. In the eye, the lens and corneal epithelium are surface ectoderm, but the retina and its pigment epithelium are outgrowths of the brain. In the tooth, enamel is surface ectoderm while dentin is made by crest-derived odontoblasts."},{"type":"p","text":"Surface ectoderm also forms placodes, local thickenings that give rise to the lens, the otic vesicle (inner ear membranous labyrinth), the olfactory epithelium, and some sensory neurons of cranial nerve ganglia V, VII, IX, and X, which share these ganglia with neural crest. Mammary glands bud from paired mammary ridges (milk lines) of surface ectoderm running from axilla to groin; extra nipples (polythelia) appear along this line. Skin appendages arise from shared placode signaling, which is why X-linked hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (EDA gene) causes sparse hair, absent sweat glands with heat intolerance, and missing or conical teeth together."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has a lens defect and a pituitary tumor arising from the sella that contains calcifications. Which shared germ layer links the lens and this tumor?","explain":"Surface ectoderm. The lens forms from the lens placode, and craniopharyngioma arises from remnants of Rathke pouch, an upgrowth of oral surface ectoderm. The posterior pituitary and retina, by contrast, are neuroectoderm.","check":"The derivatives table and the paragraph on adjacent structures with different origins."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C02","obj":"IN-2.2","title":"Neural crest derivatives and the regional origin of the dermis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neural crest cells are multipotent, and their fate depends largely on the route they take and the signals they meet. In the trunk, crest cells follow two main routes. The ventral pathway runs through the rostral half of each sclerotome; cells on it become dorsal root ganglia, sympathetic chain ganglia, Schwann cells, and chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla. The dorsolateral pathway runs between the dermomyotome and the surface ectoderm; cells on it become melanoblasts. Vagal and sacral crest colonize the gut wall as enteric ganglia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Trunk neural crest routes","steps":["Crest cells leave the dorsal neural tube","Ventral route through rostral sclerotome","Dorsal root and sympathetic ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla","Dorsolateral route under the surface ectoderm","Melanoblasts enter dermis, then epidermis and hair bulbs"],"caption":"The route determines the fate: ventral cells form neurons, glia, and chromaffin cells, and dorsolateral cells form pigment cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Neural crest derivatives and the disorders they explain","cols":["Derivative","Function","Clinical link"],"rows":[["Melanocytes","Melanin synthesis in skin, hair, uvea, and inner ear (stria vascularis)","Piebaldism, Waardenburg syndrome, melanoma"],["Schwann cells","Myelin and support for peripheral axons","Schwannoma, neurofibroma"],["Dorsal root and autonomic ganglia","Sensory and autonomic neurons","Neuroblastoma arises in the sympathetic lineage"],["Enteric ganglia","Myenteric and submucosal plexuses","Hirschsprung disease"],["Adrenal medulla chromaffin cells","Catecholamine secretion","Pheochromocytoma, neuroblastoma"],["Odontoblasts","Dentin of the tooth","Dentin is crest-derived even though the enamel over it is surface ectoderm"],["Cranial crest mesenchyme","Facial bones, cartilage, and dermis; pia and arachnoid over the forebrain; thyroid C cells","Treacher Collins syndrome, medullary thyroid carcinoma (C cells)"]],"caption":"Tumors and congenital defects of these tissues are grouped as neurocristopathies."},{"type":"h","text":"Why facial dermis is neural crest and back dermis is somitic"},{"type":"p","text":"The epidermis is surface ectoderm everywhere, but the dermis forms from whatever mesenchyme lies beneath it, and that mesenchyme differs by region. Somites begin in the occipital region, so there are no somites under the face. Instead, cranial neural crest streams into the frontonasal process and pharyngeal arches and forms most of the facial mesenchyme, which becomes facial bone, cartilage, connective tissue, and dermis. Over the back, each somite's dermomyotome releases dermatome cells that spread beneath the overlying ectoderm and form the dorsal dermis. In the limbs and ventral body wall, the dermis comes from the somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm."},{"type":"table","title":"Origin of the two skin layers by region","cols":["Region","Epidermis","Dermis"],"rows":[["Face","Surface ectoderm","Cranial neural crest"],["Back","Surface ectoderm","Paraxial mesoderm (somite dermatomes)"],["Limbs and ventral body wall","Surface ectoderm","Lateral plate mesoderm (somatic layer)"]],"caption":"In every region, the epidermis and dermis of the same patch of skin can come from different germ layers."},{"type":"p","text":"Dermal mesenchyme signals to the overlying epidermis and helps decide what kind of appendages form, so the dermis shapes regional skin character. Do not confuse the embryonic dermatome (the part of a somite that forms dorsal dermis) with the clinical dermatome (the skin area supplied by one spinal nerve): limb skin has a segmental nerve supply even though its dermis is lateral plate mesoderm."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out the origin of one patch of skin","text":"Take a biopsy from the cheek and one from the skin over the lower thoracic spine. Both epidermal layers are surface ectoderm, and the melanocytes in both are neural crest. The difference lies in the dermis. The cheek lies over the first pharyngeal arch, which has no somites beneath it and is filled with cranial crest mesenchyme, so its dermis is neural crest. The back lies directly over the somites, so its dermis comes from somite dermatomes, which is paraxial mesoderm. A forearm biopsy would differ again, since limb buds are built from lateral plate mesoderm."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C03","obj":"IN-2.3","title":"Fetal skin development","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fetal skin turns a single sheet of surface ectoderm into a stratified, keratinized barrier in a fixed order. During the second month, that single layer divides into a deep basal (germinative) layer and a superficial periderm of flattened cells, which seals the embryo while the layers beneath it mature. Proliferation of the basal layer then adds an intermediate layer, and by about the end of the fourth month the epidermis has its definitive arrangement of basal, spinous, granular, and cornified layers. As keratinization proceeds, periderm cells are shed into the amniotic fluid."},{"type":"p","text":"The barrier is not mature until late gestation. The stratum corneum becomes an effective barrier at about 32 to 34 weeks, so a very preterm infant has thin, permeable skin that loses water and heat quickly and absorbs substances applied to it more readily."},{"type":"table","title":"Approximate timeline of fetal skin development","cols":["Time","Event"],"rows":[["Second month (weeks 5 to 8)","Single ectodermal layer divides into basal layer and periderm"],["First trimester","Melanoblasts migrate from the neural crest into the dermis and then the epidermis"],["About weeks 9 to 12","Hair buds grow down into the dermis; dermal papillae form at their tips"],["By about week 16","Epidermis has its adult layers; periderm is being shed"],["About weeks 17 to 20","Lanugo covers the body, after the first hairs appear on brow and upper lip near the end of month 3; vernix caseosa begins to accumulate"],["About weeks 32 to 34","Stratum corneum barrier becomes functional"],["Around birth","Most lanugo has been shed; vernix covers the term infant"]],"caption":"Timings are approximate and vary slightly between embryology texts."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanoblasts from the neural crest migrate under the surface ectoderm, cross the dermis, and enter the epidermis during the first trimester, where they settle among basal cells and in developing hair bulbs. They begin producing melanin in fetal life, but skin color continues to darken after birth as melanin production increases."},{"type":"p","text":"Hair forms through reciprocal signaling between epithelium and mesenchyme. Basal cells thicken into a placode, which grows down into the dermis as a hair bud. Dermal mesenchyme condenses at the tip of the bud as the dermal papilla, and the bud end invaginates around it to form the bulb. The papilla then signals the surrounding matrix cells to divide and produce the hair shaft. Sebaceous glands bud from the side of the follicle wall, and the bulge forms below them, where the arrector pili muscle (from mesenchyme) attaches. The first hairs, lanugo, are fine and unpigmented; most are shed into the amniotic fluid late in gestation and are replaced by vellus and terminal hairs. Preterm infants are often still covered in lanugo."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hair follicle formation","steps":["Basal cells thicken into a hair placode","Hair bud grows down into the dermis","Mesenchyme condenses at the tip as the dermal papilla","Bud end invaginates around the papilla to form the bulb","Matrix cells divide and form the hair shaft","Sebaceous gland buds from the follicle wall"],"caption":"The dermal papilla instructs the epithelium; without it, no hair shaft forms."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal sebaceous glands are active in late gestation under androgen stimulation. Their sebum mixes with shed periderm and epidermal cells, water, and lanugo to form vernix caseosa, a whitish coat that protects the skin from maceration during months in amniotic fluid. Vernix also contains antimicrobial proteins and helps moisturize the skin after birth. A post-term infant often has little vernix left and shows dry, peeling skin, while a preterm infant may have vernix but an immature barrier beneath it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C04","obj":"IN-2.4","title":"Dermal melanocytosis and other newborn skin lesions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Melanoblasts reach the epidermis by crossing the dermis, and almost all dermal melanocytes normally disappear or move on by birth. Some persist, most often over the lumbosacral region and buttocks. These arrested dermal melanocytes are spindle-shaped, lie deep in the reticular dermis, and keep making melanin. The result is dermal melanocytosis (Mongolian spot): a flat, uniform, blue-gray patch present at birth. It is common in infants of Asian, African, Native American, and Hispanic descent and uncommon in those of European descent. Most patches fade during early childhood, though those outside the sacral area more often persist."},{"type":"p","text":"The color is explained by depth. Epidermal melanin absorbs most visible light and looks brown, while deep dermal melanin is reached mainly by longer wavelengths and the shorter blue wavelengths scatter back from the tissue above it (the Tyndall effect), so the patch looks blue-gray. The same principle colors blue nevi. Because a sacral or extensive patch can be mistaken for bruising in suspected child abuse, documenting it at birth matters. Unlike a bruise, it does not change color over days and is not tender."},{"type":"flow","title":"From arrested migration to a blue-gray patch","steps":["Melanoblasts cross the dermis toward the epidermis","Some remain in the deep dermis, usually sacral","Dermal melanocytes keep producing melanin","Overlying tissue scatters blue light back (Tyndall effect)","Flat blue-gray patch present at birth","Most fade during early childhood"],"caption":"The lesion is benign; its color comes from the depth of the pigment, not from a different pigment."},{"type":"p","text":"Three other common newborn lesions are separated by the cell involved and by their course. A congenital melanocytic nevus is a proliferation of nevus cells present from fetal life, often reaching deep into the dermis and around adnexa, so it is brown and may grow coarse hair; large and giant lesions carry increased melanoma risk. An infantile hemangioma is a benign proliferation of GLUT1-positive endothelium, so it is absent or faint at birth, grows rapidly for months, then involutes over years. A port-wine stain is a capillary malformation of mature, dilated dermal capillaries and venules, usually from a somatic GNAQ mutation, so it is present at birth, grows only in proportion to the child, never regresses, and tends to darken and thicken in adulthood. Unilateral involvement of the ophthalmic (V1) distribution can accompany leptomeningeal and ocular vascular malformation (Sturge-Weber syndrome), with seizures and glaucoma. Nevus simplex, the far more common faint midline patch on glabella, eyelids, or nape, fades over the first years and carries no such risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing pigmented and vascular lesions at birth","cols":["Lesion","Cells involved","At birth","Course"],"rows":[["Dermal melanocytosis","Arrested melanocytes in the deep dermis","Flat blue-gray patch, usually lumbosacral","Usually fades in early childhood"],["Congenital melanocytic nevus","Nevus cells in dermis and around adnexa","Brown, sometimes hairy, may be raised","Grows with the child; melanoma risk mainly in large lesions"],["Infantile hemangioma","Proliferating GLUT1-positive endothelium","Absent or faint mark","Rapid growth over months, then slow involution"],["Port-wine stain","Mature dilated capillaries (GNAQ)","Flat pink to red patch","Persists, darkens, may thicken; V1 suggests Sturge-Weber"]],"caption":"A tumor grows faster than the child and may regress; a malformation grows in proportion and persists."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has a unilateral red patch covering one side of the forehead and the upper eyelid, fully formed at birth and grown only in proportion at 6 months. A second infant has no lesion at birth but a rapidly enlarging red nodule at 6 weeks. Which lesion will regress?","explain":"The second. A lesion that appears after birth and grows faster than the child is an infantile hemangioma, a proliferating endothelial tumor that later involutes. The first is a port-wine stain, a capillary malformation that never regresses, and its V1 location raises concern for Sturge-Weber syndrome.","check":"The newborn lesion table and the tumor versus malformation distinction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-02-01-C05","obj":"IN-2.5","title":"Piebaldism versus albinism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A white patch of skin can arise in three ways: melanocytes never arrived (piebaldism), they arrived but cannot make melanin (albinism), or they were destroyed after arriving (vitiligo)."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanoblasts depend on the KIT receptor tyrosine kinase. Its ligand, stem cell factor, is produced along their migratory route, and KIT signaling keeps melanoblasts alive and drives their proliferation and migration. Piebaldism is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by a loss-of-function KIT mutation. With reduced signaling, melanoblasts fail to reach the sites farthest from their dorsal origin or to survive there. Those sites lack melanocytes permanently, giving a white forelock and white patches on the central forehead, ventral trunk, and mid-arms and legs, usually sparing the back, hands, and feet. The patches are stable for life and often contain islands of normal or darker pigment. Biopsy shows absent or nearly absent melanocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Oculocutaneous albinism is autosomal recessive and affects pigment synthesis, not migration. Melanocyte number and distribution are normal, but melanin output is reduced. In type 1, tyrosinase, the copper-dependent enzyme that converts tyrosine to DOPA and DOPA to dopaquinone, is absent or deficient. Other types involve proteins needed for melanosome function, such as the OCA2 (P) protein. Because every melanocyte is affected, hypopigmentation involves all skin, hair, and eyes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Melanin synthesis and where albinism fails","steps":["Tyrosine enters the melanosome","Tyrosinase converts tyrosine to DOPA","Tyrosinase converts DOPA to dopaquinone","Dopaquinone forms eumelanin or pheomelanin","Melanosomes transfer to keratinocytes"],"caption":"Type 1 albinism blocks the first two steps; the melanocytes are present but produce little or no melanin."},{"type":"p","text":"The eye findings show where pigment matters for development. The retinal pigment epithelium is neuroectoderm, not neural crest, but it uses tyrosinase too, so albinism reduces its pigment. Melanin is needed for normal foveal development and for routing of optic nerve fibers, so albinism causes foveal hypoplasia, excessive crossing of fibers at the optic chiasm, nystagmus, reduced acuity, and photophobia with iris transillumination. Lacking melanin, the skin has little protection from ultraviolet light and develops sun damage and carcinomas, especially squamous cell carcinoma. In piebaldism the eyes are normal, because the retinal pigment epithelium does not depend on crest migration. When a crest disorder does affect the eye and ear, as in Waardenburg syndrome (PAX3, MITF, SOX10), iris heterochromia and deafness appear, since melanocytes of the iris stroma and cochlear stria vascularis are crest-derived."},{"type":"table","title":"White skin: absent melanocytes or absent melanin","cols":["Feature","Piebaldism","Oculocutaneous albinism","Vitiligo"],"rows":[["Defect","KIT; failed melanoblast migration or survival","Tyrosinase or melanosome protein; failed melanin synthesis","Acquired autoimmune destruction of melanocytes"],["Inheritance","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","Not Mendelian; polygenic susceptibility"],["Melanocytes in white skin","Absent","Normal number","Absent"],["Onset and pattern","Birth; forelock, ventral and limb patches; stable","Birth; generalized skin, hair, eyes","Acquired; often periorificial and acral; may spread"],["Eyes","Normal","Nystagmus, foveal hypoplasia, low acuity","Usually normal"]],"caption":"Count the melanocytes first, then ask whether the defect is present at birth and whether it is localized or generalized."},{"type":"example","title":"Two pale children","text":"Child A has a white forelock and white patches on the abdomen and knees since birth, with normal eyes; a parent has the same forelock. Child B has white hair, very pale skin everywhere, and nystagmus; both parents are normally pigmented. In A, the patchy, stable pattern and dominant inheritance point to piebaldism, and a biopsy of a white patch would show no melanocytes, because melanoblasts never colonized those ventral and distal sites. In B, the generalized pattern, eye findings, and unaffected carrier parents point to recessive albinism. A biopsy would show melanocytes in normal numbers that make little melanin, and in type 1 the defect is tyrosinase."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and genetics explain the patterns. The ectoderm splits into three lineages in one event, so the same signaling defect can involve skin, nervous system, and crest derivatives, as in ectodermal dysplasias and Waardenburg syndrome. Neural crest cells share migration machinery, which is why defects in genes such as SOX10 or EDNRB can produce pigment loss, deafness, and Hirschsprung disease together, and why piebaldism and Hirschsprung disease are both failures of crest colonization. Regional dermal origin (crest, somite, or lateral plate) explains why facial anomalies follow pharyngeal arch boundaries and why dorsal dermis is organized segmentally."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and pathology explain the pigment findings. Melanin synthesis begins at tyrosinase, so tyrosinase loss gives albinism, and in untreated phenylketonuria, low tyrosine and high phenylalanine reduce melanin and lighten hair and skin. Histology answers most pigment questions: absent melanocytes (piebaldism, vitiligo), normal melanocytes with little pigment (albinism), or melanocytes in the wrong place (dermal melanocytosis). The limits are clinical: a Mongolian spot cannot be told from a bruise by color alone, and only the course over weeks separates an early hemangioma from a capillary malformation."}],"summary":["BMP inhibition from the notochord makes neural plate; high BMP leaves surface ectoderm; the border forms neural crest by epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition.","Surface ectoderm forms epidermis, hair, nails, cutaneous and mammary glands, lens, corneal epithelium, anterior pituitary, and enamel.","Neuroectoderm forms CNS neurons and macroglia, retina and pigment epithelium, posterior pituitary, and pineal gland; microglia are mesodermal.","Neural crest forms melanocytes, Schwann cells, peripheral ganglia, adrenal medulla, odontoblasts, and facial mesenchyme including facial dermis.","Dermis comes from cranial crest in the face, somite dermatomes in the back, and lateral plate mesoderm in the limbs and ventral body wall.","Fetal epidermis passes from one layer to basal layer plus periderm to adult layers by about the fourth month; the barrier matures at about 32 to 34 weeks.","The dermal papilla induces hair formation; lanugo appears around the fifth month, and vernix is sebum plus shed cells that protects skin in amniotic fluid.","Dermal melanocytosis is arrested dermal melanocytes that look blue-gray by the Tyndall effect and usually fade; it must not be mistaken for bruising.","Infantile hemangioma (GLUT1 tumor) appears after birth, grows, and involutes; port-wine stain (GNAQ malformation) is present at birth and persists.","Piebaldism (dominant KIT) lacks melanocytes in stable patches with normal eyes; albinism (recessive, tyrosinase in type 1) has normal melanocytes that make little melanin."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Integumentary system; ectodermal germ layer derivatives and neural crest cells"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, disorders of pigmentation and melanocytes; blood vessels, vascular ectasias and hemangiomas"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Amino acid conversion to specialized products, melanin synthesis"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin, melanocytes and epidermal appendages"}]},"IN-3":{"unit":"IN-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01","title":"Membrane Transport and Membrane Potential","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the plasma membrane is built, how solutes and water cross it, and how ion gradients across it create the resting membrane potential. These ideas sit underneath nearly every system: cell swelling in hypoxic injury, glucose absorption and glucosuria, the effect of potassium on nerve, muscle, and the ECG, and the salty sweat and thick mucus of cystic fibrosis. Board questions usually change one variable, such as a pump, a channel, an ion concentration, or the solution around a cell, and ask you to predict cell volume, membrane potential, or excitability. If you can reason from gradients, permeability, and effective solute, you can answer them without memorizing each case.","prerequisites":["Structure of fatty acids (saturated and cis unsaturated), phospholipids, and cholesterol","Concentration units: mmol/L, mOsm/L, and mOsm/kg, and base 10 logarithms","Basic electricity: charge, voltage, current, and the idea of a driving force","ATP production by glycolysis and oxidative phosphorylation","Histology of the eccrine sweat gland (IN-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Cell adaptation and injury (IN-8)","how":"Failure of the Na+/K+-ATPase when ATP falls is the mechanism of cellular swelling, the first sign of reversible hypoxic injury."},{"to":"Skin layers and adnexa (IN-1)","how":"Eccrine duct reabsorption of NaCl through CFTR and ENaC explains why sweat is hypotonic and why it is salty in cystic fibrosis."},{"to":"Cardiac muscle contraction (CV-5)","how":"Extracellular K+ and the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger link the resting potential and the Na+ gradient to cardiac excitability, ECG changes, and contractility."},{"to":"Renal reabsorption (UR-6)","how":"SGLT2 and SGLT1 in the proximal tubule are saturable carriers, so glucose appears in the urine once their transport maximum is exceeded."},{"to":"Digestion and absorption (GI-6)","how":"Enterocytes absorb glucose and galactose by Na+-coupled SGLT1 and release them by GLUT2, a model of secondary active transport."}],"terms":[{"term":"Amphipathic","def":"Having both a water-attracting (polar) region and a water-repelling (nonpolar) region, as phospholipids and cholesterol do."},{"term":"Glycocalyx","def":"The carbohydrate coat on the outer cell surface formed by the sugar chains of glycoproteins, glycolipids, and proteoglycans."},{"term":"Transport maximum (Tmax)","def":"The highest rate a carrier-mediated process can reach once every carrier is occupied, which makes the process saturable."},{"term":"Secondary active transport","def":"Uphill movement of one solute powered by the downhill movement of another ion, usually Na+, whose gradient was built by an ATPase."},{"term":"Electrogenic","def":"Describes a transporter that moves net charge across the membrane in each cycle and therefore directly changes membrane voltage."},{"term":"Osmolality","def":"The number of osmoles of all dissolved particles per kilogram of water; osmolarity is the same count per liter of solution."},{"term":"Tonicity","def":"The effect of a solution on cell volume, determined only by the solutes that cannot cross the cell membrane."},{"term":"Effective osmole","def":"A solute that stays on one side of the membrane (reflection coefficient near 1) and therefore exerts a lasting osmotic pull on water."},{"term":"Equilibrium (Nernst) potential","def":"The membrane voltage at which the electrical force on an ion exactly balances its concentration gradient, so its net flux is zero."},{"term":"Resting membrane potential","def":"The steady voltage of the cell interior relative to the outside in an unstimulated cell, set mainly by K+ permeability and the K+ gradient."},{"term":"Gibbs-Donnan effect","def":"The uneven distribution of diffusible ions and the extra osmotic load that result when impermeant charged molecules are trapped on one side of a membrane."},{"term":"Inactivation gate","def":"The part of a voltage-gated Na+ channel that closes during sustained depolarization and reopens only after the membrane repolarizes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C01","obj":"IN-3.1","title":"Plasma membrane components and membrane fluidity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The plasma membrane is a lipid bilayer about 7.5 to 10 nm thick with proteins set into it. Phospholipids are amphipathic: a polar head group attached to two fatty acid tails. In water they assemble spontaneously into a bilayer, heads facing the cytosol and the extracellular fluid and tails forming a hydrophobic core. That core is the barrier. Small nonpolar molecules such as O2, CO2, and steroids dissolve through it, small uncharged polar molecules cross slowly, and ions and larger polar molecules such as glucose essentially cannot cross without a protein."},{"type":"p","text":"The two leaflets differ. Phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin predominate in the outer leaflet, while phosphatidylserine, phosphatidylethanolamine, and phosphatidylinositol lie mainly in the inner leaflet, an asymmetry maintained by ATP-dependent flippases that return aminophospholipids to the inner leaflet. Phosphatidylserine exposed on the outer surface signals phagocytes to remove an apoptotic cell, and inner leaflet phosphatidylinositol, once phosphorylated to phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2), is the substrate that phospholipase C cleaves into the second messengers IP3 and diacylglycerol."},{"type":"p","text":"Integral proteins cross the bilayer, usually as alpha helices of about 20 hydrophobic amino acids, and can be removed only with detergents that dissolve the lipid; they form channels, carriers, pumps, receptors, and adhesion molecules. Peripheral proteins attach to lipid heads or integral proteins by ionic and hydrogen bonds; on the cytoplasmic side many link the membrane to the cytoskeleton, such as spectrin and ankyrin in red cells. The glycocalyx, built from the sugar chains of glycoproteins, glycolipids, and proteoglycans, lies only on the outer surface. Sialic acid and sulfated sugars give it a net negative charge, and its sugars serve as recognition and adhesion sites, including the ABO blood group antigens."},{"type":"p","text":"Fluidity describes how freely lipids and proteins move sideways within a leaflet; movement between leaflets is rare without enzymes. It depends on how tightly the tails pack. A cis double bond puts a fixed kink in a tail, so unsaturated tails cannot pack closely and fluidity rises. Shorter tails make fewer van der Waals contacts and also raise fluidity, while long saturated tails pack tightly and order the membrane. Cholesterol acts as a buffer rather than a one-way stiffener. Its rigid ring system lies beside the upper part of the tails: at body temperature it restrains tail motion, lowering fluidity and permeability to small water-soluble molecules, and at low temperature it wedges the tails apart and prevents them from freezing into a gel. Fluidity therefore stays nearly constant over a range of temperatures."},{"type":"table","title":"Plasma membrane components","cols":["Component","Structure","Main role","Exam link"],"rows":[["Phospholipids","Polar head, two fatty acid tails; asymmetric leaflets","Barrier to ions and polar solutes","Outer phosphatidylserine marks apoptotic cells"],["Cholesterol","Rigid steroid rings with a small polar hydroxyl group","Buffers fluidity; lowers permeability","Excess red cell membrane cholesterol in severe liver disease produces spur cells (acanthocytes)"],["Integral proteins","Span the bilayer; detergent needed to remove","Channels, carriers, pumps, receptors","Targets of most transport defects, such as CFTR (C07)"],["Peripheral proteins","Bound to the surface by ionic and hydrogen bonds","Cytoskeletal links, enzymes, signaling","Spectrin and ankyrin defects in hereditary spherocytosis"],["GPI-anchored proteins","Outer proteins tethered by a glycolipid anchor","Enzymes, receptors, complement regulators","Lost CD55 and CD59 in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria"],["Glycocalyx","Sugar chains on outer glycoproteins, glycolipids, proteoglycans","Negative surface charge, recognition, adhesion","ABO blood group antigens"]],"caption":"Each component, its structure, and the clinical example most often used to test it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C02","obj":"IN-3.2","title":"Mechanisms of membrane transport","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Solutes cross the membrane through the lipid itself, through a channel, on a carrier, or inside a vesicle. Two questions classify every mechanism. Does the solute move down its electrochemical gradient (passive) or against it (active)? If active, is the energy taken directly from ATP or borrowed from another ion's gradient?"},{"type":"p","text":"Simple diffusion is net movement down a gradient without binding to a carrier. Its rate follows Fick's law: flux equals permeability times membrane area times the concentration difference, and permeability rises with lipid solubility and falls with molecular size and membrane thickness. Because no binding sites fill up, the rate rises in a straight line with the gradient and never saturates. O2, CO2, and lipid-soluble drugs cross this way. Diffusion through open ion channels, which are selective and gated by voltage, ligand, or stretch, is also unsaturated over the physiological range."},{"type":"p","text":"Facilitated diffusion uses a carrier that binds the solute on one side, changes shape, and releases it on the other. It moves solute only down its gradient and needs no metabolic energy, but there are a limited number of carriers, each with a finite cycling rate, so the rate approaches a maximum (Vmax, or Tmax in the kidney) as concentration rises. Carriers are also specific (D-glucose but not L-glucose) and open to competition by similar molecules. The GLUT family shows how carrier properties fit tissue roles: GLUT1 gives steady uptake into red cells and across the blood-brain barrier, GLUT2 has a high Km so that liver and pancreatic beta cell uptake tracks blood glucose, and insulin moves GLUT4 into the membrane of skeletal muscle and adipocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary active transport hydrolyzes ATP at the transporter itself to move a solute uphill: the Na+/K+-ATPase, the plasma membrane and sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPases, and the gastric H+/K+-ATPase. Secondary active transport couples the downhill entry of Na+ to the uphill movement of another solute. In cotransport (symport) both move the same way, as when SGLT1 carries 2 Na+ with 1 glucose or galactose into the enterocyte. In countertransport (antiport) they move in opposite directions, as with the Na+/H+ exchanger and the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (3 Na+ in for 1 Ca2+ out). The carrier hydrolyzes no ATP, but it fails whenever the Na+/K+-ATPase fails. Both active forms are saturable: glucose appears in the urine when plasma glucose exceeds about 180 to 200 mg/dL (10 to 11 mmol/L) because proximal tubule SGLT2 and SGLT1 are then working at capacity."},{"type":"p","text":"Vesicular transport moves fluid, macromolecules, and particles and needs ATP. Pinocytosis takes in extracellular fluid, phagocytosis engulfs particles such as bacteria, and receptor-mediated endocytosis concentrates ligands in clathrin-coated pits, as when LDL receptors take up LDL. Exocytosis releases vesicle contents, usually triggered by a rise in cytosolic Ca2+."},{"type":"table","title":"Membrane transport compared","cols":["Mechanism","Energy source","Direction","Saturable","Example"],"rows":[["Simple diffusion","None (the gradient itself)","Down gradient","No","O2 and CO2 across the lipid"],["Facilitated diffusion","None","Down gradient","Yes","GLUT1 in red cells; GLUT4 in muscle and fat"],["Primary active","ATP hydrolyzed by the transporter","Against gradient","Yes","Na+/K+-ATPase (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in)"],["Secondary active","Na+ gradient built by the Na+/K+-ATPase","Against gradient for the coupled solute","Yes","SGLT1 (2 Na+ with 1 glucose); Na+/Ca2+ exchanger"],["Endocytosis","ATP","Into the cell in vesicles","Yes when receptor-mediated","LDL receptor in clathrin-coated pits"]],"caption":"Carrier-mediated and receptor-mediated processes saturate because the number of binding sites is limited; simple diffusion does not."},{"type":"flow","title":"Glucose absorption across the enterocyte","steps":["Basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps Na+ out, keeping cell Na+ low","Apical SGLT1 carries 2 Na+ and 1 glucose into the cell","Glucose rises inside the cell above its plasma level","Basolateral GLUT2 releases glucose by facilitated diffusion","Glucose enters portal blood"],"caption":"Primary active, secondary active, and facilitated transport working in series across one epithelial cell."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C03","obj":"IN-3.3","title":"The Na+/K+-ATPase, cell volume, and hypoxic cell swelling","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Na+/K+-ATPase is a P-type ATPase. Its alpha subunit carries the ion binding sites, the ATP site, and an extracellular site where cardiac glycosides bind; its beta subunit is needed for assembly and delivery to the membrane. Each cycle hydrolyzes one ATP and moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in, both against their gradients, so it exports one net positive charge and is electrogenic. It keeps intracellular Na+ near 10 to 15 mmol/L and K+ near 140 mmol/L, and it consumes a large share of a cell's resting ATP, especially in neurons."},{"type":"p","text":"The pump is also the cell's volume regulator. Cells contain impermeant anionic proteins and organic phosphates that draw in cations and water (the Gibbs-Donnan effect, C05). The membrane leaks Na+ slowly, and by expelling Na+ as fast as it enters, the pump makes Na+ behave as if it were an impermeant extracellular solute that balances the pull of the trapped anions. This is the pump-leak model of steady cell volume."},{"type":"p","text":"When the pump stops, the consequences unfold at different speeds. Its small electrogenic contribution disappears at once, so the membrane depolarizes (becomes less negative) by a few millivolts. Over minutes, Na+ leaks in and K+ leaks out down gradients that are no longer restored, Cl- enters as the interior becomes less negative, and water follows the net gain of solute, so the cell swells. As the K+ gradient runs down, E(K) becomes less negative and the membrane depolarizes further, first raising and then lowering excitability (C06). Partial inhibition by a cardiac glycoside such as digoxin shows a milder form in cardiac myocytes: higher intracellular Na+ weakens the gradient that drives Ca2+ out through the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, more Ca2+ is stored and released by the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and contractility rises. Digoxin competes with K+ at its binding site, which is why hypokalemia increases its binding."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicted effects of stopping the Na+/K+-ATPase","cols":["Variable","Change","Reason"],"rows":[["Intracellular Na+","Rises","Na+ leak continues without removal"],["Intracellular K+","Falls","K+ leaks out and is not pumped back"],["Cell volume","Increases","Na+ and Cl- gain draws water in"],["Membrane potential","Depolarizes","Loss of electrogenic current, then a smaller K+ gradient"],["Intracellular Ca2+","Rises","Weaker Na+ gradient slows Ca2+ extrusion by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger"]],"caption":"The same pattern follows a pump inhibitor, cold, or a lack of ATP."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxic injury reaches the same chain by removing ATP instead of blocking the pump. Without oxygen, oxidative phosphorylation stops, ATP falls, the pump slows, and Na+ and water enter. Anaerobic glycolysis briefly supplies ATP but depletes glycogen and produces lactic acid, lowering intracellular pH and clumping nuclear chromatin, and ribosomes detach from the rough endoplasmic reticulum, so protein synthesis falls. Under the microscope this is cellular swelling, or hydropic change: enlarged, pale cells with small clear vacuoles of distended endoplasmic reticulum. It is the earliest change of reversible injury and resolves if oxygen returns. Sustained Ca2+ influx, severe mitochondrial damage, and loss of membrane integrity mark the move toward irreversible injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxic cell swelling","steps":["Hypoxia halts oxidative phosphorylation; ATP falls","Na+/K+-ATPase slows","Na+ accumulates inside; K+ leaks out","Water follows the gained solute into the cell","Hydropic swelling, membrane blebs, swollen mitochondria, dilated ER","Reversible if oxygen and ATP are restored"],"caption":"The causal chain from ATP depletion to the first visible sign of reversible cell injury."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Red cells stored for transfusion at 4 °C (39 °F) have almost no Na+/K+-ATPase activity. Predict their intracellular K+, the K+ concentration of the surrounding fluid, and their volume.","explain":"With the pump stopped by cold, K+ leaks out and Na+ leaks in, so cell K+ falls, the K+ of the storage fluid rises, and Na+, Cl-, and water entry swell the cells.","check":"Compare your answer with the table of predicted effects of stopping the pump."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C04","obj":"IN-3.4","title":"Osmosis, osmolarity, and tonicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osmosis is net diffusion of water across a membrane that is more permeable to water than to solute, from the side with fewer solute particles toward the side with more. Water crosses the lipid bilayer slowly and crosses much faster through aquaporin channels. The pressure needed to stop osmosis is the osmotic pressure, which by the van't Hoff relation is proportional to the concentration of osmotically active particles. At body temperature each 1 mOsm/L difference corresponds to about 19.3 mmHg, so even small solute differences move large amounts of water."},{"type":"p","text":"Osmolarity is osmoles per liter of solution, and osmolality is osmoles per kilogram of water; in body fluids they are nearly equal, and laboratories measure osmolality by freezing point depression. Both count every dissolved particle, so 1 mmol of NaCl gives close to 2 mOsm because it dissociates. Normal plasma osmolality is about 285 to 295 mOsm/kg and can be estimated as 2 x Na+ (mmol/L) + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8, with glucose and BUN in mg/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Tonicity is a different question: what will this solution do to cell volume? Only effective osmoles count, meaning solutes that cannot cross the cell membrane (reflection coefficient near 1). Water moves until the effective osmolality inside and outside is equal, and cells hold about 290 mOsm/kg of effectively impermeant solute, mostly K+ with its anions. A solution with the same effective osmolality is isotonic, one with less is hypotonic and swells cells, and one with more is hypertonic and shrinks them. Because cells behave approximately as osmometers, their water volume changes in inverse proportion to the effective osmolality around them."},{"type":"p","text":"A permeant solute adds to osmolarity but not to tonicity. Urea crosses most cell membranes quickly, through the lipid and through urea transporters, so its reflection coefficient is near 0: it reaches the same concentration inside and outside and exerts no lasting pull. Na+ with its accompanying anions sits at the other extreme, with a coefficient near 1. An isosmotic urea solution therefore acts like pure water for a red cell: urea diffuses in, water follows, and the cell swells and lyses. In the body, a high BUN in renal failure raises measured osmolality without shrinking cells, and ethanol behaves the same way. By contrast, a rise in plasma Na+, or in glucose when insulin action is lacking, draws water out of cells, which is why hyperglycemia lowers plasma Na+ by dilution."},{"type":"table","title":"Cell volume in different solutions","cols":["Solution","Osmolarity","Effective osmolality vs cell","Water movement","Cell volume"],"rows":[["0.9% NaCl","About 308 mOsm/L calculated (behaves as about 286)","Equal","None","No change"],["0.45% NaCl","About 154 mOsm/L","Lower","Into cell","Swells"],["3% NaCl","About 1,026 mOsm/L","Higher","Out of cell","Shrinks"],["300 mOsm/L urea","Isosmotic","Lower (urea enters the cell)","Into cell","Swells and lyses"],["150 mOsm/L NaCl plus 150 mOsm/L urea","Isosmotic","Lower (only the NaCl counts)","Into cell","Swells, as in 0.45% NaCl"]],"caption":"Osmolarity counts all particles; tonicity, and therefore cell volume, depends only on the solutes that stay outside."},{"type":"example","title":"Isosmotic is not the same as isotonic","text":"A red cell with an effective osmolality of 290 mOsm/kg is placed in a solution of 150 mOsm/L NaCl plus 150 mOsm/L urea. Step 1: total osmolarity is 300 mOsm/L, so the solution is isosmotic. Step 2: urea crosses the membrane and equalizes, so it contributes nothing to tonicity; the effective osmolality outside is 150. Step 3: water enters until the inside also reaches about 150, which requires the cell water to nearly double (290/150 is about 1.9). Step 4: a red cell has a fixed surface area and can swell only until it becomes a sphere, so it lyses before reaching that volume. The solution is isosmotic but hypotonic."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C05","obj":"IN-3.5","title":"Equilibrium potentials and the resting membrane potential","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"If a membrane is permeable to one ion only, that ion diffuses down its concentration gradient and carries charge with it, building a voltage that opposes further movement. The equilibrium (Nernst) potential is the voltage at which the electrical force exactly balances the chemical force, so net flux stops. Only a tiny number of ions must cross to create this voltage, so bulk concentrations are essentially unchanged."},{"type":"p","text":"At 37 °C (98.6 °F) the Nernst equation simplifies to E = (61/z) x log10([ion]out/[ion]in) in millivolts, where z is the valence and E is the inside relative to the outside. A cation concentrated outside has a positive E, one concentrated inside a negative E, and for an anion the sign flips because z is negative. A tenfold gradient gives about 61 mV for a monovalent ion and about 30 mV for a divalent ion."},{"type":"table","title":"Typical concentrations and equilibrium potentials","cols":["Ion","Outside (mmol/L)","Inside (mmol/L)","Equilibrium potential"],"rows":[["K+","4","140","About -94 mV"],["Na+","142","14","About +61 mV"],["Cl-","About 105","About 5 to 10 (varies by cell)","About -60 to -80 mV"],["Ca2+ (ionized)","About 1.2","About 0.0001","About +120 to +130 mV"]],"caption":"Values follow Guyton for a large nerve fiber; intracellular Cl- and therefore E(Cl) vary between cell types."},{"type":"p","text":"Real membranes pass several ions, so the potential is a weighted compromise given by the Goldman equation: Vm = 61 x log10[(PK[K]out + PNa[Na]out + PCl[Cl]in) / (PK[K]in + PNa[Na]in + PCl[Cl]out)], where P is permeability. The Cl- terms are inverted because Cl- is negative. The potential lies closest to the equilibrium potential of the most permeable ion and changes whenever permeability changes, not only when concentrations change. That is the basis of the action potential, in which a surge of Na+ permeability pulls Vm toward E(Na)."},{"type":"p","text":"At rest, K+ leak channels (two-pore domain and inward rectifier) are open, and K+ permeability is about 100 times Na+ permeability in Guyton's large nerve fiber. The potential therefore sits near E(K), pulled slightly positive by the Na+ leak. This is a steady state, not an equilibrium: the leaks continue, and only the pump keeps the gradients from running down. Guyton builds the value in steps: K+ diffusion alone gives about -94 mV, the Na+ leak brings it to about -86 mV, and the electrogenic pump adds about -4 mV, totaling about -90 mV. Typical resting values are about -70 mV in many neurons and about -90 mV in skeletal and ventricular muscle. In many cells Cl- distributes passively, so E(Cl) lies near Vm and Cl- follows the potential rather than setting it."},{"type":"p","text":"The Gibbs-Donnan effect explains a further constraint. Proteins and organic phosphates are trapped inside and mostly negatively charged at body pH. To keep electroneutrality they hold extra diffusible cations inside and push diffusible anions out, until the products of the diffusible ion concentrations are equal on both sides ([K+]in x [Cl-]in = [K+]out x [Cl-]out). The result is a small inside-negative Donnan potential and more osmotically active particles inside than outside, so water would keep entering until the cell burst. The Na+/K+-ATPase prevents this by keeping Na+ effectively outside (C03)."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating E(K) and seeing why extracellular K+ matters","text":"With K+ of 4 mmol/L outside and 140 inside, the ratio is 4/140 = 0.029, whose log10 is about -1.54; multiplied by 61 this gives E(K) of about -94 mV. Now raise extracellular K+ to 7 mmol/L. The ratio becomes 7/140 = 0.05, log10 about -1.30, and E(K) about -79 mV. A rise of only 3 mmol/L outside moves E(K) about 15 mV toward zero, and because the resting potential tracks E(K), the cell depolarizes by a similar amount. A 3 mmol/L fall inside, from 140 to 137, would change E(K) by less than 1 mV."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C06","obj":"IN-3.6","title":"Potassium, resting potential, and excitability","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because resting K+ permeability is high, the resting potential tracks E(K), and E(K) is far more sensitive to extracellular than intracellular K+. Intracellular K+ is about 140 mmol/L, while plasma K+ is normally only about 3.5 to 5.0 mmol/L, so a change of a few mmol/L outside alters the ratio greatly (C05 example). Only about 2% of total body K+ is extracellular, so shifts between cells and plasma can change plasma K+ quickly."},{"type":"p","text":"In hyperkalemia the K+ gradient shrinks, E(K) becomes less negative, and the resting membrane depolarizes. A small depolarization brings the membrane closer to threshold, which is the voltage at which inward Na+ current first exceeds outward K+ current and depolarization becomes self-sustaining, so excitability may rise briefly. Sustained depolarization does the opposite because of how voltage-gated Na+ channels behave. Each has an activation gate that opens quickly on depolarization and an inactivation gate that closes more slowly and stays closed while the membrane remains depolarized; it reopens only after the membrane repolarizes to a sufficiently negative voltage. In a chronically depolarized cell a growing fraction of Na+ channels is therefore inactivated. Fewer available channels give a slower, smaller upstroke, slower conduction, and finally failure to fire, so skeletal muscle weakens and can become paralyzed even though the voltage sits closer to threshold."},{"type":"p","text":"In the heart, the slower phase 0 upstroke widens the QRS, and slowed atrial conduction flattens the P wave. High extracellular K+ also increases the conductance of several K+ channels, so repolarization speeds up and T waves become tall and peaked. At severe levels the QRS merges into the T wave in a sine wave pattern before ventricular fibrillation or asystole."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypokalemia works the other way: the gradient is larger, E(K) is more negative, and the membrane hyperpolarizes, so a larger stimulus is needed to reach threshold. Skeletal muscle weakness and cramps and smooth muscle hypomotility with ileus follow. In the heart, low extracellular K+ reduces K+ channel conductance, which delays repolarization: T waves flatten, U waves become prominent, and early and delayed afterdepolarizations make ectopic beats more likely. When K+ conductance falls far enough, cardiac cells can paradoxically depolarize, an exception to the simple Goldman prediction. Extracellular Ca2+ acts by a separate mechanism: it binds negative charges near Na+ channels and shifts their opening to more depolarized voltages, so hypocalcemia lowers the threshold and causes tetany without changing the resting potential much."},{"type":"table","title":"Hyperkalemia and hypokalemia compared","cols":["Feature","Hyperkalemia (above about 5.0 mmol/L)","Hypokalemia (below about 3.5 mmol/L)"],"rows":[["E(K)","Less negative","More negative"],["Resting potential","Depolarized","Hyperpolarized (paradoxical depolarization if severe in heart)"],["Na+ channel availability","Reduced by inactivation","Normal or increased"],["Nerve and skeletal muscle","Brief hyperexcitability, then weakness and paralysis","Weakness, cramps; ileus in smooth muscle"],["Cardiac repolarization","Faster (higher K+ conductance)","Slower (lower K+ conductance)"],["ECG","Peaked T, flat P, wide QRS, sine wave","Flat T, prominent U waves, ectopic beats"]],"caption":"Both extremes reduce excitability of nerve and skeletal muscle, by different mechanisms."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sustained hyperkalemia to weakness","steps":["Extracellular K+ rises","E(K) becomes less negative","Resting membrane depolarizes","Na+ channel inactivation gates close and stay closed","Fewer Na+ channels available to open","Slower upstroke, slowed conduction, failure to fire","Muscle weakness, wide QRS, arrhythmia"],"caption":"Why a membrane closer to threshold becomes less excitable, not more."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Plasma K+ rises from 4 to 8 mmol/L over a day. Predict the resting potential of a skeletal muscle fiber, the number of Na+ channels available to open, and muscle strength.","explain":"E(K) and the resting potential become less negative; sustained depolarization keeps inactivation gates closed, so fewer Na+ channels are available, action potentials fail, and strength falls.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on inactivation gates and the hyperkalemia flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-03-01-C07","obj":"IN-3.7","title":"CFTR in the sweat duct and the airway","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) is encoded on chromosome 7 and belongs to the ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter family, but it works as an anion channel. It opens when cAMP-dependent protein kinase A phosphorylates its regulatory domain and ATP binds its nucleotide-binding domains. It conducts Cl- and HCO3- and also regulates other transporters, notably the epithelial Na+ channel (ENaC). As a channel it does not choose a direction: Cl- moves down its electrochemical gradient, which points inward in an absorbing epithelium and outward in a secreting one."},{"type":"p","text":"The eccrine sweat gland has two parts. The secretory coil makes a primary fluid nearly isotonic with plasma; on cholinergic stimulation its Cl- secretion relies largely on Ca2+-activated Cl- channels, so people with cystic fibrosis (CF) produce a fairly normal volume of sweat. In the duct, cells reabsorb Cl- through apical CFTR and Na+ through ENaC, and because the duct is relatively impermeable to water, salt is removed without water and the sweat reaching the skin is hypotonic. In CF, Cl- cannot be reabsorbed. Na+ reabsorption also falls, because the retained Cl- leaves the lumen more negative and because ENaC activity in the duct depends on working CFTR, so the sweat stays salty. A sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher supports CF, 30 to 59 mmol/L is intermediate, and below 30 mmol/L makes CF unlikely. Heavy sweating can deplete salt, producing hyponatremic, hypochloremic dehydration with metabolic alkalosis."},{"type":"p","text":"In the airway the same channel secretes. Basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporters, powered by the Na+ gradient, load the cell with Cl-, and apical CFTR lets it flow into the lumen; Na+ follows between the cells and water follows the salt. This keeps the periciliary liquid deep enough for cilia to beat. In CF, Cl- and HCO3- secretion fail and ENaC-mediated Na+ absorption increases, so salt and water are drawn out of the airway surface liquid. Mucus becomes dehydrated, acidic, and poorly expanded, cilia are flattened, and clearance fails. Retained mucus is colonized, commonly first by Staphylococcus aureus and Haemophilus influenzae and later by mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and neutrophil-driven inflammation leads to bronchiectasis. The abnormally negative nasal potential difference in CF reflects this excess Na+ absorption."},{"type":"table","title":"One channel, opposite jobs","cols":["Feature","Sweat duct","Airway epithelium"],"rows":[["Normal CFTR role","Cl- reabsorption from lumen","Cl- and HCO3- secretion into lumen"],["ENaC in CF","Decreased","Increased"],["Water movement","Little; duct is water-tight","Follows salt across the epithelium"],["Result in CF","Salty sweat; salt depletion with heavy sweating","Dehydrated mucus, infection, bronchiectasis"],["Measured finding","Sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or higher","More negative nasal potential difference"]],"caption":"Loss of CFTR raises salt in sweat but removes water from airway mucus."},{"type":"p","text":"The same failure of secretion plugs other ducts: the pancreas (exocrine insufficiency with fat malabsorption and deficient vitamins A, D, E, and K), the newborn ileum (meconium ileus), bile ductules (focal biliary cirrhosis), and the vas deferens, which is absent on both sides in nearly all affected males. CF is autosomal recessive. The most common mutation, F508del, misfolds the protein, which is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum and degraded, so little CFTR reaches the membrane."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cystic fibrosis airway disease","steps":["CFTR mutation such as F508del; protein degraded","Airway cells cannot secrete Cl- and HCO3-","ENaC Na+ absorption increases","Airway surface liquid depleted; mucus dehydrates","Mucociliary clearance fails","Chronic infection and neutrophil inflammation cause bronchiectasis"],"caption":"From a misfolded channel to structural airway damage."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with CF receives a cholinergic stimulus to the skin. Predict the volume of sweat and its chloride concentration compared with a healthy child.","explain":"Volume is near normal because the coil secretes through Ca2+-activated Cl- channels, but chloride is high because the duct cannot reabsorb Cl- through CFTR.","check":"Reread the sweat gland paragraph and the sweat duct column of the table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Membrane transport links every Part I discipline through one principle: the ion gradients that the Na+/K+-ATPase builds are the energy supply for volume control, nutrient uptake, and electrical signaling. Biochemistry explains why fatty acid saturation and cholesterol change fluidity and why hypoxia removes the ATP that powers the pump. Physiology turns gradients into numbers with the Nernst and Goldman equations and predicts cell volume from effective osmoles. Pathology reads the same chain backward, from hydropic swelling to pump failure, and from a wide QRS to K+-driven Na+ channel inactivation. Genetics and microbiology meet in cystic fibrosis, where a misfolded channel changes salt movement in opposite directions in two epithelia and leaves the airway open to chronic infection."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits matter. The Goldman equation assumes steady permeabilities, so it cannot predict paradoxical depolarization when K+ conductance itself falls in severe hypokalemia. Cells are not perfect osmometers, because part of their volume is solid and many regulate volume actively over minutes to hours. Reference values for intracellular ions, resting potentials, and sweat chloride vary between sources and cell types, so learn the direction of each change and the reasoning rather than a single number."}],"summary":["Cis double bonds and short fatty acid tails increase membrane fluidity; cholesterol buffers fluidity, lowering it when warm and preventing gel formation when cold.","Simple diffusion is not saturable; facilitated diffusion and primary and secondary active transport use a limited number of carriers and saturate at a maximum rate.","SGLT1 and SGLT2 use no ATP themselves but depend on the Na+ gradient built by the Na+/K+-ATPase; GLUT carriers move glucose only down its gradient.","The Na+/K+-ATPase moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP; stopping it raises cell Na+, swells the cell, and depolarizes the membrane.","ATP loss in hypoxia stops the pump, and Na+ and water entry produces hydropic swelling, the earliest sign of reversible injury.","Osmolarity counts all particles; tonicity counts only effective osmoles, so urea raises osmolarity but not tonicity, and isosmotic urea lyses red cells.","E = (61/z) x log(out/in); E(K) is about -94 mV and E(Na) about +61 mV, and the resting potential lies near E(K) because resting K+ permeability is highest.","Trapped intracellular anions (Gibbs-Donnan effect) would swell the cell; the pump prevents this by keeping Na+ effectively outside.","Hyperkalemia depolarizes and, if sustained, inactivates Na+ channels, lowering excitability; hypokalemia hyperpolarizes and slows cardiac repolarization.","CFTR reabsorbs Cl- in the sweat duct and secretes Cl- in the airway, so CF gives sweat chloride of 60 mmol/L or higher and dehydrated airway mucus."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of Substances Through Cell Membranes"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Transport of solutes and water; electrophysiology of the cell membrane"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Phospholipid and cholesterol structure and membrane lipids"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell injury and cell death; cystic fibrosis in genetic and pediatric diseases"}]},"IN-4":{"unit":"IN-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01","title":"Thermoregulation","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the hypothalamus holds core temperature near its set point by balancing heat loss through the skin against heat conservation and production, and what happens when that balance fails. It covers the hypothalamic controller, the physics of heat loss, cold defenses including brown fat, fever, heat illness, malignant hyperthermia, hypothermia, and cold injury. Board questions turn on a few distinctions: whether the set point has moved (fever) or the controller is overwhelmed (hyperthermia), whether thermoregulation still works (heat exhaustion) or has failed (heat stroke), and which route of heat loss remains when the environment is hotter than the skin.","prerequisites":["Sympathetic adrenergic and cholinergic receptors and their second messengers (NE-9)","Chemiosmotic coupling of electron transport to ATP synthase","Skeletal muscle excitation-contraction coupling, DHP receptor, RYR1, and SERCA (MS-7)","Innate immune recognition, cytokines, and the cyclooxygenase pathway (IM-6, IM-13)","Cardiac action potential and the normal ECG (CV-7)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hypothalamic and limbic function (NE-12)","how":"The preoptic and posterior hypothalamic nuclei that set and defend core temperature sit beside the centers for thirst, feeding, and pituitary control, so one lesion often disturbs several of these."},{"to":"Skin layers and adnexa (IN-1)","how":"Eccrine sweat glands, arrector pili muscles, dermal arteriovenous anastomoses, and subcutaneous fat are the effectors and insulation this chapter puts to work."},{"to":"Muscle contraction (MS-7)","how":"Shivering, malignant hyperthermia, and exertional rhabdomyolysis are all explained by how skeletal muscle handles Ca2+ and ATP."},{"to":"Cell and cytokine signaling (IM-6)","how":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha released in infection and injury reset the hypothalamic set point through PGE2."},{"to":"Conduction system and ECG (CV-7)","how":"Cooling slows pacemaker and conduction currents, producing bradycardia, interval prolongation, Osborn waves, and fibrillation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Core temperature","def":"Temperature of the deep tissues (brain, thoracic and abdominal organs), held near about 37 °C (98.6 °F) while skin temperature varies."},{"term":"Set point","def":"The core temperature the hypothalamus defends; signals above it trigger heat loss and signals below it trigger heat conservation and production."},{"term":"Preoptic area","def":"Anterior hypothalamic region containing warm-sensitive neurons; it acts as the heat loss center."},{"term":"Evaporative heat loss","def":"Heat removed as water changes to vapor on skin and airways, about 0.58 kcal per gram, requiring a water vapor pressure gradient rather than a temperature gradient."},{"term":"Nonshivering thermogenesis","def":"Heat production without muscle contraction, mainly by sympathetic activation of UCP1 in brown fat."},{"term":"UCP1 (thermogenin)","def":"Inner mitochondrial membrane protein of brown adipocytes that lets protons re-enter the matrix without passing through ATP synthase, releasing the gradient as heat."},{"term":"Endogenous pyrogen","def":"A host cytokine, chiefly IL-1, IL-6, or TNF-alpha, that raises the hypothalamic set point."},{"term":"Fever","def":"A regulated rise in core temperature caused by an increase in the set point."},{"term":"Hyperthermia","def":"A rise in core temperature with an unchanged set point, because heat gain or production exceeds heat loss."},{"term":"Heat stroke","def":"Core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with central nervous system dysfunction."},{"term":"Malignant hyperthermia","def":"Inherited skeletal muscle hypermetabolic crisis, usually from an RYR1 mutation, triggered by volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine."},{"term":"Osborn (J) wave","def":"A positive deflection at the junction of the QRS complex and ST segment that appears and grows as core temperature falls."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C01","obj":"IN-4.1","title":"The hypothalamic thermostat","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Core temperature stays within about 0.6 °C (1 °F) of its set point across a wide range of environments, while skin temperature rises and falls with the surroundings. Oral core temperature averages about 37 °C (98.6 °F), with a daily rhythm that is lowest in the early morning and highest in the late afternoon; rectal readings run a few tenths of a degree higher. The controller is the hypothalamus, which works as a negative feedback thermostat: it compares sensed temperature with the set point and drives heat loss or heat gain to cancel the difference."},{"type":"p","text":"Two sets of sensors feed it. Core sensing occurs mainly in the preoptic and anterior hypothalamus, where warm-sensitive neurons increase their firing as the temperature of the blood perfusing them rises; they outnumber cold-sensitive neurons there by about three to one. Deep receptors in the spinal cord, abdominal viscera, and great veins add core information. Skin thermoreceptors, mostly cold receptors (several times more numerous than warm receptors), are free nerve endings with temperature-gated TRP channels such as TRPM8 for cooling. Their signals reach the posterior hypothalamus, which integrates them with the preoptic signals. Skin input lets the body respond before the core changes, so stepping into cold air triggers vasoconstriction and shivering while core temperature is still normal."},{"type":"p","text":"The effector side is divided. The preoptic and anterior hypothalamus is the heat loss center: when sensed temperature exceeds the set point, it withdraws sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone to the skin, activates active cutaneous vasodilation, and drives sweating through sympathetic cholinergic fibers acting on M3 receptors of eccrine glands. It also inhibits the heat production centers. The posterior hypothalamus is the heat conservation and production center: when temperature falls below the set point, it drives sympathetic cutaneous vasoconstriction, piloerection, shivering from the primary motor center for shivering in its dorsomedial portion, and sympathetic and thyroid driven thermogenesis. Behavioral responses, such as seeking shelter or adding clothing, are the most powerful of all and depend on conscious perception of comfort."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Negative feedback against a rise in core temperature","steps":["Core temperature rises above the set point","Preoptic warm-sensitive neurons fire faster","Skin vasodilation and sweating increase","Heat loss exceeds heat production","Core temperature falls toward the set point","Warm-sensitive firing returns to baseline"],"caption":"The same loop runs in reverse in the cold, with reduced warm-sensitive firing releasing posterior hypothalamic vasoconstriction and shivering."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypothalamic regions in temperature control","cols":["Region","Main role","Responses driven","Effect of a lesion"],"rows":[["Preoptic and anterior hypothalamus","Core sensing; heat loss center","Cutaneous vasodilation, sweating, inhibition of heat production","Hyperthermia in a warm environment, because heat cannot be shed"],["Posterior hypothalamus","Integrates skin and core input; heat conservation and production center","Cutaneous vasoconstriction, piloerection, shivering, thermogenesis","Poikilothermia: core temperature drifts toward ambient, most dangerously in the cold"]],"caption":"Each region controls one half of the response, so the lesion site predicts which extreme the patient cannot tolerate."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the halves are anatomically separate, lesions are predictable. Anterior damage from stroke, trauma, tumor, or surgery removes heat loss and produces hyperthermia. A posterior lesion destroys the heat conservation centers and also interrupts descending pathways from the anterior region, so the patient becomes poikilothermic. A high cervical cord transection cuts hypothalamic control of sympathetic vasomotor tone and sweating below the lesion, impairing defense in both heat and cold."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient recovering from surgery for a craniopharyngioma that damaged the posterior hypothalamus is placed in a cool room. What happens to core temperature, and why?","explain":"Core temperature falls toward room temperature, because the posterior centers that trigger vasoconstriction and shivering are gone and descending heat loss pathways are also interrupted, leaving poikilothermia.","check":"Compare with the table of hypothalamic regions and the paragraph on lesions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C02","obj":"IN-4.2","title":"Routes of heat loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood carries core heat to the skin, so skin blood flow sets how fast heat reaches the surface. Skin flow ranges from nearly zero to about 30% of cardiac output, much of it through arteriovenous anastomoses (direct shunts from arterioles to venules) in the hands, feet, ears, and nose. Once heat reaches the skin, four physical routes remove it. At rest in a comfortable room, about 60% of heat loss is by radiation, 15% by conduction to air with convection, 3% by conduction to objects, and 22% by evaporation (Guyton and Hall)."},{"type":"p","text":"Radiation is the emission of infrared energy from the skin to cooler surfaces that are not in contact. It works in both directions, so the body also absorbs radiant heat from the sun and hot surfaces. Conduction is direct transfer of heat between surfaces in contact, such as skin and a cold bench or the thin layer of air touching the skin; once that air layer warms, conduction stops unless the layer is replaced. Convection is that replacement: moving air or water carries the warmed layer away, so wind and immersion multiply heat loss. Water conducts heat far better than air, so immersion cools much faster than air at the same temperature."},{"type":"p","text":"Evaporation works differently. Converting water to vapor absorbs its latent heat, about 0.58 kcal per gram, so it cools the skin even when the surroundings are warmer than the body. Insensible loss through skin and airways (about 600 to 700 mL per day) runs continuously, and sweating adds a regulated route."},{"type":"table","title":"The four routes of heat loss","cols":["Route","Physical basis","Driving gradient","When it fails or reverses"],"rows":[["Radiation","Infrared emission to surfaces not in contact","Skin warmer than surrounding surfaces","Reverses in sun or near hot surfaces"],["Conduction","Direct transfer between touching surfaces","Skin warmer than the object or air in contact","Reverses in hot air or water"],["Convection","Moving air or water replaces the warmed layer","Skin warmer than the moving fluid","Adds heat when air or water is hotter than skin"],["Evaporation","Latent heat of vaporization, about 0.58 kcal per gram","Water vapor pressure at wet skin higher than in air","Fails in high humidity or when sweating is impaired"]],"caption":"Only evaporation is independent of the temperature gradient, so it is the only route left in a hot environment."},{"type":"p","text":"This leads to the key prediction. Radiation, conduction, and convection move heat from warmer to cooler. When ambient temperature, or the temperature of radiating surroundings, exceeds skin temperature (about 33 °C (91.4 °F) in comfortable conditions and up to about 35 °C (95 °F) in the heat), those three routes add heat to the body, and evaporation of sweat becomes the only way to lose heat. High humidity shrinks the vapor pressure gradient, so sweat accumulates and drips off without evaporating; it removes no heat, yet the water and salt are still lost from plasma. Anything that impairs sweating, such as anticholinergic drugs, extensive scarring or burns, ectodermal dysplasia, or dehydration, removes the last route. Acclimatization over about one to six weeks roughly doubles maximal sweat rate, and aldosterone lowers sweat NaCl so less salt is lost per liter."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a humid day overheats a runner","text":"A 70 kg runner produces about 800 kcal per hour in air hotter than the skin, so evaporation must remove all of it. Evaporating 1.4 L of sweat removes about 800 kcal (1,400 g times 0.58 kcal), so in dry air 1.4 L per hour keeps balance. In very humid air, suppose only half evaporates: about 400 kcal per hour is stored. Tissue needs about 0.83 kcal per kg per °C, or about 58 kcal per °C for this runner, so core temperature climbs about 7 °C per hour and reaches heat stroke range within about 30 minutes, while the full 1.4 L is still lost from plasma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C03","obj":"IN-4.3","title":"Heat conservation and heat production","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In the cold, the posterior hypothalamus first reduces heat loss and then increases heat production. Sympathetic norepinephrine acting on alpha-adrenergic receptors constricts cutaneous arterioles and closes arteriovenous anastomoses. Warm blood stays in the core, and skin and subcutaneous fat become an insulating shell. In the limbs, deep arteries run beside their accompanying veins, so cooled venous blood is rewarmed by arterial blood (countercurrent exchange) and heat returns to the core. Piloerection (arrector pili contraction through alpha-1 receptors) traps air in furred mammals but adds little in humans."},{"type":"p","text":"When conservation is not enough, the primary motor center for shivering in the dorsomedial posterior hypothalamus, normally inhibited by the anterior heat center, sends descending signals to spinal motor neurons. Tone rises, then agonist and antagonist muscles contract rhythmically. Because almost no external work is done, nearly all the ATP energy used appears as heat, and maximal shivering can raise heat production about four to five fold. Shivering depends on glycogen, glucose, and oxygen, and it fails when fuel runs out or when the core cools enough to depress the controller (C07)."},{"type":"p","text":"Nonshivering (chemical) thermogenesis takes place mainly in brown adipose tissue. Sympathetic norepinephrine acts on beta-3 receptors of brown adipocytes, and Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A activate hormone-sensitive lipase. The released fatty acids serve both as fuel for beta oxidation and as activators of UCP1 (thermogenin) in the inner mitochondrial membrane. UCP1 gives protons a path back into the matrix that bypasses ATP synthase, so the energy of the proton gradient is released as heat. With the gradient continuously dissipated, electron transport is no longer held back by ADP availability and runs at a high rate. Brown adipocytes have many small lipid droplets, abundant cytochrome-rich mitochondria (the brown color), dense capillaries, and direct sympathetic innervation. Sympathetic stimulation also induces type 2 deiodinase in brown fat, raising local T3, which increases UCP1 expression."},{"type":"flow","title":"Nonshivering thermogenesis in brown fat","steps":["Cold sensed; sympathetic outflow increases","Norepinephrine binds beta-3 receptors on brown adipocytes","Gs, cAMP, and PKA activate hormone-sensitive lipase","Free fatty acids are released from lipid droplets","Fatty acids activate UCP1 and fuel beta oxidation","Protons re-enter the matrix, bypassing ATP synthase","Proton gradient energy is released as heat"],"caption":"Uncoupling converts oxidative energy to heat instead of ATP."},{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid hormone acts more slowly. Prolonged cold increases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) and therefore thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and thyroid hormone output, and triiodothyronine (T3) raises basal metabolic rate by increasing Na+/K+ ATPase activity, mitochondrial oxidative capacity, and catecholamine sensitivity. The effect builds over days to weeks, supporting acclimatization, and explains cold intolerance in hypothyroidism and heat intolerance in hyperthyroidism."},{"type":"p","text":"Neonates depend on brown fat. They have a high surface area to mass ratio, little subcutaneous fat for insulation, and a limited ability to shiver, so they lose heat quickly and lack the adult's main production route. Brown fat, which accumulates in late gestation between the scapulae, in the neck and axillae, and around the kidneys, adrenals, and great vessels, can roughly double an infant's heat production, whereas chemical thermogenesis raises adult heat production only about 10 to 15%. Preterm infants have less brown fat and glycogen and cool fastest, and cold stress raises oxygen and glucose use, risking hypoxemia, hypoglycemia, and metabolic acidosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Responses to cold","cols":["Response","Effector and receptor","Speed and size"],"rows":[["Cutaneous vasoconstriction","Sympathetic NE on alpha receptors of skin arterioles and AV anastomoses","Seconds; first line"],["Piloerection","Sympathetic NE on alpha-1 receptors of arrector pili","Immediate; minor in humans"],["Shivering","Posterior hypothalamic motor center to somatic motor neurons","Minutes; up to about 4 to 5 fold heat production"],["Nonshivering thermogenesis","Sympathetic NE on beta-3 receptors of brown fat; UCP1 uncoupling","Minutes; major in neonates, small in adults"],["Thyroid hormone","TRH, TSH, and T3 raising basal metabolic rate","Days to weeks; acclimatization"]],"caption":"Heat conservation acts first; heat production adds as cold persists."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C04","obj":"IN-4.4","title":"Fever and hyperthermia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fever is a regulated rise in core temperature caused by an upward shift of the hypothalamic set point. It begins with exogenous pyrogens, microbial products such as lipopolysaccharide (LPS) of gram-negative bacteria, lipoteichoic acid, and other pathogen-associated molecular patterns. These bind pattern recognition receptors, for example LPS on TLR4 with CD14, on macrophages and other innate cells, which then release the endogenous pyrogens IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha. Tissue injury, malignancy, and autoimmune disease release the same cytokines."},{"type":"p","text":"These cytokines do not freely cross the blood-brain barrier. They act on the endothelium of brain vessels and on the organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis (OVLT), a circumventricular organ beside the preoptic area whose capillaries are fenestrated. There they induce cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2), and the prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) produced acts on EP3 receptors of preoptic neurons, reducing warm-sensitive neuron firing. The controller now reads a normal blood temperature as too cold. Cytokines do not heat the body directly; they change the reference value, and the body's own heat conservation and production then raise core temperature."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of fever","steps":["Microbial products bind Toll-like receptors on macrophages","Macrophages release IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha","Cytokines act at the OVLT and brain endothelium","COX-2 induction increases PGE2 synthesis","PGE2 on preoptic EP3 receptors raises the set point","Vasoconstriction and shivering raise core temperature to the new set point"],"caption":"Fever is produced by the normal cold defenses responding to a higher target."},{"type":"p","text":"The phases follow from the moving set point. If the set point rises to 39 °C (102.2 °F), a core temperature of 37 °C (98.6 °F) is now below target, so the posterior hypothalamus drives skin vasoconstriction (pale skin), shivering (chills or rigors), and the feeling of cold, until core temperature reaches the new value. During the plateau, heat loss and production balance at the higher level. When the cytokine stimulus ends, or when cyclooxygenase inhibition by nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or acetaminophen lowers PGE2 synthesis, the set point falls. Core temperature is now above target, so the anterior hypothalamus drives sweating and cutaneous vasodilation (the flush or crisis). Fever itself is usually self-limited and rarely exceeds about 41 °C (105.8 °F), and each 1 °C of fever raises metabolic rate and oxygen consumption by roughly 10 to 13%, with matching rises in heart rate and ventilation."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperthermia is an elevated core temperature with an unchanged set point. Heat gain or production exceeds heat loss, so the controller is overwhelmed or bypassed while still trying to cool: the skin is usually flushed and sweating is maximal unless sweating itself is blocked. Causes fall into three groups: excess environmental heat (heat stroke), excess production (malignant hyperthermia, thyroid storm, sympathomimetic drugs such as cocaine and amphetamines, prolonged seizures, serotonin syndrome, neuroleptic malignant syndrome), and impaired loss (anticholinergic drugs, anterior hypothalamic lesions). Because PGE2 did not raise the set point, blocking PGE2 synthesis does not lower the temperature."},{"type":"table","title":"Fever compared with hyperthermia","cols":["Feature","Fever","Hyperthermia"],"rows":[["Set point","Raised by PGE2","Unchanged"],["Cause","Endogenous pyrogens (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha)","Heat gain or production exceeds loss, or loss is impaired"],["Hypothalamic response while temperature rises","Conserves and produces heat: vasoconstriction, chills, rigors","Tries to lose heat: vasodilation, sweating (if possible)"],["Usual ceiling","Rarely above about 41 °C (105.8 °F)","No regulated ceiling; can exceed 42 °C (107.6 °F)"],["Effect of COX inhibition","Lowers temperature","No effect"]],"caption":"The question to ask is whether the hypothalamus is trying to warm or to cool the body."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient's temperature is 39.5 °C (103.1 °F) and still rising. The skin is cold and pale, and the patient is shaking under blankets. Is this fever or hyperthermia?","explain":"Fever. The hypothalamus is actively conserving and producing heat, which means the set point is above the current core temperature. In hyperthermia the controller would be driving vasodilation and sweating.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on fever phases and the fever compared with hyperthermia table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C05","obj":"IN-4.5","title":"Heat exhaustion and heat stroke","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Heat exhaustion is mainly a circulatory failure, with thermoregulation still working. Sweating depletes water and NaCl, and cutaneous vasodilation diverts a large share of cardiac output to the skin, so venous return, stroke volume, and blood pressure fall. Core temperature is raised but usually below 40 °C (104 °F), mental status is normal, and the person sweats heavily with weakness, headache, nausea, tachycardia, and orthostatic hypotension. The controller is intact; the circulation cannot supply both skin and organs."},{"type":"p","text":"Heat stroke is a core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with central nervous system dysfunction, such as confusion, delirium, ataxia, seizures, or coma. The CNS finding defines the diagnosis because the brain is the most heat sensitive organ. Heat at these levels denatures proteins, destabilizes membranes, and uncouples oxidative phosphorylation. A systemic inflammatory response amplifies the damage: blood shunted to the skin leaves the splanchnic bed ischemic, the gut barrier leaks endotoxin, and cytokines together with heat-injured endothelium activate coagulation."},{"type":"table","title":"Heat exhaustion, classic heat stroke, and exertional heat stroke","cols":["Feature","Heat exhaustion","Classic heat stroke","Exertional heat stroke"],"rows":[["Core temperature","Usually 37 to 40 °C (98.6 to 104 °F)","Above 40 °C (104 °F)","Above 40 °C (104 °F)"],["Mental status","Normal","Altered","Altered"],["Who","Anyone in heat with volume loss","Older adults, infants, chronically ill","Young healthy athletes, soldiers, laborers"],["Onset","Hours","Days, during heat waves","Hours of exertion"],["Main mechanism","Volume depletion and skin vasodilation","Impaired heat loss and reserve","Heat production exceeds loss"],["Skin","Moist, heavy sweating","Often hot and dry","Sweating often persists"],["Rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury (AKI), disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Absent","Possible, less common","Common and often severe"]],"caption":"CNS dysfunction, not a single temperature reading, separates heat stroke from heat exhaustion."},{"type":"p","text":"Classic heat stroke overwhelms people with reduced reserve: impaired thirst and mobility, limited ability to raise cardiac output, and drugs that block sweating (anticholinergics, many antipsychotics) or deplete volume (diuretics). Exertional heat stroke occurs in healthy people because muscle produces heat faster than it can be shed, especially in humid heat (C02); sweating often persists, so moist skin does not exclude it."},{"type":"p","text":"The complications follow from cell injury. Rhabdomyolysis is breakdown of skeletal muscle membranes from heat and ATP depletion, releasing creatine kinase, myoglobin, K+, phosphate, and urate. Filtered myoglobin forms casts with uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) in acidic tubular fluid and its heme iron injures proximal tubules, while volume depletion lowers glomerular filtration, producing acute kidney injury. Urine is dark and dipstick positive for blood with few red cells, because the test detects heme. DIC follows endothelial injury and tissue factor exposure: consumption of platelets and clotting factors gives low platelets, prolonged prothrombin time (PT) and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), low fibrinogen, and high D-dimer, with both bleeding and microthrombi. Hyperkalemia follows muscle lysis and AKI, and hepatocellular injury raises aminotransferases."},{"type":"flow","title":"From heat stroke to multiorgan injury","steps":["Heat gain exceeds heat loss","Core temperature rises above 40 °C (104 °F)","Proteins denature; neurons fail, causing CNS dysfunction","Splanchnic ischemia lets gut endotoxin enter blood","Cytokines and endothelial injury trigger DIC","Muscle lysis releases myoglobin, K+, and CK","Myoglobin casts and heme toxicity cause AKI"],"caption":"Direct heat injury and systemic inflammation act together to damage brain, kidney, liver, and the clotting system."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the laboratory picture in exertional heat stroke","text":"A 22-year-old runner collapses late in a summer race, confused, with wet skin and a rectal temperature of 41.2 °C (106.2 °F). CK is very high, K+ is 6.1 mmol/L (reference about 3.5 to 5.0), creatinine is rising, urine is cola colored and heme positive, platelets are low, and PT is prolonged. Confusion above 40 °C (104 °F) defines heat stroke rather than heat exhaustion, and wet skin fits the exertional form. Massive CK with heme-positive urine means rhabdomyolysis, which explains the hyperkalemia and AKI. Low platelets with a prolonged PT point to DIC."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C06","obj":"IN-4.6","title":"Malignant hyperthermia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Malignant hyperthermia is a pharmacogenetic disorder of skeletal muscle. In most susceptible families it is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait caused by mutations in RYR1 on chromosome 19, which encodes the ryanodine receptor type 1, the Ca2+ release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). A minority of cases involve CACNA1S, which encodes the alpha-1 subunit of the dihydropyridine (DHP) receptor. Carriers usually have normal muscle function until they are exposed to a triggering drug. RYR1 mutations also cause central core disease, a congenital myopathy whose patients carry the same susceptibility."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal excitation-contraction coupling explains the defect. An action potential in the T tubule changes the conformation of the voltage-sensing DHP receptor, which is mechanically linked to RYR1 and opens it. Ca2+ leaves the SR, binds troponin C, and allows cross-bridge cycling. Relaxation requires SERCA, the SR Ca2+ ATPase, to pump Ca2+ back into the SR, and both cross-bridge cycling and SERCA use ATP. The mutant RYR1 channel is hypersensitive: volatile halogenated anesthetics (halothane, isoflurane, sevoflurane, desflurane) and the depolarizing neuromuscular blocker succinylcholine make it open readily and stay open, so cytosolic Ca2+ rises without control."},{"type":"flow","title":"Malignant hyperthermia cascade","steps":["Volatile anesthetic or succinylcholine reaches muscle","Mutant RYR1 opens and stays open","Sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca2+ floods the cytosol","Sustained contraction and continuous SERCA pumping","Huge ATP use raises CO2, O2 use, and heat","Oxygen supply falls behind; lactate accumulates","ATP depletion breaks membranes: K+, myoglobin, CK leak"],"caption":"Every clinical sign traces back to uncontrolled Ca2+ release and the ATP it consumes."},{"type":"p","text":"The signs follow in order. Sustained Ca2+ causes sustained contraction, seen as generalized rigidity or as masseter spasm right after succinylcholine. Cross-bridge cycling and SERCA pumping consume ATP at a huge rate, so aerobic metabolism increases CO2 production and oxygen consumption. End-tidal CO2 therefore rises even though ventilation has not changed (hypercarbia), which is usually one of the earliest signs, together with sympathetically driven tachycardia. As oxygen delivery falls behind demand, glycolysis produces lactate, adding metabolic acidosis to the respiratory acidosis. ATP depletion then disrupts the sarcolemma, releasing K+ (hyperkalemia and arrhythmias), myoglobin, and CK, with the same renal consequences as exertional rhabdomyolysis (C05). Core temperature rise is often a later sign, because heat accumulates only as hypermetabolism continues."},{"type":"p","text":"Malignant hyperthermia is hyperthermia with a normal set point: heat is generated in muscle faster than it can be lost, and the hypothalamus plays no causal role. It must be distinguished from other hypermetabolic states with rigidity and high temperature."},{"type":"table","title":"Malignant hyperthermia compared with related syndromes","cols":["Feature","Malignant hyperthermia","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome"],"rows":[["Cause","RYR1 (or CACNA1S) mutation plus volatile anesthetic or succinylcholine","Dopamine D2 receptor blockade (or withdrawal of dopaminergic drugs)","Excess serotonergic activity from one or more serotonergic drugs"],["Site of the defect","Skeletal muscle SR Ca2+ release","Central dopamine pathways","Central and peripheral serotonin receptors"],["Onset","Minutes to hours after exposure","Days","Hours"],["Neuromuscular findings","Generalized rigidity, masseter spasm","Lead pipe rigidity, slowed reflexes","Clonus and hyperreflexia, worse in the legs"],["Typical early clue","Rising end-tidal CO2 under anesthesia","Altered mental status and rigidity","Agitation, tremor, diarrhea, mydriasis"]],"caption":"All three can cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis; the trigger, tempo, and reflex pattern separate them."},{"type":"stop","predict":"During anesthesia, end-tidal CO2 rises steadily although minute ventilation is unchanged and temperature is still normal. What is the mechanism?","explain":"Uncontrolled Ca2+ release drives cross-bridge cycling and SERCA pumping, so muscle ATP use and aerobic CO2 production rise at once. Heat accumulates more slowly, so temperature rises later.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the order of signs and the cascade figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-04-01-C07","obj":"IN-4.7","title":"Hypothermia and cold injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypothermia is a core temperature below 35 °C (95 °F). It results from heat loss that exceeds production, especially with immersion or wind; from reduced heat production (hypothyroidism, hypoglycemia, adrenal insufficiency, malnutrition); from impaired regulation (hypothalamic lesions, sedatives, and ethanol, which dilates skin vessels and blunts shivering and judgment); and from the limited reserve of infants and older adults. Cold slows every temperature-dependent process, so enzyme reactions, ion pumps, channel gating, and nerve conduction all slow as core temperature falls."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of hypothermia","cols":["Stage","Core temperature","Main findings and mechanism"],"rows":[["Mild","32 to 35 °C (89.6 to 95 °F)","Maximal shivering, tachycardia, tachypnea, cold diuresis, confusion, ataxia; defenses still active"],["Moderate","28 to 32 °C (82.4 to 89.6 °F)","Shivering stops, bradycardia, atrial fibrillation, Osborn waves, hypoventilation, falling consciousness"],["Severe","Below 28 °C (82.4 °F)","Coma, hypotension, apnea, high risk of ventricular fibrillation and asystole"]],"caption":"Loss of shivering marks the change from a defended state to passive cooling."},{"type":"p","text":"Below about 32 °C (89.6 °F), shivering fails and the hypothalamic controller itself is depressed, so heat production drops when it is most needed and cooling speeds up. Bradycardia comes from slowed phase 4 depolarization in the SA node and is not vagally mediated. PR, QRS, and QT intervals lengthen as conduction and repolarization slow. The Osborn (J) wave is a positive deflection at the junction of the QRS complex and ST segment whose amplitude grows as temperature falls; it is attributed to a cold-enhanced transient outward K+ current (Ito) in epicardium more than endocardium, which creates a transmural voltage gradient in early repolarization. The cold myocardium becomes irritable: atrial fibrillation is common in moderate hypothermia, and ventricular fibrillation becomes likely below about 28 °C (82.4 °F), sometimes triggered by handling the patient."},{"type":"p","text":"Other findings follow the same logic. Cold diuresis occurs because peripheral vasoconstriction shifts blood volume centrally, which suppresses antidiuretic hormone (ADH) release, and cold impairs tubular concentrating ability. The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve shifts left, so hemoglobin releases oxygen less readily. Clotting enzymes slow, causing a coagulopathy that routine PT and aPTT may miss because the tests are run at 37 °C (98.6 °F)."},{"type":"p","text":"Frostbite is freezing of tissue. Ice forms first in extracellular fluid, which becomes hyperosmolar and draws water out of cells, so they shrink and suffer electrolyte injury; rapid freezing can also form intracellular ice that ruptures membranes. Much of the damage follows rewarming: injured endothelium, release of thromboxane A2 and prostaglandin F2-alpha, platelet aggregation, and microvascular thrombosis cause progressive dermal ischemia, much like ischemia-reperfusion injury. Fingers, toes, nose, and ears are most exposed because cold-induced vasoconstriction (C03) sacrifices them to protect the core. Superficial frostbite gives erythema and clear blisters; deep frostbite gives hemorrhagic blisters and then dry gangrene."},{"type":"p","text":"Perniosis (chilblains) is a nonfreezing injury. Damp cold above freezing causes prolonged vasospasm of small cutaneous vessels, local hypoxia, and a perivascular lymphocytic inflammatory reaction, producing itchy or burning, tender red to violet papules on the fingers and toes hours after exposure that resolve over one to a few weeks."},{"type":"table","title":"Frostbite compared with perniosis","cols":["Feature","Frostbite","Perniosis (chilblains)"],"rows":[["Exposure","Tissue temperature below freezing","Damp cold above freezing"],["Primary mechanism","Ice crystals and cell dehydration, then endothelial injury and thrombosis on rewarming","Vasospasm of small vessels with perivascular lymphocytic inflammation"],["Lesions","Numb, pale or waxy tissue, blisters, then necrosis and gangrene when deep","Itchy or burning red to violet papules on fingers and toes"],["Course","Tissue loss possible; demarcation over weeks","Usually resolves without tissue loss"]],"caption":"Ice and thrombosis define frostbite; inflammation without freezing defines perniosis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Thermoregulation ties several disciplines into one control system. Anatomy places the controller in the hypothalamus and the effectors in the skin: eccrine glands with cholinergic sympathetic innervation, arteriovenous anastomoses, arrector pili muscles, and subcutaneous fat. Biochemistry explains heat production, because shivering, malignant hyperthermia, and brown fat all generate heat by spending the energy of ATP or of the proton gradient without doing external work. Immunology explains fever through IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha, and PGE2, linking the innate response to the hypothalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from which part fails. Fever is a normal controller with a raised target; hyperthermia is a normal target with an overwhelmed controller; heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia, and hypothermia injure tissue directly through protein denaturation, ATP depletion, or slowed membrane function. The same downstream injuries, rhabdomyolysis, AKI, DIC, and arrhythmias, recur across these states, so reasoning from mechanism transfers from one question to the next. Temperature thresholds are conventions that vary slightly between sources, so findings such as mental status, sweating, and shivering should be weighed together with the number."}],"summary":["The preoptic (anterior) hypothalamus senses core temperature and drives heat loss; the posterior hypothalamus integrates skin input and drives heat conservation and production.","Anterior hypothalamic lesions cause hyperthermia; posterior lesions cause poikilothermia.","Radiation, conduction, and convection need skin warmer than the environment; evaporation (about 0.58 kcal per gram) needs only a water vapor gradient.","When ambient temperature exceeds skin temperature, sweat evaporation is the only route of heat loss, and high humidity defeats it.","Cold defenses are vasoconstriction, piloerection, shivering (up to about 4 to 5 fold heat production), brown fat UCP1 uncoupling via beta-3 receptors, and slower thyroid driven increases in metabolic rate.","Neonates rely on brown fat because they have high surface area to mass, little insulation, and poor shivering.","In fever, IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha induce COX-2 and PGE2, which raises the set point; in hyperthermia the set point is unchanged and COX inhibition has no effect.","Heat stroke is core temperature above 40 °C (104 °F) with CNS dysfunction; classic is slow in vulnerable people, exertional is fast in healthy people and causes rhabdomyolysis, AKI, and DIC.","Malignant hyperthermia is uncontrolled RYR1 Ca2+ release after volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine; rising end-tidal CO2 and rigidity precede the temperature rise.","Hypothermia is core temperature below 35 °C (95 °F); shivering stops below about 32 °C (89.6 °F), bradycardia and Osborn waves appear, and VF risk rises below about 28 °C (82.4 °F); frostbite freezes tissue, perniosis does not."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Body Temperature Regulation and Fever"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Regulation of body temperature"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Bioenergetics and oxidative phosphorylation, uncoupling proteins"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity, cytokines of inflammation and their systemic effects"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and nutritional diseases, injury by physical agents (thermal injury, hyperthermia, hypothermia)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Fever and disorders of temperature regulation"}]},"IN-5":{"unit":"IN-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01","title":"Skin Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Skin is built from proteins, lipids, and carbohydrates that depend on vitamins and minerals, so it often shows the first visible sign of a nutritional or metabolic defect. This chapter follows collagen synthesis step by step, links vitamin C, copper, niacin, vitamin A, zinc, biotin, riboflavin, and essential fatty acids to specific skin findings, traces vitamin D from the epidermis to the kidney, and explains barrier lipids, sebum, and dermal glycosaminoglycans. It ends with skin signs of systemic metabolism: xanthomas that point to a lipoprotein disorder and the photosensitive blisters of porphyria cutanea tarda. Board questions usually describe a lesion and ask for the enzyme, cofactor, or pathway step behind it.","prerequisites":["Layers of the epidermis and dermis and the dermal-epidermal junction (IN-1)","Protein synthesis on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi, and secretion","Vitamin cofactor roles (NAD+, FAD, pyridoxal phosphate, biotin)","Lipoprotein classes, apolipoproteins, and lipoprotein lipase","Steps of heme synthesis and the enzymes that catalyze them"],"connections":[{"to":"Musculoskeletal system","how":"The same collagen synthesis steps build bone, tendon, and ligament, so osteogenesis imperfecta and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome affect both skin and skeleton."},{"to":"Endocrine and renal physiology","how":"Vitamin D made in skin becomes a hormone only after hepatic and renal hydroxylation, and the renal step is controlled by PTH, phosphate, and FGF23."},{"to":"Cardiovascular system and lipid metabolism","how":"Xanthomas are visible deposits of the same lipoproteins that drive atherosclerosis and, with severe hypertriglyceridemia, pancreatitis."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Porphyria cutanea tarda is a defect of heme synthesis, and iron overload is one of its strongest triggers."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal system","how":"Malabsorption, carcinoid tumors, and intestinal transport defects such as Hartnup disease and acrodermatitis enteropathica present with skin signs."}],"terms":[{"term":"Procollagen","def":"The triple-helical collagen precursor secreted by fibroblasts that still carries its N- and C-terminal propeptides."},{"term":"Tropocollagen","def":"The collagen molecule left after the propeptides are cleaved, which self-assembles into fibrils."},{"term":"Lysyl oxidase","def":"A copper-dependent extracellular enzyme that converts lysine and hydroxylysine side chains to aldehydes that form covalent cross-links between collagen molecules."},{"term":"Pellagra","def":"The disease of niacin deficiency, classically photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia."},{"term":"Acrodermatitis enteropathica","def":"Zinc deficiency, inherited from a defect in the intestinal zinc transporter or acquired, with dermatitis around body openings and on the hands and feet."},{"term":"Calcitriol","def":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, the active hormonal form of vitamin D, produced mainly by renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase."},{"term":"Lamellar bodies","def":"Secretory organelles of the stratum granulosum that release the lipids and enzymes that form the extracellular barrier of the stratum corneum."},{"term":"Glycosaminoglycan (GAG)","def":"A long unbranched polysaccharide of repeating disaccharides that binds water and, except hyaluronic acid, is sulfated and attached to a core protein."},{"term":"Xanthoma","def":"A yellow skin or tendon deposit of lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells) that reflects excess plasma lipoproteins."},{"term":"Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD)","def":"The cytosolic heme synthesis enzyme that converts uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III; its deficiency causes porphyria cutanea tarda."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C01","obj":"IN-5.1","title":"Collagen synthesis and the defect at each step","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Collagen is the main structural protein of the dermis, and type I collagen supplies most of its tensile strength. Each molecule is three left-handed alpha chains wound together into a right-handed triple helix, and each chain is a repeat of Gly-X-Y in which X is often proline and Y is often hydroxyproline. Glycine must occupy every third position because it is the only amino acid small enough to fit in the crowded center of the helix."},{"type":"flow","title":"Collagen synthesis from gene to cross-linked fibril","steps":["Pre-pro-alpha chains made on rough ER","Proline and lysine hydroxylated (vitamin C, Fe2+, O2)","Selected hydroxylysines glycosylated (galactose, glucose)","C-propeptides align three chains; triple helix zips","Procollagen packaged in Golgi and secreted","Procollagen peptidases cleave N- and C-propeptides","Tropocollagen self-assembles into fibrils","Lysyl oxidase (copper) cross-links fibrils"],"caption":"The first five steps occur inside the fibroblast and the last three outside it. A failure at any step yields weak or deficient collagen."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the cell, prolyl 4-hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase act on the chains before the helix forms. Both are dioxygenases that use O2 and alpha-ketoglutarate and hold Fe2+ at the active site; ascorbate (vitamin C) reduces the iron back to Fe2+ when it becomes oxidized. Hydroxyproline stabilizes the helix through hydrogen bonding, so underhydroxylated chains fold poorly at body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F), and are largely degraded instead of secreted. Hydroxylysine is needed for glycosylation and later for stable cross-links. Disulfide bonds between the C-propeptides bring three chains into register, and the helix then forms from the C-terminus toward the N-terminus."},{"type":"p","text":"Outside the cell, removal of the propeptides converts soluble procollagen to tropocollagen, which packs into staggered fibrils. Lysyl oxidase then oxidizes lysine and hydroxylysine side chains to aldehydes (allysine) that condense into covalent cross-links between molecules. Fibrils without these cross-links assemble but tear easily."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting the defect from the failed step","cols":["Failed step","Example","Result in connective tissue"],"rows":[["Prolyl hydroxylation","Scurvy (vitamin C deficiency)","Unstable helix, less collagen secreted; fragile vessels and poor healing"],["Lysyl hydroxylation","Kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (lysyl hydroxylase 1)","Few stable cross-links; hyperextensible skin, scoliosis"],["Triple helix formation","Osteogenesis imperfecta (glycine substitution in type I collagen)","Abnormal or reduced type I collagen; brittle bones, blue sclerae"],["Propeptide cleavage","Dermatosparaxis Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (procollagen N-peptidase)","Disorganized fibrils; very fragile, sagging skin"],["Cross-linking","Menkes disease (copper); lathyrism (lysyl oxidase inhibitor)","Weak fibrils and elastin; vascular and skin fragility"]],"caption":"No common human disease maps to the hydroxylysine glycosylation step. Classic and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome are instead caused by mutations in type V and type III collagen genes."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A glycine-to-cysteine substitution in one type I collagen chain still lets the chain be made. Why can a single substituted allele disrupt most of the collagen?","explain":"Each molecule contains two alpha1(I) chains, so with one mutant COL1A1 allele about three of four molecules contain at least one abnormal chain, and a bulky residue in the helix center blocks normal folding of the whole molecule (a dominant negative effect).","check":"Compare with the glycine requirement and the triple helix formation row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C02","obj":"IN-5.2","title":"Scurvy and Menkes disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Scurvy is the clinical result of failed collagen hydroxylation. The body has no way to make ascorbate, and stores are depleted after about one to three months of an intake near zero, so scurvy appears where fruit and vegetable intake is near zero, as in some older adults living alone, people with alcohol use disorder, and those on highly selective diets."},{"type":"p","text":"Every classic finding follows from weak collagen in a particular place. Capillaries around hair follicles have little supporting tissue, so they rupture into perifollicular hemorrhages, often on the legs where hydrostatic pressure is highest. Follicles also become plugged with keratin, and the hair shafts inside them coil into corkscrew hairs. Gum tissue turns over rapidly and is under mechanical stress, so it becomes swollen, spongy, and bleeds easily, especially where teeth are present. New wounds heal poorly because fibroblasts cannot lay down stable collagen, and old scars can break down because collagen is normally being remodeled. Bleeding into joints and under the periosteum causes pain. Platelet counts and clotting tests are normal; the bleeding comes from weak vessel support, not from a coagulation defect. Anemia is common, both from blood loss and because ascorbate normally keeps dietary iron in the absorbable ferrous form."},{"type":"p","text":"Menkes disease reaches the same final step through copper. It is an X-linked recessive disorder of the ATP7A gene, which encodes a copper-transporting ATPase that moves copper out of intestinal cells into the blood and into the secretory pathway of other cells. Copper is absorbed into enterocytes but trapped there, so serum copper and ceruloplasmin are low and copper-dependent enzymes throughout the body run short. Wilson disease involves the related ATP7B transporter in the liver and causes copper overload instead."},{"type":"table","title":"Copper enzymes and the Menkes findings they explain","cols":["Copper enzyme","Normal role","Finding when deficient"],"rows":[["Lysyl oxidase","Cross-links collagen and elastin","Tortuous, fragile arteries; loose skin; bladder diverticula"],["Tyrosinase","Melanin synthesis","Hypopigmented, light-colored hair and skin"],["Thiol oxidation in hair keratin (enzyme not characterized)","Disulfide cross-links in hair keratin","Brittle, twisted (kinky) hair, pili torti"],["Cytochrome c oxidase","Final step of the electron transport chain","Hypotonia, seizures, neurodegeneration"],["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Dopamine to norepinephrine","Autonomic dysfunction"]],"caption":"Affected boys usually present in the first months of life, and the classic severe form is often fatal in early childhood."},{"type":"p","text":"Scurvy blocks an intracellular step, so less collagen is secreted, while Menkes blocks an extracellular step, so collagen is secreted but poorly cross-linked. Both cause vascular fragility, but only Menkes adds kinky hair, hypopigmentation, and neurodegeneration from loss of other copper enzymes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C03","obj":"IN-5.3","title":"Pellagra and the tryptophan to niacin pathway","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Niacin (nicotinic acid and nicotinamide, vitamin B3) is the precursor of NAD+ and NADP+, the carriers of hydride ions in hundreds of oxidation-reduction reactions. Deficiency therefore harms tissues with high turnover and high energy demand: the skin, the lining of the gut, and the brain. Pellagra is classically remembered as dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia, and untreated it ends in death."},{"type":"p","text":"The dermatitis is photosensitive. It begins like a sunburn on exposed skin, then becomes dark, thick, scaly, and sharply bordered, as on the backs of the hands and in a band around the neck (Casal necklace). The exact cause of photosensitivity is not fully settled. A leading explanation is that UV-damaged keratinocytes depend on NAD+, the substrate of poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase, to repair DNA, so a low NAD+ supply impairs repair and survival after sun exposure."},{"type":"p","text":"Niacin comes from the diet or is made from tryptophan, and every cause of pellagra either removes tryptophan or blocks this pathway. About 60 mg of tryptophan yields 1 mg of niacin, and the pathway needs vitamin B6 (pyridoxal phosphate for kynureninase) and riboflavin (FAD for kynurenine monooxygenase)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Tryptophan to NAD+ and where each cause acts","steps":["Dietary tryptophan absorbed (lost in Hartnup disease)","Tryptophan diverted to serotonin (carcinoid syndrome)","Kynurenine formed in the liver","FAD-dependent and B6-dependent steps (isoniazid depletes B6)","Quinolinic acid to nicotinic acid mononucleotide","NAD+ and NADP+"],"caption":"Any step that removes tryptophan or its cofactors lowers NAD+ supply."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of pellagra by mechanism","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Corn (maize) based diet","Corn is low in tryptophan and its niacin is bound in a form poorly released by digestion; alkali processing (nixtamalization) frees it"],["Hartnup disease","Autosomal recessive defect of the neutral amino acid transporter (B0AT1, SLC6A19) in the small intestine and proximal tubule; tryptophan is poorly absorbed and lost in urine (neutral aminoaciduria)"],["Carcinoid syndrome","Tumor cells convert a large share of tryptophan, normally about 1%, to serotonin, leaving little for niacin"],["Isoniazid","Forms an inactive compound with pyridoxal and depletes vitamin B6, blocking the B6-dependent step"],["Alcohol use disorder, malabsorption","Low intake and absorption of niacin, tryptophan, B6, and B2"]],"caption":"Dietary niacin bypasses the pathway, so pellagra needs both a low niacin and a low tryptophan supply."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: carcinoid tumor with a rash","text":"A patient with liver metastases from a small intestinal neuroendocrine tumor has flushing, diarrhea, and wheezing, then develops a scaly, dark rash on the backs of the hands and the neck. Step 1: hepatic metastases release serotonin directly into the systemic circulation, bypassing liver inactivation, which explains flushing, diarrhea, and bronchospasm. Step 2: the tumor consumes a large fraction of dietary tryptophan to make serotonin. Step 3: less tryptophan enters the kynurenine pathway, so endogenous niacin and NAD+ synthesis falls. Step 4: NAD+-dependent repair in sun-damaged keratinocytes fails, producing photosensitive pellagra. Urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), the serotonin breakdown product, is elevated, confirming the diversion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with Hartnup disease develop pellagra-like dermatitis even though serum levels of most other amino acids stay near normal?","explain":"Other amino acids are absorbed as dipeptides and tripeptides through a separate peptide transporter, which keeps protein nutrition adequate, but free tryptophan uptake through the neutral transporter is reduced and renal loss continues, so niacin synthesis falls when dietary niacin is marginal.","check":"Compare with the Hartnup row of the table and the tryptophan pathway figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C04","obj":"IN-5.4","title":"Skin signs of vitamin A, zinc, biotin, riboflavin, and essential fatty acid deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each of these nutrients has a specific job in renewing epithelium, so the pattern and site of the skin change point to the missing one. Retinoic acid, the active form of vitamin A, binds nuclear retinoic acid receptors that guide keratinocyte differentiation and mucus cell development. Without it, epithelia undergo squamous metaplasia and overkeratinize, so follicles fill with keratin plugs (follicular hyperkeratosis, called phrynoderma) and the skin becomes dry and rough (xerosis). The same defect in the eye causes night blindness, conjunctival xerosis with Bitot spots, and corneal damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Zinc is a cofactor for more than 300 enzymes and a structural part of zinc finger transcription factors, so it is critical where cells divide quickly. Acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form, an autosomal recessive defect in the intestinal zinc transporter ZIP4 (SLC39A4), and it typically appears when an infant is weaned from breast milk, which carries zinc in a more absorbable form. Acquired deficiency follows malabsorption, alcohol use disorder, or zinc-free parenteral nutrition. The dermatitis is red, scaly, and sometimes blistered around the mouth, eyes, and anus and on the hands and feet, with alopecia, diarrhea, poor wound healing, and loss of taste."},{"type":"p","text":"Biotin is the carboxyl carrier for the four carboxylases (acetyl-CoA, pyruvate, propionyl-CoA, and 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA). Deficiency comes from large intakes of raw egg white, whose avidin binds biotin, from inherited biotinidase deficiency, or rarely from long parenteral nutrition without biotin. Loss of acetyl-CoA carboxylase activity limits fatty acid synthesis for barrier lipids, giving a scaly periorificial dermatitis, alopecia, and conjunctivitis that closely resemble zinc deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Riboflavin forms FAD and FMN. Deficiency shows first where epithelium is thin and constantly moving, at the corners of the mouth (angular cheilitis), the lips (cheilosis), and the tongue (glossitis), with seborrheic-like dermatitis. Essential fatty acid deficiency removes linoleic acid, which is built into the special ceramides of the stratum corneum (C06), so the barrier leaks water and the skin becomes dry, scaly, and slow to heal."},{"type":"table","title":"Matching the skin finding to the nutrient","cols":["Nutrient","Key biochemical role","Skin finding","Clues outside the skin"],"rows":[["Vitamin A","Retinoic acid controls epithelial differentiation","Follicular hyperkeratosis, xerosis","Night blindness, Bitot spots"],["Zinc","Enzyme and zinc finger cofactor","Periorificial and acral dermatitis, alopecia, poor healing","Diarrhea, loss of taste, onset at weaning"],["Biotin","Carboxylase cofactor","Periorificial scaly dermatitis, alopecia","Raw egg white intake, conjunctivitis"],["Riboflavin (B2)","FAD and FMN","Angular cheilitis, cheilosis","Glossitis, corneal vascularization"],["Essential fatty acids","Linoleic acid in barrier ceramides and eicosanoids","Dry, scaly dermatitis, poor healing","Fat-free parenteral nutrition, fat malabsorption"]],"caption":"Zinc and biotin deficiency look alike in the skin; history (weaning, egg white, parenteral nutrition) and measured levels separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C05","obj":"IN-5.5","title":"Vitamin D synthesis in skin and what limits it","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) can be made entirely in the body, which is why calcitriol is classed as a hormone. The starting material is 7-dehydrocholesterol, the immediate precursor of cholesterol, held in keratinocyte membranes with the highest amounts in the stratum basale and stratum spinosum. Ultraviolet B light (about 290 to 315 nm) opens its B ring to form previtamin D3, which then rearranges by a heat-dependent isomerization at skin temperature over hours to days into cholecalciferol. Vitamin D binding protein carries it from the skin into the circulation."},{"type":"flow","title":"From 7-dehydrocholesterol to calcitriol","steps":["7-dehydrocholesterol in basal and spinous keratinocytes","UVB opens B ring: previtamin D3","Thermal isomerization: cholecalciferol (D3)","Carried on vitamin D binding protein to liver","Liver 25-hydroxylase: 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol)","Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase (proximal tubule): calcitriol","Calcitriol raises gut Ca2+ and phosphate absorption"],"caption":"Dietary D2 and D3 join the pathway at the liver step. Only the renal step is tightly regulated."},{"type":"p","text":"The two hydroxylations differ in control. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is loosely regulated and depends mostly on substrate supply, so 25-hydroxyvitamin D, with a half-life of about two to three weeks, is the best measure of body vitamin D status. Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is the control point: PTH and low phosphate stimulate it, while FGF23, high phosphate, and calcitriol itself suppress it, and 24-hydroxylase diverts both forms toward inactive products. Because of this control, calcitriol is often normal or even high in early vitamin D deficiency, as rising PTH drives the remaining substrate through the kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"Anything that keeps UVB photons from reaching 7-dehydrocholesterol lowers production. Melanin absorbs UVB, so deeply pigmented skin needs several times longer exposure to make the same amount. At high latitude, in winter, and early or late in the day, sunlight passes obliquely through more atmosphere and ozone absorbs most UVB; above about 35 to 40 degrees of latitude little vitamin D is made in winter months. Sunscreen absorbs UVB by design and, applied properly, greatly reduces synthesis, as do clothing, window glass, and staying indoors. Aging reduces epidermal 7-dehydrocholesterol."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolonged sun exposure does not cause vitamin D toxicity. Once previtamin D3 accumulates, further UV converts it to inactive photoproducts (lumisterol and tachysterol), and excess cholecalciferol in the skin is also photodegraded. Toxicity therefore comes from excess supplementation or from unregulated 1-alpha-hydroxylation, as in granulomatous disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two people have the same daily sun exposure: one has deeply pigmented skin in Seattle in winter, the other light skin in the same city in summer. Whose serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D falls, and why does serum calcium change little at first?","explain":"Melanin and low winter UVB both reduce previtamin D3 formation in the first person, so 25-hydroxyvitamin D falls; calcium is defended because lower calcium absorption raises PTH, which increases renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation, bone resorption, and renal calcium reabsorption.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on renal regulation and on factors that block UVB."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C06","obj":"IN-5.6","title":"Barrier lipids, sebum, and dermal glycosaminoglycans","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The permeability barrier of the skin lies in the stratum corneum and is often described as bricks and mortar. The bricks are flattened, dead corneocytes filled with keratin and wrapped in a cross-linked protein envelope; the mortar is stacked lipid sheets (lamellae) between them. These lipids come from lamellar bodies, small secretory organelles that form in the stratum spinosum and empty their contents by exocytosis at the junction of the stratum granulosum and stratum corneum."},{"type":"p","text":"Lamellar bodies secrete precursors together with the enzymes that process them. Beta-glucocerebrosidase converts glucosylceramides to ceramides, acid sphingomyelinase yields further ceramide from sphingomyelin, and phospholipases release free fatty acids from phospholipids. The finished mixture is roughly equal molar amounts of ceramides, cholesterol, and long-chain free fatty acids. Some ceramides carry an ester-linked linoleic acid that helps hold the lamellae together, which is why essential fatty acid deficiency weakens the barrier (C04). The barrier fails in severe Gaucher disease, in which loss of glucocerebrosidase leaves glucosylceramides unconverted."},{"type":"p","text":"Sebum is a different lipid with a different job. Sebaceous glands secrete by the holocrine method, in which whole cells fill with lipid and disintegrate. Androgens drive gland size and output, which explains the rise at puberty. Sebum lubricates hair and skin and adds to surface water resistance. Squalene, a cholesterol precursor, accumulates in sebocytes instead of being converted to cholesterol, and wax esters are also unique to sebum, so both serve as markers of sebaceous lipid. Bacterial lipases from Cutibacterium acnes split triglycerides into free fatty acids on the skin surface."},{"type":"table","title":"Barrier lipid versus sebum","cols":["Feature","Stratum corneum barrier lipid","Sebum"],"rows":[["Source","Lamellar bodies of keratinocytes","Sebaceous glands (holocrine)"],["Main components","Ceramides, cholesterol, free fatty acids","Triglycerides (largest share), wax esters, squalene, small amounts of cholesterol and its esters"],["Location","Between corneocytes","Skin surface and hair follicle"],["Main function","Prevents water loss and entry of substances","Lubrication and surface protection"],["Key regulator","Keratinocyte differentiation","Androgens"]],"caption":"The barrier holds ceramide, cholesterol, and free fatty acid in near equal molar amounts, with ceramides the largest share by mass; triglycerides dominate sebum."},{"type":"p","text":"In the dermis, collagen and elastic fibers sit in a ground substance of glycosaminoglycans that bind large amounts of water and resist compression. Hyaluronic acid is a repeat of glucuronic acid and N-acetylglucosamine; it is the only GAG that is not sulfated and not attached to a core protein, and it is made at the plasma membrane rather than in the Golgi. It is the main water-holding molecule of the dermis and is abundant in loose connective tissue. Dermatan sulfate, a repeat of iduronic acid and N-acetylgalactosamine, is the main sulfated GAG of skin. It is carried on the proteoglycan decorin, which binds collagen fibrils and helps control their diameter and spacing. In Hurler and Hunter syndromes, failure to degrade dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate causes thickened skin, and Hunter syndrome can produce pebbly skin papules over the scapulae."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C07","obj":"IN-5.7","title":"Xanthomas as markers of lipoprotein disorders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A xanthoma forms when plasma lipoproteins leak through dermal or tendon capillaries and are taken up by macrophages, which fill with lipid droplets to become foam cells. The amount and type of lipoprotein determine where and how fast deposits form, so the morphology of a xanthoma points to a specific disorder."},{"type":"p","text":"Eruptive xanthomas are crops of small yellow papules with a red halo on the buttocks, thighs, and extensor surfaces that appear over days to weeks. They reflect very high triglycerides, typically above about 1,000 mg/dL (about 11 mmol/L), with chylomicrons in the plasma. In familial chylomicronemia (Fredrickson type I), lipoprotein lipase or its activator apoC-II is missing, so chylomicron triglyceride cannot be hydrolyzed in capillary beds. The same chylomicron excess causes milky plasma, lipemia retinalis (creamy white retinal vessels), and acute pancreatitis. Eruptive xanthomas also appear in type V hyperlipidemia and with poorly controlled diabetes, in which insulin deficiency lowers lipoprotein lipase activity. They resolve as triglycerides fall."},{"type":"p","text":"Tendon xanthomas are firm nodules in the Achilles tendons and the extensor tendons of the hands. They are a strong sign of familial hypercholesterolemia (type IIa), most often from mutations in the LDL receptor gene and less often in apoB-100 or PCSK9. Without receptor-mediated uptake, LDL cholesterol stays high from birth, roughly two to three times normal in heterozygotes and higher still in homozygotes, and LDL enters tendons and the arterial wall. Xanthelasma, soft yellow plaques on the eyelids, also occurs with high LDL, but about half of affected people have normal lipid levels, so it is less specific."},{"type":"p","text":"Palmar xanthomas (xanthoma striata palmaris) are yellow deposits in the creases of the palms and fingers and are characteristic of dysbetalipoproteinemia (type III). The apoE2 isoform binds poorly to hepatic remnant receptors, so homozygotes (apoE2/E2) clear chylomicron remnants and IDL slowly. Disease usually appears only with a second factor such as hypothyroidism, obesity, or diabetes, and cholesterol and triglycerides are both raised to similar degrees. Tuberoeruptive xanthomas on the elbows and knees also occur."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading a xanthoma","cols":["Xanthoma","Lipoprotein in excess","Disorder and defect"],"rows":[["Eruptive (buttocks, extensors)","Chylomicrons, VLDL","Type I (lipoprotein lipase or apoC-II deficiency), type V, uncontrolled diabetes"],["Tendon (Achilles, hand extensors)","LDL","Type IIa familial hypercholesterolemia (LDL receptor)"],["Xanthelasma (eyelids)","LDL, or none","Familial hypercholesterolemia; often normal lipids"],["Palmar crease, tuberoeruptive","Chylomicron remnants, IDL","Type III dysbetalipoproteinemia (apoE2/E2)"]],"caption":"Eruptive xanthomas follow triglyceride levels quickly; tendon and palmar xanthomas reflect long-standing cholesterol-rich lipoproteins."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-05-01-C08","obj":"IN-5.8","title":"Porphyria cutanea tarda","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) is the most common porphyria. It results from reduced activity of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD), the cytosolic enzyme of heme synthesis that removes four carboxyl groups to convert uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III. Most cases (about 75 to 80%) are sporadic, with normal UROD genes; the rest are familial, with one inherited mutant allele that halves enzyme activity in all tissues. In both forms, symptoms appear only when hepatic UROD activity falls below about 20 to 25% of normal, which requires an added trigger."},{"type":"p","text":"The triggers share one mechanism: they increase iron and oxidative stress in hepatocytes. Under those conditions uroporphyrinogen is partly oxidized to uroporphomethene, which inhibits UROD. Alcohol, hepatitis C, HIV, smoking, estrogens (oral contraceptives, hormone therapy), and iron overload, often with hemochromatosis gene (HFE) variants, are the classic factors, and most patients have more than one."},{"type":"flow","title":"From hepatic UROD inhibition to blisters","steps":["Iron and oxidative stress in hepatocytes","Uroporphomethene inhibits hepatic UROD","Uroporphyrinogen accumulates, oxidized to uroporphyrin","Water-soluble porphyrins reach skin through plasma","Porphyrins absorb visible light near 400 nm","Reactive oxygen species damage dermis and basement membrane","Subepidermal blisters, erosions, scarring, milia"],"caption":"Light, not trauma alone, starts the damage, so lesions are confined to sun-exposed skin."},{"type":"p","text":"Uroporphyrinogen and its oxidized form uroporphyrin carry eight carboxyl groups, so they are water soluble and leave in the urine rather than the bile. Absorbing light strongly near 400 nm (the Soret band, visible violet rather than UVB), the excited porphyrin transfers energy to oxygen and generates reactive oxygen species that injure dermal capillaries and the dermal-epidermal junction. Skin on the backs of the hands, forearms, and face becomes fragile, so minor trauma produces erosions and tense subepidermal blisters that heal with scarring and milia (small white keratin cysts). Hypertrichosis over the cheeks and temples, hyperpigmentation, and sclerodermoid plaques are also typical."},{"type":"p","text":"PCT has no acute neurovisceral attacks, because the block is late in the pathway, so the neurotoxic precursors aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen are not raised; acute intermittent porphyria (porphobilinogen deaminase) causes attacks without skin lesions. Urine is reddish brown and shows orange-red fluorescence under a Wood lamp, from raised uroporphyrin and heptacarboxyl porphyrin. Liver iron is often increased, and PCT carries a higher risk of hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why sunscreen fails in PCT","text":"A man with hepatitis C who drinks heavily has blisters and erosions on the backs of his hands despite daily SPF 50 sunscreen. Step 1: two triggers together raise hepatocyte iron and oxidative stress, so uroporphomethene inhibits hepatic UROD past its threshold. Step 2: uroporphyrin accumulates and deposits in the dermis. Step 3: it absorbs visible light near 400 nm, which sunscreens built to block UVB and UVA pass through, so the reactive oxygen species keep forming and the blisters continue. SPF measures UVB protection only."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The same skin finding can arise at different levels of organization, and the mechanism tells them apart. Collagen fragility can begin with a missing cofactor (vitamin C), a missing metal transporter (ATP7A in Menkes), a structural gene (type I collagen in osteogenesis imperfecta), or a processing enzyme (Ehlers-Danlos subtypes), and the extra findings point to the level: corkscrew hairs and bleeding gums for diet, kinky hair and neurodegeneration for copper, fractures and blue sclerae for type I collagen. Photosensitivity likewise has two unrelated causes in this chapter: impaired NAD+-dependent repair in pellagra, and photochemical generation of reactive oxygen species by porphyrins in PCT, which is why blisters and scarring belong to PCT and not to pellagra."},{"type":"p","text":"The skin also reports on organs far from it. Xanthomas reveal hepatic and capillary lipoprotein handling, PCT reveals hepatic iron and viral infection, vitamin D synthesis links sun exposure to renal hormone production and calcium balance, and periorificial dermatitis can be the first sign of intestinal malabsorption. Skin signs are clues, not proof: zinc and biotin deficiency overlap, xanthelasma often occurs with normal lipids, and laboratory measures such as serum zinc, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, a lipid panel, and urine porphyrins are needed to confirm the mechanism."}],"summary":["Collagen is Gly-X-Y chains hydroxylated in the ER (vitamin C, Fe2+), glycosylated, wound into procollagen, cleaved outside the cell to tropocollagen, and cross-linked by copper-dependent lysyl oxidase.","Scurvy blocks hydroxylation, so collagen is unstable: perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, bleeding gums, poor healing, with normal clotting tests.","Menkes disease (X-linked ATP7A) traps copper in enterocytes, lowering lysyl oxidase and other copper enzymes: kinky hypopigmented hair, fragile vessels, neurodegeneration.","Pellagra is photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia from low NAD+; corn diets, Hartnup disease, carcinoid diversion of tryptophan, and isoniazid-induced B6 loss all reduce niacin.","Vitamin A deficiency causes follicular hyperkeratosis; zinc and biotin cause periorificial dermatitis with alopecia; riboflavin causes angular cheilitis; essential fatty acid loss causes scaly barrier dermatitis.","UVB converts epidermal 7-dehydrocholesterol to previtamin D3; liver 25-hydroxylation makes the storage form and regulated renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation makes calcitriol; melanin, latitude, and sunscreen reduce synthesis.","Lamellar bodies supply ceramides, cholesterol, and free fatty acids for the barrier; sebum is mostly triglycerides, wax esters, and squalene; hyaluronic acid and dermatan sulfate hold dermal water and organize collagen.","Eruptive xanthomas mean very high triglycerides (LPL or apoC-II deficiency), tendon xanthomas mean familial hypercholesterolemia (LDL receptor), and palmar crease xanthomas mean dysbetalipoproteinemia (apoE2).","PCT is reduced hepatic UROD activity, triggered by iron, alcohol, hepatitis C, and estrogen; uroporphyrin absorbs visible light and causes blisters, fragility, and hypertrichosis without neurovisceral attacks."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous proteins (collagen and elastin); Vitamins; Porphyrin metabolism; Lipoprotein metabolism and the hyperlipoproteinemias"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin; Connective tissue ground substance"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation of calcium and phosphate, vitamin D metabolism"}]},"IN-6":{"unit":"IN-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-06-01","title":"Antioxidants and Free Radical Scavengers","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Aerobic life runs on the stepwise reduction of oxygen, and every partially reduced intermediate along the way is chemically aggressive. This chapter explains how superoxide, hydrogen peroxide, and hydroxyl radical are generated, how they injure lipids, proteins, and DNA, and how a layered defense of enzymes, small-molecule scavengers, and melanin contains them. It then applies the same chemistry in two directions: ultraviolet injury and photoaging in skin, and the deliberate use of reactive oxygen species by neutrophils to kill microbes. Board questions here ask you to follow electrons from a source to a lesion, or from a missing enzyme to a predictable disease.","prerequisites":["Mitochondrial electron transport, including the roles of complexes I and III and coenzyme Q","The pentose phosphate pathway and the regulation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase","Membrane structure and the chemistry of polyunsaturated fatty acids","DNA repair pathways, especially base excision repair and nucleotide excision repair","Epidermal layers, melanocyte biology, and dermal collagen and elastic fiber architecture (IN-1, IN-5)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology (HE-3, HE-9)","how":"Mature erythrocytes have no mitochondria, so the pentose phosphate pathway is their only source of NADPH, which makes G6PD deficiency a hemolytic disease driven purely by oxidant stress."},{"to":"Immunology and infection (IM-4, IN-9, IN-12)","how":"The same NADPH oxidase chemistry that damages host tissue is the neutrophil's primary microbicidal weapon, so losing it produces a specific and predictable infection pattern."},{"to":"Cell injury and neoplasia (IN-8, IN-13-G)","how":"Oxidative and photochemical DNA lesions that escape repair become the signature mutations of actinic keratosis, squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma, and melanoma."},{"to":"Skin biochemistry and nutrition (IN-5)","how":"Selenium, riboflavin, zinc, copper, and manganese are cofactors for antioxidant enzymes, and vitamins C and E work in the same radical-quenching network as glutathione and ubiquinol."},{"to":"Wound healing and connective tissue (IN-10)","how":"The matrix metalloproteinases that remodel a healing wound are the ones ultraviolet signaling turns on chronically in photoaged dermis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Free radical","def":"Any species with an unpaired electron in an outer orbital, which makes it reactive and able to propagate damage by taking an electron or hydrogen atom from a neighbor."},{"term":"Reactive oxygen species (ROS)","def":"Oxygen-derived oxidants, including the radicals superoxide and hydroxyl radical and the non-radicals hydrogen peroxide and singlet oxygen."},{"term":"Oxidative stress","def":"A state in which oxidant production exceeds antioxidant capacity, shifting the cell's redox balance toward oxidation and allowing damage to accumulate."},{"term":"Fenton reaction","def":"Reduction of hydrogen peroxide by ferrous iron (Fe2+) to yield hydroxyl radical, hydroxide ion, and ferric iron (Fe3+)."},{"term":"Dismutation","def":"A reaction in which two identical molecules are converted to two different products, one more oxidized and one more reduced, as when two superoxide anions yield hydrogen peroxide and oxygen."},{"term":"Lipid peroxidation","def":"A self-propagating chain reaction in which a radical abstracts hydrogen from a polyunsaturated fatty acid, and the resulting peroxyl radical attacks the next fatty acid."},{"term":"Cyclobutane pyrimidine dimer","def":"A covalent four-carbon ring linking two adjacent pyrimidines on the same DNA strand, formed when UVB is absorbed directly by the bases."},{"term":"Singlet oxygen","def":"An excited, non-radical form of molecular oxygen produced when a photoexcited pigment transfers energy to oxygen; it attacks double bonds directly."},{"term":"Photosensitizer","def":"A molecule that absorbs light and passes the energy or an electron to another molecule, generating reactive species without being consumed itself."},{"term":"Respiratory burst","def":"The abrupt rise in oxygen consumption by an activated phagocyte as NADPH oxidase reduces oxygen to superoxide inside the phagosome."},{"term":"Solar elastosis","def":"Accumulation of amorphous, blue-gray (basophilic) degenerated collagen and elastic material in the upper dermis of chronically sun-exposed skin."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-06-01-C01","obj":"IN-6.1","title":"Formation of reactive oxygen species and the lesions they leave","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Molecular oxygen is a poor one-electron acceptor but an excellent four-electron acceptor, which is what cytochrome c oxidase provides when it reduces O2 to water. Trouble begins whenever oxygen is reduced one electron at a time, because each partial reduction leaves a species more reactive than the one before it: superoxide, then hydrogen peroxide, then hydroxyl radical, and only then water."},{"type":"p","text":"Superoxide is the usual entry point. A small fraction of the electrons moving through mitochondrial complexes I and III escapes onto oxygen instead of continuing down the chain, and this leak rises when the chain is reduced and the proton-motive force is high. Phagocytes make superoxide deliberately through NADPH oxidase (C05), and related NOX isoforms in keratinocytes and fibroblasts generate it as a signaling molecule. Superoxide is only moderately reactive, but it inactivates iron-sulfur cluster enzymes, releases free iron, and combines with nitric oxide to form peroxynitrite."},{"type":"p","text":"Hydrogen peroxide is not a radical and is a weak oxidant, yet it is the most dangerous intermediate in practice because it is uncharged, crosses membranes, and travels well away from where it formed. Its hazard is what ferrous iron does to it: the Fenton reaction splits hydrogen peroxide into hydroxyl radical, hydroxide, and ferric iron, and superoxide reduces that ferric iron back to the ferrous state so the cycle continues. Hydroxyl radical then reacts within a few molecular diameters, damaging whatever lies next to where it formed, which is why iron overload, hemolysis, and free heme amplify oxidative injury, and why ionizing radiation is so damaging, since it splits water directly into hydroxyl radical and hydrogen atoms. In skin, UVA adds another route by exciting endogenous photosensitizers such as riboflavin and porphyrins, which pass energy to oxygen to form singlet oxygen or an electron to form superoxide."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stepwise reduction of oxygen and where each step comes from","steps":["O2 gains one electron: superoxide, from electron leak at complexes I and III or NADPH oxidase","Superoxide dismutation: hydrogen peroxide, uncharged and membrane permeable","Hydrogen peroxide meets Fe2+: Fenton reaction yields hydroxyl radical","Hydroxyl radical attacks the nearest lipid, protein, or base","Full four-electron reduction instead yields water, with no intermediate released"],"caption":"Each one-electron step creates a more reactive intermediate; the defenses in C02 intercept the chain before the Fenton step."},{"type":"table","title":"Reactive oxygen species, sources, and properties","cols":["Species","Main sources","Reactivity and reach"],"rows":[["Superoxide","Mitochondrial complexes I and III, NADPH oxidase, xanthine oxidase","Moderate; charged, so it stays in its compartment"],["Hydrogen peroxide","Superoxide dismutase, peroxisomal oxidases","Weak oxidant but uncharged, diffuses widely, acts as a signal at low levels"],["Hydroxyl radical","Fenton reaction with Fe2+, radiolysis of water by ionizing radiation","Extreme; reacts within a few molecular diameters, no enzyme removes it"],["Singlet oxygen","UVA exciting photosensitizers in skin","Attacks double bonds in lipids, guanine, and aromatic amino acids"],["Peroxynitrite","Superoxide plus nitric oxide","Strong oxidant and nitrating agent; modifies tyrosine residues"]],"caption":"Only hydroxyl radical and singlet oxygen are intrinsically uncontainable; the others are handled enzymatically."},{"type":"h","text":"Three targets, three signature lesions"},{"type":"p","text":"Membrane lipids are vulnerable because the carbon between two double bonds in a polyunsaturated fatty acid holds its hydrogen weakly, so one abstraction starts the propagating chain shown below, the membrane loses its permeability barrier, and the aldehyde breakdown products carry the injury beyond the membrane."},{"type":"p","text":"Protein oxidation hits the side chains that carry function, so activity is lost and the altered protein is degraded by the proteasome. In DNA, 8-oxoguanine is the lesion to know: it can rotate and pair with adenine, so replication over an unrepaired lesion installs a G:C to T:A transversion unless glycosylase-initiated base excision repair removes it first. Mitochondrial DNA is hit hardest, since it sits beside the source, lacks histone protection, and has a narrower repair repertoire."},{"type":"table","title":"Three targets and their signature lesions","cols":["Target","Chemical change","Consequence"],"rows":[["Polyunsaturated membrane lipids","Hydrogen abstraction, then a propagating peroxyl radical chain","Leaky membrane; malondialdehyde and 4-hydroxynonenal adducts"],["Proteins","Thiol disulfides, methionine sulfoxide, carbonylation, cross-linking","Loss of function, fragmentation, lipofuscin accumulation"],["DNA","8-oxoguanine, thymine glycol, abasic sites, strand breaks","G:C to T:A transversion, replication arrest, instability"]],"caption":"Lipid damage spreads, protein damage is lost function, and DNA damage is heritable, which is why one oxidant produces three kinds of disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"Lipid peroxidation as a chain reaction","steps":["Radical abstracts hydrogen from the bis-allylic carbon of a polyunsaturated fatty acid","Lipid radical adds O2 to form a lipid peroxyl radical","Peroxyl radical abstracts hydrogen from the adjacent fatty acid: propagation","Chain continues until vitamin E, ubiquinol, or glutathione peroxidase 4 intervenes","Malondialdehyde and 4-hydroxynonenal form and cross-link proteins and DNA"],"caption":"Propagation is why a single initiating radical can oxidize hundreds of fatty acids, and why chain-breaking antioxidants matter more than their numbers suggest."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-06-01-C02","obj":"IN-6.2","title":"Antioxidant enzymes and why glutathione depends on G6PD","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Enzymatic defense is a relay, and its logic is that hydrogen peroxide must be destroyed before it meets ferrous iron. Superoxide dismutase accelerates the dismutation of two superoxide anions into hydrogen peroxide and oxygen, which looks counterproductive until you notice that it converts a species no downstream enzyme can handle into one that two enzymes can. Its three isoforms sit in three compartments, which matters because superoxide cannot cross a membrane to reach an enzyme elsewhere."},{"type":"p","text":"Hydrogen peroxide is then removed two ways. Catalase, a heme-containing tetramer abundant in peroxisomes and in erythrocyte cytosol, converts two molecules of hydrogen peroxide into two of water and one of oxygen; its affinity for substrate is low, so it dominates when peroxide concentrations are high. Glutathione peroxidase carries selenium as selenocysteine in its active site, spends two molecules of reduced glutathione to reduce hydrogen peroxide to water, and works efficiently at the low concentrations of routine metabolism. Its isoform glutathione peroxidase 4 is the only enzyme that reduces peroxidized fatty acids while they remain esterified in the membrane, so it is the dedicated brake on lipid peroxidation."},{"type":"table","title":"The antioxidant enzymes","cols":["Enzyme","Cofactor","Location","Reaction"],"rows":[["Superoxide dismutase 1","Copper and zinc","Cytosol, mitochondrial intermembrane space","2 superoxide plus 2 H+ to hydrogen peroxide plus O2"],["Superoxide dismutase 2","Manganese","Mitochondrial matrix","Same dismutation at the main site of generation"],["Superoxide dismutase 3","Copper and zinc","Extracellular space","Same dismutation outside the cell"],["Catalase","Heme iron","Peroxisomes, erythrocyte cytosol","2 hydrogen peroxide to 2 water plus O2"],["Glutathione peroxidase 1","Selenocysteine","Cytosol and mitochondria","Hydrogen peroxide plus 2 GSH to 2 water plus GSSG"],["Glutathione peroxidase 4","Selenocysteine","Membranes","Reduces esterified lipid hydroperoxides to alcohols"],["Glutathione reductase","FAD, uses NADPH","Cytosol","GSSG plus NADPH plus H+ to 2 GSH plus NADP+"]],"caption":"Note the nutritional dependencies: copper, zinc, manganese, iron as heme, selenium, and riboflavin as FAD."},{"type":"p","text":"Glutathione peroxidase consumes its reductant, so the system is only as good as its ability to regenerate reduced glutathione. Glutathione reductase does that using NADPH, which in most cells comes largely from the oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway, where glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) catalyzes the committed, rate-limiting step. G6PD is regulated exactly as this logic predicts: NADP+ activates it and NADPH inhibits it, so spending NADPH on glutathione recycling immediately increases flux."},{"type":"flow","title":"The glutathione antioxidant cycle and its NADPH supply","steps":["G6PD oxidizes glucose-6-phosphate, generating NADPH","Glutathione reductase uses NADPH to convert GSSG to 2 GSH","Selenium-dependent glutathione peroxidase uses 2 GSH to reduce hydrogen peroxide to water","GSH is oxidized back to GSSG as its thiols form a disulfide","Rising NADP+ relieves inhibition of G6PD and the cycle accelerates"],"caption":"Glutathione is recycled rather than consumed, so a small pool handles a large oxidant load as long as NADPH keeps arriving."},{"type":"p","text":"The mature erythrocyte shows what happens when this one supply line fails. With no mitochondria and no nucleus, it depends entirely on the pentose phosphate pathway and cannot synthesize new enzyme. In G6PD deficiency, an X-linked recessive disorder, baseline function is adequate but an oxidant challenge from infection, certain sulfonamides and antimalarials, or fava beans outstrips NADPH supply. Glutathione falls, hemoglobin oxidizes and denatures into inclusions seen as Heinz bodies on supravital staining, and splenic macrophages bite pieces from those cells, giving the bite cells of an acute episodic hemolysis. The network is also inducible: oxidation of reactive cysteines on Keap1 releases the transcription factor Nrf2, which raises transcription of glutamate-cysteine ligase, heme oxygenase 1, and glutathione S-transferases, so a mild exposure strengthens the response to the next one."},{"type":"example","title":"Following one molecule of hydrogen peroxide","text":"A keratinocyte generates one hydrogen peroxide molecule. Glutathione peroxidase reduces it to water, oxidizing two glutathione molecules into one GSSG, and glutathione reductase then spends one NADPH to restore both. That NADPH came from one turn of the oxidative pentose phosphate pathway, so one peroxide costs one NADPH and peroxide-handling capacity is set by glucose-6-phosphate flux. Remove selenium and the peroxidase cannot run; remove G6PD activity and the cycle stalls one step later. Either way the peroxide survives to meet ferrous iron, which is why a nutritional deficiency and an enzyme deficiency produce the same injury."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Superoxide dismutase produces hydrogen peroxide. Why does overexpressing it alone, without extra catalase or glutathione peroxidase, fail to protect a cell?","explain":"Dismutation only moves the problem one step down the chain. More superoxide dismutase means more hydrogen peroxide, and hydrogen peroxide is membrane permeable and feeds the Fenton reaction. Protection requires matched downstream capacity to destroy the peroxide before it meets ferrous iron.","check":"The oxygen reduction flow in C01 and the relay logic in the first paragraph of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-06-01-C03","obj":"IN-6.3","title":"Non-vitamin antioxidants of skin: glutathione, ubiquinol, carotenoids, and melanin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Enzymes handle defined substrates, so the cell also keeps a pool of small molecules that can absorb a radical wherever it appears, and the solubility of each one decides where it works."},{"type":"p","text":"Glutathione is the tripeptide gamma-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glycine, and its two unusual features both serve its function. The gamma-glutamyl linkage is not a normal peptide bond, so ordinary peptidases cannot degrade it, and the cysteine thiol is the reactive group that donates a hydrogen atom to a radical or an electron pair to glutathione peroxidase. Cytosolic glutathione is held at roughly a hundred to one in favor of the reduced form, so the ratio itself reads out redox state. Synthesis takes two ATP-dependent steps, of which glutamate-cysteine ligase is rate limiting, and cysteine availability usually sets the ceiling. Glutathione also conjugates electrophiles through glutathione S-transferases, which is how skin disposes of reactive products of lipid peroxidation."},{"type":"p","text":"Ubiquinol is the reduced form of coenzyme Q10, a benzoquinone ring with a ten-isoprene tail that anchors it in the hydrophobic core of membranes. There it donates hydrogen to lipid peroxyl radicals, breaking the propagation chain described in C01, and it regenerates alpha-tocopherol from the tocopheroxyl radical left when vitamin E does the same job. Carotenoids protect by different chemistry: their long conjugated polyene backbone accepts the excitation energy of singlet oxygen, dropping the oxygen to its ground state, and then releases that energy as heat and survives intact. Lycopene is acyclic and lacks the beta-ionone ring, so unlike beta-carotene it has no provitamin A activity. Carotenoids deposit in the stratum corneum and subcutaneous fat, and heavy intake yields a yellow-orange discoloration that spares the sclera, which distinguishes it from jaundice."},{"type":"table","title":"Non-vitamin antioxidants compared","cols":["Antioxidant","Solubility and site","Mechanism","How it is restored"],"rows":[["Reduced glutathione","Water soluble; cytosol, nucleus, mitochondria","Thiol donates hydrogen; reductant for glutathione peroxidase","Glutathione reductase using NADPH"],["Ubiquinol","Lipid soluble; inner mitochondrial and other membranes","Chain-breaking donor to lipid peroxyl radicals; regenerates vitamin E","Respiratory chain and cytosolic quinone reductases"],["Carotenoids such as lycopene","Lipid soluble; membranes, stratum corneum, fat","Physical quenching of singlet oxygen plus peroxyl radical trapping","Not consumed during quenching; dietary replacement otherwise"],["Melanin","Insoluble polymer in melanosomes","Broadband absorption and scattering of UV plus radical scavenging","New synthesis in melanocytes; not recycled"]],"caption":"Solubility predicts the compartment, and the compartment predicts which lesion each one prevents."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanin is the skin's structural sunscreen. Melanocytes in the basal layer synthesize it inside melanosomes from tyrosine by way of dopaquinone, the first steps being catalyzed by the copper-dependent enzyme tyrosinase, then transfer the granules to keratinocytes, where they form caps over the nuclei. The pathway branches at dopaquinone according to cysteine availability. With little cysteine, dopaquinone cyclizes toward the indole intermediates that polymerize into eumelanin, a brown-black broadband absorber that converts most of the ultraviolet energy it takes up into heat and scavenges radicals through its stable semiquinone groups. With abundant cysteine, dopaquinone instead forms cysteinyldopa and then pheomelanin, a red-yellow sulfur-containing polymer that absorbs ultraviolet poorly, is photolabile, and under UVA acts as a photosensitizer generating superoxide and hydrogen peroxide, so it adds to the oxidant burden rather than only protecting less. Loss-of-function variants in the melanocortin 1 receptor, which give red hair and fair skin, shift synthesis toward pheomelanin and also blunt receptor-dependent signaling that supports DNA repair, which is why that phenotype carries a higher melanoma risk."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two people receive the same ultraviolet dose. One has densely pigmented eumelanic skin, the other red hair and pheomelanic skin. Why is the difference in oxidative DNA damage larger than the difference in pigment quantity alone would suggest?","explain":"Eumelanin absorbs across the ultraviolet range and dissipates that energy as heat while quenching radicals, so it subtracts from the oxidant load. Pheomelanin absorbs poorly and, once excited, generates superoxide and hydrogen peroxide, so it adds to the load. The two pigments move the balance in opposite directions, and reduced melanocortin 1 receptor signaling further weakens repair.","check":"The dopaquinone branch point in this section and the ultraviolet damage mechanisms in C04."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-06-01-C04","obj":"IN-6.4","title":"UVB versus UVA injury and the mechanism of photoaging","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ultraviolet wavelength determines two things at once: how deep the photons travel and what absorbs them. Shorter wavelengths are absorbed more superficially but match the absorption spectrum of DNA, while longer wavelengths penetrate further and must work through intermediary pigments. UVC, below about 280 nm, is absorbed by stratospheric ozone and does not reach the skin from sunlight."},{"type":"p","text":"UVB is directly genotoxic. DNA bases absorb these photons themselves, and the excited state lets two adjacent pyrimidines on one strand join through their carbon 5 and carbon 6 positions as a cyclobutane pyrimidine dimer, or less often a pyrimidine-pyrimidone (6-4) photoproduct. Either lesion distorts the helix and blocks replication and transcription, and both are removed by nucleotide excision repair; inherited failure of that pathway produces xeroderma pigmentosum, with extreme photosensitivity and early skin cancer. Unrepaired dimers are copied with error, giving the signature C to T transitions at dipyrimidine sites and CC to TT tandem mutations found in TP53 in actinic keratosis and squamous cell carcinoma and in PTCH1 in basal cell carcinoma. Clinically UVB causes sunburn: keratinocytes damaged beyond repair undergo p53-dependent apoptosis and appear as sunburn cells with shrunken nuclei and eosinophilic cytoplasm, while prostaglandins, cytokines, and nitric oxide produce the erythema and edema that peak hours later. UVB also drives delayed tanning, since p53 raises keratinocyte proopiomelanocortin and the alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone released acts on melanocortin 1 receptors."},{"type":"p","text":"UVA is mostly indirectly genotoxic. It is absorbed by endogenous photosensitizers rather than by DNA, generating singlet oxygen and superoxide that leave oxidative lesions such as 8-oxoguanine and strand breaks, repaired by base excision repair instead. Because it reaches the dermis, UVA is the main source of injury to fibroblasts, collagen, and elastic fibers, and it produces immediate pigment darkening by oxidizing and redistributing existing melanin rather than making new pigment."},{"type":"table","title":"UVB and UVA compared","cols":["Feature","UVB","UVA"],"rows":[["Wavelength","About 280 to 315 nm","About 315 to 400 nm"],["Share of ground-level UV","Small minority","Large majority"],["Depth","Mostly epidermis","Mid and deep dermis"],["Chromophore","DNA bases absorb directly","Photosensitizers that transfer energy to oxygen"],["Signature DNA lesion","Cyclobutane pyrimidine dimer, (6-4) photoproduct","8-oxoguanine and strand breaks"],["Repair pathway","Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair"],["Acute effect","Sunburn with apoptotic keratinocytes","Immediate pigment darkening"],["Window glass","Blocked","Passes through"],["Chronic effect","Actinic keratosis, squamous and basal cell carcinoma","Photoaging; also contributes to carcinogenesis"]],"caption":"The mnemonic UVA for aging and UVB for burning is accurate as long as you remember both contribute to cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"Photoaging is a connective tissue disease caused by signaling, not by photons breaking collagen directly. As the figure below traces, reactive oxygen species generated in the dermis activate mitogen-activated protein kinase cascades and with them the transcription factor AP-1, which does two additive things: it induces matrix metalloproteinases, chiefly MMP-1 (interstitial collagenase), and it interferes with transforming growth factor beta signaling so that procollagen synthesis falls. Degradation rises while replacement falls, and each exposure leaves a small permanent deficit. Histologically, chronically exposed dermis shows solar elastosis: amorphous blue-gray degenerated collagen and tangled thickened elastic material in the upper dermis, with epidermal atrophy and flattening of the dermal-epidermal junction. Clinically this is coarse deep wrinkling, yellow leathery thickening, laxity, telangiectasia, and solar lentigines, which differs from intrinsic aging, where the dermis thins with fine wrinkles and no elastotic deposit."},{"type":"flow","title":"From ultraviolet exposure to photoaged dermis","steps":["UVA reaches the dermis and excites photosensitizers","Singlet oxygen and superoxide form in fibroblasts and matrix","MAP kinase cascades activate AP-1 (c-Jun and c-Fos)","AP-1 induces MMP-1, MMP-3, and MMP-9","AP-1 also blocks TGF-beta signaling, lowering procollagen synthesis","Collagen is degraded faster than replaced; solar elastosis accumulates","Coarse wrinkling, laxity, and leathery yellow skin"],"caption":"Because the mechanism is transcriptional rather than purely photochemical, damage continues after exposure ends."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-06-01-C05","obj":"IN-6.5","title":"The respiratory burst, myeloperoxidase, and chronic granulomatous disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Phagocytes turn the chemistry of oxidative injury into a weapon. When a neutrophil engulfs a microbe, the cytosolic components of NADPH oxidase assemble onto the membrane flavocytochrome already in the phagosome wall, which is the gp91phox and p22phox pair, and the completed enzyme transfers electrons from cytosolic NADPH through FAD and two heme groups to oxygen in the phagosome lumen, generating superoxide. Oxygen consumption rises sharply, which is why the event is called the respiratory burst, and the NADPH comes from the pentose phosphate pathway, the same source that serves glutathione. Because the oxidase moves electrons across a membrane without moving a counter-ion, the phagosome would quickly become too electronegative to continue; a voltage-gated proton channel compensates."},{"type":"p","text":"The products are then escalated. Superoxide dismutates to hydrogen peroxide, and myeloperoxidase, released from azurophilic granules and intensely green because of its heme, uses that peroxide with chloride to generate hypochlorous acid, the active ingredient of household bleach. Hypochlorous acid chlorinates and oxidizes microbial proteins and lipids indiscriminately and is the most potent microbicidal product the neutrophil makes; the same pigment gives pus its green color. The burst also activates granule proteases, so oxidative and non-oxidative killing act together."},{"type":"flow","title":"The neutrophil respiratory burst","steps":["Phagosome forms around the microbe and the oxidase subunits assemble","NADPH oxidase moves electrons from NADPH onto O2: superoxide in the lumen","Proton channel balances the charge so transfer continues","Superoxide dismutates to hydrogen peroxide, spontaneously and enzymatically","Myeloperoxidase combines hydrogen peroxide with chloride: hypochlorous acid","Oxidation and chlorination kill the microbe, aided by activated granule proteases"],"caption":"Each step is the same chemistry described in C01, confined to a compartment where the damage is intended."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic granulomatous disease is loss of this oxidase. About two thirds of cases are X-linked, from mutations in CYBB encoding the gp91phox membrane subunit, and the remainder are autosomal recessive, most commonly in p47phox. Phagocytosis and degranulation are intact, so microbes are ingested but survive inside the cell. Patients present in early childhood with recurrent suppurative infection of the skin, lymph nodes, lungs, liver, and bone, with abscess formation and suppurative adenitis. Granulomas form because ingested organisms and debris persist, so macrophages and T cells wall them off, and in the gastrointestinal and genitourinary tracts these masses can obstruct. The organisms that cause trouble are classically catalase-positive, as the comparison below lists. The usual teaching explanation is that catalase-negative organisms generate hydrogen peroxide of their own that the neutrophil's myeloperoxidase can borrow, whereas catalase-positive organisms destroy their own peroxide and leave the phagocyte with nothing. Diagnosis rests on showing an absent burst: dihydrorhodamine 123 flow cytometry is the quantitative test, and the older nitroblue tetrazolium slide test stays colorless instead of turning blue."},{"type":"p","text":"Myeloperoxidase deficiency shows how specific these lesions are. It is the commonest inherited neutrophil enzyme defect and is usually asymptomatic, because superoxide and hydrogen peroxide are still produced and other killing mechanisms compensate; disseminated candidal infection occurs mainly in patients who are also diabetic. The nitroblue tetrazolium test is normal, since the burst itself is intact and only the conversion to hypochlorous acid is lost. The dihydrorhodamine assay is the exception worth knowing, because oxidation of dihydrorhodamine 123 to fluorescent rhodamine is itself peroxidase dependent, so severe myeloperoxidase deficiency can lower the signal and mimic chronic granulomatous disease on a screening test even though the oxidase works."},{"type":"table","title":"Chronic granulomatous disease versus myeloperoxidase deficiency","cols":["Feature","Chronic granulomatous disease","Myeloperoxidase deficiency"],"rows":[["Defective step","NADPH oxidase; no superoxide generated","Conversion of hydrogen peroxide to hypochlorous acid"],["Inheritance","X-linked gp91phox in about two thirds; otherwise autosomal recessive","Autosomal recessive"],["Severity","Severe, presents in early childhood","Usually asymptomatic"],["Characteristic organisms","Catalase-positive: S. aureus, Serratia, Burkholderia cepacia, Nocardia, Aspergillus","Candida, mainly in diabetes"],["Granulomas","Typical, may obstruct GI or GU tract","Not a feature"],["Dihydrorhodamine and nitroblue tetrazolium","Both abnormal; nitroblue tetrazolium stays colorless","Nitroblue tetrazolium normal; dihydrorhodamine may be low because its oxidation needs peroxidase"]],"caption":"The burst is intact in myeloperoxidase deficiency, so the nitroblue tetrazolium test separates the two disorders while a low dihydrorhodamine result alone does not."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with chronic granulomatous disease clears a streptococcal pharyngitis without difficulty but develops a liver abscess from Staphylococcus aureus. What accounts for the difference?","explain":"Streptococci are catalase-negative and generate hydrogen peroxide that the neutrophil's myeloperoxidase can still convert to hypochlorous acid, so killing proceeds despite the missing oxidase. Staphylococcus aureus is catalase-positive and degrades that peroxide, leaving the phagocyte with no substrate for myeloperoxidase, so the organism survives intracellularly and is carried into tissue.","check":"The respiratory burst flow and the catalase explanation in this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"One chemistry explains the whole chapter, and the discipline changes only with the compartment it happens in. Biochemistry supplies the electrons, since the pentose phosphate pathway feeds NADPH to glutathione reductase in a keratinocyte and to NADPH oxidase in a neutrophil, and nutrition supplies the cofactors, since selenium, copper, zinc, manganese, iron, and riboflavin are each built into an antioxidant enzyme. Anatomy and histology decide what is exposed: ultraviolet wavelength sets the depth, so the epidermis takes the direct photochemical hit while the dermis takes the oxidative one, which is why the signature lesions of photodamage are keratinocyte mutations above and degraded collagen below. Immunology shows the same reactions used on purpose, and pathology shows the cost when containment fails."},{"type":"p","text":"The reasoning also has limits worth stating. Reactive oxygen species are not purely toxic; hydrogen peroxide at low concentration is a genuine second messenger, the respiratory burst is indispensable, and Nrf2 signaling means a mild oxidant exposure strengthens later defense, which is part of why large doses of single antioxidants have not performed as the simple model predicts. Carotenoids themselves can act as prooxidants at high concentration and high oxygen tension. The catalase explanation for the organism pattern in chronic granulomatous disease is the standard board answer but is incompletely supported experimentally, so hold it as the expected reasoning rather than settled mechanism, and remember that bacterial virulence and intracellular niche also shape which organisms cause disease."}],"summary":["One-electron reduction of oxygen gives superoxide, then hydrogen peroxide, then hydroxyl radical; full four-electron reduction gives water and releases nothing.","Hydrogen peroxide is the pivotal intermediate because it is uncharged and diffuses, and ferrous iron converts it to hydroxyl radical in the Fenton reaction.","Lipid peroxidation is a propagating chain on polyunsaturated fatty acids; 8-oxoguanine mispairs with adenine and yields a G:C to T:A transversion.","Superoxide dismutase is copper and zinc in the cytosol and manganese in mitochondria; catalase and selenium-dependent glutathione peroxidase then destroy the hydrogen peroxide.","Each peroxide reduced by glutathione peroxidase costs one NADPH, which in erythrocytes can only come from G6PD, so oxidant stress in G6PD deficiency gives Heinz bodies and bite cells.","Glutathione and ubiquinol are recycled electron donors in water and lipid compartments; carotenoids quench singlet oxygen physically, and lycopene has no provitamin A activity.","Eumelanin absorbs broadly and quenches radicals, while pheomelanin absorbs poorly and generates superoxide under UVA, so it adds to the oxidant load.","UVB is absorbed by DNA directly and forms cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers repaired by nucleotide excision repair; UVA works through photosensitizers and reaches the dermis.","Photoaging is transcriptional: reactive oxygen species activate AP-1, which raises MMP-1 and suppresses procollagen synthesis, leaving solar elastosis in the upper dermis.","Chronic granulomatous disease is loss of NADPH oxidase with abscesses and granulomas from catalase-positive organisms and an abnormal dihydrorhodamine test, while myeloperoxidase deficiency leaves the burst and those tests normal."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations, section on free radical mediated cell injury"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, section on actinic damage and photoaging"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Pentose Phosphate Pathway and NADPH"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Congenital and Acquired Immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Oxidative stress and cellular antioxidant defenses"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin, melanocytes and melanin synthesis"}]},"IN-7":{"unit":"IN-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01","title":"Skin Genetics","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Skin is the organ where genetics can be seen. Every division of the basal layer copies DNA that ultraviolet light has already damaged, so the genes that govern the cell cycle, DNA repair, and growth signaling determine which keratinocytes survive and which become tumors. Inherited defects in the same pathways, and in the structural and pigment genes that build the barrier, produce disorders recognizable on the skin surface. This chapter covers the G1 to S checkpoint, nucleotide excision repair, the driver mutations behind basal cell carcinoma and melanoma, pedigree reasoning and recurrence risk, the neurocutaneous syndromes, the barrier genes of ichthyosis, and the melanocortin switch that sets pigment type.","prerequisites":["Epidermal layers, keratinocyte maturation, and the keratohyalin granules of the granular layer (IN-1)","Melanocyte biology and the tyrosinase pathway from tyrosine to eumelanin (IN-5)","Ultraviolet photochemistry and reactive oxygen species in skin (IN-6)","Mendelian inheritance patterns, Punnett squares, and the two-hit model of tumor suppressor loss","Receptor signaling through Gs, cAMP, protein kinase A, and the RAS to MAPK cascade"],"connections":[{"to":"Cell adaptation, injury, and neoplasia (IN-8)","how":"The checkpoint and repair genes taught here are the molecular basis of the dysplasia to carcinoma sequence described there as tissue change."},{"to":"Malignant and premalignant skin lesions (IN-13-F, IN-13-G)","how":"Each gene in this chapter predicts a lesion's histology, distribution on sun-exposed skin, and behavior, which is how board questions connect a mutation to a biopsy description."},{"to":"Neurology and neuroembryology","how":"NF1 and tuberous sclerosis affect neural crest and neuroepithelial derivatives together, so the same mutation yields skin macules, nerve sheath tumors, cortical tubers, and seizures."},{"to":"Endocrine and reproductive physiology","how":"Placental steroid sulfatase is the same enzyme missing in X-linked ichthyosis, so the deficiency lowers maternal estriol during the pregnancy that carries an affected male."},{"to":"Immunology and allergy","how":"Filaggrin loss leaves a leaky barrier that admits allergens, which starts the sensitization underlying atopic dermatitis and the rest of the atopic march."}],"terms":[{"term":"Restriction point","def":"The point late in G1 beyond which a cell completes the division cycle without further growth factor stimulation."},{"term":"Cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK)","def":"A kinase that is inactive alone and becomes active when bound by its cyclin partner, then phosphorylates the substrates that move the cycle forward."},{"term":"Tumor suppressor gene","def":"A gene whose normal product restrains proliferation or preserves genomic integrity, so both copies must be lost before function fails."},{"term":"Oncogene","def":"A mutated gene whose product signals growth constitutively, so a single gain-of-function allele is enough to alter cell behavior."},{"term":"Pyrimidine dimer","def":"A covalent link between two adjacent pyrimidines on the same DNA strand formed directly by ultraviolet B photons, most often a cyclobutane ring between two thymines."},{"term":"UV-signature mutation","def":"A C to T change, or a tandem CC to TT change, at a site of two adjacent pyrimidines, the fingerprint of an unrepaired ultraviolet lesion."},{"term":"Nucleotide excision repair","def":"The repair pathway that recognizes bulky helix-distorting lesions, excises a short oligonucleotide containing the damage, and resynthesizes the gap."},{"term":"Oncogene-induced senescence","def":"Permanent growth arrest triggered when a strong growth signal activates checkpoint proteins, the reason a BRAF-mutant nevus stops growing."},{"term":"Hamartoma","def":"A benign overgrowth of tissue native to the site, disorganized in architecture but not invasive."},{"term":"Recurrence risk","def":"The probability that a specified future pregnancy of a given couple produces an affected child."},{"term":"Natural moisturizing factor","def":"The mixture of amino acids and their derivatives, including urocanic acid and pyrrolidone carboxylic acid, produced by filaggrin breakdown, which holds water in the stratum corneum."},{"term":"Eumelanin and pheomelanin","def":"The two melanin types: eumelanin is brown to black and absorbs ultraviolet light well, while pheomelanin is red to yellow, contains cysteine, and absorbs poorly."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01-C01","obj":"IN-7.1","title":"The G1 to S checkpoint and UV-signature TP53 mutation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A keratinocyte decides whether to divide late in G1, at the restriction point. Growth factor signaling induces cyclin D, which binds CDK4 and CDK6 and phosphorylates the retinoblastoma protein (Rb). Unphosphorylated Rb holds the transcription factor E2F inactive, so the cell stays in G1. Partial phosphorylation releases enough E2F to transcribe cyclin E, and cyclin E with CDK2 completes the job. Because E2F drives its own activating cyclin, commitment to S phase becomes irreversible."},{"type":"p","text":"Two brakes sit on this switch. The CDK inhibitor p16, encoded by CDKN2A, binds CDK4 and CDK6 and keeps Rb unphosphorylated. The second brake responds to damage: ultraviolet lesions stall replication forks and transcription, activating chiefly the kinase ATR, with ATM contributing when breaks form, and these kinases phosphorylate p53 and release it from MDM2-mediated degradation. Accumulated p53 transcribes CDKN1A, whose product p21 inhibits cyclin-CDK complexes broadly and arrests the cell in G1 long enough for repair. If damage is excessive, p53 instead transcribes BAX and PUMA and the cell dies by apoptosis, which is what the apoptotic sunburn cells of an acute burn represent."},{"type":"table","title":"Control of the G1 to S checkpoint","cols":["Protein","Normal action","Effect of loss or gain"],"rows":[["Cyclin D with CDK4 and CDK6","Phosphorylates Rb, releasing E2F","Gain speeds entry into S phase"],["Rb","Sequesters E2F in G1","Loss removes the restriction point"],["p16 (CDKN2A)","Inhibits CDK4 and CDK6","Loss leaves the brake off"],["p53 (TP53)","Induces p21 or apoptosis after damage","Loss lets damaged cells replicate and survive"],["p21 (CDKN1A)","Inhibits cyclin-CDK complexes","Loss shortens the time for repair"]],"caption":"The cyclins push and p16, p53, and p21 restrain; skin tumors arise when a push is stuck on or a restraint is lost."},{"type":"p","text":"Ultraviolet B photons, roughly 290 to 320 nm, are absorbed directly by DNA and fuse adjacent pyrimidines into cyclobutane dimers and related photoproducts. Cytosine inside such a dimer deaminates far faster than normal, becoming uracil or, if it was methylated, thymine. A polymerase reading the altered template inserts adenine, so replication fixes the change as a C to T substitution, or CC to TT when both members of the dimer were cytosines. That is the UV signature, and its presence in a tumor identifies sunlight as the mutagen. Ultraviolet A penetrates deeper but damages mainly through reactive oxygen species."},{"type":"p","text":"TP53 is among the earliest genes mutated in sun-exposed epidermis, and UV-signature mutations in it appear in clinically normal sun-damaged skin, in actinic keratosis, and in cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. Without p53, a keratinocyte carrying unrepaired damage neither arrests nor dies, so it survives further sun exposure and accumulates mutations while its neighbors are removed. The surviving clone expands laterally into a field of atypia, seen clinically as rough scaly patches on the face, scalp, ears, and forearms and histologically as atypia confined to the lower epidermis. Further mutations, often in CDKN2A, NOTCH1, or RAS, make the atypia full thickness and then invasive."},{"type":"flow","title":"From ultraviolet photon to squamous cell carcinoma","steps":["UVB fuses adjacent pyrimidines into a cyclobutane dimer","Cytosine in the dimer deaminates before repair","Replication fixes the change as a C to T mutation","TP53 is inactivated; arrest and apoptosis are lost","The mutant keratinocyte clone survives further sun and expands","Field atypia appears as actinic keratosis","Added mutations give full-thickness atypia, then invasion"],"caption":"Each step needs the previous one, which is why the sequence takes years of cumulative exposure rather than one burn."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two keratinocytes receive identical ultraviolet damage, one with intact TP53 and one without. Why does only the second found a tumor clone?","explain":"The first arrests in G1 through p21 and repairs the lesion, or dies by apoptosis, so its damaged genome never reaches a daughter cell. The second replicates past the lesion, survives, and passes every new mutation on.","check":"The checkpoint table and the explanation of the UV signature above it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01-C02","obj":"IN-7.2","title":"Nucleotide excision repair and xeroderma pigmentosum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A pyrimidine dimer is not a wrong base but a physical distortion of the helix, so neither mismatch repair nor base excision repair can handle it. Nucleotide excision repair is the pathway for bulky helix-distorting lesions. It works in two modes that differ only in how the lesion is found: global genome repair, in which the XPC complex surveys the whole genome, and transcription-coupled repair, in which RNA polymerase II stalling on the transcribed strand recruits the CSA and CSB proteins. Both converge on the same machinery."},{"type":"flow","title":"Steps of nucleotide excision repair","steps":["XPC complex or stalled RNA polymerase II detects the lesion","TFIIH, with the XPB and XPD helicases, opens the duplex","XPA verifies the damage and stabilizes the opened bubble","XPG cuts 3 prime to the lesion and XPF with ERCC1 cuts 5 prime","A 25 to 30 base oligonucleotide carrying the lesion is released","DNA polymerase fills the gap using the intact strand as template","DNA ligase seals the remaining nick"],"caption":"Repair is error free because the undamaged complementary strand supplies the template; the protein names correspond to the XP complementation groups."},{"type":"p","text":"Xeroderma pigmentosum is autosomal recessive loss of one of these components, so carriers are clinically normal and affected children inherit a mutant allele from each parent. The consequence is cumulative. Every sun exposure leaves dimers that persist in epidermal cells instead of being excised, and when replication forks reach them the cell must use error-prone translesion polymerases, so UV-signature mutations accumulate far faster than normal. Because the mutation rate rises in every exposed cell type, the disease produces basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and melanoma together rather than one tumor type."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical picture follows the mechanism in sequence. Infants burn severely or blister after minimal sun. Freckle-like pigmented macules appear on exposed skin in the first years of life, abnormal at that age and density, alternating with hypopigmented atrophic areas and telangiectasia, which gives the poikilodermatous appearance that names the disorder. Dry skin follows chronic epidermal damage. Skin cancers appear in childhood, commonly before age 10, roughly fifty years earlier than in the general population. The cornea and conjunctiva are affected because they are exposed and unpigmented, giving photophobia, keratitis, and scarring. A subset of complementation groups, including XPA and XPD, also develop progressive neurologic degeneration with hearing loss, ataxia, and cognitive decline, attributed to failure of transcription-coupled repair of oxidative lesions in neurons that cannot be replaced."},{"type":"table","title":"Repair pathways and the diseases that follow their loss","cols":["Pathway","Lesion repaired","Representative disease"],"rows":[["Nucleotide excision repair","Bulky distortions: pyrimidine dimers, chemical adducts","Xeroderma pigmentosum; Cockayne syndrome and trichothiodystrophy from other defects in the same proteins"],["Base excision repair","Single damaged or deaminated bases, oxidized guanine","MUTYH-associated polyposis"],["Mismatch repair","Base mispairs and small loops left by replication","Lynch syndrome"],["Homologous recombination","Double-strand breaks","Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer (BRCA1, BRCA2)"],["Nonhomologous end joining","Double-strand breaks, including those in antigen receptor gene rearrangement","DNA ligase IV and Artemis deficiency, with radiosensitive immunodeficiency; ataxia-telangiectasia instead reflects defective ATM damage signaling"]],"caption":"Matching the lesion to its pathway predicts the tissue and the tumor: only nucleotide excision repair removes ultraviolet dimers, so only its loss gives childhood skin cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"Two contrasts clarify the mechanism. In albinism melanin synthesis fails but repair is intact, so burning is severe and squamous cell carcinoma becomes common in sun-exposed skin, yet the tumors appear in adulthood rather than childhood. In xeroderma pigmentosum pigmentation is present, even increased, but the damage that does occur is not excised, which is far more mutagenic. The variant form of the disease is instructive in the other direction: nucleotide excision repair is normal and the defect lies in polymerase eta, encoded by POLH, the translesion polymerase that copies past a dimer with relatively few errors, so the phenotype is milder and later in onset."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01-C03","obj":"IN-7.3","title":"Driver mutations in basal cell carcinoma, nevi, and melanoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each common skin tumor keeps proliferating because one signaling pathway is stuck in the on position. Basal cell carcinoma depends on the hedgehog pathway, in which the receptor PTCH1 inhibits the transducer Smoothened until hedgehog ligand relieves that inhibition. When both PTCH1 alleles are lost, Smoothened signals continuously, GLI transcription factors enter the nucleus, and basal keratinocytes proliferate without a ligand. A minority of tumors instead carry activating Smoothened mutations, which gives the same result and shows that the pathway, not the particular gene, is what matters."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hedgehog signaling in basal cell carcinoma","steps":["Both PTCH1 alleles are lost, somatically or germline plus one hit","Smoothened is released from PTCH1 inhibition","Suppressor of fused no longer holds GLI in the cytoplasm","GLI enters the nucleus and transcribes proliferation genes","Basal cells proliferate as nests with peripheral palisading"],"caption":"Loss of an inhibitory receptor, not gain of a ligand, is what switches the pathway on."},{"type":"p","text":"Gorlin syndrome, also called nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome, is a germline PTCH1 mutation inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Because every cell already carries one mutant allele, a single somatic hit completes the loss, so basal cell carcinomas appear by the second and third decades, in large numbers, and on skin that is not heavily sun exposed. The syndrome also brings odontogenic keratocysts of the jaw, palmar and plantar pits, calcification of the falx cerebri, macrocephaly, rib and vertebral anomalies, and a raised risk of medulloblastoma, which reflects the role of hedgehog signaling in cerebellar development."},{"type":"p","text":"Melanocytic lesions follow the RAS to MAPK cascade. The BRAF V600E substitution locks the kinase in its active conformation and drives MEK and ERK without upstream input. It is present in most acquired nevi, which makes an important point: a single activating oncogene is not sufficient for cancer. Strong continuous ERK signaling induces p16 and triggers oncogene-induced senescence, so the melanocytes stop dividing and the lesion stays a small stable nevus for decades. Melanoma requires escape from that arrest, most often through CDKN2A loss, then TERT promoter mutations that restore telomerase, with PTEN or TP53 loss in advanced lesions. NRAS mutations activate the same cascade one step upstream, accounting for a smaller share of melanomas and for many large congenital nevi, and the two are essentially mutually exclusive in one tumor because either alone saturates the pathway."},{"type":"table","title":"Drivers, pathways, and the tumors they produce","cols":["Gene","Pathway effect","Tumor association","Class"],"rows":[["PTCH1","Loss frees Smoothened; GLI stays on","Basal cell carcinoma; Gorlin syndrome if germline","Suppressor, two hits"],["TP53","Loss removes G1 arrest and apoptosis","Actinic keratosis, squamous cell carcinoma","Suppressor, two hits"],["BRAF V600E","Constitutive MEK and ERK activity","Most acquired nevi; about half of melanomas","Oncogene, one hit"],["NRAS","GTP-locked RAS, same cascade","A minority of melanomas; large congenital nevi","Oncogene, one hit"],["CDKN2A (p16)","Loss releases CDK4 and CDK6 from restraint","Familial melanoma if germline; nevus progression if somatic","Suppressor, two hits"],["KIT","Activated receptor tyrosine kinase","Acral and mucosal melanoma","Oncogene, one hit"]],"caption":"Oncogenes act as a stuck accelerator and need one allele; tumor suppressors are brakes and need both alleles lost, which a germline mutation half completes in every cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Germline CDKN2A mutation is the commonest cause of familial melanoma. The locus encodes two proteins from overlapping reading frames: p16, which inhibits CDK4 and CDK6, and p14ARF, which stabilizes p53 by inhibiting MDM2, so one mutation can disable both the Rb arm and the p53 arm of checkpoint control. Carriers show many clinically atypical nevi, develop melanoma young and often at more than one site, and carry a raised risk of pancreatic adenocarcinoma, which is why melanoma plus pancreatic cancer in a family suggests this syndrome. Rare families instead carry an activating CDK4 mutation at the site where p16 would bind, reaching the same phenotype from the opposite direction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01-C04","obj":"IN-7.4","title":"Reading pedigrees of skin disorders and calculating recurrence risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A pedigree is read by asking how many working copies of the gene a person needs. In an autosomal dominant disorder one mutant allele is enough, so the trait appears in every generation, affects both sexes about equally, and can pass from father to son. The risk to each child of an affected heterozygote is one in two, independent of earlier outcomes. NF1, tuberous sclerosis, and epidermolysis bullosa simplex, which follows dominant negative mutations in keratin 5 or keratin 14, all behave this way."},{"type":"p","text":"Two features of dominant disorders complicate the pedigree. A high rate of new mutation means the first affected person often has unaffected parents, so about half of NF1 cases and most tuberous sclerosis cases are sporadic, which does not change the 50 percent risk to that person's own children. Variable expressivity means severity differs widely within one family, so a parent may be recognized as affected only on careful examination."},{"type":"p","text":"In an autosomal recessive disorder both alleles must be mutant, as in oculocutaneous albinism and xeroderma pigmentosum. Carriers are unaffected, so the trait skips generations and appears in siblings rather than parents, and consanguinity raises the chance that both parents carry the same rare allele. One derived figure is worth memorizing: an unaffected sibling of an affected child cannot be homozygous mutant, so two of the three remaining genotypes are carrier, giving a risk of two in three."},{"type":"p","text":"X-linked recessive disorders such as X-linked ichthyosis affect males almost exclusively, because a male has one X chromosome and no second copy to compensate. There is no male to male transmission, since an affected father gives his sons a Y, but he gives his X to every daughter, so all his daughters are obligate carriers. Affected males therefore appear in a pattern that travels through unaffected women, classically as affected maternal uncles."},{"type":"table","title":"Inheritance patterns and recurrence risk in skin disease","cols":["Pattern","Skin examples","Parental genotypes","Risk for each pregnancy"],"rows":[["Autosomal dominant","NF1, tuberous sclerosis, epidermolysis bullosa simplex, Gorlin syndrome","One affected heterozygote, one unaffected","50 percent affected, either sex"],["Autosomal recessive","Albinism, xeroderma pigmentosum","Both unaffected carriers","25 percent affected, 50 percent carriers"],["Autosomal recessive","Albinism, xeroderma pigmentosum","One affected, one carrier","50 percent affected, 50 percent carriers"],["X-linked recessive","X-linked ichthyosis","Carrier mother, unaffected father","Half of sons affected, half of daughters carriers"],["X-linked recessive","X-linked ichthyosis","Affected father, noncarrier mother","No affected children; all daughters carriers"]],"caption":"The pattern names the genotypes, and the genotypes give the risk; the risk is the same for every pregnancy regardless of earlier children."},{"type":"example","title":"Carrier risk for the healthy sibling of a child with xeroderma pigmentosum","text":"A couple has a son with xeroderma pigmentosum and a healthy daughter. Both parents must be carriers, so each child had a 1 in 4 chance of being affected, 1 in 2 of being a carrier, and 1 in 4 of inheriting two normal alleles. Being unaffected eliminates the first outcome, leaving three equally likely genotypes of which two are carrier, so the daughter's carrier risk is 2 in 3. If her partner comes from a population in which 1 person in 300 carries a mutant allele in the same gene, the chance of an affected child is 2 divided by 3, times 1 divided by 300, times 1 divided by 4, or about 1 in 1,800."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with X-linked ichthyosis and an unrelated woman have two sons and two daughters. How many are affected, and how many can pass the condition on?","explain":"None of the four is affected. Each son received his father's Y and his mother's normal X, so he cannot transmit the allele. Each daughter received her father's mutant X and her mother's normal X, so both are unaffected obligate carriers who will pass the allele to half of their children.","check":"The X-linked rows of the recurrence risk table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01-C05","obj":"IN-7.5","title":"Neurofibromatosis type 1 and tuberous sclerosis: losing a brake on growth signaling","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both neurocutaneous syndromes are autosomal dominant tumor suppressor disorders in which the lost protein normally switches a growth pathway off. Each cell inherits one mutant allele, and a somatic second hit in a susceptible cell releases the pathway locally, which is why lesions are multiple, scattered, and appear progressively with age rather than being present everywhere at birth."},{"type":"p","text":"NF1 lies on chromosome 17 and encodes neurofibromin, a GTPase-activating protein for RAS. RAS is active when bound to GTP and inactive after hydrolysis to GDP, and intrinsic hydrolysis is slow, so neurofibromin is what returns RAS to the off state. Losing it leaves RAS-GTP elevated and the MAPK and mTOR pathways tonically stimulated in neural crest derived cells. The result is proliferation of Schwann cells, fibroblasts, and melanocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Cutaneous neurofibromas are soft, skin colored, and sessile or pedunculated, and they appear and increase around puberty and in pregnancy; plexiform neurofibromas are larger, involve nerve trunks, are present early, and carry a risk of transformation into malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor. Pigmented signs are the earliest: six or more café-au-lait macules, flat and uniformly light brown, exceeding 5 mm before puberty or 15 mm after it, and freckling in the axilla and groin, where sun exposure cannot explain it. Lisch nodules of the iris, optic pathway glioma, sphenoid wing dysplasia, tibial pseudarthrosis, and learning disability complete the picture, and hypertension should raise the question of pheochromocytoma or renal artery stenosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why neurofibromin loss produces tumors","steps":["A growth factor loads RAS with GTP","Neurofibromin normally accelerates GTP hydrolysis","Both NF1 alleles are lost in a Schwann cell","RAS-GTP persists; MAPK and mTOR signaling continue","The cell proliferates with its fibroblasts and mast cells","A neurofibroma forms along the nerve"],"caption":"The mutation does not create a signal; it prevents an existing signal from being turned off."},{"type":"p","text":"Tuberous sclerosis arises from mutation in TSC1, encoding hamartin, or TSC2, encoding tuberin. The two proteins form a complex in which tuberin acts as a GTPase-activating protein for Rheb, the small GTPase that activates mTORC1, and hamartin stabilizes tuberin. Loss of either leaves Rheb GTP-bound and mTORC1 constitutively active, so cells increase protein synthesis, enlarge, and proliferate regardless of nutrient and growth factor status. Because mTORC1 regulates cell size as well as number, the lesions are hamartomas of disorganized native tissue rather than carcinomas."},{"type":"p","text":"Cutaneous findings in tuberous sclerosis follow a predictable order: hypomelanotic ash-leaf macules are present at birth or in infancy and are easiest to see under a Wood lamp, especially in fair skin; facial angiofibromas appear over the nose and cheeks in childhood; a shagreen patch of thickened leathery skin develops over the lower back; and periungual fibromas emerge around the nails near adolescence. Internally there are cortical tubers with seizures that often begin as infantile spasms, subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, cardiac rhabdomyoma that may be detected prenatally and often regresses, renal angiomyolipoma, and pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis in women."},{"type":"table","title":"NF1 compared with tuberous sclerosis","cols":["Feature","Neurofibromatosis type 1","Tuberous sclerosis"],"rows":[["Gene and product","NF1 on chromosome 17; neurofibromin","TSC1 hamartin, TSC2 tuberin"],["Molecular role","GTPase-activating protein that turns RAS off","Complex that turns Rheb and therefore mTORC1 off"],["Pigment lesion","Café-au-lait macules and axillary freckling: more melanin","Ash-leaf macules: less melanin"],["Other skin lesions","Cutaneous and plexiform neurofibromas","Facial angiofibromas, shagreen patch, periungual fibromas"],["Eye finding","Lisch nodules of the iris; optic glioma","Retinal astrocytic hamartoma"],["Major systemic lesions","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor, pheochromocytoma, scoliosis","Cortical tubers and seizures, cardiac rhabdomyoma, renal angiomyolipoma"]],"caption":"Both are dominant with a somatic second hit, so the pigment finding distinguishes them at the bedside: extra pigment in NF1, missing pigment in tuberous sclerosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01-C06","obj":"IN-7.6","title":"Barrier genes: filaggrin and steroid sulfatase","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The stratum corneum works because each corneocyte is internally rigid and because the cells stay cemented together until enzymes at the surface release them. Two genetic defects break these requirements separately, and both present as ichthyosis, meaning persistent visible scale."},{"type":"p","text":"Filaggrin, encoded by FLG on chromosome 1, is made as the large precursor profilaggrin stored in the keratohyalin granules that define the granular layer. During terminal differentiation profilaggrin is dephosphorylated and cleaved into filaggrin monomers that aggregate keratin filaments into tight bundles, flattening the cell into a mechanically strong corneocyte. Filaggrin is then degraded further into free amino acids and their derivatives, including pyrrolidone carboxylic acid and urocanic acid, which make up natural moisturizing factor. This mixture is hygroscopic, so it holds water in the stratum corneum, and it is acidic, so it maintains the surface pH at which the desquamation enzymes and antimicrobial peptides work properly."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss-of-function FLG mutations therefore cause three problems at once: weaker corneocytes, a drier stratum corneum, and a less acidic surface. Ichthyosis vulgaris is the result, with fine white scale most obvious on the extensor legs, sparing of the moist flexures, palmar hyperlinearity, keratosis pilaris, and a reduced or absent granular layer on biopsy. Inheritance is semidominant, since heterozygotes have mild disease and homozygotes more severe disease, so the pedigree can look dominant with incomplete penetrance. The same alleles are the strongest known genetic risk factor for atopic dermatitis, because a leaky barrier lets water escape and lets allergens, haptens, and Staphylococcus aureus products reach antigen presenting cells. That exposure drives type 2 helper T cell sensitization, and the resulting inflammation suppresses filaggrin further, making atopic dermatitis a self-reinforcing barrier and immune problem rather than a purely immune one."},{"type":"flow","title":"Filaggrin loss to atopic sensitization","steps":["Loss-of-function FLG alleles reduce filaggrin and natural moisturizing factor","Corneocytes weaken; the stratum corneum dries and its pH rises","Transepidermal water loss rises and the barrier becomes permeable","Allergens and microbial products reach Langerhans cells","Type 2 helper T cell responses and IgE develop","Interleukin 4 and 13 further suppress filaggrin, worsening the barrier"],"caption":"A structural gene defect initiates an immune disease, and the immune response then deepens the structural defect."},{"type":"p","text":"X-linked ichthyosis is a defect of shedding rather than of corneocyte structure. Steroid sulfatase, encoded by STS on the short arm of the X chromosome, removes the sulfate group from cholesterol sulfate in the intercellular lipid of the stratum corneum. Cholesterol sulfate helps hold corneocytes together, so its desulfation is a necessary signal for orderly desquamation. Without the enzyme it accumulates, corneocytes stay adherent, and retention hyperkeratosis builds up as large, dark brown, polygonal adherent scale on the trunk, neck, and extensor limbs, usually within the first months of life in a male."},{"type":"p","text":"Asymptomatic comma-shaped corneal opacities occur in affected males and carrier mothers, and cryptorchidism is more common. The placenta uses the same enzyme to desulfate fetal dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate on the route to estriol, so maternal serum estriol is low in a pregnancy carrying an affected fetus, and labor may be delayed or prolonged. A larger contiguous deletion of neighboring genes can add Kallmann syndrome, chondrodysplasia punctata, or intellectual disability."},{"type":"table","title":"Ichthyosis vulgaris compared with X-linked ichthyosis","cols":["Feature","Ichthyosis vulgaris (FLG)","X-linked ichthyosis (STS)"],"rows":[["Defect","Reduced filaggrin and natural moisturizing factor","Steroid sulfatase deficiency with cholesterol sulfate retention"],["Mechanism of scale","Weak dry corneocytes and impaired barrier","Failure of corneocytes to separate and shed"],["Inheritance","Semidominant; both sexes","X-linked recessive; males affected"],["Scale","Fine and white, extensor legs, flexures spared","Large, dark brown, polygonal and adherent; flexures may be involved"],["Associated findings","Palmar hyperlinearity, keratosis pilaris, atopic dermatitis and asthma","Corneal opacities, cryptorchidism, low maternal estriol in the pregnancy"],["Granular layer","Reduced or absent","Normal or slightly thickened"]],"caption":"Fine white scale that spares the flexures in either sex points to filaggrin; large dark adherent scale in a male points to steroid sulfatase."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-07-01-C07","obj":"IN-7.7","title":"MC1R and the eumelanin to pheomelanin switch","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Melanocytes make two chemically different pigments from one starting material. Tyrosinase oxidizes tyrosine to dopaquinone in both cases, and what follows depends on the availability of cysteine. If cysteine or glutathione adds to dopaquinone, the product is cysteinyldopa and the pathway runs to pheomelanin, a red to yellow sulfur containing polymer. If it does not, dopaquinone cyclizes and polymerizes to eumelanin, which absorbs ultraviolet light broadly and quenches radicals. The switch is set by signaling, not by a separate enzyme."},{"type":"p","text":"The melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) on the melanocyte surface is a G protein coupled receptor that signals through Gs. Its ligand, alpha melanocyte stimulating hormone, is produced locally by keratinocytes from proopiomelanocortin, and ultraviolet exposure increases that production through p53, which links the checkpoint pathway of the first section to tanning. Receptor occupancy raises cAMP, protein kinase A phosphorylates CREB, and CREB induces the transcription factor MITF, the master regulator of the melanocyte. MITF raises tyrosinase, tyrosinase-related protein 1, and dopachrome tautomerase, and that higher enzyme activity consumes dopaquinone quickly enough to outcompete cysteine addition, so synthesis shifts toward eumelanin. Agouti signaling protein opposes alpha MSH at the receptor, and when MC1R signaling is low, cAMP falls, MITF-driven enzyme output drops, and the pathway drifts toward pheomelanin."},{"type":"flow","title":"The melanocortin pigment switch","steps":["Ultraviolet damage stabilizes p53 in keratinocytes","Keratinocytes transcribe proopiomelanocortin and release alpha MSH","Alpha MSH binds MC1R; Gs raises cAMP","Protein kinase A phosphorylates CREB, which induces MITF","MITF raises tyrosinase and related enzymes","Dopaquinone is consumed as eumelanin and transferred to keratinocytes","Eumelanin caps over nuclei and absorbs further ultraviolet light"],"caption":"A keratinocyte damage signal recruits a neighboring cell type to supply protection, which is why tanning is delayed by days."},{"type":"p","text":"Common MC1R loss-of-function variants reduce receptor signaling. Homozygotes and compound heterozygotes for the strong variants show the red hair phenotype: red or auburn hair, fair skin that burns rather than tans, dense freckling, and high lifetime melanoma and keratinocyte cancer risk. Three mechanisms combine. Pheomelanin absorbs ultraviolet light poorly, so more photons reach keratinocyte and melanocyte DNA. Pheomelanin synthesis also consumes cysteine and glutathione, and the pigment itself generates reactive oxygen species under ultraviolet light, so the melanocyte suffers oxidative damage even without direct absorption. Finally, MC1R signaling normally supports nucleotide excision repair and antioxidant defense, so a weak receptor leaves cells both more exposed and less able to repair. The last point is why risk in variant carriers exceeds what skin color alone predicts, and why the variants add risk even in people who tan reasonably well."},{"type":"example","title":"Distinguishing three causes of a pale, poorly tanning patient","text":"Consider three patients who all burn easily. In oculocutaneous albinism type 1 tyrosinase itself is defective, so melanocytes are present in normal numbers but make almost no pigment of either type, and nystagmus, iris transillumination, and foveal hypoplasia follow from absent pigment in the developing eye. In the MC1R variant red hair phenotype the enzymes work and the switch is set wrong, so pigment is made but it is pheomelanin, giving red hair and freckles with normal vision. In vitiligo the genes are normal and melanocytes have been destroyed by autoimmune attack, so depigmentation is patchy with sharp borders."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"This chapter is one mechanism applied to many diseases. A growth pathway must be switched on when tissue is needed and off otherwise, and DNA must be copied faithfully. Oncogenes such as BRAF, NRAS, and KIT jam the switch on, so a single allele changes the cell, while tumor suppressors such as TP53, PTCH1, CDKN2A, NF1, TSC1, and TSC2 are the off switches, so both alleles must fail and a germline mutation simply means the first failure is already present in every cell. That accounting explains the clinical pattern of each syndrome: early onset, multiple lesions, and bilateral or scattered distribution when the mutation is inherited, and single late lesions on the most sun-exposed skin when it is somatic. The repair genes sit upstream of all of it, since a repair defect raises the rate at which both oncogene and tumor suppressor mutations appear, which is why xeroderma pigmentosum produces every ultraviolet related skin cancer at once."},{"type":"p","text":"The chapter also shows how far one gene product reaches across disciplines. Steroid sulfatase explains dermatologic scale, an obstetric estriol value, and prolonged labor. Filaggrin explains a histologic finding, a mechanical property of the corneocyte, and the start of an immunologic disease, with cytokines then feeding back on the structural gene. MC1R connects the p53 damage response to endocrine signaling, pigment chemistry, oxidative stress, and DNA repair capacity. Two limits are worth stating. Genotype rarely predicts severity precisely, since variable expressivity, modifier genes, mosaicism, and cumulative exposure all intervene, so a mutation establishes risk rather than outcome. And the presence of a driver mutation is not equivalent to cancer, as BRAF V600E in an ordinary nevus demonstrates, because checkpoint responses such as oncogene-induced senescence still restrain a cell whose growth pathway is switched on."}],"summary":["Cyclin D with CDK4 and CDK6 phosphorylates Rb to release E2F at the restriction point, while p16 blocks those kinases and p53 acting through p21 arrests the damaged cell or removes it by apoptosis.","Ultraviolet B creates pyrimidine dimers whose cytosines deaminate, producing C to T and CC to TT signature mutations; TP53 inactivation by this mechanism is the earliest step toward actinic keratosis and squamous cell carcinoma.","Nucleotide excision repair recognizes the distortion, opens the helix with TFIIH, cuts on both sides, and resynthesizes the gap; its autosomal recessive failure in xeroderma pigmentosum gives extreme photosensitivity, childhood freckling, and all three major skin cancers before adolescence.","PTCH1 loss frees Smoothened so hedgehog and GLI signaling drives basal cell carcinoma, and germline PTCH1 produces Gorlin syndrome with early multiple tumors, jaw keratocysts, palmar pits, and falx calcification.","BRAF V600E is present in most ordinary nevi because oncogene-induced senescence arrests them; melanoma requires escape from that arrest, typically through CDKN2A loss, and germline CDKN2A causes familial melanoma with dysplastic nevi and pancreatic cancer risk.","Autosomal dominant disorders give a 50 percent recurrence risk per pregnancy with frequent new mutations, autosomal recessive disorders give 25 percent with a two in three carrier risk for an unaffected sibling, and X-linked recessive disorders give no male to male transmission and obligate carrier daughters of an affected father.","Neurofibromin is a RAS GTPase-activating protein on chromosome 17, so NF1 leaves RAS active and produces café-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, and neurofibromas; hamartin and tuberin inhibit mTORC1, so tuberous sclerosis leaves mTOR active and produces ash-leaf macules, facial angiofibromas, and hamartomas of brain, heart, and kidney.","Filaggrin bundles keratin and breaks down into natural moisturizing factor, so loss-of-function FLG alleles cause ichthyosis vulgaris with fine scale sparing the flexures and are the main genetic risk factor for atopic dermatitis.","Steroid sulfatase deficiency lets cholesterol sulfate accumulate so corneocytes cannot separate, producing large dark adherent scale in males with corneal opacities and low maternal estriol during the pregnancy.","Alpha MSH acting through MC1R raises cAMP and MITF-driven tyrosinase to favor eumelanin, so loss-of-function MC1R variants shift synthesis to pheomelanin and raise melanoma risk through poor ultraviolet absorption, oxidative damage, and reduced repair capacity."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, sections on cell cycle regulation, oncogenes, tumor suppressor genes, and DNA repair defects"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, sections on disorders of pigmentation and melanocytes and on epidermal tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders, sections on autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, and X-linked inheritance"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"DNA repair and the repair of ultraviolet damage"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin, sections on the epidermis, keratinization, and melanocytes"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia; Genetic Disease"}]},"IN-8":{"unit":"IN-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01","title":"Cell Adaptation, Injury, Pigmentation, and Neoplasia","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows a cell from stress to adaptation, to reversible injury, to death, and in some tissues to neoplasia. It covers hypertrophy, hyperplasia, atrophy, and metaplasia; the mechanisms of injury that converge on ATP depletion, calcium overload, and oxidative damage; the patterns of necrosis and the pathways of apoptosis; the pigments and deposits that record past events in a tissue; and the features that separate a benign tumor from a malignant one. Board questions describe a microscopic change or a clinical setting and ask for the mechanism behind it, so each pattern here is tied to the process that produces it.","prerequisites":["Oxidative phosphorylation, the electron transport chain, and anaerobic glycolysis","Cell cycle control by cyclins and cyclin-dependent kinases, and the G1 to S checkpoint","Membrane transport, especially the Na+/K+-ATPase and calcium handling","Normal epithelial histology: stratified squamous, pseudostratified ciliated columnar, and simple columnar","Innate and adaptive immunity, including macrophage function and cytotoxic T cell killing"],"connections":[{"to":"Skin histology and the epidermal barrier (IN-1, IN-2)","how":"Callus, actinic keratosis, and melanocytic lesions make the epidermis the easiest place to see hyperplasia, dysplasia, and neoplasia in sequence."},{"to":"Inflammation and wound healing (IN-9, IN-10)","how":"Necrosis releases danger signals that start acute inflammation, while apoptosis is cleared silently, which explains why only one of the two leaves a scar."},{"to":"Immunology (IM units)","how":"Fas ligand, TNF, and perforin with granzyme are the immune system's routes into the apoptotic machinery, and PD-L1 expression is how tumors shut that machinery off."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and renal pathology","how":"Coagulative necrosis of the myocardium, dystrophic calcification of atheromas and valves, and metastatic calcification in chronic kidney disease are the same mechanisms in other organs."},{"to":"Biochemistry of free radicals and antioxidants","how":"Superoxide dismutase, catalase, glutathione peroxidase, and vitamins C and E limit the lipid peroxidation that drives both membrane injury and lipofuscin formation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hypertrophy","def":"An increase in cell size from added structural protein and organelles, without an increase in cell number."},{"term":"Metaplasia","def":"Replacement of one mature differentiated cell type by another, through reprogramming of stem or reserve cells rather than transformation of mature cells."},{"term":"Dysplasia","def":"Disordered proliferative growth with cytologic atypia and loss of normal maturation and orientation, still confined by the basement membrane."},{"term":"Reversible injury","def":"Functional and structural change that resolves if the stress is removed, marked by cell swelling and fatty change with intact membranes."},{"term":"Mitochondrial permeability transition pore","def":"A high-conductance channel in the inner mitochondrial membrane whose opening collapses the proton gradient and ends ATP synthesis."},{"term":"Reactive oxygen species","def":"Partially reduced oxygen derivatives, including superoxide, hydrogen peroxide, and hydroxyl radical, that oxidize lipids, proteins, and DNA."},{"term":"Necrosis","def":"Cell death in living tissue with loss of membrane integrity, enzymatic digestion of the cell, and leakage of contents that provokes inflammation."},{"term":"Apoptosis","def":"Regulated, caspase-mediated cell death in which the cell shrinks and fragments into membrane-bound bodies that are phagocytosed without inflammation."},{"term":"Dystrophic calcification","def":"Deposition of calcium salts in dead or damaged tissue while serum calcium is normal."},{"term":"Metastatic calcification","def":"Deposition of calcium salts in otherwise normal tissue because the serum calcium-phosphate product is raised."},{"term":"Anaplasia","def":"Lack of differentiation, shown by pleomorphism, large hyperchromatic nuclei, a high nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio, and atypical mitoses."},{"term":"Metastasis","def":"Growth of tumor implants discontinuous with the primary tumor, the single feature that establishes malignancy."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C01","obj":"IN-8.1","title":"Cellular adaptations and the path to dysplasia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A cell under persistent stress can change its size, its number, or its type, and which one it does depends on its proliferative capacity. Labile tissues such as epidermis, gut, and bone marrow keep a dividing stem cell population and can add cells; stable tissues such as liver and kidney tubules divide only when stimulated; and permanent cells such as cardiac myocytes, skeletal myocytes, and neurons have left the cell cycle, so they adapt by changing cell size rather than cell number."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypertrophy follows mechanical stretch or trophic signaling (growth factors, angiotensin II, alpha-adrenergic agonists) that increases synthesis of contractile protein, so each cell holds more sarcomeres. Hyperplasia follows growth factor or hormone stimulation of cells that can still divide, and in the epidermis the friction of a callus drives basal keratinocyte proliferation with thickening of the stratum corneum. The two often occur together, as in the estrogen-stimulated uterus. Atrophy is the reverse: reduced workload, denervation, ischemia, poor nutrition, loss of a trophic hormone, or pressure shifts the protein balance toward degradation, with ubiquitin tagging proteins for the proteasome and autophagy delivering organelles to lysosomes. Undigested membrane lipid from autophagy remains as lipofuscin, which is why atrophic organs often look brown."},{"type":"p","text":"Metaplasia replaces one mature epithelium with another that tolerates the stress better. The switch happens in stem or reserve cells reprogrammed by signals from the injured environment, not by transformation of a mature cell. Cigarette smoke replaces pseudostratified ciliated columnar bronchial epithelium with stratified squamous epithelium, which resists smoke but cannot clear mucus, so infection risk rises. Chronic acid reflux replaces distal esophageal squamous epithelium with intestinal-type columnar epithelium containing goblet cells (Barrett esophagus), which tolerates acid but carries a risk of adenocarcinoma."},{"type":"table","title":"The four adaptations compared","cols":["Adaptation","Change in the tissue","Driving mechanism","Example"],"rows":[["Hypertrophy","Larger cells, same number","More structural protein in cells that cannot divide","Left ventricle in hypertension; skeletal muscle in training"],["Hyperplasia","More cells","Growth factor or hormone drive on dividing cells","Callus; endometrial hyperplasia from unopposed estrogen"],["Atrophy","Smaller cells, sometimes fewer","Ubiquitin-proteasome degradation plus autophagy","Disuse after immobilization; denervated muscle"],["Metaplasia","Different mature cell type","Reprogramming of stem or reserve cells","Squamous metaplasia of bronchi; Barrett esophagus"]],"caption":"All four persist while the stimulus does and regress once it is withdrawn; none of them involves genetic damage by itself."},{"type":"p","text":"The link to cancer is proliferation sustained over time. Every division risks a replication error, and persistent injury supplies both the mitogenic signal and the mutagen. Dysplasia is the result: atypical nuclei, mitoses above the basal layer, and loss of maturation from base to surface, all still behind an intact basement membrane. Whether an adaptation carries that risk depends on the stimulus, not on the word: pathologic hyperplasia from unopposed estrogen in the endometrium progresses to dysplasia and carcinoma, while benign prostatic hyperplasia and a callus do not."},{"type":"flow","title":"From chronic injury to invasive carcinoma in the bronchus","steps":["Smoke repeatedly injures ciliated columnar epithelium","Reserve cells are reprogrammed to squamous epithelium","Continued injury sustains proliferation; mutations accumulate","Dysplasia: atypia with loss of orientation","Carcinoma in situ: full-thickness atypia, basement membrane intact","Invasive squamous cell carcinoma breaches the basement membrane"],"caption":"Metaplasia itself is reversible; the risk lies in the sustained proliferation that follows it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A sedentary patient begins weight training and a cyclist's calf is immobilized in a cast. Both muscles change size. Why does neither change involve a change in cell number?","explain":"Skeletal muscle fibers are permanent cells that cannot re-enter the cell cycle, so added demand can only add sarcomeres (hypertrophy) and removed demand can only shift the protein balance toward ubiquitin-proteasome degradation and autophagy (atrophy). Satellite cells repair damage but do not expand the fiber population enough to count as hyperplasia.","check":"The proliferative capacity categories in the first paragraph and the adaptations table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C02","obj":"IN-8.2","title":"Mechanisms of cell injury and the reversible to irreversible transition","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most injuries converge on a small number of intracellular events: ATP depletion, mitochondrial damage, rising cytosolic calcium, oxidative stress, and membrane damage. Each one amplifies the others, which is why injury accelerates once it begins."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoxia stops oxidative phosphorylation first, because the electron transport chain has no terminal electron acceptor. ATP falls, and the Na+/K+-ATPase slows, so sodium and water enter and the cell swells while potassium leaks out. The cell switches to anaerobic glycolysis, which consumes glycogen and generates lactate, lowering intracellular pH and clumping nuclear chromatin. Low ATP also releases calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum and allows ribosomes to detach from rough endoplasmic reticulum, so protein synthesis falls. Hypoxia is reduced oxygen delivery from any cause, while ischemia is reduced blood flow, which also cuts off glucose delivery and allows acid and other waste to accumulate. Ischemia therefore injures faster than hypoxia alone, and it also exhausts glycolysis sooner because glucose is no longer being delivered."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytosolic calcium is the central amplifier. Normally held near 0.1 micromolar against a millimolar extracellular concentration, it rises when ATP-dependent pumps fail and membranes leak. Calcium activates phospholipases that strip membrane phospholipid, proteases that cleave cytoskeletal and membrane proteins, endonucleases that fragment DNA, and ATPases that waste what little ATP remains. It also opens the mitochondrial permeability transition pore, which collapses the proton gradient and ends any chance of restoring ATP."},{"type":"p","text":"Oxidative stress arises from leaked electrons in the respiratory chain, from the respiratory burst of neutrophils, from ionizing and ultraviolet radiation, and from Fenton chemistry when free iron or copper is present. Superoxide dismutase, catalase, glutathione peroxidase, and vitamins C and E normally keep these species in check. When they are overwhelmed, hydroxyl radical initiates lipid peroxidation, a chain reaction that propagates along membranes, while protein oxidation inactivates enzymes and DNA oxidation produces strand breaks. Reperfusion is a special case: restoring oxygen to tissue with damaged mitochondria and primed neutrophils generates a burst of radicals and complement activation, so some cells die after flow returns rather than before."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxic cell injury step by step","steps":["Oxidative phosphorylation stops; ATP falls","Na+/K+-ATPase fails; sodium and water enter; the cell swells","Anaerobic glycolysis raises lactate and lowers pH","Ribosomes detach; protein synthesis falls","Cytosolic calcium rises; degradative enzymes are activated","Permeability transition pore opens; ATP recovery becomes impossible","Membranes rupture; contents leak and inflammation follows"],"caption":"Everything above the permeability transition pore is still reversible if perfusion returns; everything below it is not."},{"type":"p","text":"The point of no return is defined by two events: mitochondria that can no longer make ATP, and extensive membrane damage. Before that point the cell shows swelling (hydropic change), clear cytoplasmic vacuoles, surface blebbing, loss of microvilli, dilated endoplasmic reticulum, mitochondrial swelling, and fatty change where lipid accumulates because injured cells cannot export triglyceride. After it, the plasma membrane ruptures and intracellular proteins enter the blood, which is why cardiac troponin becomes detectable once myocytes are irreversibly injured rather than during brief reversible ischemia. Lysosomal rupture releases hydrolases that digest the cell, and the nucleus passes through pyknosis (shrunken, condensed chromatin), karyorrhexis (fragmentation), and karyolysis (dissolution by endonucleases) until no nucleus remains."},{"type":"table","title":"Reversible versus irreversible injury","cols":["Feature","Reversible","Irreversible"],"rows":[["Cytoplasm","Swelling, vacuoles, fatty change","Increased eosinophilia from protein denaturation and RNA loss"],["Plasma membrane","Blebbing, loss of microvilli, intact","Ruptured; intracellular proteins such as troponin leak into blood"],["Mitochondria","Swollen with low-density change","Large amorphous calcium-rich densities"],["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Dilated; ribosomes detached","Fragmented"],["Lysosomes","Intact","Ruptured, releasing hydrolases"],["Nucleus","Chromatin clumping from low pH","Pyknosis, then karyorrhexis, then karyolysis"]],"caption":"Nuclear change and membrane rupture are the decisive findings; swelling and fatty change alone do not mean the cell is dead."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C03","obj":"IN-8.3","title":"Patterns of necrosis and the settings that produce them","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Necrosis is the morphologic result of enzymatic digestion of a cell that has already died, and the pattern depends on which process wins: protein denaturation, which fixes structure in place, or hydrolytic digestion, which liquefies it. Hydrolases come from the dead cell itself (autolysis) or from recruited leukocytes (heterolysis), so the lysosome and leukocyte content of the setting predicts the pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"In coagulative necrosis, ischemic acidosis denatures structural proteins and lysosomal enzymes together, so digestion is delayed and the architecture is preserved for days: cells become firm, intensely eosinophilic, and anucleate with ghost outlines still recognizable, producing a pale infarct, wedge-shaped in the kidney, spleen, and lung where the arterial supply is segmental, and regional in shape in the heart. In liquefactive necrosis, hydrolytic digestion dominates and the tissue becomes a viscous fluid. Two settings produce it: bacterial and some fungal infections, where neutrophils arrive in numbers and release their enzymes, and infarction of the central nervous system, where high lipid content, little fibrous stroma, and abundant neuronal and microglial lysosomal enzymes liquefy the tissue into a fluid-filled cyst. The brain is the standard exception to the rule that ischemia gives coagulative necrosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns of necrosis","cols":["Pattern","Classic setting","Appearance","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Coagulative","Infarct of heart, kidney, or spleen","Firm, pale, eosinophilic ghost cells without nuclei","Acidosis denatures enzymes, so architecture persists"],["Liquefactive","Brain infarct; bacterial abscess","Soft, creamy, or fluid-filled cyst","Hydrolases from leukocytes or neural tissue digest the tissue"],["Caseous","Tuberculosis; some fungal infections","Crumbly cheese-like debris inside a granuloma","Lipid-rich cell wall plus granulomatous walling off"],["Fat","Acute pancreatitis; breast trauma","Chalky white deposits in fat","Lipase frees fatty acids that bind calcium (saponification)"],["Fibrinoid","Immune vasculitis; malignant hypertension; preeclampsia","Bright pink smudgy vessel wall","Immune complexes plus fibrin deposited in the wall"],["Gangrenous","Limb ischemia, usually a lower extremity","Dry mummified tissue, or swollen and foul if infected","Coagulative necrosis, with liquefaction added by bacteria"]],"caption":"Gangrene is a clinical term for coagulative necrosis of a limb, not a separate mechanism; wet gangrene is dry gangrene with superimposed bacterial liquefaction."},{"type":"p","text":"Caseous necrosis is a granulomatous variant in which the mycobacterial lipid-rich cell wall and the walling off by macrophages leave amorphous granular debris, neither fully preserved nor fully liquefied, surrounded by epithelioid macrophages and giant cells. Fat necrosis is enzymatic rather than ischemic: lipase released from pancreatic acinar cells hydrolyzes triglyceride in peripancreatic fat, and the liberated fatty acids combine with calcium to form soaps, a saponification visible as chalky white deposits and detectable as a fall in serum calcium. Fibrinoid necrosis is seen only microscopically, where immune complexes with plasma fibrin give the vessel wall a bright pink smudged appearance."},{"type":"p","text":"Each pattern has a time course that board questions exploit. Nuclear loss and eosinophilia in a myocardial infarct appear over roughly 4 to 12 hours, neutrophils peak at 1 to 3 days, macrophages clear debris over the following week, and granulation tissue and collagenous scar follow over weeks. Tissue that cannot regenerate heals by scar, so a healed cardiac infarct is fibrous while liquefied brain leaves a cyst bordered by glial scar, and debris that is not cleared becomes a site for dystrophic calcification."},{"type":"example","title":"Two infarcts, two patterns","text":"An embolus occludes a branch of the left anterior descending artery, and a second embolus from the same left atrium occludes a middle cerebral artery branch. Both tissues die by the same mechanism of ATP depletion and calcium overload. Two days later the affected myocardium is pale and mottled, made of eosinophilic anucleate fibers whose outlines are still visible, with neutrophils at the margin, because ischemic acidosis denatured the lysosomal enzymes along with the structural proteins. The brain lesion is already softening, because neural tissue is lipid-rich, has little collagen stroma, and supplies abundant hydrolases. Same mechanism of death, different morphology, because the balance of denaturation against digestion differs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C04","obj":"IN-8.4","title":"Apoptosis, cytotoxic killing, and how apoptosis differs from necrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Apoptosis is a programmed, ATP-requiring death in which the cell dismantles itself using caspases, cysteine proteases that cleave after aspartate residues. Initiator caspases (8, 9, and 10) head each pathway and activate executioner caspases (3, 6, and 7), which cleave cytoskeletal and nuclear matrix proteins and activate caspase-activated DNase, which cuts DNA between nucleosomes. The result is a shrunken cell with condensed chromatin whose membrane stays intact."},{"type":"p","text":"The intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway handles DNA damage, misfolded protein accumulation, and withdrawal of survival signals such as growth factors or hormones. It is governed at the outer mitochondrial membrane by the BCL-2 family: antiapoptotic members (BCL-2, BCL-XL, MCL1) hold the membrane closed, proapoptotic effectors BAX and BAK oligomerize to open it, and BH3-only sensors (BIM, BID, PUMA, NOXA, several induced by p53) tip the balance. Once the membrane is permeabilized, cytochrome c enters the cytosol, binds APAF-1 to form the apoptosome, and activates caspase-9. Overexpression of BCL-2, as in follicular lymphoma with the t(14;18) translocation, keeps the membrane shut so damaged cells survive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Intrinsic (mitochondrial) apoptosis","steps":["DNA damage or loss of survival signals activates BH3-only sensors","The sensors neutralize BCL-2 and free BAX and BAK","BAX and BAK permeabilize the outer mitochondrial membrane","Cytochrome c reaches the cytosol and binds APAF-1","The apoptosome activates caspase-9","Caspases 3 and 7 cleave substrates and fragment DNA","Macrophages phagocytose apoptotic bodies; no inflammation"],"caption":"The decision point is the BCL-2 to BAX and BAK balance at the mitochondrion, not the caspase cascade itself."},{"type":"p","text":"The extrinsic (death receptor) pathway begins outside the cell. Fas ligand on an activated T cell crosslinks Fas (CD95), or tumor necrosis factor binds TNF receptor 1, and the clustered death domains recruit adaptor proteins (FADD for Fas) that activate caspase-8. In cells with abundant caspase-8 this is enough; in others caspase-8 cleaves BID to a truncated form that engages the mitochondrial pathway, so the two routes amplify one another. The same system removes self-reactive lymphocytes, and inherited loss of Fas causes an autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes and natural killer cells have a third route. Perforin polymerizes in the target membrane to deliver granzymes into the cytosol, and granzyme B cleaves and activates caspases directly, bypassing both the receptor and the mitochondrion. The same cells also express Fas ligand, so one lymphocyte can use either mechanism. All three routes converge on executioner caspases 3 and 7."},{"type":"table","title":"Apoptosis versus necrosis","cols":["Feature","Apoptosis","Necrosis"],"rows":[["Cell size","Shrunken","Swollen"],["Plasma membrane","Intact; phosphatidylserine flipped outward","Ruptured"],["Energy requirement","ATP dependent and regulated","Follows ATP depletion; unregulated collapse"],["Nucleus","Condensed; internucleosomal DNA fragments","Pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis by random digestion"],["Contents","Retained in apoptotic bodies","Spilled into the interstitium"],["Inflammation","Absent","Present, driven by danger signals"],["Scope","Single scattered cells","Confluent groups of cells"],["Typical cause","Turnover, DNA damage, immune killing","Ischemia, toxins, infection, trauma"]],"caption":"The externalized phosphatidylserine is the eat-me signal; because nothing leaks, no danger signals reach the innate immune system."},{"type":"p","text":"The two deaths are not mutually exclusive. In one infarct, severely ischemic cells die by necrosis while marginal cells that retain some ATP die by apoptosis, and necroptosis blurs the boundary further. Apoptosis also runs physiologically: involution of embryonic structures, loss of hormone-dependent endometrium, keratinocyte death in sunburn, and deletion of self-reactive lymphocytes. When clearance of apoptotic cells fails, nuclear material persists and can drive antinuclear antibody formation, a mechanism relevant to lupus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A cell line lacking BAX and BAK is irradiated, then separately exposed to an activated cytotoxic T cell. In which case does it still die by apoptosis?","explain":"It survives the radiation, because DNA damage signals through p53 and BH3-only proteins to BAX and BAK, and without those effectors the mitochondrion cannot release cytochrome c. The T cell still kills it, because granzyme B activates executioner caspases directly, bypassing the mitochondrion.","check":"The intrinsic apoptosis flow and the paragraph on perforin and granzyme."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C05","obj":"IN-8.5","title":"Endogenous and exogenous pigments","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A pigment is colored material visible in tissue without staining. Endogenous pigments are synthesized or generated by the body, and each one records a specific process; exogenous pigments arrive from outside and persist because macrophages hold them."},{"type":"p","text":"Lipofuscin is the wear and tear pigment, a yellow-brown granular complex of peroxidized membrane lipid and protein that accumulates in lysosomes because it cannot be fully digested. It is not injurious in itself; it is a marker of long-standing free radical damage and lipid peroxidation, and so accumulates with age and with atrophy in heart, liver, and neurons. Gross brown discoloration of an atrophic heart is called brown atrophy. Melanin is the only endogenous brown-black pigment that is deliberately made: melanocytes polymerize tyrosine through tyrosinase into eumelanin and pheomelanin within melanosomes and transfer the melanosomes to keratinocytes, where the pigment caps the nucleus and absorbs ultraviolet radiation."},{"type":"p","text":"Hemosiderin is aggregated ferritin micelles, a golden-brown pigment that forms when iron exceeds the storage capacity of ferritin. Local excess follows hemorrhage: macrophages phagocytose extravasated red cells, degrade hemoglobin, and store the iron, which is why a bruise passes through red-blue (hemoglobin), then green-blue (biliverdin and bilirubin), then golden-brown (hemosiderin). Systemic excess from transfusion, hereditary hemochromatosis, or ineffective erythropoiesis deposits hemosiderin in liver, pancreas, heart, and skin. The Prussian blue (Perls) reaction converts the ferric iron to a blue ferric ferrocyanide, so iron appears blue and is distinguished from lipofuscin and melanin, which do not react. Bilirubin is the yellow-green product of heme catabolism, not an iron-containing pigment; when conjugation or excretion fails or hemolysis overwhelms the liver, it stains skin, sclera, and mucosae as jaundice, and in neonates unconjugated bilirubin can cross the immature blood-brain barrier to stain basal ganglia (kernicterus)."},{"type":"table","title":"Pigments and what each one tells you","cols":["Pigment","Origin","Color and location","Identifying feature"],"rows":[["Lipofuscin","Endogenous: peroxidized lipid in lysosomes","Yellow-brown, perinuclear in heart, liver, neurons","Accumulates with age and atrophy; no iron stain"],["Melanin","Endogenous: tyrosine polymerized by tyrosinase","Brown-black in melanosomes, basal epidermis","Blackens with Fontana-Masson silver; melanocytes are S100 and melan-A positive"],["Hemosiderin","Endogenous: iron beyond ferritin capacity","Golden-brown in macrophages and parenchyma","Stains blue with Prussian blue (Perls)"],["Bilirubin","Endogenous: heme catabolism","Yellow-green in skin, sclera, liver canaliculi","No iron; jaundice when serum level rises"],["Carbon (anthracosis)","Exogenous: inhaled soot","Black in alveolar macrophages and hilar nodes","Inert unless dose is heavy enough for fibrosis"],["Tattoo ink","Exogenous: injected into dermis","Variable, in dermal macrophages and fibroblasts","Persists for life; no inflammation unless allergic"]],"caption":"Hemosiderin, lipofuscin, and melanin all look brown on routine stain, so the iron stain and the clinical setting separate them."},{"type":"p","text":"Exogenous pigments are defined by where they lodge. Inhaled carbon is taken up by alveolar macrophages, carried along lymphatics, and concentrated in hilar and peribronchial lymph nodes, where blackened tissue constitutes anthracosis. In city dwellers the dose is low and the pigment is inert, while in coal workers the heavy dose provokes macrophage activation, fibrogenic cytokine release, and coal workers pneumoconiosis, which shows that an inert pigment becomes injurious at high dose. Tattoo ink is phagocytosed by dermal macrophages and fibroblasts that stay in place rather than migrating to nodes, so the pattern persists for life, with replacement macrophages taking up pigment released when an older cell dies. Delayed hypersensitivity to a pigment, classically red inks containing mercury sulfide, produces a localized granulomatous reaction."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why a bruise changes color","steps":["Red cells extravasate; hemoglobin gives red-purple","Macrophages phagocytose red cells and degrade hemoglobin","Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, seen as green","Biliverdin is reduced to bilirubin, seen as yellow","Released iron exceeds ferritin and is stored as hemosiderin","Golden-brown hemosiderin remains and stains blue with Prussian blue"],"caption":"The color sequence is a visible timeline of heme catabolism inside macrophages."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C06","obj":"IN-8.6","title":"Pathologic calcification and intracellular or extracellular infiltrates","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pathologic calcification is abnormal deposition of calcium salts, usually calcium phosphate in a hydroxyapatite-like crystal, sometimes with iron and other minerals. There are two forms, and serum calcium separates them."},{"type":"p","text":"Dystrophic calcification occurs in dead or dying tissue with a normal serum calcium. It begins when membrane phospholipids of damaged cells and shed membrane vesicles expose phosphate groups that bind calcium; phosphatases in the vesicles generate more phosphate, and the crystal propagates on a protein scaffold. Classic sites are atheromatous plaques, aging or damaged aortic and mitral valves, caseous necrosis in healed tuberculosis, old fat necrosis, and the laminated psammoma bodies of meningioma, papillary thyroid carcinoma, and serous ovarian tumors. Calcification is usually only a marker of previous injury, but in a valve cusp it stiffens the leaflet enough to cause stenosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissue when the serum calcium-phosphate product is raised, as in hyperparathyroidism (primary or secondary to renal failure), bone destruction from metastases or myeloma, vitamin D excess, including sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disease in which macrophages activate vitamin D, and chronic kidney disease with phosphate retention. Deposits favor the interstitium of gastric mucosa, kidney (nephrocalcinosis), lung, systemic arteries, and pulmonary veins, because these tissues lose acid and so have a relatively alkaline internal environment in which calcium phosphate is less soluble."},{"type":"table","title":"Dystrophic versus metastatic calcification","cols":["Feature","Dystrophic","Metastatic"],"rows":[["Serum calcium","Normal","Raised, or raised calcium-phosphate product"],["State of the tissue","Necrotic or chronically damaged","Histologically normal"],["Initiating event","Phosphate on damaged membranes nucleates crystal","Supersaturation of the extracellular fluid"],["Distribution","Local, at the site of injury","Widespread, favoring alkaline interstitium"],["Typical settings","Atheroma, aged valves, caseous necrosis, psammoma bodies","Hyperparathyroidism, chronic kidney disease, vitamin D excess, myeloma"],["Underlying problem","Tissue injury","Calcium and phosphate homeostasis"]],"caption":"The question to ask on a board item is whether the serum calcium is normal; if it is, the lesion came first and the calcium followed."},{"type":"p","text":"Infiltrates are substances that accumulate where they do not belong. Steatosis is triglyceride within parenchymal cells, most often hepatocytes, appearing as clear vacuoles that displace the nucleus. It occurs whenever fatty acid delivery or synthesis outstrips export: alcohol raises NADH and shifts metabolism toward fatty acid synthesis, insulin resistance raises fatty acid delivery from adipose tissue, and protein malnutrition, carbon tetrachloride, and some drugs block apolipoprotein synthesis. Steatosis is reversible, but sustained lipid loading produces oxidative stress and inflammation (steatohepatitis) that can proceed to fibrosis and cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Cholesterol accumulates in macrophages that have taken up modified lipoprotein and cannot export it, producing foam cells with abundant fine vacuoles. Foam cells populate the atheromatous plaque, cluster in dermis and tendons as xanthomas and in the eyelid as xanthelasma, and appear in the gallbladder wall as cholesterolosis. Amyloid is a different kind of deposit: not one substance but any of many proteins misfolded into a beta-pleated sheet fibril, including immunoglobulin light chains in plasma cell dyscrasia (AL), serum amyloid A in chronic inflammation (AA), beta-2 microglobulin in long-term dialysis, transthyretin in familial and senile forms, and islet amyloid polypeptide in type 2 diabetes. Because the fibril is regular and extracellular, Congo red aligns along the fibril axis and produces apple-green birefringence under polarized light. Amyloid causes disease by physically replacing tissue, so the heart stiffens, the kidney leaks protein, and the tongue and nerves enlarge."},{"type":"flow","title":"How alcohol produces hepatic steatosis","steps":["Ethanol oxidation generates excess NADH","High NADH inhibits fatty acid oxidation and favors synthesis","Acetate and acetyl-CoA feed triglyceride assembly","Lipoprotein assembly and export from the hepatocyte fall","Triglyceride accumulates as cytoplasmic vacuoles","Reversible if ethanol exposure stops; sustained loading drives steatohepatitis"],"caption":"Steatosis is an imbalance between fatty acid supply and triglyceride export, not a form of cell death."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C07","obj":"IN-8.7","title":"Benign and malignant neoplasms, naming, grade, and stage","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A neoplasm is a clonal proliferation that has become independent of normal growth controls and continues after the initiating stimulus is gone. That autonomy separates it from hyperplasia, which stops when the stimulus stops. Its transformed parenchymal cells determine its behavior and name; its stroma of connective tissue and vessels is recruited."},{"type":"p","text":"Benign and malignant tumors differ along four lines: differentiation, growth rate, local invasion, and metastasis. Differentiation is how closely tumor cells resemble their normal counterpart, and anaplasia is the morphologic expression of losing it. Growth rate generally tracks differentiation, so a fast-growing tumor may outstrip its blood supply and develop central necrosis. Malignant invasion is infiltrative, so there is no true capsule, while a benign tumor expands and compresses neighboring tissue into a fibrous rim that allows it to be shelled out. Metastasis is the one unequivocally malignant feature: no benign tumor metastasizes."},{"type":"table","title":"Benign versus malignant","cols":["Feature","Benign","Malignant"],"rows":[["Differentiation","Well differentiated, resembles parent tissue","Variable, up to anaplastic"],["Nuclei and mitoses","Uniform; few normal mitoses","Anaplasia: pleomorphism, hyperchromatic coarse nuclei, prominent nucleoli, nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio toward 1 to 1 (normally 1 to 4 or 1 to 6), atypical mitoses, lost polarity, tumor giant cells"],["Growth","Slow, often self-limited","Faster, with necrosis and hemorrhage"],["Border","Expansile, encapsulated","Infiltrative, no true capsule"],["Metastasis","Never","Defining feature when present"],["Effect","Local pressure; sometimes hormone secretion","Local destruction, cachexia, paraneoplastic syndromes"]],"caption":"Benign does not mean harmless: a meningioma compresses the brain and an insulinoma can cause fatal hypoglycemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Naming is the tissue of origin plus a suffix: -oma for a benign mesenchymal tumor, sarcoma for a malignant one, carcinoma for any malignant epithelial tumor, and papilloma, adenoma, or cystadenoma for benign epithelium by pattern. A mixed tumor shows divergent differentiation within one germ layer, as in pleomorphic adenoma, while a teratoma arises from more than one. A hamartoma is a disorganized but mature overgrowth of native tissue and a choristoma is normal tissue in the wrong place; neither is a true neoplasm."},{"type":"table","title":"Naming rules and exceptions","cols":["Tissue of origin","Benign","Malignant"],"rows":[["Squamous epithelium","Squamous papilloma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["Glandular epithelium","Adenoma, cystadenoma","Adenocarcinoma"],["Adipose tissue","Lipoma","Liposarcoma"],["Smooth muscle","Leiomyoma","Leiomyosarcoma"],["Blood vessels","Hemangioma","Angiosarcoma"],["Melanocytes","Nevus","Melanoma"],["Lymphocytes","None","Lymphoma"],["Plasma cells","None","Multiple myeloma"]],"caption":"Melanoma, lymphoma, myeloma, mesothelioma, and seminoma all end in -oma and are malignant."},{"type":"p","text":"Between normal epithelium and invasive cancer lies a graded sequence. Dysplasia is disordered growth with cytologic atypia, mitoses above the basal layer, and loss of orientation and surface maturation, and it is reversible if the stimulus is removed. When atypia occupies the full epithelial thickness but the basement membrane is intact, the lesion is carcinoma in situ, which cannot metastasize because no cell has reached a vessel. Invasion requires loss of E-cadherin adhesion and degradation of collagen IV and laminin by matrix metalloproteinases."},{"type":"p","text":"Grade and stage answer different questions. Grade is microscopic: how far the tumor has departed from its parent tissue, by differentiation, nuclear atypia, and mitotic count. Stage is anatomic: how far it has spread, by TNM, with T for size or depth of invasion, N for regional nodes, M for distant metastasis. Stage predicts outcome better in most solid tumors, because whether the tumor is still locally confined determines whether local control is possible. Carcinomas usually spread first through lymphatics, sarcomas hematogenously to lung."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One colon biopsy shows full-thickness atypia with an intact basement membrane; another shows well differentiated glands that have crossed into the submucosa. Which patient needs node staging?","explain":"The second. The first is carcinoma in situ and cannot have metastasized, because no cell has reached a lymphatic or venule. The second has invaded, so despite its lower grade it can seed nodes, and stage carries more prognostic weight.","check":"The dysplasia to carcinoma in situ sequence and the grade and stage paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-08-01-C08","obj":"IN-8.8","title":"Hallmarks of cancer and the genes behind them","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Carcinogenesis is a multistep accumulation of genetic and epigenetic change in one cell lineage, involving four classes of gene: proto-oncogenes, whose gain of function drives growth; tumor suppressors, whose loss removes brakes; apoptosis regulators; and DNA repair genes."},{"type":"p","text":"Self-sufficiency in growth signals comes from activating mutations anywhere along a growth factor pathway. In BRAF, a single substitution of glutamate for valine at position 600 locks the kinase in its active conformation, so the MAP kinase cascade signals without an upstream growth factor. The same mutation is present in most common acquired nevi and in a large share of melanomas, so one activating mutation is not sufficient for cancer. RAS mutations prevent GTP hydrolysis and leave RAS GTP-bound, while MYC amplification drives cell cycle transcription. Oncogenes act dominantly, because the mutant protein signals whether or not a normal allele is present."},{"type":"p","text":"Insensitivity to growth inhibition comes from losing tumor suppressors, and because one normal allele still makes enough protein, both copies must be lost: the two-hit requirement. In hereditary retinoblastoma the first hit is inherited, so tumors appear early and are often bilateral, while sporadic cases need both hits in one somatic cell. Unphosphorylated RB holds E2F at the G1 to S restriction point until cyclin D with CDK4 or CDK6 phosphorylates RB and frees E2F, so cyclin D amplification, loss of p16, and loss of RB each leave that checkpoint unguarded. TP53 induces p21 for G1 arrest and repair, and proapoptotic proteins such as PUMA when repair fails, so losing both alleles removes arrest and apoptotic backup at once."},{"type":"table","title":"Hallmarks and genes","cols":["Hallmark","Mechanism","Example"],"rows":[["Self-sufficient growth signaling","Gain of function in a growth pathway","BRAF V600E; RAS; HER2"],["Loss of growth inhibition","Two-hit loss of a suppressor","RB; TP53; APC in colon"],["Evasion of apoptosis","Antiapoptotic excess or sensor loss","BCL-2 in follicular lymphoma; TP53"],["Limitless replication","Telomerase maintains telomeres","TERT upregulation"],["Sustained angiogenesis","Shift to proangiogenic factors","VEGF from HIF-1 alpha"],["Invasion and metastasis","Lost adhesion, matrix degradation","E-cadherin loss; metalloproteinases"],["Altered metabolism","Aerobic glycolysis for precursors","Warburg effect; GLUT1 upregulation"],["Immune evasion","Checkpoint ligands; antigen loss","PD-L1; loss of MHC class I"],["Genomic instability","Defective repair raises mutation rate","Mismatch repair loss; BRCA1 and BRCA2"]],"caption":"Most tumors acquire several of these, which is why single-gene explanations fall short."},{"type":"p","text":"Three hallmarks need a mechanistic note. Telomeres shorten with each division and once critically short trigger p53-dependent senescence, so telomerase reactivation makes a lineage effectively immortal. Angiogenesis is required because diffusion supports a cell mass only to roughly 1 to 2 mm, beyond which hypoxia stabilizes hypoxia-inducible factor 1 alpha, which transcribes VEGF. Warburg metabolism favors glycolysis with lactate even when oxygen is available: less ATP per glucose, but a rapid supply of carbon skeletons for nucleotides and lipids."},{"type":"p","text":"Genomic instability is an accelerator rather than a capability: losing a repair gene raises the mutation rate genome-wide, so oncogene activation and suppressor loss arrive faster. The immune system is the outer constraint, because tumor antigens appear on MHC class I and are recognized by cytotoxic T cells, which is why immunocompromised patients develop more malignancies. Tumors escape by losing the antigen, downregulating MHC class I (which leaves them vulnerable to natural killer cells), recruiting regulatory T cells, and expressing PD-L1, which engages PD-1 and switches off the T cell."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a nevus with BRAF V600E stays a nevus","text":"A melanocyte acquires BRAF V600E, so MAP kinase signaling runs without a growth factor and the cell divides to form a nevus. Sustained signaling then induces p16 and engages the p53 pathway while telomeres shorten, so the clone enters oncogene-induced senescence, which is why most nevi are stable for decades. Progression needs further events: loss of p16 or RB to release the G1 checkpoint, TP53 loss or BCL-2 excess to evade apoptosis, telomerase, and VEGF-driven vessels. A growth advantage is not malignancy."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The sequence in this chapter is one continuous story read at different magnifications. Biochemistry sets the first step, because every injury pathway runs through ATP supply, calcium gradients, and the balance between radical production and the antioxidant defenses of superoxide dismutase, catalase, glutathione peroxidase, and vitamins C and E. Histology and anatomy decide what the injury looks like, which is why the same ischemic death produces a firm pale wedge in the heart and a liquefied cyst in the brain, and why the lipid-rich mycobacterial wall gives caseous rather than liquefactive debris. Immunology supplies both the executioners and the brakes: Fas ligand, TNF, and perforin with granzyme are immune routes into the caspase cascade, while PD-L1 and loss of MHC class I are how a tumor turns that same system off."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology then reads the record backward, and that is the reasoning board questions ask for. A brown pigment plus a positive Prussian blue reaction means iron and therefore a previous hemorrhage or iron overload; the same brown color without iron in an atrophic heart means lipofuscin and therefore years of lipid peroxidation. Calcium in damaged tissue with a normal serum calcium points to a local injury that has already happened, while calcium in normal tissue points to a systemic problem of calcium and phosphate handling. Apple-green birefringence under polarized light means a beta-pleated fibril, and the clinical setting then names which protein. The limits are worth stating plainly: morphology gives a pattern, not a cause, several processes can produce the same appearance, and the timing of changes after an injury varies with tissue and with the patient, so the microscope is read together with the history and the laboratory values."}],"summary":["Which adaptation a stressed cell shows depends on whether it can divide: permanent cells only hypertrophy, labile cells can also undergo hyperplasia, and stem cell reprogramming produces metaplasia.","Metaplasia and pathologic hyperplasia carry cancer risk because the stimulus sustains proliferation, and dysplasia then develops behind an intact basement membrane.","Injury converges on ATP depletion, mitochondrial failure, calcium overload, and oxidative damage, and calcium is the amplifier that activates phospholipases, proteases, endonucleases, and the permeability transition pore.","Cell swelling and fatty change are reversible; membrane rupture with enzyme leakage and the nuclear changes pyknosis, karyorrhexis, and karyolysis are not.","The necrosis pattern reflects whether denaturation or digestion dominates: coagulative in infarcts of heart, kidney, and spleen, liquefactive in brain infarcts and abscesses, caseous in tuberculosis, enzymatic fat necrosis in pancreatitis, and fibrinoid in immune vasculitis.","Intrinsic apoptosis is decided by the BCL-2 to BAX and BAK balance, releasing cytochrome c to activate caspase-9; extrinsic apoptosis uses Fas or TNF receptor 1 to activate caspase-8, and granzyme B activates caspases directly.","Apoptosis shrinks a single cell, keeps the membrane intact, and causes no inflammation, while necrosis swells groups of cells, ruptures them, and provokes inflammation.","Prussian blue identifies hemosiderin, lipofuscin marks chronic lipid peroxidation, exogenous carbon concentrates in alveolar macrophages and hilar nodes, and Congo red with apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid.","Dystrophic calcification lands on damaged tissue with a normal serum calcium; metastatic calcification lands on normal tissue because the calcium-phosphate product is raised.","Metastasis alone proves malignancy, carcinoma in situ cannot metastasize because the basement membrane is intact, and stage usually outweighs grade in prognosis because it measures how far the tumor has already gone; getting that far requires oncogene gain, two-hit suppressor loss, apoptosis evasion, telomerase, angiogenesis, and immune evasion together."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Epithelial tissue; skin"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Effector mechanisms of T cell-mediated immunity; immunity to tumors and transplants"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Bioenergetics and oxidative phosphorylation; the pentose phosphate pathway, NADPH, and reactive oxygen species"}]},"IN-9":{"unit":"IN-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-09-01","title":"Infection Principles in Skin Disease","subject":"Integumentary System (INTG)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Skin is colonized from birth yet rarely infected, because a dry, acidic, constantly shedding surface and a resident flora adapted to it deny pathogens the steps they need. This chapter explains that defense, then follows what happens when it fails: how an organism enters, adheres, invades, and spreads, and why the depth it reaches names the disease. It separates damage caused by direct invasion from damage caused by toxins acting at a distance, and it ends with the host defects that invite specific organisms. Board questions usually give a lesion, a depth, or a host context and ask for the mechanism or the organism behind it.","prerequisites":["Epidermal layers, keratinization, and the structure of the stratum corneum barrier (IN-1, IN-2)","Desmosomes, desmogleins, and the dermoepidermal junction (IN-3)","Pilosebaceous unit and sweat gland anatomy, and the composition of sebum (IN-4)","Innate immunity: neutrophil recruitment, pattern recognition receptors, and complement (IM-2, IM-3)","Antigen presentation on MHC class II and T cell receptor structure (IM-5, IM-6)"],"connections":[{"to":"Bacterial, viral, and fungal skin pathogens (IN-12)","how":"This chapter supplies the general rules of entry, depth, and toxin action that the organism-by-organism chapter then applies."},{"to":"Inflammation and wound healing (IN-6, IN-7)","how":"Much of the redness, swelling, and tissue loss in a skin infection is the host inflammatory response, not the organism itself."},{"to":"Immunodeficiency (IM-13)","how":"The pattern of skin infection identifies the arm of immunity that is missing, because neutrophils, T cells, and antibody each cover different organisms."},{"to":"Autoimmune blistering disease (IN-13-H)","how":"Staphylococcal exfoliative toxin and pemphigus foliaceus autoantibodies attack the same protein, desmoglein 1, and split skin at the same level."},{"to":"Lymphatic and venous circulation (CV-12)","how":"Impaired lymphatic drainage and venous stasis leave protein rich interstitial fluid that clears bacteria poorly, which explains recurrent leg cellulitis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Resident flora","def":"Microorganisms that live stably on normal skin and reproduce there without causing disease."},{"term":"Transient flora","def":"Organisms deposited on skin from the environment that do not establish long-term residence and are removed by washing and desquamation."},{"term":"Acid mantle","def":"The slightly acidic film on the skin surface, pH about 4.5 to 5.5, produced by sweat, sebum breakdown, and keratinocyte metabolism."},{"term":"Colonization resistance","def":"Suppression of pathogens by resident flora that occupy attachment sites, consume nutrients, and release inhibitory products."},{"term":"Antimicrobial peptide","def":"A small secreted peptide that damages microbial membranes; the cationic ones, such as beta-defensins and cathelicidin LL-37, are attracted to the anionic microbial surface, while sweat dermcidin is anionic and acts without that attraction."},{"term":"Adhesin","def":"A microbial surface molecule that binds a host structure, for example staphylococcal MSCRAMMs that bind fibronectin and collagen."},{"term":"Invasin or spreading factor","def":"A secreted enzyme that opens a path through tissue, such as hyaluronidase degrading matrix, streptokinase dissolving the fibrin that would contain the organism, or DNase liquefying pus."},{"term":"Exfoliative toxin","def":"A staphylococcal serine protease (ETA, ETB) that cleaves the extracellular domain of desmoglein 1 and separates the upper epidermis."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A toxin that cross-links MHC class II to the T cell receptor variable beta region outside the peptide groove, activating T cells without antigen processing."},{"term":"Nikolsky sign","def":"Separation of the epidermis under light lateral pressure on skin that looks intact, indicating loss of keratinocyte adhesion."},{"term":"Ecthyma gangrenosum","def":"A necrotic ulcer with a black eschar and surrounding erythema, caused by bacterial invasion of dermal vessel walls, classically by Pseudomonas aeruginosa in neutropenia."},{"term":"Portal of entry","def":"The specific break in the barrier, such as a fissure, bite, ulcer, catheter, or injection site, through which an organism reaches living tissue."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-09-01-C01","obj":"IN-9.1","title":"Normal skin flora and how intact skin resists colonization","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal skin carries a dense resident flora that does no harm on intact tissue, and most of these organisms are poorly equipped to invade. Staphylococcus epidermidis is the most abundant resident, a coagulase negative, catalase positive gram positive coccus that is harmless on the surface but forms biofilm readily, so it becomes a real pathogen once foreign material such as a catheter or prosthesis bypasses the barrier. Cutibacterium acnes, formerly called Propionibacterium acnes, is an anaerobic, lipophilic gram positive rod that lives deep in the pilosebaceous unit where oxygen is low and sebum is plentiful. Corynebacterium species, often called diphtheroids, favor moist intertriginous folds, and Malassezia yeasts are lipid dependent and therefore concentrate on sebaceous areas of the scalp, face, and upper trunk. Each organism occupies the niche its metabolism requires, which is why the site of a lesion is itself a clue to the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus aureus is not a true resident of healthy skin, but about 20 to 30 percent of people carry it in the anterior nares at any time. This matters because the carrier's own nasal strain is the usual source of that person's later skin, soft tissue, and surgical site infection, and because carriage explains how an organism with real virulence factors stays close to a barrier it can exploit."},{"type":"table","title":"Resident flora and the niche each one requires","cols":["Organism","Type","Site","Why it lives there"],"rows":[["S epidermidis","Coagulase negative gram positive cocci","Whole surface, most abundant","Tolerates dryness and salt; forms biofilm"],["C acnes","Anaerobic gram positive rod","Deep pilosebaceous unit","Anaerobic and lipophilic; hydrolyzes sebum"],["Corynebacterium","Gram positive rods (diphtheroids)","Axilla, groin, toe webs","Needs moisture and higher pH of folds"],["Malassezia","Lipid dependent yeast","Scalp, face, upper trunk","Cannot make its own long chain fatty acids"],["S aureus","Coagulase positive gram positive cocci","Anterior nares, not healthy skin","Moist mucosa supports carriage"]],"caption":"Flora distribution follows metabolic need, so site of disease predicts organism."},{"type":"p","text":"The defenses of intact skin work by denying a pathogen each step it needs. The stratum corneum is a mechanically tough, water poor sheet of corneocytes in a lipid matrix that few organisms can grow in or cross. The surface sits at pH about 4.5 to 5.5, which slows many pathogens, including S aureus, while the residents tolerate it. Sebum triglycerides are hydrolyzed by host and bacterial lipases into free fatty acids that inhibit many bacteria and dermatophytes, one reason tinea capitis is largely a childhood disease, before sebum output rises at puberty. Desquamation continuously sheds the outer corneocytes with whatever has attached to them. Keratinocytes add beta-defensins and cathelicidin LL-37, both strongly induced by injury and pattern recognition receptor signaling; being cationic, they are drawn to the anionic microbial membrane and disrupt it, while eccrine sweat contributes dermcidin, an unusual anionic peptide that is active without that electrostatic attraction. The resident flora then supply colonization resistance by occupying binding sites, consuming nutrients, and releasing inhibitory products."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Why colonization normally fails","steps":["Organism lands on the surface","Dryness and tight corneocyte barrier limit growth","Acid mantle and sebum free fatty acids inhibit it further","Antimicrobial peptides disrupt its membrane","Resident flora occupy the remaining sites and nutrients","Desquamation sheds it before it establishes"],"caption":"Each defense blocks a different requirement, so single defenses can fail without infection following."},{"type":"table","title":"Each defense and what removes it","cols":["Defense","What breaks it"],"rows":[["Stratum corneum","Cuts, bites, burns, fissures, eczema, maceration, catheters, injections"],["Acid mantle","Alkaline soaps, occlusion, incontinence, chronic exudate"],["Sebum free fatty acids","Prepubertal low sebum output, degreasing agents"],["Desquamation","Retention keratinization disorders, occlusive dressings"],["Antimicrobial peptides","Reduced expression in atopic dermatitis, which helps explain S aureus overgrowth there"],["Colonization resistance","Broad spectrum antibiotics, antiseptic overuse"],["Neutrophil and T cell killing","Neutropenia, diabetes, T cell deficiency, immunosuppression"]],"caption":"Infection usually needs both a barrier break and a reason the next defense could not finish the job."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-09-01-C02","obj":"IN-9.2","title":"Stages of a skin infection and what the depth reached tells you","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A skin infection proceeds through a fixed sequence, and each step must be completed before the next becomes possible. Entry comes first: because intact stratum corneum excludes bacteria, nearly every bacterial skin infection begins at a break that exposes living tissue, such as a cut, bite, burn, fissure, chronic ulcer, follicular opening, or catheter. Adherence follows, mediated by adhesins binding matrix proteins exposed at the break: staphylococcal MSCRAMMs (microbial surface components recognizing adhesive matrix molecules) bind fibronectin, collagen, and fibrinogen, while Streptococcus pyogenes uses protein F, lipoteichoic acid, and M protein, which also resists phagocytosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Colonization becomes invasion when the organism multiplies and secretes enzymes that open a path. Hyaluronidase degrades dermal hyaluronic acid, streptokinase and staphylokinase activate plasminogen and dissolve the fibrin the host lays down to contain the infection, DNase liquefies the DNA of dying neutrophils in pus, and streptolysins and staphylococcal alpha toxin lyse host cells outright. Spread then follows the path anatomy leaves open: superficial lymphatics, the loose subcutis, or the plane of the deep fascia, where blood supply is poor. Damage comes from two sources that are easy to conflate, microbial toxins and the host's own neutrophil proteases, reactive oxygen species, vascular leak, and microvascular thrombosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stages of a bacterial skin infection","steps":["Entry through a breach in the stratum corneum","Adherence by adhesins to fibronectin, collagen, or fibrinogen","Multiplication and local colonization","Invasion using hyaluronidase, kinases, and DNase","Spread along lymphatics, subcutis, or fascial plane","Damage from microbial toxins plus host inflammation"],"caption":"Each stage must be completed, so interrupting any one of them prevents the disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Depth determines both appearance and severity. In the upper dermis and its superficial lymphatics, lymphatic anatomy contains the process, which produces a raised, sharply demarcated plaque; in the lower dermis and fat there is no such boundary, so the edge is flat and indistinct. Infection in the fascial plane destroys the perforating vessels and small nerves crossing it, which is why pain is out of proportion to the visible skin, the skin can become anesthetic, and spread is fast with little surface change at first."},{"type":"table","title":"Depth ladder of skin and soft tissue infection","cols":["Disease","Level","Usual organism","Appearance"],"rows":[["Impetigo","Superficial epidermis","S aureus, S pyogenes","Honey colored crust; flaccid bullae only in the toxin mediated bullous form, which is S aureus"],["Folliculitis","Follicular ostium","S aureus; Pseudomonas after hot tubs","Pustules centered on hairs"],["Furuncle, carbuncle","Whole follicle into dermis; carbuncle spans several","S aureus","Tender nodule, then fluctuance"],["Erysipelas","Upper dermis and superficial lymphatics","S pyogenes","Raised, sharply demarcated plaque, early fever"],["Cellulitis","Lower dermis and subcutaneous fat","S pyogenes, S aureus","Flat, warm, poorly demarcated erythema"],["Abscess","Dermis or subcutis, walled off","S aureus","Fluctuant collection of pus"],["Necrotizing fasciitis","Deep fascia","S pyogenes (type II) or mixed aerobes and anaerobes (type I)","Pain out of proportion, anesthesia, crepitus with gas formers"]],"caption":"Border definition and systemic severity both track with depth."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from border to level","text":"A patient has a tender, bright red facial plaque whose edge you can trace with a fingertip, and fever began within hours. A sharp palpable border places the process in the upper dermis and its superficial lymphatics, which contain spread, so this is erysipelas and the organism is S pyogenes. Had the erythema faded into normal skin with no palpable edge, the level would be the lower dermis and fat, where no boundary exists, and S aureus would be plausible as well. Had the pain far exceeded the appearance with numb overlying skin, the level would be fascia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A diabetic patient with fissured interdigital tinea pedis develops erythema spreading up the leg with no palpable border. Which stage did the fungus supply, and what does the absent border say about depth?","explain":"Name the stage, the level reached, and why the edge is indistinct.","check":"Compare with the stages flow and the depth ladder."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-09-01-C03","obj":"IN-9.3","title":"Invasion versus toxin: exfoliative toxins and superantigens","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Skin disease from a bacterium follows one of two mechanisms, and separating them explains findings that otherwise look contradictory. In direct invasion the organism is in the lesion, so the lesion yields it on culture and damage concentrates where the bacteria are. In toxin mediated disease the organism may sit at a distant, trivial focus while its toxin travels in the blood, so the affected skin is sterile and widely involved."},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome is the clearest example of a toxin acting at a distance. Exfoliative toxins A and B are glutamate specific serine proteases that cleave the extracellular domain of desmoglein 1, the desmosomal cadherin holding keratinocytes together in the superficial epidermis. Cutting it splits the skin high, within the stratum granulosum, giving flaccid blisters, a positive Nikolsky sign, and sheets of peeling that heal without scarring because dermis and basal layer are untouched. Mucosa is spared because it relies chiefly on desmoglein 3. The focus is usually the nose, conjunctiva, or umbilicus, so blister fluid is sterile and culture must come from there. The toxin is cleared renally, so infants, whose clearance is immature and who lack protective antitoxin antibody, and adults with renal impairment reach damaging systemic levels. Bullous impetigo is the same toxin acting locally, so its lesions do contain organisms."},{"type":"p","text":"Superantigens damage skin by a different route: they do not injure keratinocytes at all, they hijack T cell activation. Staphylococcal TSST-1 and the streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins bind MHC class II and the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain at once, outside the peptide binding groove. Antigen processing and peptide specificity are bypassed, so every T cell carrying a susceptible V beta family activates regardless of what it recognizes, up to roughly a fifth of the repertoire. The resulting flood of interleukin 2, tumor necrosis factor, and interferon gamma produces high fever (at or above 38.9 degrees Celsius, 102 degrees Fahrenheit), diffuse erythema resembling sunburn, capillary leak with shock, and later desquamation of palms and soles. Staphylococcal toxic shock often has no bacteremia and only a colonized focus, whereas streptococcal toxic shock accompanies invasive infection."},{"type":"flow","title":"Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","steps":["S aureus colonizes a distant focus: nose, umbilicus, conjunctiva","Exfoliative toxins A and B enter the circulation","Poor renal clearance allows systemic toxin levels","Toxin cleaves desmoglein 1 in the stratum granulosum","Flaccid, sterile bullae with positive Nikolsky sign"],"caption":"Organism and lesion sit apart, which is why the blister is sterile."},{"type":"flow","title":"Superantigen mediated shock","steps":["TSST-1 or a streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin is released","Toxin bridges MHC class II and the T cell receptor V beta region","Up to about a fifth of T cells activate without antigen processing","Massive IL-2, TNF, and interferon gamma release","Fever, diffuse erythema, capillary leak, shock"],"caption":"The rash is cytokine driven vasodilation, not bacteria in skin."},{"type":"table","title":"Superficial blistering: three attacks on the same adhesion system","cols":["Feature","Scalded skin syndrome","Pemphigus foliaceus","Toxic epidermal necrolysis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Protease cleaves desmoglein 1","IgG autoantibody to desmoglein 1","Drug triggered keratinocyte apoptosis"],["Split level","Stratum granulosum","Stratum granulosum","Subepidermal, with necrosis of the full epidermal thickness"],["Mucosa","Spared","Spared","Involved"],["Organism in lesion","No, focus is distant","No","No"],["Typical host","Infants; renal impairment","Adults","Days to weeks after a drug"]],"caption":"A protease and an autoantibody hit desmoglein 1 at the same level; TEN splits lower."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out which mechanism is operating","text":"An infant has nasal crusting and widespread flaccid blisters, with negative blister culture and a normal mouth. Sterile, widely involved skin excludes invasion there, so a circulating toxin is acting, and a high split with spared mucosa identifies desmoglein 1 cleavage."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does staphylococcal toxic shock often have negative blood cultures while streptococcal toxic shock does not?","explain":"Relate the answer to where each organism sits and what circulates.","check":"Compare with the superantigen flow and the first paragraph here."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-INTG-09-01-C04","obj":"IN-9.4","title":"Host predisposition and predicting the organism from context","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Host factors are not vague risk; each removes a specific defense, and the defense that is gone predicts the organism. Diabetes mellitus removes three at once: neuropathy abolishes the pain that would have revealed a wound, vascular disease limits delivery of oxygen and leukocytes, and sustained hyperglycemia impairs neutrophil chemotaxis, phagocytosis, and oxidative killing. The diabetic foot therefore carries a chronic ulcer colonized from skin and bowel flora, which is why established infection there is polymicrobial. Ketoacidosis adds a separate problem: acidosis releases iron from transferrin and Rhizopus possesses a ketone reductase, so mucormycosis clusters in that metabolic state."},{"type":"p","text":"Venous insufficiency and lymphedema act through drainage rather than killing. Protein rich fluid that is not cleared supports bacterial growth, and poor lymphatic flow delays delivery of organisms and antigen to regional nodes where a response would be mounted. Beta hemolytic streptococci thrive here, and each episode scars lymphatics further, so recurrence becomes self reinforcing. Tinea pedis matters because macerated interdigital fissures are a reliable portal for those same streptococci, which is why interdigital disease and recurrent leg cellulitis travel together. Injection drug use delivers surface and oral flora straight into dermis, subcutis, or vein, so S aureus including methicillin resistant strains dominates, and spore forming Clostridium species may follow contaminated material into devitalized tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Immune cell defects give the most predictable patterns. Neutrophils are the main defense against pyogenic bacteria, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and molds, so neutropenic patients develop Pseudomonas and Aspergillus or Fusarium lesions, and because pus is largely neutrophils those lesions are necrotic rather than purulent. Ecthyma gangrenosum shows the mechanism directly: bacteria invade dermal vessel walls, thrombose them, and infarct the overlying skin into a black eschar. T cells control intracellular and chronic surface pathogens, so T cell deficiency from advanced HIV infection or transplant immunosuppression brings severe or disseminated herpes simplex and varicella zoster, persistent molluscum and warts, and mucocutaneous candidiasis, with the Th17 and interleukin 17 axis specifically responsible for keeping Candida off skin and mucosa."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The lymphedema and cellulitis cycle","steps":["Impaired lymphatic drainage leaves protein rich fluid","Bacterial clearance and antigen delivery to nodes are delayed","Streptococcal cellulitis develops","Inflammation scars lymphatic channels further","Drainage worsens, so the next episode comes more easily"],"caption":"Each episode increases susceptibility, which explains recurrence in the same limb."},{"type":"table","title":"Host defect, defense lost, and expected organism","cols":["Host context","Defense removed","Expected organism"],"rows":[["Diabetic foot ulcer","Sensation, perfusion, neutrophil function","Polymicrobial: S aureus, streptococci, gram negative rods, anaerobes"],["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Iron sequestration and phagocyte function","Rhizopus and other Mucorales"],["Lymphedema or venous stasis","Lymphatic clearance","Beta hemolytic streptococci, chiefly group A"],["Interdigital tinea pedis","Barrier at a portal of entry","Beta hemolytic streptococci ascending the leg"],["Injection drug use","Barrier bypassed into deeper tissue","S aureus including MRSA; Clostridium in devitalized tissue"],["Neutropenia","Neutrophil killing","Pseudomonas (ecthyma gangrenosum), Aspergillus, Fusarium"],["Advanced HIV or transplant","T cell control","HSV, VZV, extensive molluscum and warts, mucocutaneous Candida"],["Atopic dermatitis","Barrier and antimicrobial peptides","S aureus colonization; eczema herpeticum with HSV"]],"caption":"Read the question backwards: the missing defense names the organism."},{"type":"table","title":"Exposure clues that override host factors","cols":["Exposure","Organism"],"rows":[["Hot tub","Pseudomonas aeruginosa folliculitis"],["Cat bite or scratch","Pasteurella multocida"],["Dog bite","Pasteurella, Capnocytophaga canimorsus"],["Human bite","Eikenella corrodens with oral anaerobes"],["Fresh water laceration","Aeromonas hydrophila"],["Salt water or raw shellfish with cirrhosis","Vibrio vulnificus, hemorrhagic bullae"],["Fish handling","Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae"],["Thorn or moss injury","Sporothrix schenckii, nodules along lymphatics"]],"caption":"A stated exposure usually determines the organism directly."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A neutropenic patient and a patient with advanced HIV infection both develop skin lesions. Why does the first get a necrotic ulcer with little pus and the second get widespread vesicles?","explain":"Base the answer on what each cell type defends against and on what pus is made of.","check":"Compare with the third paragraph of this section and the host defect table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology set the rules this chapter follows. The layered structure of skin and the position of lymphatics, loose subcutaneous tissue, and deep fascia decide how far an organism can spread and therefore what the lesion looks like, while the molecular biology of the desmosome explains why one toxin splits the epidermis high and spares mucosa. Microbiology supplies the virulence factors, immunology supplies the defenses and the two toxin mechanisms, and pathology supplies the final common pathway, since neutrophil proteases, vascular leak, and microvascular thrombosis cause much of the visible damage in both invasive and toxin mediated disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Reasoning on the exam runs in either direction. Given a lesion, work from depth and border to level, then to the organisms that reach that level. Given a host, work from the defense that is missing to the organisms it was holding back. Both chains have limits worth respecting: depth and appearance overlap in early disease, cultures of superficial lesions and ulcers are often contaminated with colonizers rather than the causative organism, carriage of an organism is not the same as infection by it, and an exposure history in a vignette frequently outweighs the host factors. Where two mechanisms could produce the same picture, the distinguishing evidence is usually the level of the split, whether the affected skin itself contains organisms, and whether mucosa is involved."}],"summary":["Resident flora occupy niches set by their metabolism: S epidermidis across the surface, C acnes deep in sebaceous follicles, Corynebacterium in moist folds, Malassezia where sebum lipids are plentiful.","Intact skin resists colonization through dryness, an acid mantle of pH about 4.5 to 5.5, sebum derived free fatty acids, desquamation, antimicrobial peptides (beta-defensins, cathelicidin LL-37, dermcidin), and colonization resistance from the flora themselves.","S aureus is carried in the anterior nares by about 20 to 30 percent of people, and the carrier's own strain is the usual source of later skin and surgical site infection.","Infection proceeds through entry, adherence by adhesins to matrix proteins, invasion using hyaluronidase, kinases, and DNase, spread along tissue planes, then damage from both toxins and host inflammation.","Depth names the disease: epidermis in impetigo, follicle in folliculitis, upper dermis and lymphatics in erysipelas with a sharp raised border, lower dermis and fat in cellulitis with an indistinct edge, walled off pus in abscess, and fascia in necrotizing fasciitis.","Pain out of proportion to the skin, anesthesia, and rapid spread indicate fascial involvement, because perforating vessels and nerves are destroyed before the surface changes.","Exfoliative toxins A and B cleave desmoglein 1 in the stratum granulosum, producing sterile flaccid bullae with a positive Nikolsky sign and spared mucosa, since mucosa relies on desmoglein 3.","Scalded skin syndrome affects infants and adults with renal impairment because the toxin is cleared renally, and the staphylococcal focus is distant, so culture the focus rather than the blister.","Superantigens bridge MHC class II and the T cell receptor V beta region outside the peptide groove, activating up to about a fifth of T cells and producing cytokine driven fever, diffuse erythema, shock, and late palmar desquamation.","The missing defense predicts the organism: polymicrobial infection in the diabetic foot, group A streptococci with lymphedema or tinea fissures, S aureus with injection drug use, Pseudomonas and molds in neutropenia, and herpesviruses, molluscum, and Candida in T cell deficiency."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Normal human microbiota"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The staphylococci; the streptococci, enterococci, and related genera"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Skin, infections and inflammatory disorders"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity; congenital and acquired immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious diseases"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Skin"}]}});
